loop for iconic photos.","Always check under your boots and camping gear for desert insects like scorpions or beetles."],nearbyAttractions:["Goldfield Ghost Town","Canyon Lake","Apache Trail Scenic Byway","Superstition Mountain Museum"]},{id:"red-rock-state-park",name:"Red Rock State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Day-use park near Sedona protecting Oak Creek's streamside habitat in a red sandstone canyon.",fullDescription:"Red Rock State Park covers 286 acres five miles southwest of Sedona, centered on a red sandstone canyon along Oak Creek. It is a day-use park focused on preserving the creek's riparian habitat and on environmental education. It has a visitor center with exhibits and about 5 miles of trails.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Visitor center","Birdwatching","Junior Ranger programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lime Kiln Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The Lime Kiln Trail connects Dead Horse Ranch State Park and Red Rock State Park."}],campingInfo:"Check the official park website for current camping details.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Javelina","Coyotes","Skunks","Herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Red Rock State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(928) 282-6907"},hours:"Park 8:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m. (last entry 4:30 p.m.); Visitor Center 9:00 a.m.–4:30 p.m. Thanksgiving and Christmas Eve 8:00 a.m.–2:00 p.m.; closed Christmas.",entranceFees:"Adult (14+) $10; Youth (7–13) $5; Child (0–6) free; Individual/walk-in $5",address:"4050 Red Rock Loop Rd, Sedona, AZ 86336",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Red%20Rock%20State%20Park%20arizona",officialWebsite:"https://azstateparks.com/red-rock",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall are generally the most comfortable seasons for hiking the park's 5-mile trail system along Oak Creek, and birders can time a visit for the guided bird walks held Wednesdays and Saturdays.",tips:["Leave pets at home. Pets are not allowed and swimming is not permitted in the park.","Join the free-with-entry daily guided nature walk at 10 a.m. led by a volunteer naturalist covering geology, wildlife, history and plants.","Arrive by 4:30 p.m., the last entry time. Some programs, such as the geology and archaeology hikes and the star party, require reservations or registration."],nearbyAttractions:["Dead Horse Ranch State Park","Cathedral Rock","Bell Rock"]},{id:"catalina-state-park",name:"Catalina State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Tucson-area park at the base of the Santa Catalina Mountains with nearly 5,000 saguaros.",fullDescription:"Catalina State Park covers 5,500 acres of foothills and canyons at the base of the Santa Catalina Mountains, minutes from Tucson, at nearly 3,000 feet. It is home to nearly 5,000 saguaros and more than 150 bird species. Trails include the Romero Ruin, Romero Canyon and Canyon Loop trails, some connecting into Coronado National Forest.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Birdwatching","Biking","Horseback riding","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1 mile",description:"An easy hike with about 124 ft of elevation gain, used for the park's guided Catalina Nights hikes."},{name:"50-Year Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"8.6 miles",description:"A moderate to intermediate 8.6-mile route through desert mountain terrain, popular with mountain bikers."},{name:"Canyon Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy to Moderate",length:"1 mile",description:"A 1-mile loop described as an easy to moderate ride."}],campingInfo:"The park has 120 campsites with electric and water hookups, reservable at (877) MY-PARKS (877-697-2757) or online.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Coyote","Javelina","Mule deer","Coues whitetail deer","Coatimundi"],visitorCenter:{name:"Catalina State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the 