llDescription:"Dead Horse Ranch State Park covers 423 acres on the Verde River at about 3,300 feet near Cottonwood, in the Verde River Greenway. It has 10 miles of hiking trails, about 150 campsites and trailhead access to the Dead Horse Trail System in Coconino National Forest. It hosts the Verde Valley Birding and Nature Festival each April.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Birdwatching","Horseback riding","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Dead Horse Trail System",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"20+ miles",description:"A non-motorized, shared-use mix of doubletrack and singletrack with four trailheads in the park leading up to the high desert."}],campingInfo:"The park offers RV and tent camping and eight river-adjacent log camping cabins.",fishingBoating:"Three lagoons in the park offer fishing, and canoeing and kayaking are popular on the river and lagoons.",wildlife:["Bald eagle","Skunk","Tarantula","Scorpion","Snakes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Dead Horse Ranch State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(928) 634-5283"},hours:"Open year-round; gates close at dark. Day use: Fall/Winter 7 a.m.–6 p.m., Spring 6 a.m.–7 p.m., Summer (from June 8) 6 a.m.–8 p.m. Visitor Center/Park Store 8 a.m.–4:30 p.m. daily. Thanksgiving and Christmas Eve 8 a.m.–2 p.m.; closed Christmas.",entranceFees:"$10 per vehicle (1 person); $20 per vehicle (2–4 people); $5 individual/bicycle",address:"675 Dead Horse Ranch Road, Cottonwood, AZ 86326",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Dead%20Horse%20Ranch%20State%20Park%20arizona",officialWebsite:"https://azstateparks.com/dead-horse",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall are good for hiking in this high-desert park, but check the seasonal day-use hours before you go.",tips:["Campers who arrive before 12 noon on their arrival day are charged the park entrance fee.","Check-in for reservations is 2 p.m. and checkout is noon.","Gates close at dark, hours change by season, and the park is closed on Christmas."],nearbyAttractions:["Fort Verde State Historic Park","Coconino National Forest"]},{id:"tonto-natural-bridge-state-park",name:"Tonto Natural Bridge State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/80755d269dc8886d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Arizona park near Payson with what is believed to be the world's largest natural travertine bridge.",fullDescription:"Tonto Natural Bridge State Park lies off State Route 87, ten miles north of Payson. Its natural bridge, believed to be the largest travertine bridge in the world, stands 183 feet high over a 400-foot-long tunnel up to 150 feet wide. Trails lead to viewpoints and down to the creek, and the Gowan family's historic lodge still stands.",thingsToDo:["Hiking to the bridge","Viewpoints","Historic lodge"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Gowan Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"Not listed",description:"Open as of the latest official update; like all park trails it is under a half mile but steep and strenuous."},{name:"Waterfall Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"Not listed",description:"Open as of the latest official update; like all park trails it is under a half mile but steep and strenuous."},{name:"Pine Creek Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"Not listed",description:"Currently closed due to rain and flooding, according to the official park page."}],campingInfo:"No camping in the park.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Tonto Natural Bridge State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(928) 476-4202"},hours:"9:00 a.m.–5:00 p.m. (last entry 4:00 p.m.); trails close one hour before the park closes",entranceFees:"Adult (14+) $10; Youth (7–13) $5; Child (0–6) free",address:"Nf-583A, Pine, AZ 85544",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tonto%20Natural%20Bridge%20State%20Park%20arizona",officialWebsite:"https://azstateparks.com/tonto",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring through fall is pleasant for hiking in this Mogollon Rim country park, but check trail status after rain, since flooding can close the trails below the bridge.",tips:["All trails are steep and strenuous and may take an hour or more, so pack plenty of water.","Dogs are not allowed on the trails.","Arrive early: last entry is at 4:00 p.m., and trails close one hour before the park closes."],nearbyAttractions:[]},{id:"picacho-peak-state-park",name:"Picacho Peak State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/f72165fdf57c16b6.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Arizona park around 3,374-foot Picacho Peak between Phoenix and Tucson, known for spring wildflowers.",fullDescription:"Picacho Peak State Park surrounds Picacho Peak, a spire rising to 3,374 feet beside I-10 between Casa Grande and Tucson. Trails to the summit use cables and catwalks on steep sections. The park is near the site of the Battle of Picacho Pass, a Civil War skirmish in Arizona Territory, and is known for spring wildflowers.",thingsToDo:["Summit hike (cables)","Wildflower viewing (mid-Feb to early April)","Hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hunter Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The Hunter Trail climbs to the top of Picacho Peak, where hikers can enjoy 360-degree views."},{name:"Sunset Vista Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The scenic Sunset Vista Trail leads to the top of Picacho Peak."},{name:"Calloway Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The Calloway Trail leads up to the Calloway overlook, which sits under the face of Picacho Peak."}],campingInfo:"The park's campground has 78 electric sites suitable for both tent and RV camping.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["black-tailed jackrabbit","desert cottontail rabbit","round-tail ground squirrel","Harris antelope squirrel","rock squirrel"],visitorCenter:{name:"Picacho Peak State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(520) 466-3183"},hours:"Park open year-round 5 a.m.–8 p.m. (gates close); trails open sunrise to sunset; Visitor Center 8 a.m.–5 p.m. Sept. 26–May 1 and 9 a.m.–3 p.m. May 2–Sept. 25; closed Christmas.",entranceFees:"$10 per vehicle (1 person); $20 per vehicle (2-4 people); $5 individual/bicycle",address:"I-10 Exit 219, Picacho Peak, AZ (mailing: P.O. Box 907, Eloy, AZ 85131)",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Picacho%20Peak%20State%20Park%20arizona",officialWebsite:"https://azstateparks.com/picacho",bestTimeToVisit:"October through April is the best time to visit, with milder desert weather and ranger-guided hikes, and spring often brings wildflowers.",tips:["Hike prepared: bring plenty of food and water and wear proper footwear.","Trails are closed from sunset to sunrise, so plan to finish your hike before dark.","One trail to the peak is rated moderate for the first 2 miles, then becomes challenging because hikers must use cables to climb to the summit."],nearbyAttractions:["Rooster Cogburn Ostrich Ranch","Skydive Arizona","Casa Grande Ruins National Monument"]}],california:[{id:"emerald-bay-state-park",name:"Emerald Bay State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/367470e69b351156.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"One of Lake Tahoes most iconic and photographed bays, featuring Fannette Island and the historic Vikingsholm Castle.",fullDescription:"Emerald Bay State Park is a jaw-dropping national natural landmark nestled on Lake Tahoe's west shore. Known for its brilliant blue-green waters, steep pine-clad granite peaks, and Tahoe's only island (Fannette Island), it is one of the most scenic gems in California. The park is home to Vikingsholm, a stunning 38-room mansion constructed in 1929, which stands as one of the finest examples of Scandinavian architecture in the United States. Visitors can hike, kayak, and take in the majestic clifftop vistas.",thingsToDo:["Touring the historic Vikingsholm Castle","Kayaking or paddleboarding to Fannette Island","Viewing Eagle Falls","Scuba diving in the Emerald Bay Maritime Heritage Trail"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Rubicon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.5 miles",description:"A beautiful trail tracing the lakeside cliffs from Emerald Bay to D.L. Bliss State Park, offering continuous, stunning vistas of Lake Tahoe's crystal-clear waters."},{name:"Vikingsholm Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.0 miles",description:"A steep downhill walk from the highway parking lot to the shoreline of the bay, leading directly to the historic Vikingsholm mansion."}],campingInfo:"The park features the Eagle Point Campground (94 sites, closed in winter) and a unique, boat-in campground situated on the lake shoreline with primitive campsites.",fishingBoating:"Boating is highly popular inside Emerald Bay. Motorized boat speed is strictly regulated. Kayak and paddleboard rentals are available at the beach. Fishing is excellent for Mackinaw and Rainbow trout.",wildlife:["Black bears (frequent)","Bald eagles","Osprey (nesting on Fannette Island)","Steller's jays","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Vikingsholm Visitor Center",info:"Provides park details, historical information about Mrs. Lora Knight's estate, and tickets for castle tours.",phone:"(530) 541-3030",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM (Summer only)"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily (Road closures can occur in winter).",entranceFees:"$10.00 per vehicle daily parking fee.",address:"138 Emerald Bay Rd, South Lake Tahoe, CA 96150",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Emerald+Bay+State+Park+Tahoe+CA",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=506",bestTimeToVisit:"June to September for lake sports and castle tours, and October for quiet hiking and beautiful crisp weather.",tips:["The hike down to Vikingsholm is 1 mile long and drops 400 feet; the walk back up is steep, so pace yourself.","Rent a kayak early in the morning from South Lake Tahoe or Baldwin Beach to enjoy flat, glassy water inside the bay.","Strictly store all food inside bear-proof lockers, as active black bears roam the campgrounds."],nearbyAttractions:["D.L. Bliss State Park","Tallac Historic Site","South Lake Tahoe Downtown","Heavenly Mountain Resort"]},{id:"big-basin-redwoods",name:"Big Basin Redwoods State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0dc4535ed475b433.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Californias oldest state park, famous for its ancient, towering coast redwood trees.",fullDescription:"Big Basin Redwoods State Park, established in 1902, is California's oldest state park. Located in the Santa Cruz Mountains, it is home to the largest continuous stand of ancient Coast Redwoods south of San Francisco. Some of these colossal trees are over 1,000 to 1,800 years old, reaching heights of more than 300 feet and circumferences of 50 feet. The park is a lush, mossy coastal rainforest with cascading waterfalls, bubbling streams, and over 80 miles of hiking trails.",thingsToDo:["Hiking amongst ancient giant redwoods","Viewing Berry Creek Falls","Participating in guided Redwood walks","Stargazing in deep mountain canyons"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Redwood Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.6 miles",description:"A flat, fully accessible loop leading past some of the park's most famous giant trees, including the Mother of the Forest and Father of the Forest."},{name:"Skyline-to-the-Sea Trail (Section)",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"6.0 miles",description:"Winds downhill through redwood groves and lush fern canyons, tracing Waddell Creek towards the Pacific ocean."}],campingInfo:"Campgrounds are recovering from wildfire; limited drive-in campsites and walk-in tent sites are available by reservation only. Backpacking sites on the Skyline-to-the-Sea route require wilderness permits.",fishingBoating:"No boating is available. Limited stream-side fishing is permitted in Waddell Creek seasonally, subject to California Department of Fish and Wildlife rules.",wildlife:["Coast redwood salamanders","Black-tailed deer","Steller's jays","Banana slugs (the iconic yellow mascot of the redwood forest)","Bobcats"],visitorCenter:{name:"Big Basin Temporary Visitor Center",info:"Provides trail updates, park maps, and educational exhibits on redwood ecology and forest fire recovery.",phone:"(831) 338-8860",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},hours:"8:00 AM to Sunset daily.",entranceFees:"$10.00 per vehicle parking fee (Day-use reservations are highly recommended).",address:"21600 Big Basin Way, Boulder Creek, CA 95006",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Big+Basin+Redwoods+State+Park+Boulder+Creek+CA",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=540",bestTimeToVisit:"April to June for roaring waterfalls and lush ferns, or October for crisp, quiet mountain trail walks.",tips:["Keep an eye out on the damp trail beds for bright yellow Banana Slugs—they are a beloved, unique symbol of the old-growth forest.","Due to post-fire recovery, some trails may have detours; check in with rangers at the visitor center for the latest map updates.","Cell service is non-existent in the deep canyons; download offline maps and trails before departing."],nearbyAttractions:["Henry Cowell Redwoods State Park","Santa Cruz Beach Boardwalk","Roaring Camp Railroads","Castle Rock State Park"]},{id:"anza-borrego-desert-state-park",name:"Anza-Borrego Desert State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e0095b8030236405.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"California's largest state park: 650,000 acres of Colorado Desert around Borrego Springs.",fullDescription:"Anza-Borrego Desert State Park, created in 1932, covers about 650,000 acres of the Colorado Desert, including one-fifth of San Diego County, making it California's largest state park. It is named for Spanish explorer Juan Bautista de Anza and 'borrego', Spanish for sheep. Wildlife includes bighorn sheep, roadrunners and golden eagles, and spring wildflower blooms draw visitors.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Wildflower viewing (spring)","Desert driving","Bird watching","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Borrego Palm Canyon Nature Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3 miles",description:"A rocky round-trip trail to a palm oasis with about 700 feet of elevation gain that takes at least two hours."},{name:"Visitor Center/Campground Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This is the trail in the park where dogs are allowed."}],campingInfo:"Camping includes Borrego Palm Canyon and Tamarisk Grove campgrounds, Vern Whitaker Horse Camp and remote backcountry camping, with reservations available October 1 through April 30.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Peninsular bighorn sheep","Roadrunner","Desert cottontail","Black-tailed jackrabbit","Coyote"],visitorCenter:{name:"Anza-Borrego Desert State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(760) 767-4205"},hours:"Visitor Center: Oct 1–May 31 daily 9 a.m.–5 p.m.; summer (June 1–Sept 30) Fri–Sun 9 a.m.–5 p.m., including holidays. Borrego Palm Canyon Trailhead parking: sunrise to sunset.",entranceFees:"$10 vehicle day use ($9 seniors 62+); special rates may apply at peak times",address:"200 Palm Canyon Dr, Borrego Springs, CA 92004",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Anza-Borrego%20Desert%20State%20Park%20california",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=638",bestTimeToVisit:"The cooler months from October through April are best, when campsite reservations are offered and the visitor center is open daily; summer brings reduced hours.",tips:["Coyote Canyon Road is closed 5.2 miles ahead at the 3rd crossing, and parking at the closure gate is limited.","Dogs are allowed only in campgrounds, on designated park roads and on the Visitor Center/Campground Trail, on a leash shorter than 6 feet.","Off-highway vehicles are prohibited in the park. Only street-legal vehicles on designated roads are allowed. OHV riding is permitted at nearby Ocotillo Wells SVRA."],nearbyAttractions:["Ocotillo Wells State Vehicular Recreation Area","Borrego Springs","Julian"]},{id:"julia-pfeiffer-burns-state-park",name:"Julia Pfeiffer Burns State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/b77d464fd6447708.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Big Sur park known for McWay Falls, an 80-foot waterfall that drops onto the beach by the Pacific.",fullDescription:"Julia Pfeiffer Burns State Park lies on the Big Sur coast, 12 miles south of Pfeiffer Big Sur State Park. Its best-known feature is McWay Falls, which drops 80 feet over a cliff into the Pacific. The park also has 300-foot redwoods, two hike-in environmental campsites with ocean views, and an underwater area popular with scuba divers.",thingsToDo:["McWay Falls viewpoint","Hiking","Scuba diving","Hike-in camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Partington Cove Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Leads to Partington Cove, site of a former tanbark shipping port, reached by walking through a tunnel blasted through rock; the trail is currently open."},{name:"Waterfall Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short round-trip trail with about 40 feet of elevation gain."},{name:"Ewoldsen Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"3.4 miles",description:"Starts from the Canyon Trail and gains elevation through a redwood forest, splitting into a loop around the canyon after 1.5 miles; it is currently CLOSED with other trails east of Highway 1."}],campingInfo:"The park offers two hike-in, environmental tent campsites that can be reserved online.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["gray whales"],visitorCenter:{name:"Julia Pfeiffer Burns State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(831) 667-1112"},hours:"8am to Sunset",entranceFees:"Vehicle Day Use: $10.00 (special rates may apply for peak season, holidays and weekends)",address:"52801 CA-1, Big Sur, CA 93920",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Julia%20Pfeiffer%20Burns%20State%20Park%20california",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=578",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer brings daytime temperatures typically in the 70s-80s, with morning fog that usually burns off by midday; weather can be changeable, so dress in layers and check fire and road conditions before going.",tips:["There is no beach or ocean access at McWay Falls. The beach, Saddle Rock and McWay Falls areas beyond the fences are off limits, and trespassing can lead to citation and arrest.","Dogs are not allowed in the park.","Check Caltrans road conditions before traveling. All backcountry trails east of Highway 1 are closed indefinitely while the Timber and Plaskett fires remain active."],nearbyAttractions:["Pfeiffer Big Sur State Park","Limekiln State Park","Pfeiffer Beach"]},{id:"crystal-cove-state-park",name:"Crystal Cove State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/f822e09a265e283b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Orange County coastal park with 3.2 miles of beach, chaparral canyons and historic beach cottages.",fullDescription:"Crystal Cove State Park covers 3,936 acres between Newport Beach and Laguna Beach, with 3.2 miles of Pacific coastline and inland chaparral canyons. Its historic district has 46 beach cottages from the 1920s and 1930s, some available to rent. The offshore waters are a marine conservation area used by divers, and the inland trails are popular with mountain bikers.",thingsToDo:["Swimming and surfing","Tidepooling","Scuba diving","Mountain biking","Hiking","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Moro Canyon",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A local hiking guide describes a Moro Canyon loop as Easy/Medium, about 2 hours, under 5 miles, with an estimated 827 ft of elevation gain."},{name:"Crystal Cove Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"OnX Maps lists the Crystal Cove Loop as a hiking trail in the park."}],campingInfo:"The park offers tent and RV camping, including Moro Campground and backcountry primitive sites such as Deer Canyon, with reservations through ReserveCalifornia at (800) 444-7275.",fishingBoating:"The official page lists fishing, boating, scuba diving/snorkeling and surfing, and the park's offshore waters include the Crystal Cove State Marine Conservation Area, so check marine regulations before fishing.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Crystal Cove State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(949) 494-3539"},hours:"Day use 6:00 AM to sunset; Historic District 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM",entranceFees:"$15 per vehicle day use; $20 on special holiday/seasonal/weekend dates",address:"8471 North Coast Highway, Laguna Beach, CA 92651",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Crystal%20Cove%20State%20Park%20california",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=644",bestTimeToVisit:"The park is open year-round with a Mediterranean climate; summer mornings are often foggy but usually clear by midmorning, and weekdays avoid the higher $20 weekend and holiday rate.",tips:["Dogs are allowed on paved areas only. Except for service animals, they are not allowed on the beach, in the backcountry, or in the Deer Canyon, Lower Moro and Upper Moro campgrounds.","Backcountry trails are open 6 AM to sunset for hiking, biking and horseback riding, and they may be closed at times. Stay on the designated trails.","Collecting sea glass and driftwood (up to 50 lbs) is allowed. Shells, sand, rocks and living creatures may not be taken."],nearbyAttractions:["Heisler Park","Thousand Steps"]},{id:"mount-san-jacinto-state-park",name:"Mount San Jacinto State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/b5e1cacb1a306559.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Southern California mountain park around 10,834-foot Mount San Jacinto, reached by the Palm Springs Aerial Tramway.",fullDescription:"Mount San Jacinto State Park, in the San Jacinto Mountains above Idyllwild and Palm Springs, includes the granite summit of Mount San Jacinto, at 10,834 feet the second-highest peak in Southern California. The summit area is reached by the Palm Springs Aerial Tramway and lies on the Pacific Crest Trail. Much of the park is wilderness with old-growth forest.",thingsToDo:["Palm Springs Aerial Tramway","Hiking to San Jacinto Peak","Pacific Crest Trail","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Deer Springs Trail (to San Jacinto Peak)",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"19 miles (round trip to San Jacinto Peak)",description:"Hiking Guy calls the San Jacinto Peak hike via the Deer Springs Trail one of the toughest and most beautiful hikes in Southern California, rated Very Hard."},{name:"Mount San Jacinto via Mountain Station",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"11.5 miles",description:"Modern Hiker describes this high-altitude trek as moderately easy, with about 2,190 ft of elevation gain, starting from the Palm Springs Aerial Tramway's Mountain Station."}],campingInfo:"Idyllwild and Stone Creek campgrounds offer tent sites ($25/night), sites with electric ($35/night) and full hook-up sites ($45/night), reservable through ReserveCalifornia, and wilderness camping requires a $5/person permit.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Bobcat","Coyote","Songbirds","Raptors"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mount San Jacinto State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(951) 659-2607"},hours:"Park Headquarters (Idyllwild): daily 8:00 AM–4:00 PM; Long Valley Ranger Station: open daily, hours may vary",entranceFees:"$10.00 per vehicle (day use parking)",address:"25905 Highway 243, Idyllwild, CA 92549",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mount%20San%20Jacinto%20State%20Park%20california",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=636",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring through fall is generally best for high-country hiking and wildlife viewing, but check current fire restrictions and trail conditions before visiting.",tips:["You need a permit to enter the wilderness. Hikers under 18 must be with a parent or guardian or have signed consent.","Dogs are allowed only in the Idyllwild and Stone Creek campgrounds. They are not allowed in the wilderness or on the Palm Springs Aerial Tramway (service animals excepted).","Drones are prohibited, and campfires are not allowed in wilderness areas. Only portable stoves with an on/off switch may be used there."],nearbyAttractions:["Palm Springs Aerial Tramway","Palm Springs Windmill Tours","Estudillo Mansion"]},{id:"point-lobos-state-natural-reserve",name:"Point Lobos State Natural Reserve",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/cf5718a79e5a55b3.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Coastal reserve south of Carmel with sea otters, harbor seals, Monterey cypress and a top dive spot.",fullDescription:"Point Lobos State Natural Reserve lies on the Pacific coast between Carmel Highlands and Big Sur. It adjoins 'one of the richest marine habitats in California', protected by two marine reserves, where harbor seals and sea otters live. Several miles of trails follow the coast, and Whaler's Cove is a launch point for scuba divers.",thingsToDo:["Coastal trails","Wildlife viewing (seals, sea otters)","Scuba diving (Whaler's Cove)","Kayaking","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cypress Grove Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A short trail in the reserve listed at 0.8 miles with easy difficulty."},{name:"Bird Island Trail",difficulty:"Easy to Moderate",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A 0.8-mile trail in the reserve rated easy to moderate."},{name:"Lace Lichen Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A new accessible trail and one of the reserve's ADA-compliant trails."}],campingInfo:"No camping in the park.",fishingBoating:"Visitors who bring a boat pay a $25 boat launch fee in addition to the $10 per-car entry fee, and the offshore underwater park can only be explored by certified divers.",wildlife:["sea otters","harbor seals","lingcod","rockfish","sea stars"],visitorCenter:{name:"Point Lobos State Natural Reserve Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(831) 624-4909"},hours:"Day Use Area: 8:00 a.m.–7:00 p.m., last entry 6:30 p.m.",entranceFees:"Vehicle Day Use $10; Small Bus $50; Large Bus $100 (special rates may apply at peak times)",address:"Highway 1, Carmel-by-the-Sea, CA 93923 (entrance 3 miles south of Carmel)",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Point%20Lobos%20State%20Natural%20Reserve%20california",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=571",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall are good choices because the central coast has dry summers with fog from July through September and occasional winter rains.",tips:["Dogs are not allowed anywhere in the reserve and cannot be left inside parked vehicles.","Vehicles over 21 ft long, including motorhomes and live-in vans, may not drive or park in the reserve.","Visitors report that parking lots fill by about 9 a.m., so arrive early; otherwise expect to park along Highway 1 and walk in."],nearbyAttractions:["Carmel-by-the-Sea","Big Sur","Monterey Bay National Marine Sanctuary"]},{id:"calaveras-big-trees-state-park",name:"Calaveras Big Trees State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d2e3c99e05b1dca4.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Sierra Nevada park near Arnold protecting two groves of giant sequoias, a tourist draw since 1852.",fullDescription:"Calaveras Big Trees State Park, four miles northeast of Arnold, preserves two groves of giant sequoias: the North Grove, with about 100 mature trees, and the South Grove, with about 1,000. The trees have drawn visitors since 1852. North Grove sights include the Discovery Tree stump and the fallen Pioneer Cabin tunnel tree. The park covers 6,498 acres.",thingsToDo:["North Grove trail","South Grove hike","Swimming in the Stanislaus River","Fishing","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Grove Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5 miles",description:"A 5-mile round-trip hike through the South Grove of giant sequoias, for which visitors should allow 3 to 4 hours."}],campingInfo:"The park offers tent, group and remote campsites from $35 per night ($40 on holidays), reservable through ReserveCalifornia.",fishingBoating:"Visitors can fish in Beaver Creek or the Stanislaus River, but should use caution at the river, which has dangerous Class V rapids.",wildlife:["Bears"],visitorCenter:{name:"Calaveras Big Trees State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(209) 795-2334"},hours:"Day use: sunrise to sunset; open on holidays.",entranceFees:"Vehicle day use $10 ($12 on holidays, weekends and peak season); seniors 62+ $9 ($11 holiday)",address:"1170 CA-4, Arnold, CA 95223",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Calaveras%20Big%20Trees%20State%20Park%20california",officialWebsite:"https://www.parks.ca.gov/?page_id=551",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer and early fall are best, when all areas are open. Walter W. Smith Parkway closes at the first significant snowfall or on December 1, cutting vehicle access to the Stanislaus River and South Grove until about May 1.",tips:["Dogs are allowed only in campgrounds and on fire roads, not on trails.","Allow 3 to 4 hours and bring drinking water for the longer South Grove hike.","Park fees must be paid before entering the park."],nearbyAttractions:["White Pines Lake","California Caverns","Angels Camp"]}],colorado:[{id:"eldorado-canyon-state-park",name:"Eldorado Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6e79142e6b3878ec.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A world-renowned rock climbing destination featuring sheer sandstone cliffs and scenic hiking trails.",fullDescription:"Eldorado Canyon State Park is a treasure of Colorado, located just south of Boulder. Famed globally for its gold-colored, sheer sandstone cliffs, the canyon attracts rock climbers from all over the world who tackle over 1,000 technical routes. For hikers, the park offers beautiful trails winding along the rushing waters of South Boulder Creek, climbing through dense pine forests to reveal spectacular panoramic views of the continental divide.",thingsToDo:["Technical rock climbing on golden sandstone walls","Hiking through lush foothills and dense pine forests","Fly fishing in South Boulder Creek","Watching climbers tackle historic, sheer routes"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Eldorado Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"Climbs steadily through evergreen forests, passing old homestead ruins and offering spectacular views of the red-rock canyon and distant peaks."},{name:"Fowler Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.9 miles",description:"A flat, wheelchair-accessible path clinging to the canyon wall, providing incredible close-up views of rock climbers."}],campingInfo:"No camping is permitted in Eldorado Canyon State Park to protect the delicate environment, but camping is available at nearby Golden Gate Canyon State Park.",fishingBoating:"South Boulder Creek offers excellent trout fishing, particularly for brown and rainbow trout. No boating is allowed due to rocky rapids and shallow creek depths.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Golden eagles (nesting on the canyon cliffs)","Prairie falcons","Abert's squirrels","Mountain lions (rare)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Eldorado Canyon Visitor Center",info:"Provides rock climbing guides, historical photos, geologic maps, and displays on the canyon's industrial mining history.",phone:"(303) 494-3943",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM (Summer weekends)"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily (Vehicle timed-entry reservations required on summer weekends).",entranceFees:"$10.00 daily vehicle pass (or $4.00 per person for walk-ins).",address:"9 Kneale Rd, Eldorado Springs, CO 80025",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Eldorado+Canyon+State+Park+CO",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/eldorado-canyon-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"September to October for comfortable hiking temperatures, and April to May for rushing creek flows.",tips:["Timed-entry reservations are mandatory on weekends and holidays from mid-May through mid-September; book early!","Sit on one of the wooden benches along the Fowler Trail with a pair of binoculars to watch world-class climbers ascend the Bastille wall.","South Boulder Creek flows fast in spring; keep children and pets well away from the banks during the high run-off."],nearbyAttractions:["Boulder Chautauqua Park","Rocky Mountain National Park","Flatirons Trailhead","Downtown Boulder Pearl Street"]},{id:"roxborough-state-park",name:"Roxborough State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/dd876041034157be.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Colorado park 20 miles south of Denver known for tilted red sandstone formations; a National Natural Landmark.",fullDescription:"Roxborough State Park covers 3,339 acres in Douglas County, 20 miles south of Denver. Established in 1975 and named a National Natural Landmark in 1980, it is known for tilted red sandstone hogbacks and spires. A visitor center has exhibits, and six easy to moderate trails include the climb to Carpenter Peak.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Carpenter Peak trail","Visitor center","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Rim Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.8 miles",description:"A single-track trail with roughly 500 feet of elevation gain."},{name:"Carpenter Peak Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"6.2 miles",description:"An out-and-back hike to a foothill summit that overlooks the park's red rocks."},{name:"Fountain Valley Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Visitors can tour the rock formations along this trail in an electric golf cart."}],campingInfo:"Check the official park website for current camping details.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not allowed in the park, but the nearby Chatfield reservoir offers fishing and boating.",wildlife:["Black bear","Mule deer"],visitorCenter:{name:"Roxborough State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(303) 973-3959"},hours:"Park: daily 7 a.m.–7 p.m.; Office: daily 9 a.m.–4 p.m.; open year-round including holidays",entranceFees:"Daily Vehicle Pass $10–$15; Individual Daily Pass $4",address:"4751 Roxborough Dr, Littleton, CO 80125",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Roxborough%20State%20Park%20colorado",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/roxborough-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall bring mild hiking weather, and weekdays are quieter. The park limits large groups on weekends from May through October.",tips:["Pets, including dogs, are not allowed anywhere in the park.","Cell signal is extremely limited in the park. Rideshare users should prearrange a pickup and expect to walk the 2.5-mile entrance road to reach the trails.","Off-trail travel is prohibited. Groups of 10 or more need an approved Special Activity Agreement before arriving."],nearbyAttractions:["Chatfield Reservoir"]},{id:"state-forest-state-park",name:"State Forest State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2ff1e79be360e55c.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"70,838-acre Colorado park in the Medicine Bow Range near Walden, known for its moose.",fullDescription:"State Forest State Park covers 70,838 acres in the Medicine Bow Range east of Walden, established in 1970. About 52,000 acres are forested. The park calls moose its 'claim to fame', and bighorn sheep, black bear and elk also live there. It has a moose visitor center, 187 campsites, cabins and yurts, boat ramps and 94 miles of trails.",thingsToDo:["Moose viewing","Hiking","Fishing","Boating","Camping, cabins and yurts"],hikingTrails:[],campingInfo:"The park has 228 campsites plus cabins, yurts and huts, with backcountry camping also offered.",fishingBoating:"Wakeless boating and fishing are allowed at North Michigan Reservoir, which has two boat ramps on its north side, but no watercraft of any type are allowed on Ranger Lakes.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"State Forest State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"970-723-8366"},hours:"Office: Summer daily 9 a.m.-5 p.m.; Fall daily 9 a.m.-4 p.m.; Winter 9 a.m.-4 p.m., closed Wednesdays",entranceFees:"Daily Vehicle Pass: $12-$17",address:"56750 Highway 14, Walden, CO 80480",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=State%20Forest%20State%20Park%20colorado",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/state-forest-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer and early fall are best for this high-mountain park, since campgrounds begin closing in October and water is shut off at the end of October.",tips:["All visitors entering the park must visibly display a current Colorado State Parks pass.","Boat inspections are mandatory at the North Michigan Reservoir ramps. Watercraft of any type are not allowed on Ranger Lakes.","Daily passes are valid until noon on the day after purchase."],nearbyAttractions:[]},{id:"golden-gate-canyon-state-park",name:"Golden Gate Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/66243e704e489c38.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Front Range park northwest of Golden with 42 miles of trails and a Continental Divide overlook.",fullDescription:"Golden Gate Canyon State Park covers 12,119 acres northwest of Golden and was established in 1960. It has 42 miles of hiking trails, 27 of them open to horses and bikes, a visitor center and over 100 campsites. Panorama Point Scenic Overlook looks out over about 100 miles of the Continental Divide.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Horseback riding","Mountain biking","Panorama Point overlook","Camping and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Raccoon Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.5 miles",description:"The Raccoon Loop Trail is an easy, 2.5-mile loop hike in Golden Gate Canyon State Park that is short and has a variety of scenery."},{name:"Burro Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A visitor review describes it as a nice hiking trail directly accessible from the campground."}],campingInfo:"Overnight options range from tent and RV sites at Reverend's Ridge Campground to rustic backcountry sites with no amenities, with 156 campsites in total.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in any stream or pond in the park, and the daily bag limit is four fish.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Golden Gate Canyon State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"303-582-3707"},hours:"Park: day use 5 a.m. to 10 p.m.; Visitor Center: Mon.-Sun. 9 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Campground Office: closed for the season",entranceFees:"Daily Vehicle Pass $12-$17; Individual Daily Pass $4",address:"92 Crawford Gulch Road, Golden, CO 80403",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Golden%20Gate%20Canyon%20State%20Park%20colorado",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/golden-gate-canyon-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"All of the park's trails are open year-round, but late spring through early fall is the most comfortable time to visit; in winter the dump station is closed and water is shut off.",tips:["Nine self-pay kiosks are located throughout the park, and passes can also be bought at the Visitor Center during business hours.","If you are bringing horses, the Kriley Overlook, Nott Creek and Aspen Meadow Campground lots are the most trailer-friendly. Call 303-582-3707 if your group will have multiple trailers.","Arrive very early in the morning, especially on weekends."],nearbyAttractions:["Golden","Clear Creek"]},{id:"mueller-state-park",name:"Mueller State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Year-round park near Divide on the west side of Pikes Peak, with 55 miles of trails and elk.",fullDescription:"Mueller State Park covers 5,112 acres outside Divide, west of Colorado Springs. It has 55 miles of trails for hiking, biking and horseback riding and year-round camping. In winter it allows snowshoeing, sledding and snowtubing. Wildlife includes elk, black bear, eagles and bighorn sheep.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Biking","Horseback riding","Camping","Snowshoeing and sledding (winter)","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Grouse Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1 mile",description:"Colorado Parks and Wildlife calls it a great little adventure at one mile round-trip with 152 feet of elevation gain."},{name:"Children's Dragonfly Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A self-guided nature trail at Dragonfly Pond, according to the park brochure."},{name:"Wapiti Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A self-guided nature trail at the Visitor Center, according to the park brochure."}],campingInfo:"The park has 138 campsites, and its cabins require a two-night minimum stay.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["elk","black bear","mule deer","hawks","eagles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mueller State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(719) 687-2366"},hours:"Park: daily 5 a.m.–10 p.m.; Office/Visitor Center: daily 9 a.m.–4 p.m.",entranceFees:"Daily vehicle pass $10–$12 (Colorado plates) or $15–$17 (out-of-state plates); Individual Daily Pass $4",address:"21045 CO-67, Divide, CO 80814",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mueller%20State%20Park%20colorado",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/mueller-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer and fall suit hiking, camping and wildlife viewing at this park at the base of Pikes Peak, while winter offers snowshoeing and cross-country skiing.",tips:["Pets are prohibited on all trails and in the backcountry. They are allowed only in campsites, picnic areas and on paved roads.","Daily passes are valid until noon on the day after purchase.","Cabins require a two-night minimum stay. One-night cabin stays are not permitted."],nearbyAttractions:["Colorado Wolf and Wildlife Center","Pikes Peak","Florissant Fossil Beds National Monument"]},{id:"cheyenne-mountain-state-park",name:"Cheyenne Mountain State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Colorado Springs park on the eastern flank of Cheyenne Mountain, with trails and campsites.",fullDescription:"Cheyenne Mountain State Park covers 2,701 acres beneath the eastern flank of Cheyenne Mountain in Colorado Springs. It was acquired in 2000 to protect one of the last large open spaces on the southern Front Range. The park has a network of trails, full-hookup and walk-in campsites, and is open for day use.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain biking","Camping","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Raccoon Ridge",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"One of the few trails in the park where leashed dogs are allowed."},{name:"Acorn Alley",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A dog-friendly trail where leashed dogs are permitted."},{name:"Dixon",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"One of the park trails where horses are allowed."}],campingInfo:"The park has 61 campsites; reservations are required, camping is allowed only in designated areas, and the maximum stay is 14 days within a 28-day period.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Black bear","Mountain lion","Bobcat","Coyote"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cheyenne Mountain State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"719-576-2016"},hours:"Park: daily 5 a.m.–10 p.m.; Office/Visitor Center: daily 9 a.m.–4 p.m.",entranceFees:"Daily Vehicle Pass $10–$15; Individual Daily Pass $4",address:"410 JL Ranch Heights Rd, Colorado Springs, CO 80926",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cheyenne%20Mountain%20State%20Park%20colorado",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/cheyenne-mountain-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring through fall offers the most comfortable trail weather, though the park's campground is open year-round and its trails are also hiked in winter.",tips:["Dogs are allowed only on the Acorn Alley, Bobcat Way and Raccoon Ridge trails and the marked portion of the Soaring Kestral Trail. The dog trails cannot be reached from Limekiln Trailhead.","Two first-come, first-served EV charging stations are in the Visitor Center parking lot and are available 6 a.m.–10 p.m. daily.","Campground check-in is 1 p.m. and check-out is noon."],nearbyAttractions:["Cheyenne Mountain Zoo","Will Rogers Memorial Shrine of the Sun","Manitou Cliff Dwellings"]},{id:"chatfield-state-park",name:"Chatfield State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Denver-area park around Chatfield Reservoir, fed by the South Platte River at the mouth of Waterton Canyon.",fullDescription:"Chatfield State Park in Douglas and Jefferson counties, near Littleton, centers on Chatfield Reservoir, a 1,423-acre lake fed by the South Platte River and Plum Creek. The park has a swim beach, boat ramps, paved trails, campsites and a dog off-leash area.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Boating","Biking","Camping","Dog off-leash area"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Chatfield Equestrian Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"3.2 miles",description:"A 3.2-mile loop used for horseback riding that is listed as dog friendly."},{name:"Audubon Nature Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"AllTrails lists it among the popular summer hiking trails in Chatfield State Park."}],campingInfo:"The park has 197 campsites in four campgrounds, all with electrical hookups and many with full hookups, plus 10 reservable group camping sites.",fishingBoating:"Visitors can boat and water ski on the lake, and boat rentals are available; an unofficial guide lists trout, bass, walleye, channel catfish, yellow perch, crappie and bluegill among the fish.",wildlife:["mallard","deer","walleye","channel catfish","yellow perch"],visitorCenter:{name:"Chatfield State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"303-791-7275"},hours:"Park: daily 5 a.m.–10 p.m.; Office: Mon–Fri 8 a.m.–4 p.m.; Campground Office: Wed–Sun 9 a.m.–4 p.m. (closed Mon–Tue)",entranceFees:"Daily Vehicle Pass $12–$17; Individual Daily Pass $4",address:"11500 N. Roxborough Park Rd., Littleton, CO 80125",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Chatfield%20State%20Park%20colorado",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/chatfield-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"The park draws visitors year-round: late spring through early fall suits water recreation, and in winter the trails are open for cross-country skiing and snowshoeing.",tips:['Do not enter "Chatfield State Park" into your mapping app because it gives wrong directions; use the official GPS directions instead.',"Daily passes are valid until noon on the day after purchase.","The park has a 69-acre off-leash area for dogs."],nearbyAttractions:["Denver Botanic Gardens at Chatfield Farms","Hudson Gardens","Roxborough State Park"]},{id:"cherry-creek-state-park",name:"Cherry Creek State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Aurora park with an 880-acre reservoir, swim beach and 35 miles of trails in prairie and wetlands.",fullDescription:"Cherry Creek State Park in Arapahoe County surrounds an 880-acre reservoir shared by powerboats, sailboats and paddle craft, with a sand swim beach on the northeast side. Around it are prairie and wetlands, 35 miles of multi-use trails for running, cycling and horseback riding, campsites, a shooting range and a dog off-leash area.",thingsToDo:["Boating and paddling","Swimming","Biking","Horseback riding","Camping","Dog off-leash area"],hikingTrails:[{name:"12 Mile Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"AllTrails lists it among the popular winter hiking trails in Cherry Creek State Park."},{name:"Cottonwood Creek Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Gaia GPS lists a 3.5-mile Cottonwood Creek / Cherry Creek hike that uses this trail."}],campingInfo:"The park has a campground with sites reservable through CPW's online system (cpwshop.com), and park pass fees are not included in camping fees.",fishingBoating:"The 880-surface-acre Cherry Creek Reservoir draws water skiers, anglers and boaters, with fishing for walleye, trout, crappie and bass.",wildlife:["walleye","trout","crappie","bass"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cherry Creek State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"303-690-1166"},hours:"Visitor Center/Campground Office: daily, Oct 1–31 9 a.m.–6 p.m.; Nov 1–Dec 31 9 a.m.–5 p.m.",entranceFees:"Daily Vehicle Pass $10–$15; Dog Off-leash Daily Pass $3; Daily Water Basin Authority fee $1 (Annual $3)",address:"4201 South Parker Road, Aurora, CO 80014",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cherry%20Creek%20State%20Park%20colorado",officialWebsite:"https://cpw.state.co.us/state-parks/cherry-creek-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through fall is best for trails and the reservoir, while winter can still offer snowshoeing or cross-country skiing on the park's trails.",tips:["The dog off-leash area is closed Oct. 26–30, and the park plans to reopen it Oct. 31.","Daily park passes are valid until noon on the day after purchase.","Camping fees do not include the park pass, so budget for both."],nearbyAttractions:["Denver Botanic Gardens"]}],hawaii:[{id:"waimea-canyon-state-park",name:"Waimea Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d31c29344537bc06.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Kauaʻi park overlooking Waimea Canyon, the 'Grand Canyon of the Pacific', up to 3,000 feet deep.",fullDescription:"Waimea Canyon State Park on Kauaʻi's west side overlooks Waimea Canyon, about ten miles long and up to 3,000 feet deep, known as the Grand Canyon of the Pacific. Its name means 'reddish water', for the canyon's red soil. The park has a scenic drive, canyon lookouts, a viewpoint toward Niʻihau, and the Iliau Nature Loop and Kukui Trail.",thingsToDo:["Canyon lookouts","Scenic drive","Iliau Nature Loop","Kukui Trail hike"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Iliau Nature Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"One of the trails named on the official Waimea Canyon State Park page."},{name:"Kukui Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"One of the trails named on the official Waimea Canyon State Park page."}],campingInfo:"No camping in the park.",fishingBoating:"The official park page lists seasonal trout fishing.",wildlife:["trout","pigs","goats"],visitorCenter:{name:"Waimea Canyon State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(808) 274-3444"},hours:"Daily during daylight hours",entranceFees:"Non-residents $5/person (children 3 and under free) plus $10/vehicle parking; Hawaii residents free with HI ID/DL",address:"Waimea, HI 96796",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Waimea%20Canyon%20State%20Park%20hawaii",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/kauai/waimea-canyon-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Go on a clear morning once the park has reopened, because canyon views depend on the weather and trails can be muddy after rain.",tips:["Status as of Oct 2026: closed (hurricane recovery, official notice 10/19/26). Check the official page before going.","Parking tickets are valid at both the Waimea Canyon State Park and Kokee State Park parking lots, so you only need to pay once.","Non-residents pay the entry and parking fees at an automatic ticket booth, and no reservation is needed.","Trails in the area can be slippery, muddy and dangerous, so wear shoes with good traction."],nearbyAttractions:["Kōkeʻe State Park"]},{id:"-akaka-falls-state-park",name:"ʻAkaka Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7253454185fc35ef.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Hawaiʻi Island park north of Hilo with 442-foot ʻAkaka Falls, seen from a short rainforest loop.",fullDescription:"ʻAkaka Falls State Park lies about 11 miles north of Hilo, above Honomū at the end of Route 220. A 0.4-mile paved loop through tropical vegetation leads to viewpoints of ʻAkaka Falls, which plunges 442 feet into a stream-eroded gorge, and of 300-foot Kahūnā Falls.",thingsToDo:["Paved loop walk","ʻAkaka Falls viewpoint","Kahūnā Falls viewpoint"],hikingTrails:[{name:"ʻAkaka Falls Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.4 miles",description:"A paved, self-guided loop through lush tropical vegetation to vista points overlooking Kahuna Falls and the 442-foot ʻAkaka Falls."}],campingInfo:"Check the official park website for current camping details.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"ʻAkaka Falls State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(808) 961-9540"},hours:"Daily 8:30am–5:00pm",entranceFees:"Non-residents $5/person plus $10 parking per non-commercial vehicle; Hawaii residents free with ID or driver's license; children 3 and under free; credit card only",address:"875 Akaka Falls Rd, Honomu, HI 96728",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=%CA%BBAkaka%20Falls%20State%20Park%20hawaii",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/hawaii/akaka-falls-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"The park is open year-round from 8:30am to 5:00pm, so arrive early to explore the rainforest loop before the busier part of the day.",tips:["Status as of Oct 2026: open, part of the loop trail closed (fallen trees, notice 10/19/26). Check the official page before going.","Pay parking and entrance fees before you arrive because cell reception at the park is limited; payment is credit card only.","Allow about half an hour for the full loop. To see only ʻAkaka Falls, take the left (south) path at the first junction.","Stay on the trail. Mountain biking and open fires are not allowed, and you should pack out what you pack in."],nearbyAttractions:["Hawaii Tropical Botanical Garden","Umauma Falls","Rainbow Falls"]},{id:"h-ena-state-park",name:"Hāʻena State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d166da8963f963bb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"'End of the road' park on Kauaʻi's north shore with Keʻe Beach and the Kalalau Trail trailhead.",fullDescription:"Hāʻena State Park covers 230 acres at the end of the Kūhiō Highway on Kauaʻi's north shore. It has Keʻe Beach, ancient Hawaiian sites including sea caves estimated at more than 4,000 years old, and the trailhead of the 11-mile Kalalau Trail, the only land access to the Nā Pali Coast. A parking or entry reservation is required, with a daily limit of 900 people.",thingsToDo:["Keʻe Beach","Kalalau Trail (first section)","Ancient Hawaiian sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Kalalau Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"11 miles",description:"Hāʻena State Park is home to the trailhead of the world-famous Kalalau Trail, and hiking past Hanakāpīʻai Valley requires a Nāpali Coast State Wilderness Park camping permit."},{name:"Hanakāpīʻai Falls trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"4 miles",description:"Following part of the Kalalau Trail to Hanakāpīʻai Valley, the hike from Hāʻena SP to Hanakāpīʻai Waterfalls is 8 miles round trip and is included with a park entry or parking reservation."}],campingInfo:"As of the official 10/19/2026 park notice, all Kauaʻi state park camping remains closed.",fishingBoating:"The park offers beach-related activities including shore fishing and swimming at Kēʻē Beach.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Hāʻena State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(808) 274-3444"},hours:"Summer (from Apr 1): 7:00am–7:45pm; Winter (from day after Labor Day): 7:00am–6:45pm",entranceFees:"Non-residents: $5/person entry plus $10/vehicle parking, advance reservation required. Hawaii residents free with HI DL/ID. Children 3 and under free.",address:"End of Kūhiō Highway (Hwy 56), Hāʻena, Kauaʻi, HI 96714",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=H%C4%81%CA%BBena%20State%20Park%20hawaii",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/kauai/haena-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"The park is open year-round with daily visitor limits, so plan any visit around a reservation booked up to 30 days ahead. In autumn 2026, also check the Park Notices site because of recent hurricane-related closures.",tips:["Status as of Oct 2026: reopened Oct 15, 2026. Check the official page before going.","Non-residents must buy both entry and parking reservations in advance. Reservations open 30 days ahead, and parking reservations no longer cover passengers.","Parking reservations are only valid for the time slots you purchase. Staying from opening to closing requires buying all three slots.","Bring a valid parking voucher (print or digital) to show on arrival. Miloliʻi camping permits are not accepted for park entry."],nearbyAttractions:["Nāpali Coast State Wilderness Park"]},{id:"na-pali-coast-state-wilderness-park",name:"Na Pali Coast State Wilderness Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/87b47417211c4194.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A breathtaking wilderness park featuring towering emerald cliffs, hidden sea caves, and pristine beaches along Kauai's rugged northern coastline.",fullDescription:"Na Pali Coast State Wilderness Park encompasses 6,175 acres of rugged land along the northwest coast of Kauai. The park is defined by its dramatic pali, or cliffs, which rise up to 4,000 feet above the Pacific Ocean. Historically, the valleys were home to ancient Hawaiian settlements, and today the area remains a protected wilderness accessible primarily by foot, boat, or helicopter, preserving its pristine natural beauty and geological significance.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the Kalalau Trail","Boat tours for snorkeling and sea cave exploration","Helicopter sightseeing tours","Photography of the dramatic cliff faces","Backpacking and wilderness camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Kalalau Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"11 miles",description:"A world-famous, challenging trek that traverses five valleys and ends at the secluded Kalalau Beach."},{name:"Hanakapi'ai Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"The first section of the Kalalau Trail leading to a beautiful beach and a nearby waterfall."}],campingInfo:"Camping is allowed only at Kalalau Beach and requires a valid state-issued permit. There are no facilities, and campers must pack out all trash.",wildlife:["Hawaiian monk seals","Green sea turtles","Spinner dolphins","Feral goats","Various seabirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kokee State Park Ranger Station",info:"Provides information, safety warnings, and permit assistance for the Na Pali Coast region.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(808) 335-9975"},hours:"Open 24 hours for permitted hikers; access is weather-dependent.",address:"End of Highway 560, Haena, HI 96714",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Na+Pali+Coast+State+Wilderness+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/kauai/napali-coast-state-wilderness-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"May through September when ocean conditions are calmer.",tips:["Always check the weather forecast as flash floods can make trails dangerous.","Secure your camping permits months in advance as they sell out quickly.","Bring plenty of water and a filtration system, as there is no potable water on the trail."],nearbyAttractions:["Haena State Park","Limahuli Garden and Preserve","Hanalei Bay","Kee Beach"],entranceFees:"Entry is free, but camping permits require a fee of $30 per person per night for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"Boating is permitted via licensed commercial tour operators; private vessels require specific permits and adherence to strict environmental regulations."},{id:"makapuu-point-state-scenic-shoreline",name:"Makapuʻu Point State Scenic Shoreline",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/374f365425d73a20.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Experience breathtaking panoramic views of Oahu's eastern coastline and the historic Makapuʻu Lighthouse from this scenic coastal trail.",fullDescription:"Makapuʻu Point State Scenic Shoreline offers visitors a unique opportunity to explore the rugged volcanic landscape of southeastern Oahu. The park is home to the iconic Makapuʻu Point Lighthouse, built in 1909, and provides excellent vantage points for whale watching during the winter months. The area is geologically significant, featuring dramatic sea cliffs and tide pools formed by ancient lava flows.",thingsToDo:["Whale watching","Coastal hiking","Photography","Bird watching","Lighthouse viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Makapuʻu Point Lighthouse Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A paved, steady incline trail that offers spectacular views of the Pacific Ocean and the offshore islands."},{name:"Makapuʻu Tide Pools Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A steep, unpaved descent from the main trail leading down to the natural tide pools along the shoreline."}],campingInfo:"Camping is not permitted at Makapuʻu Point State Scenic Shoreline.",wildlife:["Humpback whales","Great frigatebirds","Wedge-tailed shearwaters","Green sea turtles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Makapuʻu Point Information Kiosk",info:"Informational signage regarding the lighthouse history and local marine life.",hours:"Daily: 7:00 AM - 6:45 PM",phone:"(808) 587-0300"},hours:"7:00 AM - 6:45 PM daily",address:"Kalanianaʻole Hwy, Waimanalo, HI 96795",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Makapuu+Point+State+Scenic+Shoreline",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/oahu/kaiwi-state-scenic-shoreline/",bestTimeToVisit:"Winter months for whale watching or early morning to avoid midday heat.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as there is no shade along the paved trail.","Wear sturdy shoes if you plan to hike down to the tide pools.","Arrive early in the morning to secure parking in the limited lot."],nearbyAttractions:["Sea Life Park Hawaii","Sandy Beach Park","Halona Blowhole","Waimanalo Beach"],entranceFees:"Free entrance and free parking."},{id:"kokee-state-park",name:"Kōkeʻe State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/61fed93b53eeb7e7.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched high above the Waimea Canyon, Kōkeʻe State Park offers cool mountain air, lush rainforests, and breathtaking vistas of the Na Pali Coast.",fullDescription:"Kōkeʻe State Park encompasses 4,345 acres of mountainous terrain on the island of Kauaʻi. Located at elevations ranging from 3,200 to 4,200 feet, the park is characterized by its native koa and ʻōhiʻa forests, diverse birdlife, and dramatic overlooks. The area is geologically significant for its proximity to the Alakaʻi Swamp, one of the highest elevation swamps in the world, and its role as a gateway to the rugged cliffs of the Na Pali Coast.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail exploration","Bird watching","Picnicking at Puʻu Hinahina","Photography of Waimea Canyon","Stargazing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pihea Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"This trail offers stunning views of the Kalalau Valley and connects to the Alakaʻi Swamp Trail."},{name:"Awaʻawapuhi Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"6.2 miles",description:"A challenging descent through native forest that ends at a dramatic cliff-top overlook of the Na Pali Coast."}],campingInfo:"The park offers tent camping at the Kōkeʻe Campground and cabin rentals managed by the Kōkeʻe Lodge. Permits are required for all camping activities.",wildlife:["ʻApapane","ʻIʻiwi","Feral goats","Black-tailed deer","Kauaʻi ʻelepaio"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kōkeʻe Natural History Museum",info:"Provides educational exhibits on the park's unique flora, fauna, and history, along with trail maps and weather updates.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(808) 335-9975"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily",address:"Kokee Rd, Kekaha, HI 96752",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kokee+State+Park+Hawaii",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/kauai/kokee-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Early morning to avoid afternoon clouds and mist.",tips:["Bring layers as temperatures can be significantly cooler than at sea level.","Check trail conditions at the museum before heading out, as paths can be very muddy.","Pack plenty of water and snacks as there are limited services within the park."],nearbyAttractions:["Waimea Canyon State Park","Puʻu o Kila Lookout","Waimea Town","Polihale State Park"],entranceFees:"Free entrance for Hawaii residents; non-residents must pay a parking fee of $10.00 per vehicle.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in designated streams during specific seasons with a valid Hawaii freshwater fishing license."},{id:"diamond-head-state-monument",name:"Diamond Head State Monument",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/bcf57b75b59fabf0.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Diamond Head State Monument offers iconic panoramic views of the Honolulu coastline and the Pacific Ocean from the summit of a historic volcanic crater.",fullDescription:"Diamond Head, known to Native Hawaiians as Lēʻahi, is a volcanic tuff cone that was formed approximately 300,000 years ago. The park encompasses over 475 acres and features a challenging trail that leads to the summit, where visitors can explore historic military bunkers and observation decks built in the early 20th century as part of Oahu's coastal defense system.",thingsToDo:["Hiking to the summit","Photography of the Honolulu skyline","Exploring historic military bunkers","Bird watching","Learning about volcanic geology"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Diamond Head Summit Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.6 miles",description:"A steep, paved and dirt trail that climbs 560 feet from the crater floor to the summit, featuring stairs and a tunnel."},{name:"Crater Floor Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A gentle walk around the base of the interior crater, offering views of the surrounding volcanic walls."}],campingInfo:"Camping is not permitted within the Diamond Head State Monument.",wildlife:["Pacific Golden Plover","White-rumped Shama","Northern Cardinal","Common Waxbill"],visitorCenter:{name:"Diamond Head Visitor Center",info:"Provides educational exhibits on the geology and military history of the crater, along with park information and souvenirs.",hours:"Daily: 6:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(808) 587-0300"},hours:"Daily: 6:00 AM - 6:00 PM (Last entry at 4:00 PM)",address:"Diamond Head Rd, Honolulu, HI 96815",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Diamond+Head+State+Monument",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/oahu/diamond-head-state-monument/",bestTimeToVisit:"Early morning to avoid the midday heat and peak crowds.",tips:["Reservations are required for all visitors and vehicles.","Bring plenty of water and wear sturdy hiking shoes.","The trail is steep and involves climbing stairs, so prepare accordingly."],nearbyAttractions:["Waikiki Beach","Kapiolani Regional Park","Honolulu Zoo","Shangri La Museum"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per person entry fee and $10.00 per vehicle parking fee.",fishingBoating:"Not applicable as the park is located inland within a volcanic crater."},{id:"palaau-state-park",name:"Palaʻau State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-palaau-state-park-hawaii.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Palaʻau State Park offers breathtaking views of the Kalaupapa Peninsula and a glimpse into Molokaʻi's rich cultural history.",fullDescription:"Located on the northern coast of Molokaʻi, Palaʻau State Park is a 234-acre park known for its dramatic cliffs and historical significance. The park features the famous Phallic Rock, a site of cultural importance, and provides a stunning overlook of the Kalaupapa National Historical Park, where the historic leper colony was established. The area is characterized by its lush eucalyptus and ironwood forests, offering a cool, serene environment for visitors.",thingsToDo:["Hiking to the Kalaupapa Lookout","Viewing the historic Phallic Rock","Picnicking in the shaded forest areas","Bird watching","Photography of the coastal cliffs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Kalaupapa Lookout Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, paved path leading to a spectacular overlook of the Kalaupapa Peninsula and the world's highest sea cliffs."},{name:"Phallic Rock Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.2 miles",description:"A brief walk through a beautiful ironwood forest to reach the culturally significant Phallic Rock formation."}],campingInfo:"Camping is currently not permitted at Palaʻau State Park.",wildlife:["Axis deer","Wild goats","Various native forest birds","Feral pigs"],visitorCenter:{name:"Palaʻau State Park Information Kiosk",info:"The park provides informational signage regarding the history of the Kalaupapa settlement and local cultural sites.",hours:"Daily: 7:00 AM - 7:00 PM",phone:"(808) 984-8109"},hours:"7:00 AM - 7:00 PM daily",address:"Kalae Hwy, Hoʻolehua, HI 96729",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Palaʻau+State+Park+Molokai",officialWebsite:"https://dlnr.hawaii.gov/dsp/parks/molokai/palaau-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Early morning for the clearest views of the Kalaupapa Peninsula before afternoon clouds roll in.",tips:["Bring a light jacket as the elevation makes the park significantly cooler than the coast.","Check the weather forecast, as the lookout is often obscured by fog or low clouds.","Respect the cultural significance of the Phallic Rock and do not climb on or disturb the site."],nearbyAttractions:["Kalaupapa National Historical Park","Molokaʻi Museum and Cultural Center","Kaunakakai Town","Papohaku Beach"],entranceFees:"Free entrance"}],idaho:[{id:"farragut-state-park",name:"Farragut State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/b9a937dce1c2fdab.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"North Idaho park on Lake Pend Oreille, on the site of a World War II naval training station.",fullDescription:"Farragut State Park covers 4,000 acres at the southern tip of Lake Pend Oreille in the Coeur d'Alene Mountains, about 30 miles northeast of Coeur d'Alene. It was the Farragut Naval Training Station during World War II, where over 293,000 sailors trained; the Museum at the Brig occupies its former confinement building. The park offers camping, boating, swimming, hiking and disc golf.",thingsToDo:["Camping","Boating","Swimming","Hiking","Disc golf","Museum at the Brig"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Shoreline Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2 miles",description:"An easy, fairly flat loop trail with very little elevation gain."},{name:"Squirrel Cache Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"An easy loop suitable for casual walkers and families."},{name:"Highpoint Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The park recommends taking the Highpoint Trail to the Scout Trail for hikers who like a challenge."}],campingInfo:"The park has 223 individual campsites, 10 camping cabins and 7 group camps; in fall 2026 Waldron campground is open with vault toilets only, and water to the sites is turned off.",fishingBoating:"Fishing and boating are offered at the park, which sits on the southern tip of Lake Pend Oreille.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Farragut State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(208) 262-4444"},hours:"Day-use 7am–10pm; Visitor Center 9am–4pm (Fall/Winter 2026); Museum at the Brig open Memorial Day–Labor Day",entranceFees:"$7 resident / $14 non-resident motor vehicle entrance fee, due on arrival year-round (per IDPR guide; confirm current rate)",address:"13550 E. Hwy 54, Athol, ID 83801",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Farragut%20State%20Park%20idaho",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/state-park/farragut-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer, between Memorial Day and Labor Day, is best for the full experience, since the Museum at the Brig is open and campground services are on; fall is quieter but cool, with seasonal closures.",tips:["State Highway 54 construction through the park means detours and alternate entrances; flaggers direct arriving visitors.","The Museum at the Brig is closed for the season and reopens Memorial Day weekend 2027.","Dogs must be confined or on a leash no longer than 6 feet and never left unattended."],nearbyAttractions:["Lake Pend Oreille","Farragut WMA"]},{id:"ponderosa-state-park",name:"Ponderosa State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2bd304546a4c71d9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"McCall park on a forested peninsula in Payette Lake, with trails, beaches and cabins.",fullDescription:"Ponderosa State Park covers 1,515 acres on a peninsula in Payette Lake at the northeast edge of McCall, plus a North Beach unit at the lake's northern end. It has hiking and biking trails, beaches, guided walks, evening programs, a visitor center and cabins, with skiing and snowshoeing in winter.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Biking","Beaches","Osprey Point view","Cabins and camping","Cross-country skiing (winter)"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Peninsula Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"4.0 miles",description:"AllTrails lists it as the best place to hike in the park, with about 134 ft of elevation gain."},{name:"Meadow Marsh Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"It is temporarily closed after the Meadow Marsh 2 under burn and will reopen once the park deems it safe for pedestrians."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 163 serviced campsites, 22 standard sites, 7 serviced ADA sites, 9 deluxe cabins and 1 camper cabin, and no overnight parking or camping in vehicles is allowed in the campgrounds.",fishingBoating:"The park has boat ramps and handling docks, canoe and kayak rentals for paddling the North Fork of the Payette River, and fishing supplies sold at the Visitor Center.",wildlife:["Canada geese","bald eagles","beavers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ponderosa State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(208) 634-2164"},hours:"Day-use 7 am–10 pm (may change at park manager discretion)",entranceFees:"$7–$14 per vehicle, single-day entry (per Visit Idaho)",address:"1920 N Davis Ave, McCall, ID 83638",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ponderosa%20State%20Park%20idaho",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/state-park/ponderosa-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer brings Junior Ranger and naturalist programs on Payette Lake, while winter offers groomed Nordic ski and snowshoe trails at this 5,050-ft mountain park.",tips:["Pets must be on a leash no longer than 6 feet, electronic shock collars do not meet this requirement, and pets are not allowed in cabins.","Expect brief road closures in October near the Water Tower 2 area during forest restoration work.","Book campsites and cabins through park reservations at 1-888-9-CAMPID, and note there is no camping in your vehicle in any campground."],nearbyAttractions:["Central Idaho Historical Museum","Brundage Mountain Resort"]},{id:"bruneau-dunes-state-park",name:"Bruneau Dunes State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/293aa9f91480de09.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Idaho park home to North America's tallest single-structured sand dune, about 470 feet high.",fullDescription:"Bruneau Dunes State Park covers 4,800 acres in Owyhee County, fifteen miles south of Mountain Home. It has large sand dunes and small lakes, including North America's tallest single-structured dune at about 470 feet. Visitors climb and sandboard the dunes (no vehicles allowed), and a public observatory opens for stargazing on weekend evenings.",thingsToDo:["Sandboarding","Dune climbing","Stargazing at the observatory","Fishing","Horseback riding","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"6 Mile Dune Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"6 miles",description:"A six-mile interpretive trail that crosses the country's tallest single-structured sand dune, rated moderately strenuous on the dune tops and taking 3–6 hours."},{name:"Bruneau Dunes Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.4 miles",description:"A moderate loop with 538 ft of elevation gain that takes about 1–1.5 hours."}],campingInfo:"The park has 82 serviced and 35 standard campsites plus 2 cabins; reservations are required for the Broken Wheel and Eagle Cove campgrounds (1-888-922-6743), while the 19-site Equestrian Campground is first-come, first-served.",fishingBoating:"You can fish for bluegill and bass in the lakes at the foot of the dune, and the boat ramps allow electric-motor boats only.",wildlife:["bluegill","bass"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bruneau Dunes State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(208) 366-7919"},hours:"Day-use areas, including the dunes, open 7 am–10 pm. Hours may change at the park manager's discretion. Visitor Center and entrance kiosk hours vary.",entranceFees:"$7 Motor Vehicle Entrance Fee per vehicle (bring cash or check)",address:"27608 Sand Dunes Rd, Mountain Home, ID 83647",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bruneau%20Dunes%20State%20Park%20idaho",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/state-park/bruneau-dunes-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall are most comfortable because summer heat on the dunes is intense; peak season starts in mid-March, and winter brings Saturday-night observatory programs.",tips:["Bring cash or a check for the $7 vehicle entrance fee, because Visitor Center and kiosk hours vary and not paying can result in a citation.","For the 6 Mile Dune Trail, pack 1–2 quarts of water per person and sturdy shoes, and avoid the intense summer heat.","Sandboards (stand-up and sled styles) rent at the Visitor Center for $15/day for one or $25/day for two, and renters must be 18 or older."],nearbyAttractions:["Bruneau Canyon Overlook","CJ Strike Wildlife Management Area","Three Island Crossing State Park"]},{id:"harriman-state-park",name:"Harriman State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/fa33e554d9a673f9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled within an 11,000-acre wildlife refuge in the heart of the Greater Yellowstone Ecosystem, Harriman State Park offers stunning landscapes and world-class fly fishing.",fullDescription:"Harriman State Park is located inside the Harriman Wildlife Refuge, featuring the famous Henrys Fork of the Snake River. Once a private retreat for the Union Pacific Railroad investors, the park preserves historic buildings and provides a sanctuary for trumpeter swans, moose, and elk. The landscape is defined by lodgepole pine forests, lush meadows, and volcanic geology.",thingsToDo:["Fly fishing on the Henrys Fork","Wildlife viewing and bird watching","Guided historic ranch tours","Cross-country skiing in winter","Mountain biking on designated trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Railroad Ranch Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.5 miles",description:"A scenic, relatively flat trail that winds through the historic ranch grounds and offers excellent views of the river."},{name:"Thayer Creek Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.2 miles",description:"This trail leads hikers through dense forests and open meadows, providing frequent opportunities to spot local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park offers several yurts for rent and a group camp area, but does not provide traditional RV hookup sites.",wildlife:["Moose","Elk","Trumpeter Swans","Bald Eagles","Sandhill Cranes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Harriman State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, wildlife conservation, and trail maps.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(208) 558-7368"},hours:"7:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"3489 Green Canyon Rd, Island Park, ID 83429",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Harriman+State+Park+Idaho",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/parks/harriman/",bestTimeToVisit:"June through September for hiking and fishing; December through March for winter sports.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities near the river.","Be prepared for rapidly changing weather conditions due to the high elevation.","Purchase a day-use permit at the entrance station upon arrival."],nearbyAttractions:["Yellowstone National Park","Island Park Reservoir","Mesa Falls Scenic Byway","West Yellowstone"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle per day",fishingBoating:"The Henrys Fork is world-renowned for fly fishing; however, motorized boating is prohibited to protect the wildlife refuge."},{id:"castle-rocks-state-park",name:"Castle Rocks State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9e1ab2573622b599.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A stunning high-desert landscape featuring massive granite rock formations and rich historical significance near Almo, Idaho.",fullDescription:"Castle Rocks State Park is a geological wonderland located in the Albion Mountains. The park is renowned for its towering granite spires and boulders, which attract rock climbers from across the globe. Historically, the area served as a landmark for pioneers traveling the California Trail, and the park preserves remnants of this rich frontier history alongside its unique natural rock architecture.",thingsToDo:["Rock climbing and bouldering","Hiking and trail running","Bird watching","Photography","Stargazing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Castle Rocks Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the base of the massive granite formations, offering excellent views of the rock spires."},{name:"Emigrant Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A gentle path that follows the historic route used by pioneers, featuring interpretive signs about the area's history."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a variety of camping options, including tent sites, RV sites with electrical hookups, and unique yurts available for reservation.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Golden eagles","Red-tailed hawks","Mountain lions","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Castle Rocks State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on climbing routes, park history, and trail maps.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(208) 824-5901"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"3875 N. Boise Ave, Almo, ID 83312",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Castle+Rocks+State+Park+Almo+ID",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/parks/castle-rocks/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and climbing conditions.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the high-desert climate is very dry.","Check in at the visitor center for current climbing route closures.","The park is a designated dark sky area, making it perfect for night photography."],nearbyAttractions:["City of Rocks National Reserve","Sawtooth National Forest","Almo","Twin Falls"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle per day",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not a primary activity at this park, as there are no major lakes or rivers within the park boundaries."},{id:"priest-lake-state-park",name:"Priest Lake State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-priest-lake-state-park-idaho.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the Selkirk Mountains of northern Idaho, Priest Lake State Park offers crystal-clear waters and pristine forests perfect for outdoor adventure.",fullDescription:"Priest Lake State Park is located in the northern panhandle of Idaho, surrounded by the majestic Selkirk Mountains. The park is renowned for its deep, clear, cold water and dense forests of cedar, fir, and hemlock. The area was shaped by glacial activity, resulting in the deep basin that holds the lake today. Visitors can explore the diverse ecosystem, which serves as a habitat for various wildlife, while enjoying recreational activities ranging from boating to winter snowshoeing.",thingsToDo:["Boating and water skiing","Fishing for lake trout and kokanee","Hiking through old-growth cedar forests","Cross-country skiing and snowshoeing","Wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Indian Creek Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through the forest, offering excellent opportunities to observe local flora and fauna."},{name:"Lion Head Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"4.0 miles",description:"A challenging climb that rewards hikers with breathtaking panoramic views of Priest Lake and the surrounding Selkirk Mountains."}],campingInfo:"The park features multiple campgrounds including Indian Creek, Dickensheet, and Lionhead, offering a mix of tent sites, RV sites with hookups, and rustic cabins.",wildlife:["Moose","White-tailed deer","Black bears","Bald eagles","Osprey"],visitorCenter:{name:"Indian Creek Visitor Center",info:"Provides park information, maps, educational displays about local ecology, and gift shop items.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(208) 443-2200"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"4223 N Indian Creek Rd, Coolin, ID 83821",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Priest+Lake+State+Park+Idaho",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/parks/priest-lake/",bestTimeToVisit:"July through September for water activities, or January through March for winter sports.",tips:["Book campsites well in advance as they fill up quickly during summer months.","Bring insect repellent, especially during the early summer season.","Check the official website for current water safety conditions and boat ramp status."],nearbyAttractions:["Coolin, Idaho","Kaniksu National Forest","Sandpoint, Idaho","Schweitzer Mountain Resort"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle for day use",fishingBoating:"The park offers multiple boat ramps and docks. Fishing is popular for lake trout, kokanee, and cutthroat trout."},{id:"thousand-springs-state-park",name:"Thousand Springs State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/45a75f43891bd7a6.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Thousand Springs State Park is a collection of unique units along the Snake River, famous for its dramatic waterfalls, crystal-clear springs, and rich agricultural history.",fullDescription:"Thousand Springs State Park consists of several distinct units including Malad Gorge, Niagara Springs, Ritter Island, and Billingsley Creek. The park showcases the unique geology of the Snake River Plain, where water from the Eastern Snake River Plain Aquifer emerges from the canyon walls as massive springs. Visitors can explore the deep, narrow gorge of Malad Gorge, view the powerful Niagara Springs, or enjoy the historic charm of Ritter Island, which was once a world-renowned dairy farm.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and nature photography","Fishing in the Snake River","Picnicking at scenic overlooks","Bird watching","Exploring historic farm sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Malad Gorge Rim Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A relatively flat trail that offers spectacular views of the 250-foot deep Malad Gorge and the Devil's Washbowl."},{name:"Ritter Island Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail winds around the historic island, providing lush greenery and close-up views of the springs emerging from the canyon walls."}],campingInfo:"While there is no traditional tent or RV camping within the park units, there are nearby private campgrounds and lodging options in the town of Hagerman.",wildlife:["Great Blue Herons","Mule Deer","Red-tailed Hawks","River Otters","Rainbow Trout"],visitorCenter:{name:"Thousand Springs State Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, educational brochures, and information regarding the various units of the park.",hours:"Monday - Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(208) 837-4505"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"20053 Hwy 30, Hagerman, ID 83332",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Thousand+Springs+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/parks/thousand-springs/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and early summer when the waterfalls are at peak flow and the wildflowers are in bloom.",tips:["Visit the Malad Gorge unit early in the morning to avoid crowds and capture the best light for photography.","Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities along the Snake River.","Check the official website before visiting, as some units like Ritter Island may have seasonal access restrictions."],nearbyAttractions:["Hagerman Fossil Beds National Monument","Balanced Rock","City of Rocks National Reserve","Twin Falls"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to most units, though some specific parking areas may require a small fee or Idaho State Parks Passport.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Snake River and designated areas of the park; however, there are no boat rentals available on-site."},{id:"hells-gate-state-park",name:"Hells Gate State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-hells-gate-state-park-idaho.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located at the gateway to the deepest river gorge in North America, this park offers stunning views of the Snake River and rich Nez Perce cultural history.",fullDescription:"Hells Gate State Park is situated along the banks of the Snake River in Lewiston, Idaho. The park serves as the entrance to the Hells Canyon National Recreation Area, featuring dramatic basalt cliffs and a unique high-desert ecosystem. The area is historically significant as the homeland of the Nimiipuu (Nez Perce) people and was passed through by the Lewis and Clark Expedition in 1805.",thingsToDo:["Jet boat tours of Hells Canyon","Camping and RV site stays","Fishing for steelhead and salmon","Cycling on the paved greenbelt trail","Educational programs at the visitor center"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Discovery Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle, flat path that winds through the park's riparian zone, offering excellent birdwatching opportunities."},{name:"Jack O'Connor Center Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail climbs slightly above the river valley, providing panoramic views of the Snake River and the surrounding canyon walls."}],campingInfo:"The park features 64 campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as several tent-only sites and a group camping area.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","California quail"],visitorCenter:{name:"Jack O'Connor Hunting Heritage and Education Center",info:"Features exhibits on the life of Jack O'Connor, local history, and the natural environment of the Hells Canyon region.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(208) 799-5015"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"5100 Hells Gate Rd, Lewiston, ID 83501",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hells+Gate+State+Park+Lewiston+ID",officialWebsite:"https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/parks/hells-gate/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal wildlife viewing.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the canyon floor can get very hot during summer months.","Check the river conditions before planning any water-based activities.","Visit the Lewis and Clark interpretive displays to learn about the local indigenous history."],nearbyAttractions:["Hells Canyon National Recreation Area","Nez Perce National Historical Park","City of Lewiston","Chief Timothy Park"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle per day",fishingBoating:"The park provides a public boat ramp with access to the Snake River, popular for fishing and recreational boating."}],montana:[{id:"lewis-and-clark-caverns-state-park",name:"Lewis and Clark Caverns State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/5f7f542071c817f9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Explore one of the most highly decorated limestone caverns in the American Northwest, featuring spectacular stalactites, stalagmites, and helictites.",fullDescription:"Lewis and Clark Caverns State Park is Montana's first and best-known state park. The park features a massive limestone cavern system formed by ancient geological processes. Visitors can take guided tours through the cool, subterranean chambers to witness stunning rock formations. Above ground, the park offers rugged hiking trails through juniper forests and limestone cliffs, providing panoramic views of the Jefferson River valley.",thingsToDo:["Guided cavern tours","Hiking and trail running","Wildlife viewing","Camping","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cave Loop Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the park's unique limestone landscape and offers great views of the surrounding mountains."},{name:"Eastside Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"This trail traverses the eastern side of the park, passing through diverse habitats and offering excellent opportunities for bird watching."}],campingInfo:"The park features a campground with 40 sites, including RV-friendly spots, tent sites, and three camping cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Bighorn sheep","Mountain lions","Golden eagles","Bats"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lewis and Clark Caverns Visitor Center",info:"The center provides educational exhibits on the geology and history of the caverns, as well as tour ticket sales and gift shop items.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(406) 287-3541"},hours:"8:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily (Cavern tours are seasonal)",address:"25 Lewis & Clark Caverns Rd, Whitehall, MT 59759",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lewis+and+Clark+Caverns+State+Park+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/lewis-and-clark-caverns",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn",tips:["Book your cavern tour tickets well in advance as they sell out quickly during peak season.","Wear comfortable walking shoes with good grip, as the cave paths can be slippery.","Bring a light jacket, as the temperature inside the caverns remains a constant 50 degrees Fahrenheit year-round."],nearbyAttractions:["Whitehall","Three Forks","Headwaters State Park","Bozeman"],entranceFees:"Entrance is free for Montana residents with a vehicle registration fee; non-residents pay a daily vehicle entrance fee of $8.00.",fishingBoating:"While there is no direct water access for boating within the park, the nearby Jefferson River offers excellent fishing and floating opportunities."},{id:"makoshika-state-park",name:"Makoshika State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e3c14c10100ab2bb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Montana's largest state park, Makoshika features spectacular badlands formations and a rich history of dinosaur fossil discoveries.",fullDescription:"Makoshika State Park encompasses over 11,000 acres of badlands, characterized by dramatic rock formations, deep ravines, and hoodoos. The park is world-renowned for its paleontological significance, having yielded numerous fossils of dinosaurs such as Tyrannosaurus rex and Triceratops. Visitors can explore the rugged landscape that tells a story of the Cretaceous period, offering a unique blend of geological wonder and scientific discovery.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and backpacking","Fossil hunting and viewing exhibits","Wildlife photography","Picnicking","Disc golf"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cap Rock Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible loop that offers excellent views of the badlands and unique geological formations."},{name:"Hungry Joe Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail winds through scenic coulees and offers a more immersive experience into the heart of the badlands."}],campingInfo:"The park offers two campgrounds, Cains Coulee and Hungry Joe, with a total of 28 campsites. Amenities include fire rings, picnic tables, and vault toilets, but no water or electrical hookups are available.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Wild turkeys","Golden eagles","Bobcats","Mountain lions"],visitorCenter:{name:"Makoshika Visitor Center",info:"Features impressive dinosaur fossil displays, a gift shop, and educational information about the park's geology.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(406) 377-6256"},hours:"Sunrise to sunset daily",address:"1301 Snyder Ave, Glendive, MT 59330",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Makoshika+State+Park+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/makoshika",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and trail conditions.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as there is no potable water available in the backcountry.","Wear sturdy hiking boots to navigate the loose, uneven terrain of the badlands.","Visit during the early morning or late evening to avoid the intense midday heat and see more wildlife."],nearbyAttractions:["Glendive Dinosaur and Fossil Museum","Frontier Gateway Museum","Yellowstone River","Medicine Rocks State Park"],entranceFees:"$8.00 daily vehicle fee for non-residents; free for Montana residents.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not a primary activity within the park, though the nearby Yellowstone River offers excellent fishing opportunities."},{id:"bannack-state-park",name:"Bannack State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/69864e6ac7967be3.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Bannack State Park is a remarkably well-preserved ghost town that offers a vivid glimpse into Montana's gold rush history.",fullDescription:"Bannack was the site of Montana's first major gold discovery in 1862. Today, it stands as one of the best-preserved ghost towns in the United States, featuring over 60 original structures that visitors can explore. The park provides a unique historical experience, showcasing the rugged life of early settlers, miners, and outlaws in the Montana Territory.",thingsToDo:["Guided historical tours","Gold panning","Photography","Wildlife viewing","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bannack Bench Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle trail offering panoramic views of the historic townsite and the surrounding Grasshopper Valley."},{name:"Bannack Creek Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A short, scenic walk along the creek that runs through the heart of the historic district."}],campingInfo:"The park features the Vigilante Campground with 24 campsites, including some suitable for RVs, though no hookups are available.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Pronghorn","Red-tailed hawks","Great horned owls","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bannack Visitor Center",info:"Located in the historic Meade Hotel, the visitor center provides maps, historical exhibits, and information on guided tours.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Summer season)",phone:"(406) 834-3413"},hours:"8:00 AM - 9:00 PM daily",address:"4200 Bannack Rd, Dillon, MT 59725",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bannack+State+Park+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/bannack",bestTimeToVisit:"June through September for full access to historic buildings and pleasant weather.",tips:["Bring comfortable walking shoes as the townsite has uneven dirt roads and boardwalks.","Visit during Bannack Days in July for historical reenactments and demonstrations.","Pack plenty of water and sunscreen as there is limited shade in the townsite."],nearbyAttractions:["Dillon, MT","Beaverhead-Deerlodge National Forest","Clark Canyon Reservoir"],entranceFees:"$8.00 daily vehicle fee for non-residents; free for Montana residents.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Grasshopper Creek, which runs through the park, primarily for trout."},{id:"giant-springs-state-park",name:"Giant Springs State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/62b706ffb70605c3.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to one of the largest freshwater springs in the world, this park offers stunning views of the Missouri River and lush riparian landscapes.",fullDescription:"Giant Springs State Park is located along the banks of the Missouri River in Great Falls, Montana. The park features one of the largest freshwater springs in the world, which flows at a rate of 156 million gallons of water per day at a constant temperature of 54 degrees Fahrenheit. The area was discovered by the Lewis and Clark Expedition in 1805 and remains a significant historical and natural landmark. The park's geology is defined by the Madison Limestone aquifer, which feeds the crystal-clear spring water into the river.",thingsToDo:["Bird watching","Fishing in the Missouri River","Picnicking","Cycling on the River's Edge Trail","Photography","Visiting the Fish Hatchery"],hikingTrails:[{name:"River's Edge Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A paved, accessible trail that follows the Missouri River, offering excellent views of the water and local wildlife."},{name:"Springs Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, scenic loop that takes visitors directly around the main spring head and the surrounding lush vegetation."}],campingInfo:"There is no overnight camping available within the boundaries of Giant Springs State Park.",wildlife:["Great Blue Herons","Bald Eagles","White-tailed Deer","Rainbow Trout","Canada Geese"],visitorCenter:{name:"Giant Springs State Park Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides educational exhibits about the history of the springs, the Lewis and Clark expedition, and local geology.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(406) 727-1212"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"4800 Giant Springs Rd, Great Falls, MT 59405",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Giant+Springs+State+Park+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/giant-springs",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and wildlife viewing.",tips:["Bring a camera to capture the vibrant blue color of the spring water.","Visit the adjacent fish hatchery to feed the trout.","Wear comfortable walking shoes to explore the extensive River's Edge Trail system."],nearbyAttractions:["C.M. Russell Museum","Lewis and Clark Interpretive Center","First Peoples Buffalo Jump State Park","Great Falls Historic District"],entranceFees:"Free for Montana residents; $8.00 daily vehicle fee for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Missouri River within the park boundaries, provided you have a valid Montana fishing license. No boat ramps are located within the park."},{id:"flathead-lake-state-park",name:"Flathead Lake State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/de17172fd1b9a2a7.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Flathead Lake State Park is a collection of six unique units surrounding the largest natural freshwater lake in the western United States.",fullDescription:"Flathead Lake State Park consists of six distinct units: Big Arm, Finley Point, Wayfarers, West Shore, Yellow Bay, and Wild Horse Island. The park offers stunning views of the Mission Mountains and crystal-clear waters formed by glacial activity. Visitors can explore diverse ecosystems, ranging from rocky shorelines to dense forests, while enjoying the recreational opportunities provided by the massive lake.",thingsToDo:["Boating and sailing","Swimming and sunbathing","Wildlife viewing","Camping","Fishing for lake trout and whitefish"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Wayfarers Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, scenic loop that winds through the forest and offers beautiful views of the lake and surrounding cliffs."},{name:"Wild Horse Island Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A trail that traverses the interior of Wild Horse Island, providing opportunities to spot wild horses, bighorn sheep, and mule deer."}],campingInfo:"The park offers various camping options across its units, including tent sites, RV sites with electrical hookups, and group camping areas. Reservations are highly recommended during the summer season.",wildlife:["Bighorn sheep","Mule deer","Bald eagles","Osprey","Wild horses"],visitorCenter:{name:"Flathead Lake State Park Headquarters",info:"Provides information on park regulations, recreational opportunities, and educational exhibits about the lake's ecology.",hours:"Monday - Friday: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(406) 837-3041"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"490 North Meridian Road, Kalispell, MT 59901",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Flathead+Lake+State+Park+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/flathead-lake",bestTimeToVisit:"June through September for water activities and pleasant weather.",tips:["Check the specific unit you plan to visit, as they are located in different areas around the lake.","Always wear a life jacket when boating or swimming in the lake.","Book campsites well in advance, especially for weekends during the peak summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Bigfork","Polson","Glacier National Park","Swan Lake"],entranceFees:"$8.00 daily vehicle fee for non-residents; free for Montana residents.",fishingBoating:"The park features multiple boat ramps and docks across its units, making it a premier destination for fishing, kayaking, and motorboating."},{id:"lone-pine-state-park",name:"Lone Pine State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-lone-pine-state-park-montana.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched high above the Flathead Valley, Lone Pine State Park offers breathtaking panoramic views of the valley, Flathead Lake, and the surrounding mountain ranges.",fullDescription:"Lone Pine State Park is a 270-acre park located just outside of Kalispell, Montana. It serves as a gateway to the outdoors, featuring a diverse landscape of coniferous forests and open meadows. The park is renowned for its extensive trail system that caters to hikers, mountain bikers, and horseback riders, while providing educational opportunities through its visitor center which highlights the local ecosystem and wildlife.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain Biking","Wildlife Viewing","Picnicking","Educational Programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lone Pine Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through dense forest and provides several vantage points overlooking the Flathead Valley."},{name:"Cliff Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A steeper path that leads to dramatic rock outcroppings and offers the most expansive views in the park."}],campingInfo:"Lone Pine State Park does not offer overnight camping or RV hookups; it is strictly a day-use facility.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Black bears","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Mountain lions"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lone Pine State Park Visitor Center",info:"The center features interactive exhibits on local flora and fauna, geology, and history, along with a gift shop and information desk.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(406) 755-2706"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"300 Lone Pine Rd, Kalispell, MT 59901",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lone+Pine+State+Park+Kalispell+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/lone-pine",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal trail conditions and clear mountain views.",tips:["Bring binoculars to take full advantage of the expansive valley views from the overlooks.","Check the visitor center schedule for seasonal guided nature walks and educational workshops.","Be bear aware and carry bear spray, as the park is located in active black bear habitat."],nearbyAttractions:["Flathead Lake","Glacier National Park","Downtown Kalispell","Herron Park"],entranceFees:"Free for Montana residents; non-residents pay a $8.00 daily vehicle entrance fee.",fishingBoating:"There is no fishing or boating available within the park boundaries as there are no lakes or rivers on the property."},{id:"pictograph-cave-state-park",name:"Pictograph Cave State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2244cd075316fc90.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Explore ancient history at this archaeological site featuring rock paintings created by prehistoric hunters over 2,000 years ago.",fullDescription:"Pictograph Cave State Park is a 23-acre park that features three main caves: Pictograph, Middle, and Ghost caves. These caves served as a home for generations of prehistoric hunters. Archaeologists have recovered over 30,000 artifacts from the site, including stone tools, weapons, and paintings that provide a window into the lives of the people who inhabited the Yellowstone River valley thousands of years ago.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the interpretive trail","Bird watching","Photography","Educational tours","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pictograph Cave Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.25 miles",description:"A paved, loop trail that leads visitors past the three main caves with interpretive signs explaining the history and geology of the site."},{name:"Canyon Rim Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short trail offering scenic views of the surrounding sandstone cliffs and the valley below."}],campingInfo:"There is no camping available at Pictograph Cave State Park. The park is strictly for day-use only.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Red-tailed hawks","Great horned owls","Cottontail rabbits","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pictograph Cave Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center features exhibits on the archaeological findings and the history of the rock art found within the caves.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(406) 254-7342"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"3401 Coburn Rd, Billings, MT 59101",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pictograph+Cave+State+Park+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/pictograph-cave",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal hiking conditions.",tips:["Bring binoculars to get a better view of the pictographs, as they are protected and cannot be approached closely.","Wear sturdy walking shoes as the trail has some incline and uneven surfaces.","Visit during the early morning to avoid the heat of the day and see more wildlife."],nearbyAttractions:["Billings","Yellowstone River","Rimrocks","ZooMontana"],entranceFees:"$8.00 per vehicle for non-residents; Montana residents enter for free with their vehicle registration fee."},{id:"medicine-rocks-state-park",name:"Medicine Rocks State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/33e680b041ca39f9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Medicine Rocks State Park is a unique geological site featuring massive sandstone pillars and ancient petroglyphs in the heart of eastern Montana.",fullDescription:"Medicine Rocks State Park is renowned for its dramatic sandstone formations that rise from the prairie, sculpted by wind and water over millions of years. Historically significant to Native American tribes, the park contains numerous petroglyphs and pictographs. The site offers a surreal landscape that has captivated visitors for centuries, including Theodore Roosevelt, who famously described the area as 'as fantastically beautiful a place as I have ever seen.'",thingsToDo:["Hiking among sandstone pillars","Wildlife photography","Stargazing","Picnicking","Bird watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Medicine Rocks Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through the base of the towering sandstone formations, offering close-up views of the unique rock textures."},{name:"Prairie Vista Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail climbs to higher ground, providing panoramic views of the surrounding Montana prairie and the park's rock formations from above."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 12 primitive campsites suitable for tents and small RVs. There are no hookups, and water is not available on-site, so visitors must pack in their own supplies.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Pronghorn antelope","Golden eagles","Rattlesnakes","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Medicine Rocks Information Kiosk",info:"Self-service information kiosk providing maps, historical context, and safety guidelines for exploring the rock formations.",hours:"Daily: Sunrise to Sunset",phone:"(406) 377-6256"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"Medicine Rocks Rd, Ekalaka, MT 59324",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Medicine+Rocks+State+Park+MT",officialWebsite:"https://fwp.mt.gov/stateparks/medicine-rocks",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the most comfortable weather and accessible roads.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as there are no potable water sources within the park.","The sandstone is fragile; please do not climb on or deface the rock formations.","The park is an excellent location for night photography due to minimal light pollution."],nearbyAttractions:["Carter County Museum in Ekalaka","Makoshika State Park","Custer National Forest","Ekalaka"],entranceFees:"Free for Montana residents; non-residents pay a daily vehicle fee of $8.00.",fishingBoating:"There are no lakes or rivers within the park boundaries, so fishing and boating are not available."}],nevada:[{id:"valley-of-fire-state-park",name:"Valley of Fire State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/cd684528956b00e5.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nevada's oldest and largest state park, famous for its stunning 40,000-year-old Aztec sandstone formations and ancient petroglyphs.",fullDescription:"Valley of Fire State Park covers nearly 46,000 acres and is known for its bright red sandstone formations that appear to be on fire when reflecting the sun's rays. The park features ancient petroglyphs carved into the rock by the Basketmaker culture and later the Paiute people. Geologically, the park is a showcase of complex uplift and faulting, resulting in dramatic landscapes that have served as backdrops for numerous films and television productions.",thingsToDo:["Photography of red rock formations","Hiking through slot canyons","Viewing ancient petroglyphs","Camping under the desert stars","Scenic driving tours"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Fire Wave Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A popular trail leading to a unique, striped sandstone formation that resembles a wave."},{name:"White Domes Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.25 miles",description:"A loop trail that takes hikers through a slot canyon and past remnants of an old movie set."}],campingInfo:"The park offers two campgrounds, Atlatl Rock and Arch Rock, featuring a total of 72 units with water, tables, grills, and restrooms. Some sites offer electrical and water hookups.",wildlife:["Desert bighorn sheep","Coyotes","Kit foxes","Spotted skunks","Desert tortoises"],visitorCenter:{name:"Valley of Fire Visitor Center",info:"Provides educational exhibits on the park's geology, ecology, and history, along with a gift shop and ranger assistance.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(702) 397-2088"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"29450 Valley of Fire Hwy, Overton, NV 89040",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Valley+of+Fire+State+Park+NV",officialWebsite:"https://parks.nv.gov/parks/valley-of-fire",bestTimeToVisit:"October through April to avoid extreme summer heat.",tips:["Bring at least one gallon of water per person if hiking during the day.","Visit the petroglyphs at Atlatl Rock early in the morning to avoid crowds.","Check the park website for seasonal road closures or extreme heat warnings."],nearbyAttractions:["Lake Mead National Recreation Area","Overton","Lost City Museum","Las Vegas"],entranceFees:"$10.00 per vehicle for Nevada residents, $15.00 per vehicle for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"There is no fishing or boating available within the boundaries of Valley of Fire State Park."},{id:"cathedral-gorge-state-park",name:"Cathedral Gorge State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7c6e71b8e106f12c.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Experience the dramatic, towering bentonite clay spires and narrow slot canyons of this unique Nevada desert landscape.",fullDescription:"Cathedral Gorge State Park is a geological wonder located in a long, narrow valley in southeastern Nevada. The park is famous for its dramatic, cathedral-like spires and narrow slot canyons carved into soft, light-colored bentonite clay. Formed by volcanic activity millions of years ago and shaped by erosion, the landscape offers a surreal, labyrinthine environment that is unlike anywhere else in the state.",thingsToDo:["Hiking through slot canyons","Photography of unique rock formations","Picnicking in shaded areas","Stargazing in the dark desert sky","Camping under the stars"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Miller Point Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, scenic trail leading to an overlook with panoramic views of the entire gorge."},{name:"Moon Caves Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.25 miles",description:"A short but adventurous path that takes you deep into the narrow, winding slot canyons."}],campingInfo:"The park features 22 campsites equipped with tables, grills, and fire pits. There are restrooms with showers and a dump station available for RVs.",wildlife:["Coyotes","Black-tailed jackrabbits","Great horned owls","Red-tailed hawks","Desert lizards"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cathedral Gorge Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on the park's geology, history, and trail maps, along with interpretive exhibits.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(775) 728-4460"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for day use and camping.",address:"111 Cathedral Gorge State Park Rd, Panaca, NV 89042",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cathedral+Gorge+State+Park+NV",officialWebsite:"https://parks.nv.gov/parks/cathedral-gorge",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall, when temperatures are mild and comfortable for hiking.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the desert air is very dry.","Wear sturdy shoes for navigating the sandy and uneven canyon floors.","Visit during the golden hour for the best photography lighting on the clay spires."],nearbyAttractions:["Echo Canyon State Park","Spring Valley State Park","Kershaw-Ryan State Park","Town of Panaca"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle for Nevada residents, $10.00 per vehicle for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"Fishing and boating are not available at Cathedral Gorge State Park, as it is a land-based geological park."},{id:"lake-tahoe-nevada-state-park",name:"Lake Tahoe Nevada State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7e09899428509f12.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Lake Tahoe Nevada State Park features pristine alpine scenery, crystal-clear waters, and rugged mountain terrain along the eastern shore of Lake Tahoe.",fullDescription:"Spanning over 14,000 acres, Lake Tahoe Nevada State Park encompasses diverse landscapes ranging from the sandy beaches of Sand Harbor to the high-altitude wilderness of the Marlette-Hobart backcountry. The park is renowned for 