mations."},{name:"Prospector Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A longer trail that circles the perimeter of the park, offering panoramic views of the surrounding Mimbres Valley."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 52 campsites, including 22 sites with 30/50 amp electrical hookups and water, plus several primitive tent sites and a group camping area.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Javelina","Cottontail rabbits","Great horned owls","Red-tailed hawks"],visitorCenter:{name:"City of Rocks Visitor Center",info:"Provides interpretive exhibits on the volcanic history of the area, park maps, and information on local flora and fauna.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(575) 536-2800"},hours:"Open 24 hours for registered campers; day use from sunrise to sunset.",address:"327 NM-61, Faywood, NM 88034",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=City+of+Rocks+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/city-of-rocks-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures, or Winter for clear night skies.",tips:["Bring a telescope or binoculars as the park is a designated dark sky area.","Wear sturdy hiking shoes to navigate the uneven terrain around the boulders.","Visit during the early morning or late afternoon for the best lighting on the rock formations."],nearbyAttractions:["Gila Cliff Dwellings National Monument","Silver City","Faywood Hot Springs","Mimbres Valley"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle day-use fee.",fishingBoating:"Not applicable; this is a high-desert park with no significant bodies of water for boating or fishing."},{id:"bottomless-lakes-state-park",name:"Bottomless Lakes State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8251c629cdfbfaa0.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"New Mexico's first state park features a series of unique sinkhole lakes nestled against dramatic red bluffs.",fullDescription:"Established in 1933, Bottomless Lakes State Park is located along the Pecos River valley. The park is famous for its nine small, deep lakes formed by the collapse of underground salt deposits, creating sinkholes that range from 17 to 90 feet deep. The water appears deep blue or green due to the depth and aquatic vegetation, contrasting sharply with the surrounding arid desert landscape.",thingsToDo:["Swimming in Lea Lake","Scuba diving","Bird watching","Picnicking","Camping","Hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Wetland Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that winds through the marshy areas near the lakes, offering excellent opportunities for birding."},{name:"Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2 miles",description:"This trail climbs the red bluffs surrounding the lakes, providing panoramic views of the entire park and the Pecos Valley."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 32 developed campsites with water and electric hookups, as well as primitive camping areas. Reservations are highly recommended during peak season.",wildlife:["Painted turtles","Great blue herons","Roadrunners","Coyotes","Various migratory waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bottomless Lakes Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on the unique geology of the sinkhole lakes and local history.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(575) 988-3638"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"545 A Bottomless Lakes Rd, Roswell, NM 88201",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bottomless+Lakes+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/bottomless-lakes-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and active bird migration.",tips:["Bring polarized sunglasses to better see into the deep, clear water of the sinkholes.","The park is a popular spot for scuba diving; ensure you have proper certification and permits.","Stay on marked trails to protect the fragile desert ecosystem and avoid steep drop-offs."],nearbyAttractions:["Roswell Museum and Art Center","International UFO Museum and Research Center","Bitter Lake National Wildlife Refuge"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle per day.",fishingBoating:"Lea Lake is open for non-motorized boating and fishing. The lake is stocked with rainbow trout during the winter months."},{id:"cimarron-canyon-state-park",name:"Cimarron Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0582eb3ebce7f40d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the heart of the Sangre de Cristo Mountains, this park offers stunning granite palisades and excellent trout fishing along the Cimarron River.",fullDescription:"Cimarron Canyon State Park is a scenic gem located within the Colin Neblett Wildlife Area. The park is defined by the dramatic Palisades, which are towering granite cliffs that rise 400 feet above the Cimarron River. The area is rich in history, having served as a vital route for travelers on the Santa Fe Trail. Today, it provides a rugged landscape for outdoor enthusiasts, featuring diverse ecosystems ranging from ponderosa pine forests to high-mountain meadows.",thingsToDo:["Fly fishing for trout","Wildlife viewing","Hiking","Picnicking","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Tolby Meadow Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that climbs through dense forests to reach a beautiful high-mountain meadow."},{name:"Palisades Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A steep climb that rewards hikers with breathtaking panoramic views of the canyon and the iconic granite cliffs."}],campingInfo:"The park features several campgrounds including Maverick, Ponderosa, and Cimarron, offering both tent and RV sites with basic amenities like fire rings and picnic tables.",wildlife:["Elk","Black bear","Mule deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cimarron Canyon Ranger Station",info:"Provides information on trail conditions, fishing regulations, and local wildlife safety.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(575) 377-6271"},hours:"Open 24 hours for camping; day use from sunrise to sunset.",address:"28897 US-64, Eagle Nest, NM 87718",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cimarron+Canyon+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/cimarron-canyon-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for pleasant weather and active wildlife.",tips:["Bring polarized sunglasses to help spot trout in the river.","Be bear aware and store all food in bear-proof containers.","Check the weather forecast as mountain conditions can change rapidly."],nearbyAttractions:["Eagle Nest Lake State Park","Philmont Scout Ranch","Village of Cimarron","Angel Fire Resort"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle day-use fee.",fishingBoating:"The Cimarron River is a premier destination for fly fishing, known for its healthy populations of rainbow and brown trout."},{id:"elephant-butte-lake-state-park",name:"Elephant Butte Lake State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/53c58f8582453a87.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"New Mexico's largest state park, Elephant Butte Lake offers expansive water recreation and desert landscapes in the heart of the Rio Grande Valley.",fullDescription:"Elephant Butte Lake State Park is centered around the massive Elephant Butte Reservoir, created by the construction of the Elephant Butte Dam in 1916. The park serves as a premier destination for boating, fishing, and water sports in the high desert. Beyond the water, the park features rugged volcanic terrain and diverse desert flora, providing a unique backdrop for camping, hiking, and birdwatching throughout the year.",thingsToDo:["Boating and jet skiing","Fishing for striped bass and walleye","Swimming at designated beach areas","Birdwatching along the shoreline","Camping in developed sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Desert Cove Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path offering scenic views of the lake and opportunities to spot local desert wildlife."},{name:"Lions Beach Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 mile",description:"A relaxing walk along the shoreline that provides easy access to swimming areas and picnic spots."}],campingInfo:"The park offers over 200 developed campsites, many with electric and water hookups, as well as primitive camping areas along the shoreline.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Coyotes","Great blue herons","Bald eagles","Roadrunners"],visitorCenter:{name:"Elephant Butte Lake State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, current lake levels, fishing regulations, and local wildlife exhibits.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(575) 744-5923"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping; day use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"101 NM-195, Elephant Butte, NM 87935",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Elephant+Butte+Lake+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/elephant-butte-lake-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal water recreation conditions.",tips:["Check current lake levels on the official website before planning your boating trip.","Bring plenty of sunscreen and water, as the desert sun is intense even in cooler months.","Reservations for campsites are highly recommended during summer weekends and holidays."],nearbyAttractions:["Truth or Consequences","Caballo Lake State Park","Bosque del Apache National Wildlife Refuge"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle per day for day-use entry.",fishingBoating:"The park features multiple boat ramps and extensive shoreline access for fishing; common catches include striped bass, walleye, and catfish."},{id:"hyde-memorial-state-park",name:"Hyde Memorial State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6a970de2efcb0a85.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the Sangre de Cristo Mountains, Hyde Memorial State Park offers a high-altitude escape just minutes from Santa Fe.",fullDescription:"Hyde Memorial State Park is New Mexico's first state park, established in 1938. Located at an elevation of 8,500 feet, the park is surrounded by the Santa Fe National Forest and features stunning views of the surrounding peaks. The park is a popular destination for year-round recreation, including cross-country skiing in the winter and hiking through lush aspen and conifer forests during the warmer months.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Cross-country skiing","Picnicking","Camping","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Big Tesuque Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through dense forest and offers beautiful views of the Tesuque Creek valley."},{name:"Hyde Park Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A gentle path perfect for families, showcasing the park's diverse alpine flora and fauna."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 7 developed campsites with electrical hookups and several tent-only sites, along with a group shelter area.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Black bear","Wild turkey","Abert's squirrel","Mountain bluebird"],visitorCenter:{name:"Hyde Memorial Park Office",info:"Provides information on trail conditions, park maps, and local wildlife education.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(505) 983-7175"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"740 Hyde Park Rd, Santa Fe, NM 87501",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hyde+Memorial+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/hyde-memorial-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for hiking, or winter for cross-country skiing.",tips:["Bring layers as temperatures drop significantly at this high elevation.","Drink plenty of water to acclimate to the 8,500-foot altitude.","Visit in late September to see the spectacular golden aspen leaves."],nearbyAttractions:["Santa Fe Ski Basin","Santa Fe Plaza","Museum of International Folk Art","Canyon Road Art District"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle fee for day use.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not a primary activity at this park, though small streams are present in the surrounding national forest."},{id:"sugarite-canyon-state-park",name:"Sugarite Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/ba243dc1ce1b7ae1.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the Sangre de Cristo Mountains, Sugarite Canyon State Park offers stunning alpine lakes and a rich history of coal mining.",fullDescription:"Sugarite Canyon State Park is located on the border of New Mexico and Colorado, featuring two beautiful lakes, Lake Maloya and Lake Alice. The park is renowned for its dramatic volcanic geology and the remnants of the historic Sugarite coal mining camp, which operated in the early 20th century. Visitors can explore the rugged terrain, enjoy high-altitude fishing, and observe diverse wildlife in this pristine mountain environment.",thingsToDo:["Fishing","Hiking","Bird watching","Camping","Historical site exploration"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Little Coal Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail winds through the historic mining area, offering glimpses of old foundations and beautiful canyon views."},{name:"Ponderosa Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"3.5 miles",description:"A challenging climb that rewards hikers with panoramic vistas of the surrounding Sangre de Cristo Mountains."}],campingInfo:"The park features two campgrounds, Soda Pocket and Prairie Dog, offering a mix of primitive and developed sites with some electric hookups available.",wildlife:["Black bear","Elk","Mule deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagle"],visitorCenter:{name:"Sugarite Canyon Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and educational exhibits about the local mining heritage.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(575) 445-5607"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"210 State Park Rd, Raton, NM 87740",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sugarite+Canyon+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/sugarite-canyon-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for pleasant temperatures and wildlife viewing.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird and elk watching opportunities.","Check the weather forecast as mountain conditions can change rapidly.","Visit the historic coal camp ruins to learn about the area's industrial past."],nearbyAttractions:["Raton","Capulin Volcano National Monument","Philmont Scout Ranch"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle entrance fee.",fishingBoating:"Lake Maloya is a premier destination for trout fishing; non-motorized boats and electric trolling motors are permitted."},{id:"oliver-lee-memorial-state-park",name:"Oliver Lee Memorial State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8d3dc51735f89913.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled at the base of the Sacramento Mountains, this park offers a stunning desert landscape and a glimpse into the history of 19th-century ranching.",fullDescription:"Oliver Lee Memorial State Park is located in the Tularosa Basin, providing a dramatic backdrop of the Sacramento Mountains. The park features the historic Frenchy's Cabin and the restored Oliver Lee Ranch house, which dates back to the late 1800s. Visitors can explore the unique Chihuahuan Desert ecosystem, characterized by rugged canyons, diverse flora, and spectacular views of the White Sands area.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Bird watching","Historical site tours","Picnicking","Stargazing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Dog Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"5.5 miles",description:"A challenging climb that rewards hikers with panoramic views of the Tularosa Basin and the surrounding desert landscape."},{name:"Riparian Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A gentle, educational walk through the lush vegetation near the canyon stream, perfect for spotting local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 44 developed campsites, including sites with water and electric hookups, as well as primitive tent sites and a dump station.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Javelina","Coyotes","Golden eagles","Various lizard species"],visitorCenter:{name:"Oliver Lee Memorial State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features exhibits on the history of the ranch, local geology, and the diverse plant and animal life of the Chihuahuan Desert.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(575) 437-8284"},hours:"Daily: 7:00 AM - 9:00 PM",address:"409 Dog Canyon Rd, Alamogordo, NM 88310",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Oliver+Lee+Memorial+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/oliver-lee-memorial-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall, when temperatures are mild and desert wildflowers are in bloom.",tips:["Bring plenty of water, as the desert climate is extremely dry.","Wear sturdy hiking boots if you plan to tackle the Dog Canyon Trail.","Check the weather forecast before hiking, as flash floods can occur in the canyons."],nearbyAttractions:["White Sands National Park","New Mexico Museum of Space History","Cloudcroft","Lincoln National Forest"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle entrance fee.",fishingBoating:"There is no fishing or boating available at this park."},{id:"heron-lake-state-park",name:"Heron Lake State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/eaa86f04acf80b7b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A serene high-altitude reservoir known for its quiet waters, sailing, and excellent kokanee salmon fishing.",fullDescription:"Heron Lake State Park is located in the high mountains of northern New Mexico at an elevation of 7,100 feet. The lake is a popular destination for those seeking tranquility, as it enforces a 'no-wake' policy for motorboats, making it ideal for sailing, kayaking, and canoeing. Surrounded by ponderosa pine forests and rugged terrain, the park offers a peaceful retreat with abundant opportunities for wildlife viewing and outdoor recreation.",thingsToDo:["Sailing and kayaking","Fishing for kokanee salmon and trout","Hiking and mountain biking","Bird watching","Winter cross-country skiing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Capulin Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through the ponderosa pine forest and offers beautiful views of the lake."},{name:"Heron Lake Shoreline Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"6.0 miles",description:"A longer trail that follows the perimeter of the lake, providing excellent access to secluded coves and fishing spots."}],campingInfo:"The park features several developed campgrounds including Willow Creek, La Jara, and Blanco, offering a mix of primitive and electric hookup sites.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Ospreys","Mule deer","Elk","Black bears"],visitorCenter:{name:"Heron Lake State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, local wildlife, and current lake conditions.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(575) 588-7470"},hours:"Open 24 hours for camping; day use from sunrise to sunset.",address:"649 State Road 95, Los Ojos, NM 87551",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Heron+Lake+State+Park+NM",officialWebsite:"https://www.emnrd.nm.gov/spd/find-a-park/heron-lake-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for water activities; winter for snow sports.",tips:["Bring a windbreaker as temperatures can drop significantly at this high elevation.","The lake is a no-wake zone, making it perfect for non-motorized watercraft.","Check the official website for current fire restrictions before planning your trip."],nearbyAttractions:["El Vado Lake State Park","Tierra Amarilla","Chama","Cumbres & Toltec Scenic Railroad"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle per day.",fishingBoating:"The lake is famous for kokanee salmon fishing; boat ramps are available at the main recreation areas."}],oregon:[{id:"silver-falls-state-park",name:"Silver Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e52cf5d637086bff.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Known as the crown jewel of the Oregon State Parks system, this park features the spectacular Trail of Ten Falls.",fullDescription:"Silver Falls State Park is Oregon's largest state park, encompassing over 9,000 acres of lush temperate rainforest. The park is world-renowned for its dramatic waterfalls, including the iconic South Falls, which plunges 177 feet over a basalt ledge. The geology of the area is defined by ancient lava flows that have been carved by the Silver Creek drainage over millennia, creating a unique canyon environment that supports diverse flora and fauna.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the Trail of Ten Falls","Picnicking in the historic South Falls Day Use Area","Mountain biking on designated trails","Horseback riding on equestrian trails","Wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail of Ten Falls",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"7.2-mile loop",description:"A world-class hike that takes you past ten stunning waterfalls, including several you can walk behind."},{name:"Maple Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop through old-growth forest that offers beautiful views of the canyon and lush ferns."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a large campground with 48 electrical sites with water, 43 tent sites, and 14 rustic cabins. Reservations are highly recommended during peak season.",wildlife:["Black-tailed deer","Pileated woodpeckers","Douglas squirrels","Pacific giant salamanders","Barred owls"],visitorCenter:{name:"South Falls Lodge and Visitor Center",info:"Provides historical information, park maps, and educational exhibits about the Civilian Conservation Corps.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(503) 873-8681"},hours:"8:00 AM to sunset daily",address:"20024 Silver Falls Hwy SE, Sublimity, OR 97385",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Silver+Falls+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=151",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for peak waterfall flow or Autumn for vibrant foliage.",tips:["Arrive before 9:00 AM on weekends to secure parking, as the lots fill up quickly.","Bring a waterproof jacket even in summer, as the mist from the waterfalls can be heavy.","Wear sturdy hiking boots with good traction, as the trails near the falls can be slippery."],nearbyAttractions:["Salem, Oregon","Oregon Garden","Mount Angel","Willamette Valley Wineries"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle parking permit required.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Silver Creek, though the primary focus of the park is hiking and sightseeing rather than boating."},{id:"smith-rock-state-park",name:"Smith Rock State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/1a708cb627c28179.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Renowned globally as a premier destination for rock climbing, Smith Rock State Park features dramatic volcanic tuff cliffs and the winding Crooked River.",fullDescription:"Smith Rock State Park is a geological marvel located in the high desert of Central Oregon. The park is famous for its sheer, vertical walls of welded tuff, which were formed by volcanic activity millions of years ago. It is widely considered the birthplace of modern American sport climbing, attracting climbers from around the world to test their skills on thousands of routes. Beyond climbing, the park offers stunning vistas of the Cascade Mountains and diverse wildlife habitats along the Crooked River.",thingsToDo:["Rock climbing and bouldering","Hiking and trail running","Wildlife photography","Mountain biking","Bird watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Misery Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"3.7-mile loop",description:"A steep, challenging climb that rewards hikers with iconic panoramic views of the park and the Cascade peaks."},{name:"River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A gentle, scenic path that follows the Crooked River, perfect for spotting otters and nesting raptors."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a walk-in tent camping area known as the Bivouac Area, which is available on a first-come, first-served basis. No RV hookups are available.",wildlife:["Golden eagles","Prairie falcons","River otters","Mule deer","Beavers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Smith Rock Welcome Center",info:"Provides information on climbing routes, trail maps, and park history.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(541) 548-7501"},hours:"Dawn to dusk daily",address:"9241 NE Crooked River Dr, Terrebonne, OR 97760",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Smith+Rock+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=36",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal climbing conditions.",tips:["Arrive early, especially on weekends, as the parking lot fills up quickly.","Bring plenty of water, as there is very little shade in the high desert environment.","Respect all posted climbing closures, which are often in place to protect nesting raptors."],nearbyAttractions:["Redmond","Bend","Peter Skene Ogden State Scenic Viewpoint","Crooked River National Grassland"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle parking permit required.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the Crooked River, primarily for trout, though the river is not suitable for motorized boating."},{id:"ecola-state-park",name:"Ecola State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3ab0a8eaf796b183.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched on the edge of the Pacific Ocean, Ecola State Park offers breathtaking views of rugged cliffs, hidden coves, and the historic Tillamook Rock Lighthouse.",fullDescription:"Ecola State Park stretches along nine miles of coastline between Cannon Beach and Seaside. The park is renowned for its dramatic scenery, including the iconic Haystack Rock views and the offshore Tillamook Rock Lighthouse. Historically, the area was explored by Captain William Clark of the Lewis and Clark Expedition in 1806, who named the area 'Ecola' after the Chinook word for whale. The park's geology is defined by ancient basalt flows and active landslides that continue to shape the rugged coastline.",thingsToDo:["Wildlife viewing","Hiking","Photography","Picnicking","Surfing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Clatsop Loop Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through old-growth Sitka spruce forests and offers stunning vistas of the Pacific Ocean."},{name:"Tillamook Head Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"6.0 miles",description:"Part of the Oregon Coast Trail, this challenging path takes hikers through dense forest with significant elevation changes and historic markers."}],campingInfo:"Ecola State Park does not offer overnight camping or RV hookups; it is strictly a day-use park.",wildlife:["Roosevelt elk","Bald eagles","Gray whales","Harbor seals","Black-tailed deer"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ecola State Park Entrance Station",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and safety guidance for visitors.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(503) 812-0650"},hours:"8:00 AM to Sunset daily",address:"84318 Ecola Park Rd, Cannon Beach, OR 97110",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ecola+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=136",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Summer for whale watching and clear coastal views.",tips:["Arrive early on weekends as the parking lot fills up quickly during peak season.","Bring binoculars for spotting whales and seabirds from the cliffside viewpoints.","Check the tide charts before heading down to the beach areas to avoid getting stranded."],nearbyAttractions:["Cannon Beach","Haystack Rock","Seaside Aquarium","Hug Point State Recreation Site"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle parking permit required.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted from the shoreline in designated areas; no boat ramps are available at this park."},{id:"cape-lookout-state-park",name:"Cape Lookout State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c31a6fb3420a112f.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled on a sand spit between the Pacific Ocean and Netarts Bay, this park offers stunning coastal views and diverse recreational opportunities.",fullDescription:"Cape Lookout State Park is located on a narrow sand spit that separates the Pacific Ocean from Netarts Bay. The park is renowned for its dramatic headland, which juts two miles into the ocean, providing a prime vantage point for whale watching and observing the rugged Oregon coastline. The area is geologically significant, featuring ancient basalt formations and a rich history of coastal erosion and deposition that has shaped the unique landscape seen today.",thingsToDo:["Whale watching from the headland","Beachcombing along the sandy shore","Hiking the Cape Lookout Trail","Bird watching in the estuary","Camping in the forest or near the beach"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cape Lookout Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5.0 miles round trip",description:"This trail follows the spine of the cape, offering breathtaking panoramic views of the Pacific Ocean and surrounding cliffs."},{name:"Cape Lookout Beach Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle walk along the sandy beach that provides excellent opportunities for spotting marine life and enjoying the surf."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with over 170 tent sites, 38 full-hookup RV sites, and 13 cozy yurts available for reservation.",wildlife:["Gray whales","Bald eagles","Harbor seals","Black-tailed deer","Pelagic cormorants"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cape Lookout Park Office",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and current weather conditions for hikers.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(800) 551-6949"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"13000 Whiskey Creek Rd, Tillamook, OR 97141",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cape+Lookout+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=136",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for whale watching and mild weather.",tips:["Bring binoculars for whale watching from the headland.","Check tide charts before heading out for beachcombing.","Dress in layers as coastal weather can change rapidly."],nearbyAttractions:["Tillamook Creamery","Netarts Bay","Cape Meares State Scenic Viewpoint","Three Capes Scenic Loop"],entranceFees:"No daily entrance fee; parking is included with camping reservations or day-use access.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is popular along the beach and in Netarts Bay, though there is no boat ramp within the park itself."},{id:"fort-stevens-state-park",name:"Fort Stevens State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-fort-stevens-state-park-oregon.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Explore the historic remains of a coastal defense fort and the iconic shipwreck of the Peter Iredale on the rugged Oregon coast.",fullDescription:"Fort Stevens State Park encompasses 4,300 acres of diverse landscape, including beaches, wetlands, and forests. Originally built in 1863 to defend the mouth of the Columbia River, the fort served as a military installation through World War II. Today, visitors can explore the historic military museum, walk through the concrete bunkers, and view the skeletal remains of the 1906 shipwreck, the Peter Iredale, which rests on the sandy shore.",thingsToDo:["Tour the historic military museum and bunkers","View the Peter Iredale shipwreck","Bike the extensive paved trail system","Go beachcombing along the Pacific shoreline","Wildlife viewing at Coffenbury Lake"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Jetty Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A flat, accessible path that leads through coastal dunes and provides excellent views of the Columbia River mouth."},{name:"Coffenbury Lake Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A scenic trail that circles the freshwater lake, offering opportunities to spot waterfowl and enjoy the forest canopy."}],campingInfo:"The park features one of the largest campgrounds in the United States, offering over 470 campsites, including full hookup sites, tent sites, and several yurts and cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Black-tailed deer","Great blue herons","Elk","Harbor seals"],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Stevens State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides historical exhibits, maps, and information about the military history of the fort and local ecology.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(503) 861-3170"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"100 Peter Iredale Rd, Hammond, OR 97121",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort+Stevens+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=129",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and beach access.",tips:["Check the tide charts before visiting the Peter Iredale shipwreck to ensure it is accessible.","Bring layers as the coastal weather can change rapidly and become quite windy.","Book campsites well in advance, especially for summer weekends, as they fill up quickly."],nearbyAttractions:["Astoria Column","Maritime Memorial Park","Lewis and Clark National Historical Park","Sunset Beach State Recreation Site"],entranceFees:"No daily entrance fee is required for day-use parking at this park.",fishingBoating:"Coffenbury Lake offers excellent fishing for rainbow trout and bass, with two boat ramps available for non-motorized or electric motor watercraft."},{id:"wallowa-lake-state-park",name:"Wallowa Lake State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c2b09d28b9eff85b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled at the southern end of the spectacular Wallowa Lake, this park offers breathtaking views of the surrounding 9,000-foot peaks.",fullDescription:"Wallowa Lake State Park is located in the heart of the 'Little Switzerland of America.' The park is situated on the terminal moraine of a glacier, providing a unique geological landscape. Visitors can enjoy the crystal-clear waters of the lake, which is fed by snowmelt from the Wallowa Mountains, and explore the rich history of the Nez Perce people who once inhabited this valley.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Boating and kayaking","Wildlife viewing","Picnicking","Fishing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"East Moraine Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A scenic trail offering panoramic views of the lake and the surrounding glacial valley."},{name:"Wallowa Lake Shoreline Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that follows the edge of the lake, perfect for bird watching and photography."}],campingInfo:"The park features 121 full-hookup sites, 88 tent sites with water nearby, and 2 yurts. Reservations are highly recommended during the summer months.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Black bears","Bald eagles","Osprey","Elk"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wallowa Lake Park Office",info:"Provides information on park activities, local history, and trail maps.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(800) 551-6949"},hours:"Open year-round, 24 hours for registered campers; day-use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"72215 Marina Ln, Joseph, OR 97846",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wallowa+Lake+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=10",bestTimeToVisit:"June through September for warm weather and full access to water activities.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities.","Book campsites well in advance as they fill up quickly in the summer.","Be prepared for rapidly changing weather conditions due to the high elevation."],nearbyAttractions:["Wallowa Lake Tramway","City of Joseph","Eagle Cap Wilderness","Chief Joseph Memorial"],entranceFees:"No daily vehicle entrance fee, but camping fees apply.",fishingBoating:"The park features a public boat ramp and excellent fishing for Kokanee salmon and rainbow trout."},{id:"beverly-beach-state-park",name:"Beverly Beach State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d1ae3757496f8232.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled between the Pacific Ocean and the lush coastal forest, Beverly Beach State Park offers a stunning stretch of sandy beach and a popular, well-wooded campground.",fullDescription:"Beverly Beach State Park is located on the central Oregon coast, just north of Newport. The park is famous for its expansive beach that stretches from Yaquina Head to Cape Foulweather, providing excellent opportunities for beachcombing and whale watching. The park is situated in a sheltered valley, protecting campers from the strong coastal winds while providing easy access to the beach via a pedestrian tunnel under Highway 101.",thingsToDo:["Beachcombing for agates and fossils","Whale watching from the shoreline","Camping in the forested campground","Hiking the nearby coastal trails","Exploring tide pools at low tide"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Spencer Creek Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle trail that winds through the lush coastal forest and along the creek, offering a peaceful escape from the beach."},{name:"Cape Foulweather Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"A scenic trail that provides elevated views of the Pacific Ocean and the rugged coastline near the park."}],campingInfo:"The park features one of the largest campgrounds in the Oregon State Parks system, offering over 200 sites including full hookups, electrical sites, and tent sites, along with several yurts.",wildlife:["Gray whales","Bald eagles","Harbor seals","Black-tailed deer","Brown pelicans"],visitorCenter:{name:"Beverly Beach Ranger Station",info:"Provides park information, camping registration, and local coastal safety tips.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(800) 551-6949"},hours:"Day-use area open from sunrise to sunset daily.",address:"198 NE 123rd St, Newport, OR 97365",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Beverly+Beach+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=173",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and whale watching opportunities.",tips:["Check tide tables before heading out to explore the tide pools.","Book campsites well in advance, especially during summer weekends.","Bring layers as coastal weather can change rapidly even in summer."],nearbyAttractions:["Yaquina Head Outstanding Natural Area","Oregon Coast Aquarium","Depoe Bay","Newport Historic Bayfront"],entranceFees:"Day-use parking is $5.00 per vehicle.",fishingBoating:"Surf fishing is popular along the beach, though no boat ramps are available within the park boundaries."},{id:"jessie-m-honeyman-memorial-state-park",name:"Jessie M. Honeyman Memorial State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/96abe3d236e2bd88.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Famous for its massive, shifting sand dunes, this park offers a unique desert-like landscape nestled against the lush Oregon coast.",fullDescription:"Jessie M. Honeyman Memorial State Park is one of the most popular parks in Oregon, renowned for its expansive sand dunes that stretch for miles along the Pacific coast. The park features two freshwater lakes, Woahink and Cleawox, which provide a stark contrast to the surrounding dunes. The area was named after Jessie M. Honeyman, a prominent figure in the Oregon roadside beautification movement. The dunes are part of the Oregon Dunes National Recreation Area, offering visitors a rare opportunity to explore a vast, shifting landscape of sand that reaches heights of up to 500 feet.",thingsToDo:["Sandboarding on the dunes","Swimming in Cleawox Lake","Hiking the dune trails","Camping in the forested sites","Picnicking by the lake"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cleawox Lake Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle trail that circles the scenic Cleawox Lake, offering beautiful views of the water and surrounding forest."},{name:"Dune Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail takes hikers up onto the ridges of the massive sand dunes, providing panoramic views of the Pacific Ocean and the inland lakes."}],campingInfo:"The park features one of the largest campgrounds in the Oregon State Parks system, offering over 350 campsites, including full hookup sites, tent sites, and group camping areas.",wildlife:["Black-tailed deer","Great blue herons","Bald eagles","Osprey","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Honeyman Park Office",info:"Provides information on park activities, dune safety, and camping reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(800) 551-6949"},hours:"Open daily, 7:00 AM to 10:00 PM",address:"84505 Highway 101, Florence, OR 97439",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Jessie+M.+Honeyman+Memorial+State+Park+OR",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.oregon.gov/index.cfm?do=park.profile&parkId=93",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather for sandboarding and swimming.",tips:["Bring plenty of water and sunscreen as the dunes provide little shade.","Book your campsite well in advance during summer months as it fills up quickly.","Rent sandboards at local shops in Florence before heading to the park."],nearbyAttractions:["Old Town Florence","Sea Lion Caves","Heceta Head Lighthouse","Oregon Dunes National Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"Day-use parking permit is $5.00 per vehicle.",fishingBoating:"Both Woahink and Cleawox lakes are popular for fishing, canoeing, and kayaking. Boat ramps are available for non-motorized and small motorized watercraft."}],utah:[{id:"goblin-valley-state-park",name:"Goblin Valley State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/790664cbf1028a5c.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Utah park known for thousands of mushroom-shaped hoodoos, called goblins, that visitors can walk among.",fullDescription:"Goblin Valley State Park in Emery County is known for thousands of hoodoos, locally called goblins, formed where an erosion-resistant layer of rock tops softer sandstone. Visitors can hike freely among them in the Valley of Goblins. It is an International Dark Sky Park with a campground and yurts and is near the Little Wild Horse Canyon trailhead in the San Rafael Swell.",thingsToDo:["Hiking among the hoodoos","Camping and yurts","Slot canyons nearby","Stargazing (International Dark Sky Park)"],hikingTrails:[],campingInfo:"The park has a 24-site campground, a group site and two yurts with central restroom and shower facilities, all bookable up to 4 months in advance.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Goblin Valley State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(435) 275-4584"},hours:"Park: 6 a.m. – 10 p.m. every day; Visitor Center: 9 a.m. – 5 p.m.",entranceFees:"Day-use: $20 per private vehicle; $10 per motorcycle, bicycle or pedestrian (valid until 10 p.m. the day after purchase)",address:"18630 Goblin Valley Rd, Green River, UT 84525",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Goblin%20Valley%20State%20Park%20utah",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/goblin-valley/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall are the most comfortable times to visit, since summer brings hot, sunny conditions with temperatures in the 90s and 100s °F.",tips:["In hot weather, hike early in the day and carry at least 32 ounces of water per person per hour, plus salty snacks or electrolytes.","Check the flash flood forecast before entering any canyon. Avoid hiking canyons or camping in washes if there is any chance of rain. A daily weather report is posted at the Visitor Center.","Drone flights require a $15/day permit issued in person at the Visitor Center. Flights are allowed only over Valley 1 from the Observation Point platform, and are not allowed on weekends or holidays from March 1 to October 31."],nearbyAttractions:["Little Wild Horse Canyon","Temple Mountain Wash Pictograph Panel","San Rafael Swell"]},{id:"dead-horse-point-state-park",name:"Dead Horse Point State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/adacaced5712255a.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Utah mesa-top park near Moab with a famous overlook of the Colorado River and Canyonlands.",fullDescription:"Dead Horse Point State Park, opened in 1959, covers 5,362 acres of high desert at 5,900 feet near Moab. Its overlooks look down on the Colorado River and out to Canyonlands National Park. It has a visitor center, two campgrounds with yurts, rim trails and 17 miles of mountain bike trails.",thingsToDo:["Overlooks","Hiking the rim trails","Mountain biking","Camping and yurts","Visitor center"],hikingTrails:[{name:"West Rim Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"Listed as a moderate 2.5-mile hike among the best hikes in Dead Horse Point State Park."},{name:"Dead Horse Point Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"200 feet",description:"A very short, easy walk to the Dead Horse Point overlook."},{name:"Intrepid",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The easiest and shortest loop of the Intrepid Trail System, which offers hiking and biking trails."}],campingInfo:"The park has campgrounds with modern amenities plus Wingate and Moenkopi yurts, and only the Moenkopi Yurts are pet friendly.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Dead Horse Point State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"435-259-2614"},hours:"Park open year-round 6:00 am – 10:00 pm; Visitor Center open daily 7 a.m. – 5 p.m. (closed Thanksgiving, Christmas and New Year's Day)",entranceFees:"$20 day-use per private vehicle (up to 8 people); $2 per person after the first 8 in high-capacity vehicles",address:"Moab, UT 84532",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Dead%20Horse%20Point%20State%20Park%20utah",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/dead-horse-point/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall generally offer milder conditions for hiking this high-desert park, and sunrise and sunset are especially memorable for the canyon views.",tips:["The park closes at 10 p.m. and day-use fees only cover access until then. Only campers, yurt guests, ranger-led activities or permitted companies can stay later for stargazing.","Trails are primitive and marked with cairns, rock lines and wood. Don't move, stack or knock down rocks.","National Park passes aren't accepted here because it's a state park."],nearbyAttractions:["Arches National Park","Canyonlands National Park"]},{id:"antelope-island-state-park",name:"Antelope Island State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8a4ae7262f4f5c5d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Largest island in the Great Salt Lake, with free-roaming bison, beaches and a historic ranch.",fullDescription:"Antelope Island State Park protects the largest of the Great Salt Lake's ten islands, 42 square miles near Salt Lake City, reached by causeway. Its wildlife includes American bison, pronghorn, bighorn sheep and millions of waterfowl. Bridger Bay on the north end has a beach, swimming area, campground and marina, and the historic Fielding Garr Ranch stands by a freshwater spring.",thingsToDo:["Bison and wildlife viewing","Hiking and biking","Swimming at Bridger Bay","Camping","Fielding Garr Ranch"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Frary Peak Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Frary Peak is one of the spots in the island's backcountry trail network where hikers can take in stunning views."},{name:"Buffalo Point Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Buffalo Point is recommended as a spot for watching the sunset."},{name:"White Rock Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"White Rock Loop is a backcountry trail that hikers can reach via the backcountry trailhead and Bone Road trail."}],campingInfo:"The park offers several primitive campsites, and its listed lodging options include RV sites, tent pads and a cottage.",fishingBoating:"The main boat ramp is currently closed due to low water levels, though kayaking is listed as a park activity.",wildlife:["American bison","mule deer","bighorn sheep","pronghorn","coyote"],visitorCenter:{name:"Antelope Island State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"801-725-9263"},hours:"Open year-round 6 a.m.–10 p.m.; closed Thanksgiving and Christmas. Visitor Center 9 a.m.–5 p.m.",entranceFees:"$15 per vehicle (up to 8 people); pedestrians $3 per person",address:"4528 West 1700 South, Syracuse, UT 84075",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Antelope%20Island%20State%20Park%20utah",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/antelope-island/",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall brings the annual Bison Roundup, and the park is open year-round with millions of birds gathering along its shores, making it rewarding for birding and wildlife viewing.",tips:["Uber and Lyft do not pick up from the park, so rent a car to avoid being stranded.","Drones are prohibited March through November and allowed only by permit December through February. Day-of permits are available at the visitor center.","Bridger Bay Beach is the best place to swim or float in Great Salt Lake and has restrooms and showers."],nearbyAttractions:["Fielding Garr Ranch","Bridger Bay Beach","Lady Finger Point"]},{id:"snow-canyon-state-park",name:"Snow Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9dfac5f55255c136.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A stunning desert landscape featuring towering sandstone cliffs, lava tubes, and vibrant red rock formations in the heart of Utah's Red Rock Country.",fullDescription:"Snow Canyon State Park is located in the Red Cliffs Desert Reserve and offers a dramatic landscape of Navajo sandstone cliffs, extinct volcanic cones, and lava tubes. Formed by the erosion of the Santa Clara volcano and the shifting sands of the ancient desert, the park provides a unique geological experience. Visitors can explore the park's diverse terrain, which ranges from white and red sandstone to black basalt lava flows, offering a stark and beautiful contrast that has attracted hikers, photographers, and geologists for decades.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Rock climbing","Photography","Biking","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Jenny's Canyon",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, flat trail leading to a narrow, slot-like canyon with beautiful red rock walls."},{name:"Lava Flow Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail winds through rugged black lava fields and leads to several accessible lava tubes."}],campingInfo:"The park features the Snow Canyon Campground with 26 sites, including options for RVs up to 40 feet and tent camping, equipped with water and electrical hookups.",wildlife:["Desert tortoise","Gila monster","Coyote","Red-tailed hawk","Kit fox"],visitorCenter:{name:"Snow Canyon Visitor Center",info:"Provides educational exhibits on the park's geology, flora, and fauna, along with ranger assistance for trail conditions.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(435) 628-2255"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"1005 Snow Canyon Dr, Ivins, UT 84738",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Snow+Canyon+State+Park+UT",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/snow-canyon/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal hiking conditions.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the desert heat can be intense even in cooler months.","Visit early in the morning to avoid peak crowds and midday heat.","Check the park website for seasonal closures of certain trails due to desert tortoise protection."],nearbyAttractions:["St. George","Red Cliffs National Conservation Area","Tuacahn Amphitheatre","Zion National Park"],entranceFees:"$10.00 per vehicle daily entrance fee for Utah residents, $15.00 for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"There are no lakes or rivers within the park suitable for boating or fishing."},{id:"coral-pink-sand-dunes-state-park",name:"Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/96de440047a14545.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Experience a stunning landscape of shifting, salmon-colored sand dunes nestled between red sandstone cliffs in southern Utah.",fullDescription:"Coral Pink Sand Dunes State Park features expansive dunes created by the erosion of Navajo Sandstone cliffs. The unique pink hue comes from iron oxides in the sand. The dunes are constantly shifting due to wind patterns, creating a dynamic environment that is popular for off-highway vehicle enthusiasts, photographers, and hikers.",thingsToDo:["Sandboarding","Off-highway vehicle riding","Photography","Stargazing","Hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, interpretive trail that offers a great introduction to the local flora and the geology of the dunes."},{name:"Dune Field Trek",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A self-guided trek across the open sand dunes providing panoramic views of the surrounding red rock cliffs."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a modern campground with 22 sites, including full hookups for RVs, as well as primitive tent sites and a group camping area.",wildlife:["Coral Pink Sand Dunes Tiger Beetle","Mule deer","Coyotes","Jackrabbits","Various lizard species"],visitorCenter:{name:"Coral Pink Sand Dunes Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, geology, and sandboarding rentals.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(435) 648-2800"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"12500 Sand Dunes Rd, Kanab, UT 84741",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Coral+Pink+Sand+Dunes+State+Park+UT",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/coral-pink/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures, as summer days can be extremely hot.",tips:["Rent a sandboard at the visitor center for a fun way to navigate the dunes.","Visit during sunrise or sunset when the light makes the sand appear most vibrant.","Bring plenty of water and sun protection, as there is very little shade in the dune field."],nearbyAttractions:["Kanab","Zion National Park","Best Friends Animal Sanctuary","Pipe Spring National Monument"],entranceFees:"$10.00 daily vehicle entrance fee.",fishingBoating:"There are no lakes or rivers within the park, so fishing and boating are not available."},{id:"wasatch-mountain-state-park",name:"Wasatch Mountain State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e660c79d2fb04b38.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the scenic Heber Valley, Wasatch Mountain State Park offers year-round outdoor recreation ranging from championship golf to rugged mountain trails.",fullDescription:"Established in 1961, Wasatch Mountain State Park is one of Utah's most popular destinations, encompassing over 21,000 acres of diverse terrain. The park features a rich history of early pioneer settlement and provides a habitat for a wide variety of wildlife. Visitors can explore the mountainous landscape, which transitions from lush valleys to high-alpine ridges, offering spectacular views of the Wasatch Range.",thingsToDo:["Golfing at the 36-hole Wasatch Mountain Golf Course","Mountain biking on extensive trail networks","Horseback riding on designated equestrian paths","Snowmobiling and cross-country skiing in winter","Wildlife viewing and photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pine Creek Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle, family-friendly loop that winds through wooded areas and offers great opportunities for bird watching."},{name:"Dutch Hollow Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.2 miles",description:"A popular trail for hikers and mountain bikers that climbs through scenic canyons with panoramic views of the Heber Valley."}],campingInfo:"The park offers the Pine Creek Campground with 128 sites, including full hookups for RVs, tent sites, and group camping areas. Reservations are highly recommended during peak summer months.",wildlife:["Elk","Moose","Wild Turkey","Mule Deer","Golden Eagles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wasatch Mountain State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and educational exhibits about local flora and fauna.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(435) 654-1791"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"1281 Warm Springs Rd, Midway, UT 84049",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wasatch+Mountain+State+Park+Midway+UT",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/wasatch-mountain/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for hiking and golf; winter for snow sports.",tips:["Book your golf tee times well in advance as the courses are very popular.","Bring binoculars for wildlife viewing, especially during dawn and dusk when elk are most active.","Check the park website for seasonal trail closures due to mud or snow conditions."],nearbyAttractions:["Homestead Crater","Deer Creek Reservoir","Park City Mountain Resort","Heber Valley Historic Railroad"],entranceFees:"$15.00 daily vehicle entrance fee for up to 8 people.",fishingBoating:"While the park itself is not centered on water, it is located just minutes from Deer Creek Reservoir, which offers excellent boating, fishing, and water sports."},{id:"jordanelle-state-park",name:"Jordanelle State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8b45466e2cfbdbff.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A premier water recreation destination nestled in the mountains near Heber City, offering boating, fishing, and camping.",fullDescription:"Jordanelle State Park is located on the shores of the Jordanelle Reservoir, a massive water project completed in the 1990s. The park is divided into three main areas: Hailstone, Rock Cliff, and Ross Creek. It features diverse geological formations and provides a critical habitat for various wildlife while serving as a hub for outdoor enthusiasts seeking relief from the summer heat or winter snow sports.",thingsToDo:["Boating and jet skiing","Fishing for bass and trout","Camping in developed sites","Hiking and mountain biking","Swimming at designated beaches"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Perimeter Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"14.0 miles",description:"A long, scenic trail that circles much of the reservoir, offering panoramic views of the water and surrounding Wasatch Mountains."},{name:"Rock Cliff Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path through the riparian area of the Provo River, perfect for bird watching and spotting local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park offers multiple campgrounds including Hailstone and Rock Cliff, featuring full hookups, tent sites, and several cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Mule deer","Red-tailed hawks","Coyotes","Great blue herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Hailstone Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park regulations, fishing licenses, and educational exhibits about the reservoir's history.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(435) 649-9540"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"515 State Road 319, Heber City, UT 84032",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Jordanelle+State+Park+UT",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/jordanelle/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for water activities.",tips:["Make camping reservations well in advance during summer months as sites fill up quickly.","Check the water temperature and weather conditions before heading out on the reservoir.","Visit the Rock Cliff area for a quieter, more nature-focused experience compared to the busy Hailstone area."],nearbyAttractions:["Park City Mountain Resort","Deer Valley Resort","Heber Valley Railroad","Wasatch Mountain State Park"],entranceFees:"$20.00 daily vehicle entrance fee for up to 8 people.",fishingBoating:"The reservoir is a popular spot for smallmouth bass, largemouth bass, and rainbow trout. Multiple boat ramps are available at the Hailstone area."},{id:"kodachrome-basin-state-park",name:"Kodachrome Basin State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8d4b9ce31ba8f548.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Kodachrome Basin State Park is a geological wonderland known for its towering sandstone chimneys and vibrant, multi-colored rock formations.",fullDescription:"Named by the National Geographic Society for its vivid colors and photogenic landscapes, Kodachrome Basin features 67 monolithic stone spires that rise from the desert floor. The park's unique geology is attributed to ancient geysers that filled with sediment and hardened, leaving behind these striking sedimentary pipes as the surrounding softer rock eroded away. The area offers a serene escape with dramatic vistas, high-desert flora, and a rich history of exploration in the Grand Staircase-Escalante region.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and photography","Mountain biking","Stargazing","Camping","Horseback riding"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Panorama Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"6.0 miles",description:"A scenic loop that offers expansive views of the basin and passes by several unique rock formations like Ballerina Spire."},{name:"Angels Palace Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"This trail climbs to a ridge providing a bird's-eye view of the park's iconic sandstone chimneys and surrounding cliffs."}],campingInfo:"The park offers two campgrounds, Basin and Arch, featuring sites with full hookups, tent pads, fire pits, and modern restrooms with showers.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Coyotes","Golden eagles","Desert cottontails","Common ravens"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kodachrome Basin Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park geology, trail maps, souvenirs, and assistance from park rangers.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(435) 679-8562"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping; Visitor Center hours vary by season.",address:"22000 Kodachrome Rd, Cannonville, UT 84718",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kodachrome+Basin+State+Park+UT",officialWebsite:"https://stateparks.utah.gov/parks/kodachrome-basin/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal hiking conditions.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the high desert climate is very dry.","Visit during the early morning or late afternoon for the best light to photograph the vibrant rock colors.","The park is a certified International Dark Sky Park, so stay after sunset for incredible stargazing."],nearbyAttractions:["Bryce Canyon National Park","Grand Staircase-Escalante National Monument","Cannonville","Escalante"],entranceFees:"$10.00 daily vehicle entrance fee.",fishingBoating:"There are no lakes or rivers within the park suitable for fishing or boating."}],washington:[{id:"deception-pass-state-park",name:"Deception Pass State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/912745cd7e7188de.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Washington park around Deception Pass, the narrow strait between Whidbey and Fidalgo islands under a historic bridge.",fullDescription:"Deception Pass State Park covers 3,854 acres around Deception Pass, the strait between Whidbey and Fidalgo islands, crossed by the Deception Pass Bridge. With over two million visitors a year it is one of Washington's most visited state parks. It has campgrounds, beaches, tidepools, part of the Pacific Northwest Trail, boat launches at Cornet Bay and an interpretive center at Bowman Bay.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Beaches and tidepools","Boating","Fishing","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Sand Dunes Interpretive Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A paved ADA loop with interpretive panels about the seaside vegetation unique to the area."},{name:"Goose Rock",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"Not listed",description:"A wide, well-built trail climbs above North Beach toward the bridge and then crosses under it."},{name:"Lighthouse Point Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A loop at Bowman Bay; storms washed away part of the trail between it and Lottie Point Loop, and you may walk around that section on the beach."}],campingInfo:"The park offers camping, including winter camping and accessible campsites.",fishingBoating:"Crabbing and both fresh and saltwater fishing are popular and need a recreational license, but Pass Lake is closed because of toxic algae and the Bowman Bay boat launch is closed to trailered vessels.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Seals","Orcas"],visitorCenter:{name:"Deception Pass State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(360) 675-3767"},hours:"Summer: 6:30 a.m. - Dusk; Winter: 8:00 a.m. - Dusk",entranceFees:"Discover Pass required: $10 one-day or $45 annual",address:"41229 State Rte 20, Oak Harbor, WA 98277",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Deception%20Pass%20State%20Park%20washington",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/deception-pass-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall gives the longest hours (6:30 a.m. to dusk), and the park's interpretive center is open April to September.",tips:["Pass Lake is closed to fishing, people and pets due to toxic algae until further notice.","From November 2 to 13, 2026, the lower Bowman Bay parking lot is closed. Use the Upper Bowman Bay lot, where parking may be limited.","Vehicles over 25 feet, including trailers, motorhomes and buses, can't enter the West Beach parking area. Buses can drop off at West Beach and park at the East Cranberry Lake lot."],nearbyAttractions:["Naval Air Museum","Joseph Whidbey State Park","Fort Nugent Park"]},{id:"palouse-falls-state-park",name:"Palouse Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c9f8f32746238df9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Small Washington park at Palouse Falls, a 200-foot waterfall plunging into a 377-foot-deep canyon.",fullDescription:"Palouse Falls State Park covers 94 acres on the Palouse River about four miles above its confluence with the Snake River in southeast Washington. The falls drop about 200 feet into a canyon 377 feet deep, with a smaller upper fall upstream. A viewpoint is a short walk from the parking area, campground and picnic area.",thingsToDo:["Falls viewpoint","Picnicking","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Palouse Falls Overlook",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.3 miles",description:"A short overlook route with about 26 feet of elevation gain that takes roughly 0.5 to 1 hour to complete."}],campingInfo:"No camping in the park.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Rattlesnakes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Palouse Falls State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(509) 646-3229"},hours:"Summer: 6:30 a.m. to dusk; Winter: 8 a.m. to dusk",entranceFees:"Discover Pass required: $10 one-day or $45 annual",address:"Palouse Falls Road, LaCrosse, WA 99143",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Palouse%20Falls%20State%20Park%20washington",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/palouse-falls-state-park-heritage-site",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring is a good time to visit, when wildflowers bloom in the canyon, but check the weather first because extreme conditions are possible, and come on a weekday to avoid long parking waits.",tips:["Parking is extremely limited, so expect long waits on weekends and holidays. Trailers and RVs are not allowed.","Most of the park has no cell service and it is far from amenities and first responders, so bring plenty of water and plan ahead.","All trails into the canyon and to the base of the falls are permanently closed. Stay within the designated viewing areas and trails."],nearbyAttractions:["Lyons Ferry State Park"]},{id:"cape-disappointment-state-park",name:"Cape Disappointment State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6f54b567db0a71d6.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Washington park where the Columbia River meets the Pacific, with two lighthouses and the Lewis and Clark center.",fullDescription:"Cape Disappointment State Park, formerly Fort Canby State Park, covers 2,023 acres southwest of Ilwaco at the mouth of the Columbia River. Its sites include Fort Canby, North Head and Cape Disappointment lighthouses, and the Lewis and Clark Interpretive Center on a cliff above the river's meeting with the ocean. It offers camping, yurts and historic vacation homes.",thingsToDo:["Lewis and Clark Interpretive Center","Lighthouses","Hiking","Beaches","Boating","Camping and yurts"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cape Disappointment Lighthouse Trail",difficulty:"Easy/Moderate",length:"2.4 miles",description:"Per WTA, the round trip from the trailhead to the lighthouse is 2.4 miles with about 500 feet of elevation gain."},{name:"Northhead Lighthouse Loop Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"One of the popular fall hiking trails in Cape Disappointment State Park."},{name:"North Head Discovery Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"One of the popular fall hiking trails in Cape Disappointment State Park."}],campingInfo:"The campground has standard, partial hookup, full hookup, and five primitive hiker/biker campsites, plus yurts and historic vacation homes, and accommodates RVs and combinations of 45 feet or less.",fishingBoating:"The park has one boat ramp and 135 feet of dock on Baker Bay in the Columbia River, but the boat launch is closed Oct 1-31, 2026 for paving.",wildlife:["Deer","Elk","Raccoons","Rabbits","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cape Disappointment State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(360) 642-3078"},hours:"6:30 a.m. - Dusk year round",entranceFees:"Discover Pass required: $10 one-day or $45 annual; watercraft launch $7/day",address:"244 Robert Gray Drive, Ilwaco, WA 98624",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cape%20Disappointment%20State%20Park%20washington",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/cape-disappointment-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"May through September is best, when the North Head Lighthouse is open to visitors (through Sept. 30), though the park is open year round and offers winter camping.",tips:["The parking lots at North Head, the Lewis & Clark Interpretive Center and Beards Hollow will not accommodate RVs.","Swimming in the ocean on the Long Beach Peninsula is not advised because of beach hazards.","The Cape D Store is closed in 2026 and into 2027 for construction of a new contact station, so bring your own supplies such as firewood, ice and groceries."],nearbyAttractions:[]},{id:"mount-spokane-state-park",name:"Mount Spokane State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6af2ad3d096267b0.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Mount Spokane State Park is a massive 13,919-acre park featuring stunning mountain vistas, extensive trail networks, and year-round recreational opportunities.",fullDescription:"Located in the Selkirk Mountains, Mount Spokane State Park is one of the largest state parks in Washington. It offers a diverse landscape of sub-alpine meadows, old-growth forests, and dramatic granite peaks. Historically significant for its Civilian Conservation Corps structures, the park provides a rugged escape for hikers, mountain bikers, and winter sports enthusiasts alike. The summit offers panoramic views that can stretch as far as the Canadian border on clear days.",thingsToDo:["Downhill skiing and snowboarding","Cross-country skiing and snowshoeing","Mountain biking","Horseback riding","Wildlife photography","Picnicking at the Vista House"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Summit Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A steady climb that leads visitors to the historic Vista House at the summit, offering breathtaking 360-degree views."},{name:"Burping Brook Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle, scenic path that winds through lush forest areas and crosses several small, tranquil streams."}],campingInfo:"The park offers primitive camping at the Lower Kit Carson Loop Road area, which includes sites suitable for tents and small RVs, though no hookups are provided.",wildlife:["Moose","Black bear","White-tailed deer","Pileated woodpecker","Red squirrel"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mount Spokane Park Office",info:"Provides maps, trail condition updates, and information on park history and seasonal activities.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(509) 238-4258"},hours:"6:30 AM to dusk daily",address:"26107 N. Mt. Spokane Park Dr., Mead, WA 99021",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mount+Spokane+State+Park+WA",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/mount-spokane-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"July through September for hiking; December through March for winter sports.",tips:["Check the official website for current road conditions before driving up, as mountain weather changes rapidly.","Bring a Discover Pass, as it is required for vehicle access to all Washington state parks.","Visit the historic Vista House during summer weekends to learn about the park's CCC history."],nearbyAttractions:["City of Spokane","Green Bluff Growers","Riverside State Park","Lake Coeur d'Alene"],entranceFees:"A Washington State Discover Pass is required for vehicle access ($10 daily or $30 annually).",fishingBoating:"Fishing is available in the park's smaller ponds and streams, though the park is primarily known for its mountain terrain rather than large-scale boating."},{id:"fort-worden-state-park",name:"Fort Worden State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d7cddd91f721c5e4.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A historic former military base turned state park, Fort Worden offers stunning views of the Olympic Mountains and the Strait of Juan de Fuca.",fullDescription:"Fort Worden State Park is a 432-acre park with over two miles of saltwater shoreline. Originally built as a coastal defense fort in the early 1900s, it features well-preserved concrete bunkers and Victorian-era officer homes. The park is rich in history and natural beauty, providing visitors with a unique blend of military architecture and coastal wilderness.",thingsToDo:["Explore historic bunkers and tunnels","Beachcombing along the shoreline","Bird watching","Touring the Coast Artillery Museum","Paragliding from the bluffs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Point Wilson Lighthouse Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A flat, scenic walk along the beach leading to the iconic Point Wilson Lighthouse."},{name:"Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail winds along the high bluffs, offering panoramic views of the water and the Olympic Peninsula."}],campingInfo:"The park features 80 tent sites and 50 full-hookup RV sites, along with several historic houses available for vacation rentals.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Harbor seals","Black-tailed deer","Great blue herons","Sea otters"],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Worden State Park Office",info:"Provides information on park history, maps, and event scheduling for the various arts and educational programs held on-site.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(360) 344-4412"},hours:"6:30 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"200 Battery Way, Port Townsend, WA 98368",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort+Worden+State+Park+WA",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/fort-worden-historical-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and outdoor activities.",tips:["Bring a flashlight to safely explore the dark, underground bunkers.","Check the tide charts before heading out for beachcombing.","Book campsites well in advance as they fill up quickly during summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Port Townsend Historic District","Marine Science Center","Olympic National Park","Whidbey Island Ferry"],entranceFees:"A Discover Pass is required for vehicle access, costing $10 for a day pass or $30 for an annual pass.",fishingBoating:"The park offers excellent saltwater fishing opportunities from the beach and a boat launch nearby in Port Townsend."},{id:"moran-state-park",name:"Moran State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9ab961f3b01c4a56.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on Orcas Island, Moran State Park is a crown jewel of the San Juan Islands, featuring lush forests, freshwater lakes, and the panoramic summit of Mount Constitution.",fullDescription:"Moran State Park encompasses over 5,000 acres of diverse terrain on Orcas Island. Established in 1921 through the donation of land by Robert Moran, the park features a complex network of trails, five freshwater lakes, and the historic stone observation tower atop Mount Constitution, which offers breathtaking 360-degree views of the San Juan archipelago and beyond.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and mountain biking","Swimming and paddleboarding in Cascade Lake","Bird watching","Picnicking","Visiting the Mount Constitution observation tower"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mount Constitution Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"6.7 miles",description:"A steady climb through old-growth forest leading to the highest point in the San Juan Islands."},{name:"Cascade Falls Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 mile",description:"A short, scenic walk that leads to a beautiful waterfall tucked away in the forest."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 151 campsites across five campgrounds, including sites for tents and RVs, as well as several environmental campsites and group camps.",wildlife:["Black-tailed deer","Bald eagles","Pileated woodpeckers","River otters","Red foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Moran State Park Ranger Station",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and assistance with camping permits.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(360) 376-2326"},hours:"6:30 AM to 10:00 PM daily",address:"3572 Olga Rd, Eastsound, WA 98245",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Moran+State+Park+WA",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/moran-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and trail conditions.",tips:["Book campsites well in advance as they fill up quickly during summer months.","Bring binoculars for spotting wildlife and distant mountain ranges from the summit.","Check the ferry schedule for Orcas Island before planning your trip."],nearbyAttractions:["Eastsound Village","Turtleback Mountain Preserve","Obstruction Pass State Park","Orcas Island Historical Museum"],entranceFees:"A Discover Pass is required for vehicle access ($10 daily or $30 annually).",fishingBoating:"Cascade Lake offers excellent trout fishing and a boat launch for non-motorized watercraft."},{id:"sun-lakes-dry-falls-state-park",name:"Sun Lakes-Dry Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2e05814e5c8741d0.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A stunning geological wonder featuring the massive Dry Falls cataract and a chain of desert lakes perfect for outdoor recreation.",fullDescription:"Sun Lakes-Dry Falls State Park is a 4,027-acre camping park located in the Channeled Scablands of eastern Washington. The park is famous for Dry Falls, a 3.5-mile-wide, 400-foot-high precipice that was once the site of a massive waterfall during the last Ice Age. The area offers a unique desert landscape with basalt cliffs, glacial flood remnants, and a series of interconnected lakes created by the catastrophic Missoula Floods.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running","Swimming in Deep Lake","Fishing for rainbow trout","Golfing at the Sun Lakes Park Resort course","Bird watching and wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Dry Falls Interpretive Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, paved path leading to an overlook with spectacular views of the ancient waterfall basin."},{name:"Umatilla Rock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A loop trail that circles the massive Umatilla Rock formation, offering close-up views of basalt columns and flood-scoured terrain."}],campingInfo:"The park offers extensive camping facilities including 173 standard sites, 28 full-hookup sites, and several cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Coyotes","California quail","Great blue herons","Red-tailed hawks"],visitorCenter:{name:"Dry Falls Visitor Center",info:"Features exhibits on the Ice Age floods, local geology, and the history of the Grand Coulee.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(509) 632-5214"},hours:"6:30 AM to dusk daily",address:"34875 Park Lake Rd NE, Coulee City, WA 99115",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sun+Lakes-Dry+Falls+State+Park+WA",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/sun-lakes-dry-falls-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and blooming desert wildflowers.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the desert climate can be very dry and hot in the summer.","Visit the Dry Falls Visitor Center first to understand the geological significance of the area.","Check for burn bans before planning a campfire during the summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Grand Coulee Dam","Steamboat Rock State Park","Coulee City","Lake Lenore Caves"],entranceFees:"A Washington State Discover Pass is required for vehicle access ($10 daily or $30 annually).",fishingBoating:"The park provides boat ramps on Park Lake and Deep Lake, with excellent opportunities for trout fishing."},{id:"wallace-falls-state-park",name:"Wallace Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/a3fe2a93c5dbf942.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A stunning park featuring a series of nine waterfalls along the Wallace River, nestled in the lush forests of the Cascade Mountains.",fullDescription:"Wallace Falls State Park is a 1,380-acre park located near Gold Bar, Washington. It is renowned for its spectacular waterfall system, which drops over 600 feet in total. The park offers a dense, old-growth forest environment with moss-covered trees and ferns, providing a quintessential Pacific Northwest hiking experience. The geology of the area is defined by the steep terrain of the Wallace River valley, carved by glacial activity and river erosion over millennia.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain Biking","Wildlife Viewing","Picnicking","Photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Wallace Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5.6 miles roundtrip",description:"This popular trail leads hikers past the lower, middle, and upper falls, offering breathtaking views of the cascading water."},{name:"Greg Ball Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles roundtrip",description:"A scenic, forested trail that connects to the main falls trail, named in honor of a local trail builder."}],campingInfo:"The park offers five cabins available for reservation, which are heated and include electricity. There are no tent or RV campsites available within the park boundaries.",wildlife:["Black bears","Black-tailed deer","Cougars","Pileated woodpeckers","Douglas squirrels"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wallace Falls Ranger Station",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and safety guidance for visitors.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(360) 793-0420"},hours:"8:00 AM to dusk daily",address:"14503 Wallace Lake Rd, Gold Bar, WA 98251",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wallace+Falls+State+Park+WA",officialWebsite:"https://parks.wa.gov/find-parks/state-parks/wallace-falls-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and early summer for peak waterfall flow, or autumn for vibrant foliage.",tips:["Arrive early on weekends as the parking lot fills up quickly.","Bring a rain jacket even in summer, as the mist from the falls can be heavy.","Ensure you have a Discover Pass displayed in your vehicle to avoid fines."],nearbyAttractions:["City of Gold Bar","Stevens Pass","Skykomish River","Index Town Wall"],entranceFees:"A Washington State Discover Pass is required for vehicle access ($10 daily or $30 annual).",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Wallace River in accordance with state regulations, though there are no boat ramps within the park."}],wyoming:[{id:"glendo-state-park",name:"Glendo State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/ae51236f8b394804.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Wyoming park around Glendo Reservoir on the North Platte River, with 45 miles of bike trails and 500+ campsites.",fullDescription:"Glendo State Park surrounds Glendo Reservoir on the North Platte River near the town of Glendo, about 20 miles southeast of Douglas. It has 45 miles of mountain bike trails, more than 500 campsites, and fishing and water sports on a reservoir that has produced several state-record fish.",thingsToDo:["Fishing","Boating and water sports","Mountain biking","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Sunset Point",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.9 miles",description:"An out-and-back hike high above Glendo Reservoir to Sunset Point."}],campingInfo:"The park has over 500 campsites spread across 19 campgrounds.",fishingBoating:"Glendo Reservoir has one of the best walleye fisheries in Wyoming, and six boat ramps provide seasonal water access, though water levels vary significantly through the year.",wildlife:["mule deer","whitetail deer","pronghorn antelope","coyotes","wild turkeys"],visitorCenter:{name:"Glendo State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(307) 735-4433"},hours:"Open year-round. Day-use permits are required 6AM–10PM and camping permits 10PM–6AM. Fee booths are staffed daily from Memorial Day to Labor Day.",entranceFees:"2026: $7 per vehicle per day for Wyoming residents. The nonresident fee is not stated in the sources.",address:"397 Glendo Park Rd, Glendo, WY 82213",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Glendo%20State%20Park%20wyoming",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/glendo",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall (Memorial Day to Labor Day) is best for boating and camping when water access is seasonal, though trails and recreation are open year-round.",tips:["Water levels vary significantly through the year, so check reservoir capacity and boat-ramp status before your trip.","Outside Memorial Day–Labor Day, fee booths are unstaffed, so pay by cash, check or credit card at the canisters and kiosks.","Park headquarters is about 2 miles past the main fee booth on Glendo Park Road, on the left just past the Marina."],nearbyAttractions:["Register Cliff","Glendo Museum"]},{id:"hot-springs-state-park",name:"Hot Springs State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7dc8956fee778790.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Thermopolis park known for 135°F mineral hot springs and free soaking at the State Bath House.",fullDescription:"Hot Springs State Park in Thermopolis is known for hot springs that flow at a constant 135°F. The State Bath House offers free bathing in water cooled to 104°F. The park also has travertine terraces formed by the mineral springs, a suspension footbridge over the Bighorn River, flower gardens and a small state bison herd.",thingsToDo:["Free soaking at the State Bath House","Travertine terraces","Suspension footbridge","Bison viewing","Boat docks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"T Hill and Rim Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"AllTrails lists it among the trails in Hot Springs State Park with scenic mountain peak views."},{name:"Monument Hill Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"AllTrails lists it among the trails in Hot Springs State Park with scenic mountain peak views."},{name:"Spirit Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"AllTrails lists it among the trails in Hot Springs State Park with scenic mountain peak views."}],campingInfo:"No camping in the park.",fishingBoating:"The park offers fishing and a boat ramp along the Big Horn River.",wildlife:["Bison"],visitorCenter:{name:"Hot Springs State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"307-864-2176"},hours:"Grounds 6 a.m.–10 p.m., open year-round. Bath House summer hours are Mon–Sat 8 a.m.–7 p.m. and Sun noon–7 p.m. Winter hours are Mon–Sat 8 a.m.–5:30 p.m. and Sun noon–5:30 p.m. The Bath House is closed Thanksgiving and Christmas Day.",entranceFees:"Free; no charge to enter, and the State Bath House is a free public facility.",address:"51 US Highway 20 North, Thermopolis, WY 82443",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hot%20Springs%20State%20Park%20wyoming",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/hot-springs",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer brings colorful flower gardens and longer Bath House hours, while late fall and winter offer close-up views of the bison herd during daily feedings.",tips:["In late fall and winter, the park's bison are usually fed a daily supplement around 8:30 a.m., which is a good chance to see them up close. The park asks that bison be viewed only from a safe position.","The three large group picnic shelters are reservable and heavily used. Contact park headquarters early to reserve one, and ask whether your group needs a park liquor permit.","The free State Bath House keeps its water at 104°F for therapeutic bathing. It is closed on Thanksgiving and Christmas Day."],nearbyAttractions:["Wyoming Dinosaur Center","Legend Rock State Petroglyph Site","Wind River Canyon"]},{id:"curt-gowdy-state-park",name:"Curt Gowdy State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/a3fe1b054469b01f.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Wyoming park between Cheyenne and Laramie, known for its trail system, fishing reservoirs and Hidden Falls.",fullDescription:"Curt Gowdy State Park covers 3,395 acres on Happy Jack Road, halfway between Cheyenne and Laramie. Opened in 1971 as Granite State Park, it was renamed in 1972 for Wyoming-born sportscaster Curt Gowdy. It is known for over 35 miles of trails for biking, hiking and horseback riding, its fishing reservoirs, the Hidden Falls area and the historic Hynds Lodge.",thingsToDo:["Mountain biking","Hiking","Horseback riding","Fishing","Boating","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mo' Rocka Trail",difficulty:"Advanced",length:"1.67 miles",description:"Visit Cheyenne rates this trail advanced and says it takes around 45 minutes."},{name:"Hidden Falls",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The park calls this one of its busiest trails, and the Hidden Falls Trailhead has improvements including a new restroom, an accessible picnic shelter and formalized parking."},{name:"Stone Temple Circuit",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"AllTrails lists it as a popular fall hiking trail in Curt Gowdy State Park."}],campingInfo:"All campsites are by reservation only, and the Aspen Grove campground is next to a free public horse corral for visitors camping with horses.",fishingBoating:"Granite Reservoir offers rainbow trout and kokanee salmon fishing plus water sports, while Crystal Reservoir has shoreline fishing and boating with a 15 horsepower limit.",wildlife:["Rainbow trout","Kokanee salmon","Brown trout"],visitorCenter:{name:"Curt Gowdy State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(307) 632-7946"},hours:"Park open 365 days, 24 hrs (with camping permit); visitor center hours vary seasonally",entranceFees:"Wyoming residents $7 per day per vehicle (2026 official schedule); non-residents $12 per vehicle per cheyenne.org",address:"1264 Granite Springs Rd, Cheyenne, WY 82009",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Curt%20Gowdy%20State%20Park%20wyoming",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/curt-gowdy",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall is generally best for hiking, biking and fishing at this roughly 7,000-foot park, though the official page lists year-round activities, including winter sports.",tips:["Reserve ahead, because all campsites are by reservation only.","North Crow Reservoir is an unimproved, day-use-only fishing area with no picnic tables, water supply or comfort stations, so bring what you need.","Expect crowds at Hidden Falls, one of the park's busiest trails, which now has formalized trailhead parking."],nearbyAttractions:["Wyoming State Museum","Wyoming Territorial Prison"]},{id:"keyhole-state-park",name:"Keyhole State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/f2e909ed78927c37.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the western edge of the Black Hills, Keyhole State Park offers stunning reservoir views and exceptional outdoor recreation opportunities.",fullDescription:"Keyhole State Park is situated along the shores of the Keyhole Reservoir, which was created by the construction of the Keyhole Dam on the Belle Fourche River. The park is renowned for its diverse landscape, ranging from open ponderosa pine forests to rugged sandstone cliffs. It serves as a premier destination for water sports, fishing, and wildlife viewing, providing a peaceful retreat for visitors exploring the northeastern corner of Wyoming.",thingsToDo:["Boating and water skiing","Fishing for walleye and smallmouth bass","Bird watching","Camping","Hiking and nature photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Wind Creek Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that winds through the pine trees and offers beautiful views of the reservoir shoreline."},{name:"Arch Rock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail leads hikers through unique sandstone formations and provides elevated vantage points over the water."}],campingInfo:"The park features multiple campgrounds including Homestead, Antelope, and Tatanka, offering a mix of tent sites and RV sites with electrical hookups.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Pronghorn antelope","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Keyhole State Park Headquarters",info:"Provides park information, maps, and assistance with camping permits and regulations.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(307) 756-3596"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping; day use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"272 State Park Rd, Moorcroft, WY 82721",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Keyhole+State+Park+Wyoming",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/keyhole",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water activities and pleasant weather.",tips:["Bring plenty of sunscreen and water as the reservoir area can be very exposed to the sun.","Check the wind forecast before heading out on the water, as the reservoir can get choppy.","Make camping reservations well in advance during the summer months as sites fill up quickly."],nearbyAttractions:["Devils Tower National Monument","Moorcroft","Gillette","Black Hills National Forest"],entranceFees:"Daily vehicle entrance fee is $6.00 for Wyoming residents and $11.00 for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"The reservoir is a popular spot for fishing walleye, northern pike, and smallmouth bass; multiple boat ramps are available for public use."},{id:"boysen-state-park",name:"Boysen State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e42e4455b00e38c6.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located at the mouth of the spectacular Wind River Canyon, Boysen State Park offers expansive water recreation and stunning desert landscapes.",fullDescription:"Boysen State Park surrounds the massive Boysen Reservoir, which was created by the construction of the Boysen Dam on the Wind River. The park features diverse terrain ranging from high desert plains to dramatic red rock canyons, providing a unique geological backdrop for outdoor enthusiasts. It is a premier destination for water sports, fishing, and wildlife viewing in central Wyoming.",thingsToDo:["Boating and water skiing","Fishing for walleye and trout","Camping in scenic lakeside sites","Wildlife photography","Picnicking at designated day-use areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Wind River Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail offering panoramic views of the Wind River and the surrounding canyon walls."},{name:"Tough Creek Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A gentle path winding through the desert landscape, perfect for spotting local flora and fauna."}],campingInfo:"The park features 11 developed campgrounds with over 150 campsites, many of which offer lake views and basic amenities like fire rings and picnic tables.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Bald eagles","Pronghorn antelope","Bighorn sheep","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Boysen State Park Office",info:"Provides park information, maps, and assistance with camping permits and regulations.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(307) 876-2796"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping and recreation",address:"115 Boysen Drive, Shoshoni, WY 82649",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Boysen+State+Park+WY",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/boysen",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water temperatures and weather.",tips:["Bring plenty of sunscreen and water as the high desert sun can be intense.","Check the wind forecast before heading out on the water, as it can pick up quickly.","Keep a camera ready for the bighorn sheep often seen near the canyon entrance."],nearbyAttractions:["Wind River Canyon","Thermopolis Hot Springs","Shoshoni","Riverton"],entranceFees:"Daily vehicle fee is $7.00 for Wyoming residents and $12.00 for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"The reservoir is famous for its walleye, perch, and trout fishing, with multiple boat ramps available for public use."},{id:"buffalo-bill-state-park",name:"Buffalo Bill State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/74e9db4322a7d92d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located just outside the east entrance of Yellowstone National Park, this scenic park offers stunning views of the Absaroka Mountains and the expansive Buffalo Bill Reservoir.",fullDescription:"Buffalo Bill State Park is situated in the scenic Shoshone River valley, featuring the massive Buffalo Bill Reservoir created by the historic Buffalo Bill Dam. The park provides a gateway to the high country of Wyoming, offering visitors a unique blend of water-based recreation and rugged mountain landscapes. The area is rich in history, named after the legendary William 'Buffalo Bill' Cody, who founded the nearby town of Cody. The geology is dominated by volcanic formations and the dramatic canyon walls of the Shoshone River.",thingsToDo:["Boating and sailing on the reservoir","Fishing for trout","Wildlife viewing","Picnicking with mountain views","Camping in lakeside sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Buffalo Bill Reservoir Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A relatively flat trail that follows the shoreline of the reservoir, offering excellent opportunities for bird watching and photography."},{name:"Cedar Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"This trail climbs into the foothills, providing panoramic views of the reservoir and the surrounding Absaroka Range."}],campingInfo:"The park features two main campgrounds, North Fork and Lakeview, offering a total of 97 campsites. Many sites provide electrical hookups, and all are equipped with picnic tables and fire rings.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Bighorn sheep","Bald eagles","Osprey","Elk"],visitorCenter:{name:"Buffalo Bill State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, local wildlife, and recreational opportunities, along with educational exhibits about the dam.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(307) 587-9227"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping; day use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"4192 North Fork Hwy, Cody, WY 82414",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Buffalo+Bill+State+Park+Cody+WY",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/buffalo-bill",bestTimeToVisit:"June through September for optimal weather and water activities.",tips:["Be prepared for high winds that can develop quickly on the reservoir.","Bring binoculars for spotting bighorn sheep on the nearby canyon walls.","Always store food properly to avoid attracting local wildlife."],nearbyAttractions:["Buffalo Bill Center of the West","Old Trail Town","Yellowstone National Park East Entrance","Cody Night Rodeo"],entranceFees:"Daily vehicle entrance fee is $7.00 for Wyoming residents and $13.00 for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"The reservoir is a premier destination for trout fishing, including brown, rainbow, and cutthroat trout. Two boat ramps are available for public use."},{id:"sinks-canyon-state-park",name:"Sinks Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3c7de3a5a2817854.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in the Wind River Mountains, this park is famous for the mysterious 'Sinks' where the Popo Agie River disappears into a limestone cavern.",fullDescription:"Sinks Canyon State Park offers a unique geological experience where the Popo Agie River flows into a large cavern at the base of a 600-foot limestone cliff, only to reappear a quarter-mile downstream in a crystal-clear pool teeming with trout. The canyon provides a dramatic landscape of sheer rock walls, lush riparian zones, and diverse wildlife habitats, serving as a gateway to the Shoshone National Forest.",thingsToDo:["Trout feeding at the Rise","Rock climbing on limestone cliffs","Wildlife viewing","Hiking and backpacking","Picnicking by the river"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A scenic trail that leads hikers to the beautiful Popo Agie Falls, offering great views of the canyon floor."},{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"An accessible loop trail perfect for families, featuring interpretive signs about the local flora and geology."}],campingInfo:"The park features several campgrounds including Popo Agie, Sawmill, and Boulder Flat, offering tent and RV sites with fire rings and picnic tables.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Black bears","Moose","Golden eagles","Rainbow trout"],visitorCenter:{name:"Sinks Canyon Visitor Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the geology of the canyon, local history, and wildlife, plus a gift shop.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(307) 332-6333"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"3079 Sinks Canyon Rd, Lander, WY 82520",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sinks+Canyon+State+Park+WY",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/sinks-canyon",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn",tips:["Bring quarters to purchase fish food at the visitor center to feed the trout at the Rise.","Be bear aware and store all food properly in bear-resistant containers.","Visit in the early morning or late evening for the best chances to spot moose and deer."],nearbyAttractions:["Lander, Wyoming","Red Canyon","Shoshone National Forest","Wind River Indian Reservation"],entranceFees:"Free entrance for day use; camping fees apply.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Popo Agie River, though the 'Rise' area is a protected sanctuary where fishing is prohibited."},{id:"bear-river-state-park",name:"Bear River State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/fe91e65051f5046e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A beautiful riverside park in Evanston, Wyoming, known for its resident elk and bison herds and scenic walking trails.",fullDescription:"Bear River State Park is a unique urban oasis located along the banks of the Bear River. It serves as a gateway to Wyoming, featuring a visitor center with educational exhibits about the region's history and wildlife. The park is famous for its captive herd of elk and bison, which visitors can view from designated observation areas. The park's landscape includes lush riparian zones, open meadows, and miles of paved and natural trails that connect to the city of Evanston's trail system.",thingsToDo:["Wildlife viewing of elk and bison","Picnicking in riverside areas","Hiking and cycling on paved trails","Fishing in the Bear River","Educational programs at the visitor center"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bear River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A mostly flat, paved trail that follows the winding path of the Bear River, offering excellent bird-watching opportunities."},{name:"Bison Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle trail that circles the wildlife enclosure, providing consistent views of the park's resident bison and elk herds."}],campingInfo:"Bear River State Park does not offer overnight camping or RV hookups; however, it provides excellent day-use facilities and picnic shelters.",wildlife:["Elk","Bison","Mule Deer","Bald Eagles","Great Blue Herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bear River State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features interactive displays on local wildlife, geology, and the history of the Bear River valley.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(307) 789-6547"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"601 Bear River Dr, Evanston, WY 82930",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bear+River+State+Park+Evanston+WY",officialWebsite:"https://wyoparks.wyo.gov/index.php/places-to-go/bear-river",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal wildlife viewing and trail conditions.",tips:["Bring binoculars for better views of the elk and bison herds.","The park connects to the Evanston city trail system, perfect for longer bike rides.","Visit during the early morning or late evening for the best chance to see active wildlife."],nearbyAttractions:["Uinta County Museum","Historic Downtown Evanston","Medicine Bow-Routt National Forest"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Bear River within park boundaries; please ensure you have a valid Wyoming fishing license."}]},o={illinois:[{id:"starved-rock-state-park",name:"Starved Rock State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/466bbbd45d14a793.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Illinois River park known for its 18 sandstone canyons, seasonal waterfalls and river overlooks.",fullDescription:"Starved Rock covers 2,630 acres on the south bank of the Illinois River near Utica and is the most visited state park in Illinois. Its sandstone canyons were carved by glacial floodwaters; French, LaSalle, Ottawa and St. Louis Canyons have the longest-lasting waterfalls. Over 13 miles of trails lead to the canyons and to river overlooks such as Lover's Leap and Eagle Cliff.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Waterfall canyons","River overlooks","Camping","Fishing","Lodge and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Starved Rock and Sandstone Point Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.2 miles",description:"A 4.2-mile loop near Oglesby that is generally considered moderately challenging and takes about 1.5 hours on average."}],campingInfo:"The park campground has 133 Class-A Premium campsites, and its gates are open from 8:30 a.m. until 10 p.m. daily.",fishingBoating:"The park's activities page lists catfish, bullhead, white bass, sauger and walleye for fishing and says boats may be launched from the west end of the park.",wildlife:["Deer","Catfish","Walleye","Sauger","White bass"],visitorCenter:{name:"Starved Rock State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(815) 667-4726"},hours:"Trails 7 a.m. until sunset; visitor center 9 a.m. to 4 p.m. daily",entranceFees:"Free",address:"2678 E. 873rd Road, Oglesby, IL 61348",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Starved%20Rock%20State%20Park%20illinois",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.starvedrock.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall are good times to see the park's sandstone canyons and waterfalls, but check the posted late-fall and winter deer hunting closures before you go.",tips:["Throughout 2026, a major trail improvement project may close some trails or limit access, so check current trail availability online before visiting.","During deer hunting seasons (Nov 20-22, Dec 3-6, Dec 11-13, Dec 31-Jan 3 and Jan 15-17, 2027), areas including the Starved Rock Nature Preserve, the Hennepin Canyon Parking Lot and, for some dates, the campground are closed to the public except for hunters.","Groups of 20 or more need advance written permission, and activity permits should be submitted at least 21 days ahead."],nearbyAttractions:["Matthiessen State Park","Buffalo Rock State Park","Grand Village of the Illinois"]},{id:"matthiessen-state-park",name:"Matthiessen State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/d2-matthiessen-state-park.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Illinois park near Starved Rock with a stream-cut sandstone canyon, the Upper and Lower Dells and waterfalls.",fullDescription:"Matthiessen State Park, a few miles south of Starved Rock, is centered on a stream that runs from Matthiessen Lake to the Vermilion River through sandstone canyons. The Upper Dells start at Lake Falls and end at 45-foot Cascade Falls, where the Lower Dells begin. The 1,938-acre park was once the private estate of industrialist Frederick Matthiessen.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Canyon and waterfall views","Mountain biking","Horseback riding"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Matthiessen State Park River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.8 miles",description:"A loop trail near Oglesby that AllTrails describes as an easy route taking an average of 41 minutes to complete."},{name:"Matthiessen State Park Lake Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.4 miles",description:"An easy hike with 95 ft of elevation gain that takes about 0.5–1 hour to complete."}],campingInfo:"The official park page lists camping among park activities, and the park's activities page references equestrian camping.",fishingBoating:"Bank fishing is allowed at Matthiessen Lake on the north side of the park off Route 71, following state fishing regulations.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Cliff swallow","Frogs","Toads","Salamanders"],visitorCenter:{name:"Matthiessen State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(815) 667-4726"},hours:"7 a.m. to sunset",entranceFees:"Free",address:"2500 IL-178, Oglesby, IL 61348",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Matthiessen%20State%20Park%20illinois",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.matthiessen.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring through early fall is a good time to explore the canyons, waterfalls and rock formations of the Dells. Check closure dates before visiting in late fall and winter, when the park closes to the public for deer hunting seasons.",tips:["The park is closed to the public (except hunters) during deer seasons: Nov 20–22 and Dec 3–6 (shotgun), and Dec 11–13 (muzzleloader). During the CWD firearm seasons (Dec 31–Jan 3 and Jan 15–17, 2027), park trails stay open but Matthiessen Nature Preserve is closed.","The Lake Falls bridge/trail is closed to all hikers until further notice, with a posted detour. The restroom building at the Dells parking lot is closed for repairs, and porta-potties are available instead.","Stay on marked trails, since going off-trail is illegal. Swimming, rock climbing/rappelling, drones and alcohol are all prohibited. Bikes are not allowed on Dells Area trails and are permitted only in the Vermillion River Area."],nearbyAttractions:["Starved Rock State Park","Plum Island Eagle Sanctuary","August Hill Winery & Illinois Sparkling"]},{id:"giant-city-state-park",name:"Giant City State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2607d1e739e6e9dd.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Southern Illinois park known for its 'Giant City Streets' of huge sandstone bluffs and a 1930s CCC-built lodge.",fullDescription:"Giant City State Park covers about 4,000 acres in Jackson and Union counties in Southern Illinois. It has a lodge and cabins built by the Civilian Conservation Corps in the 1930s, now with a restaurant, and a prehistoric stone fort site listed on the National Register. The park is a National Natural Landmark.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Horseback riding","Rock climbing","Picnicking","Lodge and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Giant City Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.1 miles",description:`The park's especially popular trail, home of the "Giant City Streets," huge sandstone bluffs formed 12,000 years ago.`},{name:"Red Cedar Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Listed as one of the park's most popular hiking trails."},{name:"Trillium Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Listed as one of the park's most popular hiking trails."}],campingInfo:"The park has 85 Class A campsites reservable online at ExploreMoreIL, a Class C area with 14 walk-in campsites, a youth group camping area, and an equestrian campground.",fishingBoating:"Ponds throughout the park offer bank fishing.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Giant City State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(618) 457-4836"},hours:"Park 7 a.m. - 10 p.m.; trails 7 a.m. - dusk; Visitor Center 8 a.m. - 3:30 p.m.",entranceFees:"Free",address:"235 Giant City Road, Makanda, IL 62958",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Giant%20City%20State%20Park%20illinois",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.giantcity.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring is a good time to visit because the park is known for hundreds of species of wildflowers. Fall is also pleasant for hiking among its more than 75 tree varieties and sandstone bluffs. Note that the lodge closes from mid-December to early February.",tips:["Trails are open only from 7 a.m. to dusk, even though the park itself stays open until 10 p.m.","The lodge and cabins are seasonal, open from the first Friday in February to mid-December. Call 618-457-4921 for reservations.","Rock climbing and rappelling are permitted only in two designated locations. The Giant City Stables operate only from March 15 to November 15."],nearbyAttractions:["Shawnee National Forest","Shawnee Bluffs Canopy Tour","River to River Trail"]},{id:"cave-in-rock-state-park",name:"Cave-in-Rock State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/d2-cave-in-rock-state-park.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched on the banks of the Ohio River, this park is famous for its historic 55-foot-wide limestone cavern once used by river pirates.",fullDescription:"Cave-in-Rock State Park is located in Hardin County, Illinois, overlooking the Ohio River. The park is named for the 55-foot-wide cave carved into the limestone bluff by water erosion. Historically, the cave served as a notorious hideout for river pirates and outlaws in the late 18th and early 19th centuries. Today, the park offers scenic views of the river, rugged bluffs, and a peaceful environment for outdoor recreation.",thingsToDo:["Exploring the historic limestone cave","Picnicking with river views","Hiking the bluff trails","Bird watching","Photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pirate's Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds along the limestone bluffs, offering panoramic views of the Ohio River."},{name:"Cave Access Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.2 miles",description:"A short, accessible path leading directly from the parking area to the entrance of the historic cave."}],campingInfo:"The park features a campground with 34 sites, including some with electricity, as well as a playground and shower facilities.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Various songbirds","Squirrels"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cave-in-Rock Park Office",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and camping registration.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(618) 289-4325"},hours:"Sunrise to 10:00 PM daily",address:"1 New State Park Rd, Cave-In-Rock, IL 62919",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cave-in-Rock+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.caveinrock.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild weather and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Bring a flashlight to explore the depths of the cave safely.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as the limestone near the river can be slippery.","Check the river levels before visiting, as high water can occasionally restrict access to the cave entrance."],nearbyAttractions:["Garden of the Gods","Shawnee National Forest","Golconda Marina","Ohio River Scenic Byway"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the Ohio River shoreline; however, there is no boat ramp located directly within the park boundaries."},{id:"pere-marquette-state-park",name:"Pere Marquette State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/49ca64e10caea1d9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Illinois' largest state park, offering stunning views of the Illinois River and famous for its winter bald eagle population.",fullDescription:"Located near the confluence of the Illinois and Mississippi Rivers, Pere Marquette State Park is a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts. The park is named after Father Jacques Marquette, who arrived in the area in 1673. Its rugged landscape features massive limestone bluffs, deep ravines, and lush forests, providing a diverse habitat for wildlife and spectacular vistas for visitors.",thingsToDo:["Bald eagle watching in winter","Hiking and mountain biking","Horseback riding","Fishing on the Illinois River","Picnicking in the historic stone shelters"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Goat Cliff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"This trail winds along the base of the limestone bluffs and offers excellent opportunities to spot local wildlife."},{name:"Dogwood Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0-mile loop",description:"A scenic loop that is particularly beautiful during the spring when the dogwood trees are in full bloom."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with 80 sites, including electrical hookups, shower facilities, and a youth tent area.",wildlife:["Bald Eagles","White-tailed Deer","Wild Turkeys","Red-tailed Hawks","Great Blue Herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pere Marquette Visitor Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the history of the Illinois River, local geology, and the annual bald eagle migration.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(618) 786-3323"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"13112 Visitor Center Ln, Grafton, IL 62037",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pere+Marquette+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.peremarquette.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Winter for eagle watching or Autumn for fall foliage.",tips:["Visit in January or February for the best chance to see bald eagles.","Bring binoculars for bird watching along the river bluffs.","Check the visitor center for scheduled guided nature hikes."],nearbyAttractions:["Grafton, IL","Raging Rivers Waterpark","Great River Road","Principia College"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"The park provides a boat ramp for access to the Illinois River, which is popular for fishing bass, crappie, and catfish."},{id:"illinois-beach-state-park",name:"Illinois Beach State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/07f604d8ee5b2c28.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Illinois Beach State Park is the only remaining beach ridge shoreline left in the state, offering unique dunes and sandy beaches along Lake Michigan.",fullDescription:"Spanning over 4,000 acres, Illinois Beach State Park features a diverse landscape of sandy beaches, dunes, marshes, and forests. It is the only remaining beach ridge shoreline left in the state, providing a critical habitat for rare plants and animals. The park is divided into two units, North and South, and offers a unique glimpse into the natural history of the Lake Michigan shoreline.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the sandy beach","Hiking through the dunes","Bird watching in the marshes","Camping in the wooded areas","Fishing along the shoreline"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Dead River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through the dunes and offers views of the Dead River and surrounding wetlands."},{name:"Beach Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail traverses the unique beach ridge topography, showcasing the diverse plant life of the region."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with 241 sites, many equipped with electricity, showers, and sanitary facilities for both tents and RVs.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Red fox","Great blue heron","Migratory waterfowl","Various species of snakes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Illinois Beach State Park Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides educational exhibits on the park's unique geology, local flora, and fauna, along with ranger assistance.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(847) 662-4811"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"1 Lake Front Dr, Zion, IL 60099",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Illinois+Beach+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.illinoisbeach.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for beach activities and bird migration.",tips:["Visit during the weekdays to avoid large crowds on the beach.","Bring insect repellent, especially when hiking near the marshy areas.","Check the official website for current beach swimming advisories before arriving."],nearbyAttractions:["North Point Marina","Waukegan Municipal Beach","Six Flags Great America","Chain O'Lakes State Park"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the Lake Michigan shoreline and in the park's ponds; however, there are no boat ramps within the park boundaries."},{id:"mississippi-palisades-state-park",name:"Mississippi Palisades State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c3d10440ed5e2695.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched high above the Mississippi River, this park features dramatic limestone bluffs and breathtaking views of the river valley.",fullDescription:"Mississippi Palisades State Park is a 2,500-acre park located in Carroll County, Illinois. The park is defined by its rugged limestone bluffs, deep ravines, and ancient rock formations carved by the Mississippi River over millennia. It serves as a significant geological site and a haven for diverse wildlife, offering visitors a unique landscape that contrasts sharply with the surrounding flat prairie terrain of Illinois.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Camping","Bird watching","Picnicking","Photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Sentinel Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A relatively flat trail that offers spectacular views of the Mississippi River and the famous Sentinel rock formation."},{name:"Oak Point Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail winds through dense hardwood forests and provides access to several scenic overlooks along the bluff edge."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 241 campsites, including both tent and RV sites. Amenities include electricity, showers, and flush toilets, with some sites available for reservation.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mississippi Palisades Visitor Center",info:"Provides maps, educational displays about the local geology, and information on park history.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(815) 273-2731"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"16327A IL-84, Savanna, IL 61074",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mississippi+Palisades+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.mississippipalisades.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for fall foliage or Spring for bird migration.",tips:["Visit during the fall to see the vibrant colors of the trees against the limestone bluffs.","Bring binoculars as the park is a prime location for spotting bald eagles in the winter.","Stay on marked trails to avoid dangerous drop-offs near the cliff edges."],nearbyAttractions:["Savanna, IL","Galena, IL","Great River Trail","Thomson Causeway Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the river banks, and there is a boat ramp available for access to the Mississippi River."},{id:"rock-cut-state-park",name:"Rock Cut State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/26cc9206f8112645.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in northern Illinois, Rock Cut State Park offers over 3,000 acres of rolling hills, lush forests, and two beautiful lakes for outdoor recreation.",fullDescription:"Rock Cut State Park is a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts in Winnebago County. The park features Pierce Lake and Olson Lake, which provide excellent opportunities for fishing, boating, and ice skating. The landscape is characterized by diverse habitats including upland forests, prairie remnants, and wetlands, making it a haven for birdwatchers and nature photographers. Historically, the area was shaped by glacial activity, resulting in the unique topography that visitors enjoy today.",thingsToDo:["Fishing and boating on Pierce Lake","Hiking and mountain biking on extensive trails","Winter sports including cross-country skiing and ice fishing","Camping in wooded or open sites","Horseback riding on designated equestrian trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pierce Lake Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that circles the perimeter of Pierce Lake, offering frequent views of the water and local waterfowl."},{name:"Red Oak Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2-mile loop",description:"This trail winds through mature oak-hickory forests and provides a great opportunity to see spring wildflowers."}],campingInfo:"The park offers over 200 campsites, including Class A sites with electricity and showers, as well as primitive equestrian camping areas.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Red-tailed hawk","Beaver"],visitorCenter:{name:"Rock Cut State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and educational exhibits about local flora and fauna.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(815) 979-1237"},hours:"Sunrise to 10:00 PM daily",address:"7318 Harlem Rd, Loves Park, IL 61111",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Rock+Cut+State+Park+Loves+Park+IL",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.illinois.gov/parks/park.rockcut.html",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers and autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Check the official website for seasonal lake closures or ice thickness reports before visiting.","Bring binoculars as the park is a designated Important Bird Area.","Mountain bikers should check the trail status board at the entrance for current conditions."],nearbyAttractions:["Anderson Japanese Gardens","Discovery Center Museum","Klehm Arboretum & Botanic Garden","City of Rockford"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Pierce Lake allows motorized boats with a 10-horsepower limit and offers a public boat launch; Olson Lake is restricted to non-motorized watercraft."}],indiana:[{id:"brown-county-state-park",name:"Brown County State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/4323fff8af691623.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Indiana's largest state park, 15,776 wooded acres in the hills near Nashville, with a historic lodge.",fullDescription:"Brown County State Park in southern Indiana is the state's largest, at 15,776 acres, and draws about 1.2 million visitors a year. Its Abe Martin Lodge dates from 1932 and has a restaurant and an indoor water park, with family and rustic cabins and campgrounds nearby.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Horseback riding","Mountain biking","Scenic drives","Fishing","Lodge, cabins and camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"CCC Trail (Trail 2)",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2 miles",description:"A trail built by the Civilian Conservation Corps with impressive stone bridges, stairways and retaining walls that starts and ends behind the lodge."},{name:"Ogle Hollow Nature Preserve Trail (Trail 5)",difficulty:"Rugged",length:"0.75 mile",description:"A self-guided trail that follows a slope wooded with yellowwood trees and loops through the Ogle Hollow Nature Preserve."},{name:"Ogle Lake Trail (Trail 7)",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A hilly, wooded hike along the shores of Ogle Lake."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 401 electric, 28 non-electric and 60 rally campsites, plus a separate horsemen's campground off State Road 135 South with 118 electric and 86 non-electric sites.",fishingBoating:"Fishing and ice fishing are offered in the park, which has two lakes for fishing, including Ogle Lake and Strahl Lake.",wildlife:["timber rattlesnake","deer"],visitorCenter:{name:"Brown County State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(812) 988-6406"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"$7 per vehicle with Indiana plates; $15 out-of-state plates",address:"1405 S.R. 46 W., Nashville, IN 47448 (West Gate)",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Brown%20County%20State%20Park%20indiana",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/brown-county-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall is the classic time to visit, as Indiana's largest park is a traditional fall color hot spot with nearly 20 miles of tree-lined roads and scenic vistas, though the park is popular year-round.",tips:["All RVs and vehicles towing trailers must use the West Gate on State Road 46, because the North Gate has a historic covered bridge with a 9-foot height clearance and a 3-ton weight limit.","GPS directions may not send you to the correct entrance, so use the official mapping addresses or coordinates for the West Gate, North Gate or Horse Camp.","Pool hours may change in summer because of weather and staffing, so call the property office for exact times before your visit."],nearbyAttractions:["Yellowwood State Forest","Brown County History Center","Brown County Visitors Center"]},{id:"turkey-run-state-park",name:"Turkey Run State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9bfbaea0e36a180a.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"West-central Indiana park of sandstone gorges along Sugar Creek, with a suspension bridge and a 1919 inn.",fullDescription:"Turkey Run, Indiana's second state park (1916), sits along Sugar Creek in Parke County. It includes the Rocky Hollow Falls Canyon Nature Preserve, a suspension bridge across the creek, the historic Lusk Home and Mill Site, the Richard Lieber Log Cabin and the Turkey Run Inn, built in 1919.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the gorges","Canoeing Sugar Creek","Camping","Turkey Run Inn","Historic sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail 3",difficulty:"Very Rugged",length:"1.7 miles",description:"Known as the ladders trail, it offers unusual hiking between the steep walls of Rocky and Bear hollows, and portions may be impassable during high water."},{name:"Trail 10",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.4 miles",description:"This trail leads to 'Camel's Back' for a fine scenic view and passes many unusual formations caused by erosion."},{name:"Trail 11",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 mile",description:"A short hike high above Turkey Run Hollow leads to the Lieber Memorial and Log Church."}],campingInfo:"The park's campground has 213 electric sites, plus youth tent areas, a camp store and a dumping station.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is a listed park activity, and a third-party guide says anglers can fish Sugar Creek for bass and bluegill with a state fishing license.",wildlife:["Wild turkey","Bass","Bluegill"],visitorCenter:{name:"Turkey Run State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(765) 597-2635"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"$7 per vehicle with Indiana plates; $15 out-of-state plates",address:"8121 E. Park Road, Marshall, IN 47859",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Turkey%20Run%20State%20Park%20indiana",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/turkey-run-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer is when the ravine drainages are relatively free of water, which makes the streambed trails most passable, and arriving early helps avoid the busiest hours.",tips:["All trails use ravine streambeds in places and can be slick or impassable during high water, so treat flooded sections as closed and use alternate trails.","The ladders on Trail 3 can be hazardous for visitors with some medical conditions or disabilities, and hikers with small children or pets should use alternate trails.","Pool hours may change because of weather and staffing, so contact the property office for exact times before your visit."],nearbyAttractions:["Shades State Park"]},{id:"clifty-falls-state-park",name:"Clifty Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2934164a27aae25b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Indiana park on the Ohio River near Madison with a creek canyon and four waterfalls of 60 to 83 feet.",fullDescription:"Clifty Falls State Park covers 1,416 acres in Jefferson County near Madison. Clifty Creek Canyon runs the length of the park, and its waterfalls include Big and Little Clifty Falls (60 ft each), Hoffman Falls (78 ft) and Tunnel Falls (83 ft). Ten trails range from easy to very rugged, and the Clifty Inn and a campground offer overnight stays.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Waterfall viewing","Camping","Clifty Inn"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail 4",difficulty:"Rugged",length:"0.75 mile",description:"Starts at the Hoffman Falls parking lot, reaches a falls overlook after a short distance, and follows the canyon to Lilly Memorial, where there is a long, steep staircase."},{name:"Trail 7",difficulty:"Moderately rugged",length:"1.25 miles",description:"Starts at Clifty Shelter and passes Little Clifty Falls along rocky footing to Lookout Point, then returns via the upper Little Clifty Creek gorge."},{name:"Trail 5",difficulty:"Rugged",length:"0.875 mile",description:"Descends a long, steep staircase from Lilly Memorial and follows the canyon past the tunnel entrances and Oak Grove to Tunnel Falls."}],campingInfo:"The park campground has 106 electric and 63 non-electric sites, plus youth tent areas and a dumping station, with campground reservations available.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Clifty Falls State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(812) 273-8885"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"$7 per vehicle with Indiana plates; $15 out-of-state plates",address:"2221 Clifty Drive, Madison, IN 47250",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Clifty%20Falls%20State%20Park%20indiana",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/clifty-falls-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Winter and spring are best for seeing the park's waterfalls at their most impressive, though Clifty Canyon offers hiking year-round.",tips:["Brough's Tunnel is closed indefinitely after a rockslide. Trail 5 remains open, but avoid both tunnel entrances.","Trail 2 is mostly the stone bed of Clifty Creek and may be impassable during high water. Areas above and below all waterfalls are always off limits.","Trails in Clifty Canyon close after sunset, so plan canyon hikes for daylight."],nearbyAttractions:["Lanier Mansion State Historic Site","Historic Madison","Madison riverfront on the Ohio River"]},{id:"indiana-dunes-state-park",name:"Indiana Dunes State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/d2-indiana-dunes-state-park.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located along the southern shore of Lake Michigan, this park features massive sand dunes, expansive beaches, and diverse ecosystems.",fullDescription:"Indiana Dunes State Park consists of 2,182 acres of primitive, beautiful, historic, and unique landscape. It is located in Porter County, Indiana, and features more than three miles of beautiful beach along Lake Michigan. The park is surrounded by the Indiana Dunes National Park and offers a unique look at the geological history of the Great Lakes region, including the famous '3 Dune Challenge' which traverses the three tallest dunes in the park.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the beach","Hiking the 3 Dune Challenge","Bird watching","Picnicking","Cross-country skiing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail 8",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"1.5 miles",description:"This trail takes hikers over the three tallest dunes in the park, offering panoramic views of Lake Michigan."},{name:"Trail 9",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.75 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the dunes and wooded areas, providing a great look at the park's diverse flora."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with 140 sites, including full hookups for RVs and modern restrooms with showers.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Red fox","Wild turkey","Migratory songbirds","Sandhill cranes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Indiana Dunes State Park Nature Center",info:"The Nature Center provides educational exhibits about the dunes' ecology, history, and local wildlife, along with ranger-led programs.",hours:"Daily: 9:30 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(219) 926-1390"},hours:"7:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"1600 N. 25 E., Chesterton, IN 46304",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Indiana+Dunes+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/indiana-dunes-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for beach activities and hiking.",tips:["Arrive early on summer weekends as the parking lots fill up quickly.","Bring plenty of water and sunscreen for the exposed dune hikes.","Check the park website for current beach water quality reports before swimming."],nearbyAttractions:["Indiana Dunes National Park","Chesterton","Valparaiso","Michigan City"],entranceFees:"$7.00 for Indiana residents and $12.00 for out-of-state vehicles per day.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the Lake Michigan shoreline, though no boat launch is available within the state park boundaries."},{id:"pokagon-state-park",name:"Pokagon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e9cb1de1ee3d004d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in the lake country of northeast Indiana, Pokagon State Park offers beautiful glacial landscapes and year-round recreational activities.",fullDescription:"Pokagon State Park is nestled between Lake James and Snow Lake, offering a unique glacial landscape characterized by rolling hills and kettle lakes. Established in 1925, the park is named after the Potawatomi chiefs Leopold and Simon Pokagon. It is famous for its diverse ecosystems, including wetlands, forests, and meadows, and is home to the historic Potawatomi Inn.",thingsToDo:["Tobogganing on the refrigerated track","Swimming at the public beach","Boating and fishing on Lake James","Cross-country skiing in winter","Wildlife viewing at the nature center"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail 3",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.7 miles",description:"This trail winds through diverse forest habitats and offers scenic views of the glacial kettle holes."},{name:"Trail 4",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A beautiful loop that takes hikers through the park's wetland areas and hardwood forests."}],campingInfo:"The park features over 200 electric campsites and several non-electric sites, along with the historic Potawatomi Inn which offers lodge rooms and cabins.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Great blue herons","Red foxes","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pokagon Nature Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on local geology, wildlife, and the history of the Potawatomi people.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(260) 833-2012"},hours:"7:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"450 Lane 100 Lake James, Angola, IN 46703",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pokagon+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/pokagon-state-parktrine-state-recreation-area/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for wildflowers or winter for the famous toboggan run.",tips:["Visit the toboggan run early on weekends as it reaches capacity quickly.","Bring binoculars for bird watching near the wetlands.","Check the park website for seasonal programs and guided nature hikes."],nearbyAttractions:["Trine State Recreation Area","City of Angola","Wild Winds Buffalo Preserve"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle for Indiana residents, $9.00 for out-of-state vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The park provides boat ramps for access to Lake James and Snow Lake, with fishing opportunities for bass, bluegill, and perch."},{id:"spring-mill-state-park",name:"Spring Mill State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e725a104e91c9328.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Experience a unique blend of history and nature at Spring Mill, featuring a restored pioneer village and fascinating limestone caves.",fullDescription:"Spring Mill State Park is a unique destination that combines natural beauty with historical preservation. The park is home to a fully restored 1863 pioneer village, complete with a working water-powered grist mill. Geologically, the park is famous for its extensive karst topography, featuring numerous sinkholes and the impressive Donaldson Cave system, which offers a glimpse into the subterranean world of Indiana.",thingsToDo:["Tour the Pioneer Village","Explore the Twin Caves boat tour","Visit the Virgil I. Grissom Memorial","Hike the rugged limestone trails","Bird watching near the lake"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail 3",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail winds through the woods and offers beautiful views of the park's unique karst landscape."},{name:"Trail 4",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"1.0-mile loop",description:"A challenging hike that takes visitors through the rugged terrain surrounding the cave entrances."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with 187 electric sites and 36 primitive sites, along with modern restrooms, showers, and a camp store.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Northern cavefish","Various bat species","Great blue heron"],visitorCenter:{name:"Spring Mill Nature Center",info:"The nature center provides educational exhibits on the park's geology, cave systems, and local wildlife.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(812) 849-3534"},hours:"7:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"3333 State Road 60 East, Mitchell, IN 47446",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Spring+Mill+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/spring-mill-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild weather and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Book your cave boat tour early as they fill up quickly during peak season.","Wear sturdy hiking boots if you plan to explore the more rugged trails.","Visit the grist mill during the week to avoid weekend crowds."],nearbyAttractions:["Bluespring Caverns","Bedford","Paoli Peaks","Hoosier National Forest"],entranceFees:"$7.00 for Indiana residents and $9.00 for out-of-state vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted on the 30-acre Spring Mill Lake; boat rentals are available seasonally."},{id:"mccormicks-creek-state-park",name:"McCormick's Creek State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/f64ad001c9d69bd1.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Indiana's first state park, featuring a stunning limestone canyon, scenic waterfalls, and historic structures.",fullDescription:"Established in 1916, McCormick's Creek State Park is Indiana's oldest state park. It is renowned for its dramatic limestone canyon carved by the waters of McCormick's Creek, which flows into the White River. The park features diverse geological formations, including sinkholes and caves, and is home to the historic Canyon Inn, which was originally built as a sanitarium in the late 19th century.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the canyon trails","Swimming in the Olympic-sized pool","Bird watching","Horseback riding","Picnicking in designated areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail 3",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.8 miles",description:"This trail takes hikers through the heart of the canyon, offering close-up views of the limestone walls and the creek."},{name:"Trail 5",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the forest and provides access to the Wolf Cave nature preserve area."}],campingInfo:"The park offers over 180 electric campsites, a youth tent area, and the historic Canyon Inn which provides full-service lodging and dining.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Eastern box turtles","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"McCormick's Creek Nature Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the park's geology, local wildlife, and history, along with a resident naturalist.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(812) 829-2235"},hours:"7:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"250 McCormick Creek Park Rd, Spencer, IN 47460",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=McCormick's+Creek+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/mccormicks-creek-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for waterfall viewing or Autumn for fall foliage.",tips:["Visit the waterfall after a rainfall for the most impressive flow.","Check the Canyon Inn schedule for seasonal dining hours.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as the canyon trails can be slippery and rocky."],nearbyAttractions:["Spencer, Indiana","Bloomington, Indiana","Owen-Putnam State Forest","Lake Monroe"],entranceFees:"$7.00 for Indiana residents and $9.00 for out-of-state vehicles per day.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the White River, which borders the park; however, there are no boat ramps located directly within the park boundaries."},{id:"fort-harrison-state-park",name:"Fort Harrison State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/d2-fort-harrison-state-park.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the northeast side of Indianapolis, this urban oasis offers 1,700 acres of beautiful woods, rolling hills, and the scenic Fall Creek.",fullDescription:"Fort Harrison State Park, often called 'Fort Ben' by locals, is a former military base turned state park. It features a unique blend of history and nature, with remnants of its military past integrated into the landscape. The park is characterized by its mature hardwood forests, steep ravines, and the meandering Fall Creek, providing a peaceful retreat from the surrounding urban environment of Indianapolis.",thingsToDo:["Horseback riding at the saddle barn","Golfing at The Fort Golf Resort","Fishing in Fall Creek","Picnicking in designated areas","Bird watching along the trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Harrison Trace Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.7 miles",description:"A paved multi-use trail that loops around the park, offering great views of the lake and surrounding forest."},{name:"Lawrence Creek Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.2 miles",description:"A rugged dirt trail that winds through deep ravines and dense woodlands, perfect for hikers seeking a challenge."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a variety of camping options including equestrian campsites and modern facilities, though it is primarily known for its day-use amenities and the historic Garrison event center.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Great blue herons","Fox squirrels"],visitorCenter:{name:"The Garrison",info:"The visitor center provides information on park history, trail maps, and educational programs about the local ecosystem.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(317) 591-0904"},hours:"7:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"6000 N Post Rd, Indianapolis, IN 46216",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort+Harrison+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.in.gov/dnr/state-parks/parks-lakes/fort-harrison-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for the vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Visit during the weekdays to avoid heavy crowds on the popular hiking trails.","Check the park website for seasonal events and guided nature hikes.","Bring binoculars as the park is a prime location for bird watching near the creek."],nearbyAttractions:["Indianapolis Motor Speedway","Children's Museum of Indianapolis","White River State Park","Downtown Indianapolis"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle for Indiana residents, $9.00 for out-of-state vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Fall Creek and the park's lake; however, no motorized boats are allowed on the water."}],iowa:[{id:"backbone-state-park",name:"Backbone State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/309f8aea482b777c.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Iowa's oldest state park (1920), named for the Devil's Backbone, a narrow rock ridge above the Maquoketa River.",fullDescription:"Backbone State Park near Strawberry Point was dedicated in 1920 as Iowa's first state park. It is named for a steep bedrock ridge carved by a loop of the Maquoketa River. The Civilian Conservation Corps built Backbone Lake's dam and most of the park's trails and buildings in the 1930s.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Trout fishing","Rock climbing","Swimming","Camping and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Backbone Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1 mile",description:"Built by the CCC in 1933–1934, this loop follows the top of the narrow Backbone Ridge and passes some of the park's most popular rock-climbing spots."},{name:"West Lake and East Lake Trail Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"6.3 miles",description:"This loop combines the park's East Lake and West Lake trails, which are part of the 21-mile multi-use system and are also used by mountain bikers."},{name:"Barred Owl Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Barred Owl is one of the park's multi-use trails that the official page recommends to mountain bikers."}],campingInfo:"Campers can choose electric and non-electric sites with showers and a dump station at South Lake Campground or non-electric sites with pit latrines at Six Pine Campground, and all campsites require a reservation.",fishingBoating:"Anglers can fish for trout in a clear stream fed by Richmond Springs, and boaters on Backbone Lake can use rentals and a boat ramp, with only electric motors allowed.",wildlife:["Trout"],visitorCenter:{name:"Backbone State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(563) 924-2527"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"Free",address:"1347 129th St, Dundee, IA 52038",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Backbone%20State%20Park%20iowa",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/places-go/state-parks/all-parks/backbone-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Visit from Memorial Day weekend through Labor Day weekend for full services, or until mid-October for the CCC museum and fall color in the oak and maple forest; winter brings snowmobile and cross-country ski trails.",tips:["Campgrounds are 100% reservable and require a reservation, so book campsites and cabins in advance through the online reservation system.","Rock climbers and rappellers must register at the park office before climbing.","Check the beach monitoring dashboard or call 515-725-3434 for current beach advisories; both are updated on Fridays during the monitoring season."],nearbyAttractions:["Backbone State Forest","Long Hollow Canopy Tour"]},{id:"ledges-state-park",name:"Ledges State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/04cbfcb1cfd7b73d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Iowa park near Boone with a 100-foot-deep sandstone gorge carved by Pea's Creek.",fullDescription:"Ledges State Park, four miles south of Boone, contains a sandstone gorge cut by Pea's Creek, a tributary of the Des Moines River, up to 100 feet deep with concretions jutting from the cliffs. Woodlands of basswood, maple, oak and hickory mix with prairie, and the Des Moines River runs along the west side.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Canoeing","Fishing","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lost Lake Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A shaded, crushed-limestone path at the southern end of the park leads to a small lake, and its first 0.4 miles are fully accessible, which allows an out-and-back hike of nearly 1 mile."},{name:"Ledges Prairie Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.7 miles",description:"AllTrails rates this trail an easy hike with about 13 feet of elevation gain, taking roughly 0.5 to 1 hour."},{name:"Ledges Table Rock Trail and Inspiration Point",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.3 miles",description:"A moderate trail in Ledges State Park, part of a trail system that climbs steep slopes to scenic overlooks of the Pea's Creek canyon."}],campingInfo:"The renovated campground has electric, full hook-up and primitive hike-in sites; reservations are required for all sites, are accepted March through November, and the campground is closed December through February.",fishingBoating:"Canoeing and fishing are possible on the Des Moines River, which runs along the west side of the park.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Raccoon","Beaver","Woodchuck","Pileated woodpecker"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ledges State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(515) 432-1852"},hours:"4 a.m. to 10:30 p.m. daily",entranceFees:"Free (Iowa state parks charge no entrance fee)",address:"1515 P Ave., Madrid, IA 50156",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ledges%20State%20Park%20iowa",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/places-go/state-parks/all-parks/ledges-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall is ideal: the campground is open March through November, families can streamwalk in ankle-deep Peas Creek in warm weather, and the Friends of the Ledges concession runs on weekend evenings from Memorial Day through Labor Day.",tips:["All campsites require an advance reservation through the online reservation system. Reservations are accepted March through November, and the campground is closed December through February.","Check the Park Alerts & Notices page for current closures before you go. Low-lying areas of the park are regularly flooded by the Des Moines River.","Trails within the canyon have steps and steep terrain. For an accessible option, use the Lost Lake Trail, whose first 0.4 miles are accessible."],nearbyAttractions:["High Trestle Trail Bridge","Saylorville Lake"]},{id:"maquoketa-caves-state-park",name:"Maquoketa Caves State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/1213b321a5c0aceb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Iowa park with more caves than any other in the state, linked by trails, including Dancehall Cave.",fullDescription:"Maquoketa Caves State Park lies northwest of Maquoketa in Jackson County, in Iowa's unglaciated Driftless Area. It has more caves than any other Iowa state park, plus limestone arches and chimneys, linked by a trail system with overlooks. Dancehall Cave and a natural bridge are among the best known, and part of the park is a National Register historic district.",thingsToDo:["Cave exploring","Hiking","Natural bridge","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Caves Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"Not listed",description:"A wooded, shaded trail that briefly meanders alongside Raccoon Creek."}],campingInfo:"The park has a newly upgraded campground among pine trees plus primitive hike-in sites; all sites require a reservation, which is accepted March through November, and the campground is closed December through February.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Bats"],visitorCenter:{name:"Maquoketa Caves State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"563-652-5833"},hours:"Iowa state parks open 4 a.m. to 10:30 p.m. daily. Caves are closed Nov 15 to Apr 1. The visitor center is typically open 9 am to 5 pm Sat–Sun, Memorial Day through Labor Day.",entranceFees:"Check the official park website for current fees.",address:"9688 Caves Rd, Maquoketa, IA 52060",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Maquoketa%20Caves%20State%20Park%20iowa",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/places-go/state-parks/all-parks/maquoketa-caves-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through fall, between April and mid-November, when the caves are open; summer weekends also offer the interpretive center and nature programs.",tips:["Status as of Oct 2026: closed for the season Oct 19, 2026 – Apr 15, 2027 (DNR alert). Check the official page before going.","Bring hiking shoes and a flashlight for exploring the caves.","The trails are not accessible because of steep inclines, rocky terrain and staircases.","Check Park Alerts before you go. The caves close every year from Nov 15 to Apr 1 for hibernating bats."],nearbyAttractions:["Hurstville Interpretive Center","Jackson County Historical Museum"]},{id:"pikes-peak-state-park",name:"Pikes Peak State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e0e49ea3ec5e8370.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched high above the confluence of the Mississippi and Wisconsin Rivers, Pikes Peak State Park offers some of the most spectacular views in the Midwest.",fullDescription:"Pikes Peak State Park is renowned for its 500-foot bluffs overlooking the Mississippi River. The park features ancient Native American burial mounds, fossil-rich limestone formations, and lush hardwood forests. It serves as a premier destination for bird watching, photography, and exploring the unique geological history of the Driftless Area.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the bluff trails","Bird watching for bald eagles","Picnicking at the scenic overlooks","Exploring Native American effigy mounds","Photography of the river valley"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bridal Veil Falls Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible boardwalk trail that leads to a beautiful waterfall and lush fern-covered cliffs."},{name:"Point Ann Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2 miles",description:"This trail winds through dense forest and offers elevated vantage points of the Mississippi River valley."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 77 campsites, including 60 with electrical hookups, modern restrooms, showers, and a dump station.",wildlife:["Bald Eagles","White-tailed Deer","Wild Turkeys","Red-tailed Hawks","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pikes Peak State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features interpretive displays about the geology, history, and wildlife of the Mississippi River valley.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(563) 873-2341"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:30 PM daily",address:"32264 Pikes Peak Rd, McGregor, IA 52157",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pikes+Peak+State+Park+Iowa",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/Places-to-Go/State-Parks/Iowa-State-Parks/Pikes-Peak-State-Park",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for fall foliage or spring for eagle migration.",tips:["Visit the overlook early in the morning to avoid crowds and catch the best light for photography.","Bring binoculars as the park is a prime location for spotting bald eagles.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as some trails can be steep and rocky."],nearbyAttractions:["Effigy Mounds National Monument","McGregor, Iowa","Yellow River State Forest","Marquette, Iowa"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is available along the Mississippi River shoreline; however, there is no boat ramp located directly within the park boundaries."},{id:"mines-of-spain-state-recreation-area",name:"Mines of Spain State Recreation Area",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/f-mines-of-spain-mississippi-bluff.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A stunning 1,437-acre recreation area located along the Mississippi River, featuring rugged bluffs, diverse forests, and rich historical significance.",fullDescription:"The Mines of Spain State Recreation Area is a National Historic Landmark that offers a unique blend of natural beauty and cultural history. Once the site of Julien Dubuque's lead mining operations in the late 18th century, the park now preserves diverse ecosystems including prairies, woodlands, and limestone bluffs. Visitors can explore the E.B. Lyons Interpretive Center to learn about the area's geology, flora, and the legacy of the Meskwaki people and early European settlers.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running","Bird watching","Cross-country skiing","Educational programs at the interpretive center","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Catfish Creek Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds along the creek, offering beautiful views of the water and surrounding forest canopy."},{name:"Julien Dubuque Monument Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible path leading to the historic monument dedicated to the founder of Dubuque, providing panoramic views of the Mississippi River."}],campingInfo:"There is no overnight camping available within the Mines of Spain State Recreation Area; however, several private campgrounds are located in the nearby Dubuque area.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Wild turkeys","White-tailed deer","Red-tailed hawks","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"E.B. Lyons Interpretive Center",info:"Features exhibits on the park's natural history, geology, and the history of lead mining in the region.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM, Saturday-Sunday: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(563) 556-0620"},hours:"Sunrise to sunset daily",address:"8991 Bellevue Heights, Dubuque, IA 52003",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mines+of+Spain+State+Recreation+Area",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/places-go/state-parks/all-parks/mines-spain-state-recreation-area",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers and autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities along the river bluffs.","Check the interpretive center schedule for guided nature walks and historical talks.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as some trails feature steep, rocky terrain."],nearbyAttractions:["National Mississippi River Museum & Aquarium","Fenelon Place Elevator","Dubuque Arboretum & Botanical Gardens","Downtown Dubuque"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the banks of the Mississippi River and Catfish Creek; however, there are no boat ramps located within the park boundaries."},{id:"bellevue-state-park",name:"Bellevue State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/da9e9da339a0e52d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched high above the Mississippi River, Bellevue State Park offers breathtaking panoramic views and unique limestone bluffs.",fullDescription:"Bellevue State Park is divided into two distinct areas: the Nelson Unit and the Dyas Unit. The park is renowned for its dramatic limestone bluffs that rise 300 feet above the Mississippi River, providing some of the most spectacular vistas in Iowa. The area is rich in geological history, featuring ancient rock formations and diverse ecosystems including hardwood forests and prairie remnants.",thingsToDo:["Hiking along the bluff trails","Bird watching and wildlife photography","Picnicking in scenic overlooks","Exploring the Butterfly Garden","Camping in wooded sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"This trail winds along the edge of the limestone bluffs, offering stunning views of the Mississippi River valley."},{name:"Forest Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0-mile loop",description:"A gentle path through mature hardwood forests, perfect for spotting local songbirds and wildflowers."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 46 campsites, many with electrical hookups, along with modern restrooms and shower facilities. Reservations are recommended during peak summer months.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Red-tailed hawks","Various butterfly species"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bellevue State Park Office",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and educational displays about the local ecosystem.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(563) 872-4019"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"24718 Bellevue Park Rd, Bellevue, IA 52031",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bellevue+State+Park+Iowa",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/Places-to-Go/State-Parks/Iowa-State-Parks/Bellevue-State-Park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities along the river.","Visit the Butterfly Garden during mid-summer for the best floral displays.","Check the official DNR website for seasonal trail closures due to weather."],nearbyAttractions:["City of Bellevue","Lock and Dam No. 12","Maquoketa Caves State Park","Dubuque"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is available in the Mississippi River nearby, though the park itself is primarily focused on hiking and nature observation."},{id:"waubonsie-state-park",name:"Waubonsie State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0c31b5c9c7586b01.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Waubonsie State Park offers breathtaking views of the Loess Hills and the Missouri River valley, featuring unique geological formations and diverse ecosystems.",fullDescription:"Located in the southwest corner of Iowa, Waubonsie State Park is renowned for its dramatic Loess Hills landscape. These unique wind-deposited silt formations create a rugged, rolling terrain that is unlike anywhere else in the state. The park is rich in history, serving as a significant site for Native American tribes and later as a landmark for pioneers traveling westward. Visitors can explore a variety of habitats, including native prairies, oak-hickory forests, and deep ravines that provide a sanctuary for diverse wildlife.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the scenic Loess Hills trails","Horseback riding on designated equestrian trails","Bird watching and wildlife photography","Picnicking in the shaded woodland areas","Camping in the scenic ridge-top sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Sunset Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail follows the ridge line, offering panoramic views of the Missouri River valley and spectacular sunset vistas."},{name:"Waubonsie Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"A winding path that takes hikers through dense forests and deep ravines, showcasing the unique geology of the Loess Hills."}],campingInfo:"The park features both modern and non-electric campsites, as well as equestrian camping facilities with stalls available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Coyotes","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Waubonsie Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, trail information, and assistance with camping reservations.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(712) 382-2786"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:30 PM daily",address:"2585 Waubonsie Park Rd, Hamburg, IA 51640",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Waubonsie+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/Places-to-Go/State-Parks/Iowa-State-Parks/Waubonsie-State-Park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for the vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Bring binoculars to take full advantage of the bird watching opportunities.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as the Loess Hills terrain can be steep and uneven.","Check the official website for trail closures during wet weather to protect the fragile soil."],nearbyAttractions:["Hamburg, IA","Sidney, IA","Forney Lake Wildlife Management Area","Lewis and Clark National Historic Trail"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Small ponds are available for fishing, though the park is primarily focused on hiking and equestrian activities."},{id:"lake-macbride-state-park",name:"Lake Macbride State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/724e70fec12376da.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Lake Macbride State Park is a premier outdoor destination in Iowa, offering expansive water recreation and scenic woodland trails.",fullDescription:"Located near Solon, Lake Macbride State Park features a massive 812-acre lake that serves as the centerpiece for water-based recreation in the region. The park is characterized by its rolling hills, diverse hardwood forests, and abundant wildlife. It was developed in the 1930s and has since become a vital hub for boating, fishing, and hiking, providing a peaceful escape for visitors looking to enjoy Iowa's natural landscape.",thingsToDo:["Boating and sailing","Swimming at the beach","Fishing for walleye and muskie","Hiking and birdwatching","Picnicking in designated areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lake Macbride Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds along the shoreline, offering beautiful views of the lake and surrounding forest."},{name:"Woodland Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A gentle path through mature hardwood trees, perfect for families and bird enthusiasts."}],campingInfo:"The park offers two modern campgrounds with electrical hookups, shower facilities, and flush toilets, as well as several non-electric sites.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","Red foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Macbride Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on trail conditions, camping reservations, and local wildlife education.",hours:"Monday - Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(319) 624-2200"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:30 PM daily",address:"3525 Highway 382 NE, Solon, IA 52333",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Macbride+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.iowadnr.gov/Places-to-Go/State-Parks/Iowa-State-Parks/Lake-Macbride-State-Park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water activities and foliage.",tips:["Visit during the week to avoid heavy weekend crowds at the beach.","Bring binoculars as the park is a fantastic spot for birdwatching.","Check the Iowa DNR website for current water quality alerts before swimming."],nearbyAttractions:["Coralville Lake","Devonian Fossil Gorge","University of Iowa Museum of Natural History","Solon, IA"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"The lake features multiple boat ramps and a full-service marina offering boat rentals, including pontoons and kayaks."}],kansas:[{id:"kanopolis-state-park",name:"Kanopolis State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/1c4ef6ee21c5dccf.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Kansas' first state park, Kanopolis offers rugged sandstone bluffs, rolling hills, and the expansive Kanopolis Reservoir.",fullDescription:"Established in 1955, Kanopolis State Park is located in the Smoky Hills region of Kansas. The park surrounds the 3,500-acre Kanopolis Reservoir, which was the first federal reservoir in Kansas. The landscape is defined by dramatic sandstone canyons, prairie grasslands, and diverse wildlife habitats, making it a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts interested in geology and history.",thingsToDo:["Boating and water skiing","Fishing for walleye and wiper","Mountain biking on rugged trails","Horseback riding on designated equestrian paths","Wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Horsethief Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through sandstone canyons and offers panoramic views of the reservoir."},{name:"Buffalo Track Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.5 miles",description:"This trail traverses through unique geological formations and provides excellent opportunities for bird watching."}],campingInfo:"The park features over 200 primitive and utility campsites, including sites with water and electric hookups. Several cabins are available for rent year-round.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Coyotes","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kanopolis State Park Office",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and assistance with camping reservations and permits.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(785) 546-2565"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping; day use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"200 Horsethief Rd, Marquette, KS 67464",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kanopolis+State+Park+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Kanopolis",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and migratory bird sightings.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the trails can be exposed to direct sun.","Check the official website for current lake levels before planning water activities.","The park is popular on weekends, so arrive early to secure a prime spot."],nearbyAttractions:["Mushroom Rock State Park","Coronado Heights","Smoky Hill Museum","Lindsborg, Kansas (Little Sweden)"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle permit required for all motor vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The reservoir features multiple boat ramps and is highly regarded for its walleye, wiper, and channel catfish populations."},{id:"wilson-state-park",name:"Wilson State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/f825e7fe99b5df23.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the Smoky Hills, Wilson State Park offers stunning views of Wilson Reservoir and rugged sandstone bluffs.",fullDescription:"Wilson State Park is widely considered the most beautiful state park in Kansas. Located on the shores of Wilson Reservoir, the park features dramatic sandstone formations, rolling hills, and diverse ecosystems. It is a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts, offering world-class fishing, boating, and mountain biking trails that wind through the unique landscape of the Smoky Hills region.",thingsToDo:["Boating and water sports","Fishing for striped bass and walleye","Mountain biking on the Switchgrass Trail","Wildlife viewing and bird watching","Camping and picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Switchgrass Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"24 miles",description:"A nationally recognized mountain biking and hiking trail that traverses rugged terrain and offers panoramic views of the reservoir."},{name:"Cedar Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic, shorter loop trail perfect for families that winds through cedar groves and provides access to the shoreline."}],campingInfo:"The park offers over 100 utility campsites with water and electricity, as well as numerous primitive camping sites. Reservations are highly recommended during peak season.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Coyotes","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wilson State Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on trail conditions, fishing regulations, and park permits.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(785) 658-2465"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"3 State Park Rd, Sylvan Grove, KS 67481",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wilson+State+Park+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Wilson",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal bird migration viewing.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the trails can be exposed to direct sun.","Check the official website for current lake levels and boat ramp status.","The Switchgrass Trail is popular with mountain bikers, so hikers should stay alert."],nearbyAttractions:["Wilson Reservoir","Lucas, Kansas (Grassroots Art Capital)","Garden of Eden","Salina, Kansas"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle permit required for all motor vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Wilson Reservoir is famous for its striped bass, walleye, and channel catfish. Multiple boat ramps are available throughout the park."},{id:"little-jerusalem-badlands-state-park",name:"Little Jerusalem Badlands State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-little-jerusalem-badlands-state-park-kansas-by.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Little Jerusalem Badlands State Park features 332 acres of spectacular Niobrara Chalk formations in Logan County, Kansas.",fullDescription:"Little Jerusalem Badlands State Park is Kansas' newest state park, showcasing a massive expanse of Niobrara Chalk formations that date back to the Cretaceous period. The park offers visitors a unique opportunity to explore towering spires and deep canyons that were once part of an ancient inland sea. Managed in partnership with The Nature Conservancy, the park emphasizes conservation while providing accessible trails for public enjoyment.",thingsToDo:["Guided nature hikes","Wildlife photography","Geological exploration","Bird watching","Stargazing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Life on the Rocks Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"This trail winds through the heart of the chalk formations, offering close-up views of the unique geological structures."},{name:"Rim Rock Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 mile",description:"A scenic path along the upper rim that provides panoramic views of the badlands and the surrounding prairie landscape."}],campingInfo:"There is no camping allowed within the boundaries of Little Jerusalem Badlands State Park to protect the fragile ecosystem.",wildlife:["Ferruginous hawks","Swift foxes","Mule deer","Cliff swallows","Rattlesnakes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Little Jerusalem Information Kiosk",info:"Provides maps, safety information, and educational displays about the geology and history of the chalk formations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 8:00 PM",phone:"(785) 671-3300"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"4550 E. Gold Rd, Oakley, KS 67748",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Little+Jerusalem+Badlands+State+Park+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Little-Jerusalem-Badlands",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal hiking conditions.",tips:["Stay on marked trails at all times to protect the fragile chalk formations.","Bring plenty of water and sun protection as there is very little shade in the park.","Check the official website for potential closures due to wet weather, as the chalk becomes slippery and unstable."],nearbyAttractions:["Monument Rocks","Keystone Gallery","Buffalo Bill Cultural Center","Fick Fossil and History Museum"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle permit required.",fishingBoating:"Not applicable; there are no bodies of water suitable for fishing or boating within the park."},{id:"mushroom-rock-state-park",name:"Mushroom Rock State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d00f21f582dc0369.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"One of the smallest state parks in Kansas, featuring unique, giant mushroom-shaped rock formations.",fullDescription:"Mushroom Rock State Park is a 5-acre park located in Ellsworth County, Kansas. It is famous for its large, mushroom-shaped sandstone formations that were once part of the Dakota Formation. These unique geological structures were created by erosion, leaving behind harder rock caps atop softer pedestals. The park offers a glimpse into the prehistoric landscape of the Great Plains and is a popular spot for photography and geology enthusiasts.",thingsToDo:["Geology photography","Picnicking","Nature observation","Hiking short trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mushroom Rock Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.3 miles",description:"A short, flat trail that winds around the primary rock formations, perfect for families and casual walkers."},{name:"Pioneer Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A level, interpretive trail outlining the geological history of the unique Dakota sandstone formations."}],campingInfo:"There is no camping available at Mushroom Rock State Park. The park is strictly for day-use only.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Coyotes","Various songbirds","Raptors"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kanopolis State Park Office",info:"Mushroom Rock is managed by the staff at Kanopolis State Park, who provide information and maintenance for the site.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(785) 546-2565"},hours:"Sunrise to sunset daily",address:"2000-2098 Avenue K, Brookville, KS 67425",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mushroom+Rock+State+Park+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Mushroom-Rock",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal lighting for photography.",tips:["Bring a camera as the rock formations are highly photogenic.","The park is very small, so plan for a 30-60 minute visit.","There is no drinking water on-site, so bring your own supplies."],nearbyAttractions:["Kanopolis State Park","Faris Caves","Horsethief Canyon","Salina, Kansas"],entranceFees:"Free",fishingBoating:"There is no water access or fishing available at this park."},{id:"lake-scott-state-park",name:"Lake Scott State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-lake-scott-state-park-kansas.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"An oasis in the high plains of western Kansas, Lake Scott State Park features dramatic canyons, natural springs, and rich historical significance.",fullDescription:"Lake Scott State Park is a unique geological and historical treasure located in western Kansas. The park is situated in a canyon carved by Ladder Creek, providing a stark contrast to the surrounding flat prairie landscape. It is home to the El Cuartejo Pueblo, the northernmost Indian pueblo in the United States, and served as a hideout for the legendary outlaw Dick Yeager. The park's diverse ecosystem includes limestone bluffs, wooded canyons, and a 100-acre spring-fed lake.",thingsToDo:["Fishing and boating on the 100-acre lake","Exploring the historic El Cuartejo Pueblo ruins","Hiking the scenic canyon trails","Bird watching and wildlife photography","Swimming at the designated beach area"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lake Scott Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through the wooded areas near the lake, offering excellent opportunities to spot local flora and fauna."},{name:"Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail takes hikers along the limestone bluffs, providing panoramic views of the canyon and the surrounding prairie."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 55 utility campsites with water and electricity, as well as numerous primitive camping sites. Reservations are recommended during peak summer months.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bobcat","Great blue heron","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Scott State Park Office",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and fishing permits.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(620) 872-2061"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"101 W Scott Lake Dr, Scott City, KS 67871",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Scott+State+Park+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Lake-Scott",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and migratory bird sightings.",tips:["Bring binoculars for bird watching as the park is a major stop for migratory species.","Visit the El Cuartejo Pueblo ruins early in the morning to avoid the midday heat.","Check the official website for current fire bans before planning a campfire."],nearbyAttractions:["Scott City","Monument Rocks","Little Jerusalem Badlands State Park"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle permit required for all visitors.",fishingBoating:"The lake is well-stocked with bass, bluegill, crappie, and channel catfish. Boat ramps are available for public use."},{id:"clinton-state-park",name:"Clinton State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/02af8047d2e19a37.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Clinton Reservoir, this park offers extensive outdoor recreation including boating, fishing, and miles of scenic trails.",fullDescription:"Clinton State Park is situated on the north shore of the 7,000-acre Clinton Reservoir in Douglas County, Kansas. Established in the 1970s, the park provides a diverse landscape of rolling hills, tallgrass prairie, and hardwood forests. It serves as a premier destination for water sports enthusiasts and hikers alike, featuring a complex trail system that winds through varied terrain and offers stunning views of the lake.",thingsToDo:["Boating and sailing","Fishing for walleye and crappie","Mountain biking on dedicated trails","Camping in wooded areas","Wildlife viewing and birdwatching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"North Shore Trails",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"10 miles",description:"A series of interconnected loops that wind through dense forests and offer challenging terrain for hikers and mountain bikers."},{name:"Prairie Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path through native tallgrass prairie, perfect for observing local wildflowers and bird species."}],campingInfo:"The park features over 200 utility campsites with water and electricity, as well as numerous primitive camping sites and several reservable cabins.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","Red-tailed hawks"],visitorCenter:{name:"Clinton State Park Office",info:"Provides park information, camping permits, and local trail maps.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(785) 842-8562"},hours:"24 hours daily",address:"798 N 1415 Rd, Lawrence, KS 66049",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Clinton+State+Park+Lawrence+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Clinton",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and migratory bird sightings.",tips:["Purchase a Kansas State Park vehicle permit online before arrival to save time.","Check the water levels of the reservoir before planning a boating trip.","Bring insect repellent, especially during the summer months when hiking the wooded trails."],nearbyAttractions:["University of Kansas Natural History Museum","Downtown Lawrence (Mass Street)","Baker University Wetlands","Clinton Lake Marina"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle permit required.",fishingBoating:"The park features multiple boat ramps and a full-service marina nearby. Fishing is excellent for walleye, crappie, channel catfish, and white bass."},{id:"tuttle-creek-state-park",name:"Tuttle Creek State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/ef58447789d0c633.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in the scenic Flint Hills, Tuttle Creek State Park offers expansive water recreation and diverse outdoor activities near Manhattan, Kansas.",fullDescription:"Tuttle Creek State Park is situated on the shores of the 12,500-acre Tuttle Creek Reservoir. The park is divided into several units, including the River Pond, Fancy Creek, and Randolph areas, providing a variety of landscapes from wooded valleys to open prairie. It serves as a premier destination for boating, fishing, and equestrian activities, reflecting the natural beauty of the Kansas Flint Hills.",thingsToDo:["Boating and sailing","Fishing for crappie and catfish","Mountain biking on designated trails","Horseback riding","Swimming at the designated beach","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Fancy Creek Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5.0 miles",description:"A rugged trail that winds through limestone bluffs and offers panoramic views of the reservoir."},{name:"River Pond Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle, scenic path perfect for birdwatching and observing local flora near the water."}],campingInfo:"The park features over 100 utility campsites with water and electricity, as well as numerous primitive camping areas and several rental cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Tuttle Creek State Park Office",info:"The office provides park permits, camping registration, and information on local trail conditions and fishing regulations.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(785) 539-7941"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for registered campers; day use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"5800 River Pond Rd, Manhattan, KS 66502",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tuttle+Creek+State+Park+Manhattan+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Tuttle-Creek",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal bird migration viewing.",tips:["Check the official website for current water levels before planning boating activities.","Bring insect repellent during the summer months, especially when hiking near wooded areas.","Make camping reservations well in advance for holiday weekends as sites fill up quickly."],nearbyAttractions:["Konza Prairie Biological Station","Kansas State University","Flint Hills Discovery Center","Aggieville"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle permit required for all motor vehicles entering the park.",fishingBoating:"The reservoir is a popular destination for anglers targeting crappie, saugeye, and catfish; multiple boat ramps are available throughout the park units."},{id:"milford-state-park",name:"Milford State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/37a4348453dd0703.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Kansas' largest lake, Milford State Park offers expansive water recreation and diverse wildlife habitats.",fullDescription:"Milford State Park is situated on the shores of the 15,730-acre Milford Reservoir. The park features a diverse landscape of rolling hills, tallgrass prairie, and wooded areas. It serves as a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts, offering extensive opportunities for boating, fishing, and wildlife viewing. The area is rich in history and provides a scenic escape for those looking to enjoy the natural beauty of the Flint Hills region.",thingsToDo:["Boating and jet skiing","Fishing for walleye and catfish","Wildlife watching","Camping","Hiking and mountain biking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Eagle Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through wooded areas and offers beautiful views of the reservoir."},{name:"Prairie View Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"An accessible path through native tallgrass prairie, perfect for birdwatching and spotting local flora."}],campingInfo:"The park offers over 100 utility campsites with water and electricity, as well as numerous primitive camping sites. Reservations are recommended during peak season.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Various waterfowl","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Milford State Park Office",info:"Provides park information, camping permits, and local wildlife educational materials.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(785) 238-3014"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"3718 State Park Rd, Milford, KS 66514",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Milford+State+Park+KS",officialWebsite:"https://ksoutdoors.com/State-Parks/Locations/Milford",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn",tips:["Check the water levels before planning a boating trip.","Bring insect repellent during the summer months.","Visit during the winter for excellent bald eagle viewing opportunities."],nearbyAttractions:["Milford Nature Center","Fort Riley","Geary State Fishing Lake","Junction City"],entranceFees:"$5.00 daily vehicle permit required",fishingBoating:"Milford Reservoir is renowned for its excellent fishing, particularly for walleye, channel catfish, and smallmouth bass. Multiple boat ramps are available throughout the park."}],michigan:[{id:"tahquamenon-falls-state-park",name:"Tahquamenon Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/65219a22c22c16cd.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Upper Peninsula park on the Tahquamenon River, known for its 50-foot Upper Falls and the cascading Lower Falls.",fullDescription:"Tahquamenon Falls State Park covers 46,179 acres near Paradise and is Michigan's second-largest state park. The Tahquamenon River drops 50 feet at the Upper Falls, then runs through the rapids and cascades of the Lower Falls before reaching Lake Superior's Whitefish Bay. A paved path leads to the Upper Falls, and rowboats can be rented to reach the Lower Falls.",thingsToDo:["Waterfall viewing","Hiking","Rowboat and canoe rental","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"North Country Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"16 miles",description:"The North Country Trail runs 16 miles within the park, including a route between the Upper and Lower Falls."},{name:"Giant Pines Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"3.8 miles",description:"Part of the park's hiking trail system in the Upper Falls area, this loop is groomed for cross-country skiing in winter."},{name:"Lantern Loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1 mile",description:"A short loop in the Upper Falls area that is part of the hiking trail system and is groomed for cross-country skiing in winter."}],campingInfo:"The park has the 188-site Lower Falls Modern Campground (Hemlock loop open year-round), the 72-site Rivermouth Modern Campground and the Rivermouth Rustic Campground; reserve at 800-447-2757 or online.",fishingBoating:"Anglers on the Tahquamenon River catch brown trout, walleye, muskie, northern pike, yellow perch and smallmouth bass; there is an accessible paved boat launch near Rivermouth Campground, and rowboats can be rented at the Lower Falls from Memorial Day weekend to mid-October as conditions allow.",wildlife:["black bears","otters","porcupines","bald eagles","moose"],visitorCenter:{name:"Tahquamenon Falls State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(906) 492-3415"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"Recreation Passport required for vehicles: $15 for Michigan plates (annual); non-Michigan vehicles $12 daily or $42 annual",address:"41382 W M-123, Paradise, MI 49768",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tahquamenon%20Falls%20State%20Park%20michigan",officialWebsite:"https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/tahquamenonfalls",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring runoff brings the Upper Falls to peak volume, summer through mid-October offers shuttle service and rowboat rentals, and winter draws visitors to see colored ice at the falls and ski or snowshoe the groomed and marked trails.",tips:["Hunter Shuttle Service runs a fee-based shuttle between the Upper and Lower Falls from noon to 8 p.m., on weekends through June 21 and daily June 22 through Labor Day; book online or call or text 906-291-3422.","Drones are permitted with restrictions but are prohibited on or above the viewing platforms.","Parking is limited at the Rivermouth boat launch, and pets must stay on a 6-foot leash on all trails and viewing platforms."],nearbyAttractions:["Great Lakes Shipwreck Museum (Whitefish Point)","Crisp Point Lighthouse","Oswald's Bear Ranch"]},{id:"ludington-state-park",name:"Ludington State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/ludington-state-park-michigan.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Lake Michigan park between the big lake and Hamlin Lake, home to the 1876 Big Sable Point Lighthouse.",fullDescription:"Ludington State Park covers 4,800 acres two miles north of Ludington, between Lake Michigan and Hamlin Lake, crossed by the Big Sable River. It is home to the 112-foot Big Sable Point Lighthouse from 1876. The park has 21.5 miles of hiking trails, ski and canoe trails, and four campgrounds with 360 sites.",thingsToDo:["Beaches and swimming","Tubing the Big Sable River","Lighthouse tours","Hiking","Canoeing","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lighthouse Path",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1.8 miles",description:"This sand-and-gravel path, open to hikers and bikers, leads 1.8 miles one-way to the Big Sable Point Lighthouse and has little shade."},{name:"Lost Lake-Island Trail loop",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This very popular loop connects to the loops of the Beechwood Modern Campground near Hamlin Lake."}],campingInfo:"The park has three campgrounds, including the 147-site Beechwood Modern Campground near Hamlin Lake, plus mini cabins, all reservable at 800-447-2757 or online.",fishingBoating:"Anglers can fish spring and fall runs of salmon, lake trout, steelhead and brown trout along the Lake Michigan shoreline and in the Big Sable River, and a boating access site with trailer parking serves Hamlin Lake.",wildlife:["Piping plover","Salmon","Steelhead","Lake trout","Walleye"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ludington State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(231) 843-2423"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"Recreation Passport required for vehicles: $15 for Michigan plates (annual); non-Michigan vehicles $12 daily or $42 annual",address:"8800 M-116, Ludington, MI 49431",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ludington%20State%20Park%20michigan",officialWebsite:"https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/ludington",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer is best for the Lake Michigan and Hamlin Lake beaches and paddling, while spring and fall bring the salmon and trout runs, and lighthouse tours run May through October.",tips:["The Skyline Trail, all intersecting trails, and the park amphitheater and its access road are closed for reconstruction through mid-2027.","Most of the Lake Michigan shoreline is closed to pets to protect piping plover habitat. Use the pet-friendly beach between the beach house and the Big Sable River, with pets on a 6-foot leash.","The Lighthouse Path offers little shade and can be challenging in warm weather, so plan accordingly. Large vehicles are not recommended on Piney Ridge Road."],nearbyAttractions:["Nordhouse Dunes Wilderness Area","Ludington's North Breakwater Light at Stearns Park Beach","Historic White Pine Village"]},{id:"holland-state-park",name:"Holland State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3af722e2f1753e03.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Michigan's most visited state park: Lake Michigan beach and dunes by the Holland Harbor Light, 'Big Red'.",fullDescription:"Holland State Park covers 142 acres west of Holland, with units on Lake Michigan and Lake Macatawa beside the channel joining them. It draws 1.5 to 2 million visitors a year to its sandy beach, dunes and views of the Holland Harbor Light, known as Big Red. Each unit has a campground; the Lake Macatawa unit also has a boat launch.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Boating","Fishing","Camping","Breakwall walk"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lakeshore Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"20 miles",description:"A paved route that parallels Lakeshore Drive and runs continuously from Grand Haven State Park to Holland State Park."}],campingInfo:"The park has two modern campgrounds, Holland Modern Beach Campground (98 paved sites) and Lake Macatawa Campground (211 sites), plus two camper cabins, all reservable at 800-447-2757 or online.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is popular along the channel walkway on Lake Michigan and Ottawa County's boardwalk on Lake Macatawa, and while there is no boat launch in the park, the Lake Macatawa boating access site with a fish cleaning station is 1 mile east on Ottawa Beach Road.",wildlife:["deer","squirrels","rabbits","bass"],visitorCenter:{name:"Holland State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(616) 399-9390"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"Recreation Passport required for vehicles: $15 for Michigan plates (annual); non-Michigan vehicles $12 daily or $42 annual",address:"2459 Ottawa Beach Road, Holland, Michigan, 49424",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Holland%20State%20Park%20michigan",officialWebsite:"https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/holland",bestTimeToVisit:`Summer is the best time to enjoy the park's sugar sand beaches, buoyed swim areas, seasonal naturalist programs and sunsets over Lake Michigan by the "Big Red" lighthouse.`,tips:["The Woodstock loop of the Lake Macatawa Campground (sites 117-211) will be closed for the majority of the 2027 season for construction of a new toilet and shower building, but sites 1-116 will be open.","Watch the beach flag warning system. Red flags mean the water is unsafe and no one should swim in or enter the water.","Alcohol is prohibited anywhere in the park, and pets are allowed only at the designated dog beach along Lake Macatawa on a 6-foot leash."],nearbyAttractions:['Holland Harbor Light ("Big Red")',"Grand Haven State Park","Saugatuck Dunes State Park"]},{id:"porcupine-mountains-wilderness-state-park",name:"Porcupine Mountains Wilderness State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/90e35136bac0ab5e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Michigan's largest state park, the Porcupine Mountains Wilderness State Park encompasses 60,000 acres of old-growth forest, rugged mountains, and stunning Lake Superior shoreline.",fullDescription:"Known locally as the 'Porkies,' this park is one of the few remaining large wilderness areas in the Midwest. It features towering virgin timber, secluded waterfalls, and the dramatic Escarpment trail overlooking the Lake of the Clouds. The park's geology is defined by ancient volcanic activity and glacial carving, creating a rugged landscape that offers a true backcountry experience.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and backpacking","Downhill and cross-country skiing","Wildlife photography","Fishing in mountain streams","Scenic driving along M-107"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Escarpment Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.3 miles",description:"This iconic trail offers breathtaking panoramic views of the Lake of the Clouds and the Carp River valley from high cliff ridges."},{name:"Presque Isle River Waterfalls Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0-mile loop",description:"A scenic trail that follows the river, showcasing several spectacular waterfalls and unique rock formations carved by the rushing water."}],campingInfo:"The park offers modern campgrounds at Union Bay, rustic cabins, yurts, and extensive backcountry campsites accessible only by hiking.",wildlife:["Black bear","Gray wolf","Bald eagle","Moose","White-tailed deer"],visitorCenter:{name:"Porcupine Mountains Visitor Center",info:"Features educational exhibits on the park's natural history, geology, and local mining heritage, along with ranger assistance.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(906) 885-5275"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for recreational use",address:"33303 Headquarters Rd, Ontonagon, MI 49953",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Porcupine+Mountains+Wilderness+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.michigan.gov/recsearch/parks/porkies",bestTimeToVisit:"Late September to early October for peak fall foliage or July for warm hiking weather.",tips:["Always carry bear spray and practice proper food storage when backpacking.","Check the weather frequently as conditions near Lake Superior can change rapidly.","Visit the Lake of the Clouds overlook early in the morning to avoid large crowds."],nearbyAttractions:["Ontonagon","Copper Harbor","Lake Superior","Ottawa National Forest"],entranceFees:"Requires a Michigan Recreation Passport for vehicle entry.",fishingBoating:"Excellent trout fishing in the Presque Isle and Carp rivers; non-motorized boating is permitted on inland lakes."},{id:"mackinac-island-state-park",name:"Mackinac Island State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e0a3f2858f9aa391.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Established in 1895, Mackinac Island State Park covers over 80 percent of the island and offers breathtaking views of Lake Huron and historic geological formations.",fullDescription:"Mackinac Island State Park was the second national park in the United States, established in 1875, before being transferred to the state of Michigan in 1895. The park is renowned for its unique limestone rock formations, such as Arch Rock, and its strict prohibition of motor vehicles, preserving a turn-of-the-century atmosphere. Visitors can explore dense forests, high bluffs, and miles of trails that showcase the island's rich geological history and strategic military importance during the 18th and 19th centuries.",thingsToDo:["Bicycling the 8.2-mile perimeter road","Visiting historic Fort Mackinac","Hiking the interior forest trails","Photography at Arch Rock","Horse-drawn carriage tours"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Arch Rock Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible path leading to the iconic limestone arch overlooking the turquoise waters of Lake Huron."},{name:"Sugar Loaf Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"This trail winds through the interior of the island, bringing hikers to the base of the massive 75-foot tall Sugar Loaf rock formation."}],campingInfo:"Camping is not permitted within the boundaries of Mackinac Island State Park; visitors typically stay at the island's historic hotels, bed and breakfasts, or private cottages.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Red foxes","White-tailed deer","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mackinac Island State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides maps, historical information, and guidance on park regulations and trail conditions.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(906) 847-3328"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily, year-round",address:"7353 Main St, Mackinac Island, MI 49757",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mackinac+Island+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.mackinacparks.com/attraction/mackinac-island-state-parks/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for pleasant weather and full access to all island amenities.",tips:["Rent a bicycle upon arrival to easily navigate the island's extensive trail 