webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Experience the dramatic beauty of the St. Louis River as it carves through ancient rock formations in this historic Minnesota state park.",fullDescription:"Jay Cooke State Park is renowned for its spectacular scenery, featuring the turbulent St. Louis River flowing through a deep, rocky gorge. Established in 1915, the park preserves a unique geological landscape where the river cuts through tilted slate and greywacke rock. Visitors can explore the iconic swinging bridge, hike through diverse forests, and learn about the park's history as a former fur trade route and the legacy of the Civilian Conservation Corps.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the scenic river trails","Cross-country skiing in winter","Photography of the swinging bridge","Mountain biking on designated trails","Wildlife watching and birding"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Superior Hiking Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"10.0 miles",description:"A segment of the famous long-distance trail that offers rugged terrain and stunning overlooks of the river valley."},{name:"Swinging Bridge Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible path leading to the park's famous suspension bridge, providing excellent views of the river rapids."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 79 drive-in campsites, including 21 with electrical hookups, as well as five camper cabins and a group camp area.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Black bears","Bald eagles","Timber wolves","Ruffed grouse"],visitorCenter:{name:"Jay Cooke State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features interpretive exhibits on the park's geology, history, and natural resources, along with a gift shop and ranger assistance.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(218) 673-7000"},hours:"8:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"780 Highway 210, Carlton, MN 55718",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Jay+Cooke+State+Park+MN",officialWebsite:"https://www.dnr.state.mn.us/state_parks/park.html?id=spk00196",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for high water levels or autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Visit the swinging bridge early in the morning to avoid crowds.","Check the DNR website for trail conditions before heading out, especially after heavy rain.","Bring sturdy hiking boots as the terrain near the river can be rocky and uneven."],nearbyAttractions:["Duluth, MN","Fond du Lac Reservation","Spirit Mountain Recreation Area","Lake Superior"],entranceFees:"$7.00 daily vehicle permit or $35.00 annual permit",fishingBoating:"Fishing is popular along the St. Louis River for smallmouth bass and walleye; however, the river is generally not suitable for motorized boating due to rapids."}],missouri:[{id:"ha-ha-tonka-state-park",name:"Ha Ha Tonka State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d859a260dc81b225.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Lake of the Ozarks park known for the stone ruins of an early-1900s castle, plus caves, sinkholes and bluffs.",fullDescription:"Ha Ha Tonka State Park covers 3,751 acres on the Niangua arm of the Lake of the Ozarks near Camdenton. Its best-known feature is the ruins of Ha Ha Tonka, an early 20th-century stone mansion modeled on European castles. Fifteen miles of trails lead to the castle, caves, sinkholes and natural bridges of this karst landscape.",thingsToDo:["Castle ruins","Hiking","Boating","Fishing","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Castle Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A family-friendly trail leading to the castle ruins and a lake overlook, and one of the park's most popular routes."},{name:"Spring Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"About 0.40 mile of this trail is paved and accessible to visitors with physical limitations, and that section follows the Lake of the Ozarks shoreline."},{name:"Lake View Bend Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A park trail used for the park's 'Wednesday Walkabout' hike event."}],campingInfo:"Camping is limited to a special-use camping area, and the park file lists backcountry camping rules.",fishingBoating:"The park offers fishing, boating and kayaking on the Niangua arm of the Lake of the Ozarks, and kayak rentals are available on-site, but there are no marina slips.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Ha Ha Tonka State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(573) 346-2986"},hours:"Park: 7 a.m. to sunset, daily; Park Office/Visitor Center: 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. daily, April through October",entranceFees:"Free",address:"1491 State Road D, Camdenton, MO 65020-9801",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ha%20Ha%20Tonka%20State%20Park%20missouri",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/ha-ha-tonka-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring through fall (April–October) is a good time to visit, when the park office/visitor center keeps daily hours and the trails, boardwalks, castle-ruins overlooks and lake views are at their most enjoyable.",tips:["Parts of the castle ruins have been closed off because of mortar and stone failure, but you can see the ruins from an observation point across from the park office.","The park office telephone is monitored for messages only from 8 a.m. to 4 p.m., Monday through Friday, though park staff are usually in the park daily.","For an accessible option, about 0.40 mile of the Spring Trail is paved for visitors with physical limitations."],nearbyAttractions:["Lake of the Ozarks"]},{id:"johnson-s-shut-ins-state-park",name:"Johnson's Shut-Ins State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/1778085f021c7569.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Missouri Ozarks park where the East Fork Black River squeezes through hard volcanic rock, forming natural pools.",fullDescription:"Johnson's Shut-Ins State Park covers 9,432 acres on the East Fork Black River in Reynolds County, in the St. Francois Mountains. A 'shut-in' is a spot where hard rock narrows the river, creating chutes and pools. A paved walkway leads to an overlook of the shut-ins, and the Ozark Trail and an equestrian loop pass through.",thingsToDo:["Swimming in the shut-ins","Hiking","Rock climbing","Camping","Horseback riding"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Shut-Ins Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.4 miles",description:"A loop trail that AllTrails lists among the park's popular hikes and calls moderately challenging; a hiker's post notes it begins with a boardwalk portion."},{name:"Goggins Mountain Equestrian Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"10 miles",description:"A ten-mile loop trail in the park, according to Wikipedia."},{name:"Scour Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"St. Louis Magazine lists hiking the Scour Trail among its top things to do at the park."}],campingInfo:"The park has a campground with reservations available online or toll free at 877-ICampMO (877-422-6766), and backcountry camping requires registration.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is available in the park, and smallmouth bass are the commonly caught fish.",wildlife:["Smallmouth bass"],visitorCenter:{name:"Johnson's Shut-Ins State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(573) 546-2450"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"Free (no entrance fee at Missouri state parks)",address:"148 Taum Sauk Trail, Middle Brook, MO 63656-9601",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Johnson's%20Shut-Ins%20State%20Park%20missouri",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/johnsons-shut-ins-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer is the classic time to swim in the shut-ins of the East Fork Black River when water levels are not dangerously high, while spring and fall suit hiking in the rugged St. Francois Mountains.",tips:["Parking at the park is limited, so check the park's Limited Parking Notification before you go.","Rappelling and rock climbing require a permit from the park.","Book campsites well in advance and check river levels before swimming, since the shut-ins are used only when water levels are not dangerously high."],nearbyAttractions:["Taum Sauk Mountain State Park","Elephant Rocks State Park","Bell Mountain Wilderness Area (Mark Twain National Forest)"]},{id:"table-rock-state-park",name:"Table Rock State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/92ac49993b7a20f9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Small Missouri park on Table Rock Lake beside Branson, with a marina and campgrounds.",fullDescription:"Table Rock State Park covers 356 acres on Table Rock Lake along the south side of Branson. Its facilities include a marina, campgrounds, and trails for hiking and bicycling.",thingsToDo:["Boating (marina)","Camping","Hiking","Biking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lake View Bend Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Lake View Bend Trail is a park trail used for the park's 'Wednesday Walkabout' guided hike event."}],campingInfo:"Basic, electric and sewer/electric/water campsites wind along the lakeshore among oak and hickory trees, and visitors can also stay in one of six premium yurts.",fishingBoating:"State Park Marina rents everything from ski boats to paddleboards, and commonly caught fish include bass, sunfish, catfish, crappie, and rainbow and brown trout.",wildlife:["bass","sunfish","catfish","crappie","rainbow trout"],visitorCenter:{name:"Table Rock State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(417) 334-4704"},hours:"6 a.m. to 10 p.m. daily, year-round",entranceFees:"Free",address:"5272 State Highway 165, Branson, MO 65616-8901",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Table%20Rock%20State%20Park%20missouri",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/table-rock-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall is best for the lake-based boating, swimming and fishing the park is known for, while cooler spring and fall days suit hiking and biking.",tips:["The open picnic shelter can be reserved by contacting the park office.","There is no designated beach, but swimming is allowed in several areas of the park.","Maximum boat trailer parking length is 55 feet."],nearbyAttractions:["Branson","Top of the Rock","Dogwood Canyon"]},{id:"castlewood-state-park",name:"Castlewood State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d3ea514b1010468e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled along the Meramec River, Castlewood State Park is a premier destination for mountain biking, hiking, and river recreation near St. Louis.",fullDescription:"Castlewood State Park is renowned for its dramatic limestone bluffs overlooking the Meramec River. Historically a popular resort area in the early 20th century, the park now offers a rugged landscape characterized by deep ravines, lush forests, and extensive trail systems. The geology of the area features exposed Ordovician-age limestone, providing a scenic backdrop for outdoor enthusiasts.",thingsToDo:["Mountain biking on world-class trails","Hiking along the Meramec River bluffs","Fishing for smallmouth bass and catfish","Picnicking in the river valley","Bird watching and wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"River Scene Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.2 miles",description:"This trail winds along the river bluffs, offering spectacular views of the Meramec River and the surrounding valley."},{name:"Stinging Nettle Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A popular loop for hikers and mountain bikers that traverses through dense hardwood forests and rocky terrain."}],campingInfo:"Castlewood State Park does not offer overnight camping or cabin rentals; it is strictly a day-use park.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Red-tailed hawk","Eastern box turtle"],visitorCenter:{name:"Castlewood State Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, trail information, and assistance from park rangers regarding park regulations.",hours:"Monday - Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(636) 227-4433"},hours:"7:00 AM - Sunset daily",address:"1401 Kiefer Creek Rd, Ballwin, MO 63021",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Castlewood+State+Park+Ballwin+MO",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/castlewood-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["The park is extremely popular on weekends; arrive early to secure parking.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as the trails can be rocky and uneven.","Check the official website for trail closure notices during wet weather to protect the trails."],nearbyAttractions:["Lone Elk Park","World Bird Sanctuary","Museum of Transportation","Faust Park"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the Meramec River banks. There is no formal boat ramp for motorized watercraft, but the river is popular for kayaking and canoeing."},{id:"echo-bluff-state-park",name:"Echo Bluff State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8f2b2b63a11ee515.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled along the Current River, Echo Bluff State Park features towering limestone bluffs and pristine natural beauty in the heart of the Ozarks.",fullDescription:"Echo Bluff State Park is a premier destination in the Missouri Ozarks, centered around the dramatic, towering Echo Bluff that rises above the crystal-clear waters of the Current River. The park offers a blend of modern amenities and rugged outdoor experiences, including the historic lodge, extensive trail systems, and access to one of the most scenic rivers in the state. The geology of the area is defined by karst topography, featuring springs, caves, and dramatic rock formations that have been carved by the river over millennia.",thingsToDo:["Float trips on the Current River","Hiking and mountain biking","Fishing for smallmouth bass","Wildlife photography","Stargazing at the amphitheater"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bluff Top Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.1 miles",description:"This trail offers spectacular panoramic views of the Current River valley from the top of the limestone bluffs."},{name:"Siloam Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that winds through lush forest areas, perfect for birdwatching and observing local flora."}],campingInfo:"The park offers full-service campsites with electric and water hookups, as well as primitive tent sites and the modern Betty Lea Lodge for overnight stays.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","River otters","Great blue herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Echo Bluff State Park Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides information on park history, local geology, and current trail conditions, along with a gift shop.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(573) 751-5211"},hours:"7:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"3445 Echo Bluff Drive, Eminence, MO 65466",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Echo+Bluff+State+Park+Eminence+MO",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/echo-bluff-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Book your campsite or lodge room well in advance as they fill up quickly during peak season.","Bring water shoes if you plan on wading or floating in the Current River.","Check the park website for seasonal river level alerts before planning water activities."],nearbyAttractions:["Current River State Park","Ozark National Scenic Riverways","Alley Spring","Eminence, MO"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park; fees apply for camping and lodging.",fishingBoating:"The Current River is a popular destination for fly fishing and smallmouth bass fishing; boat access is available for canoes and kayaks."},{id:"roaring-river-state-park",name:"Roaring River State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-roaring-river-state-park-missouri.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in a deep, narrow valley in the Ozark Mountains, Roaring River State Park is one of Missouri's most popular parks, famous for its trout hatchery and stunning natural springs.",fullDescription:"Roaring River State Park is located in the heart of the Missouri Ozarks. The park is centered around a massive spring that discharges over 20 million gallons of water daily, feeding the Roaring River. The area has a rich history dating back to the 19th century, including remnants of old mills and CCC-era stone structures. The rugged landscape features steep limestone bluffs, lush forests, and diverse ecosystems, making it a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts and anglers alike.",thingsToDo:["Trout fishing","Hiking","Swimming in the pool","Wildlife viewing","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Deer Leap Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short but steep climb that rewards hikers with a breathtaking panoramic view of the valley and the river below."},{name:"Fire Tower Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This challenging trail leads hikers to the historic fire tower site, offering a rigorous workout and dense forest scenery."}],campingInfo:"The park offers multiple campgrounds ranging from basic tent sites to full-hookup RV sites, along with the historic Emory Melton Inn and Conference Center for those preferring lodge accommodations.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Rainbow trout","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Roaring River State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, fishing regulations, and local geology, along with a gift shop.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(417) 847-2539"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"12716 Farm Road 2239, Cassville, MO 65625",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Roaring+River+State+Park+Missouri",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/roaring-river-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild weather and peak fishing conditions.",tips:["Purchase your trout tag at the park store before heading to the water.","Visit during the week to avoid heavy weekend crowds at the spring.","Bring sturdy hiking boots if you plan to tackle the steeper trails like Fire Tower."],nearbyAttractions:["Cassville","Table Rock Lake","Dogwood Canyon Nature Park"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park; fishing requires a daily tag and valid Missouri fishing license.",fishingBoating:"The park is world-renowned for its trout fishing; the river is divided into zones for fly fishing and bait fishing. No private boats are allowed on the spring-fed river."},{id:"sam-a-baker-state-park",name:"Sam A. Baker State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/41dbbda168d6b664.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the St. Francois Mountains, this park offers a serene escape along the St. Francis River with lush forests and historic CCC-built structures.",fullDescription:"Sam A. Baker State Park is one of Missouri's oldest and most beloved state parks. It features the rugged beauty of the St. Francois Mountains and the clear, spring-fed waters of the St. Francis River and Big Creek. The park is renowned for its Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC) history, with many stone and timber structures still in use today. Visitors can explore diverse ecosystems ranging from oak-hickory forests to glades, providing a rich habitat for wildlife and a scenic backdrop for outdoor recreation.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and backpacking","Swimming in the St. Francis River","Fishing for smallmouth bass","Bicycling on park roads","Bird watching and nature photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mudlick Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"11.0 miles",description:"A challenging loop that traverses the rugged terrain of the Mudlick Mountain Natural Area, offering spectacular ridge-top views."},{name:"Shut-ins Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that leads visitors through a unique geological area where the river flows through narrow rock channels."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a variety of camping options including basic, electric, and sewer/electric/water sites. There are also rustic cabins and a modern motel available for reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Pileated woodpeckers","Barred owls","River otters"],visitorCenter:{name:"Sam A. Baker Visitor Center",info:"Features exhibits on the park's natural history, the CCC legacy, and local flora and fauna.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(573) 856-4411"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"5569 State Hwy 143, Patterson, MO 63956",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sam+A.+Baker+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/sam-baker-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers and autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Check the river levels before planning a swimming or floating trip.","Wear sturdy hiking boots if you plan to tackle the Mudlick Trail.","Book campsites well in advance during peak summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Johnson's Shut-Ins State Park","Taum Sauk Mountain State Park","Elephant Rocks State Park","Arcadia Valley"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is popular along the St. Francis River and Big Creek. While there are no boat rentals, visitors are welcome to bring their own canoes or kayaks for paddling."},{id:"onondaga-cave-state-park",name:"Onondaga Cave State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3ac6bb0c37c7e099.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Experience the breathtaking underground beauty of one of Missouri's most spectacular caves, featuring massive stalactites and flowstones.",fullDescription:"Onondaga Cave State Park is a National Natural Landmark that offers visitors a chance to explore a subterranean wonderland. The park is centered around the Onondaga Cave, which features a stunning array of dripstone formations, including stalactites, stalagmites, and rimstone dams. Above ground, the park encompasses the scenic Meramec River, providing opportunities for hiking, fishing, and floating through the lush Ozark landscape.",thingsToDo:["Guided cave tours","Canoeing and kayaking on the Meramec River","Hiking through Ozark forests","Fishing for smallmouth bass","Picnicking in designated areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Vilander Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.25 miles",description:"This trail offers scenic views of the Meramec River valley from high atop the bluffs."},{name:"Deer Run Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through hardwood forests, perfect for spotting local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a variety of camping options, including basic, electric, and sewer/electric/water sites, as well as several rustic camping cabins.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Little brown bats","Red fox"],visitorCenter:{name:"Onondaga Cave Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center serves as the hub for cave tour tickets, educational exhibits on cave geology, and park information.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(573) 245-6576"},hours:"8:00 AM - 8:00 PM daily",address:"7556 Hwy H, Leasburg, MO 65535",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Onondaga+Cave+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://mostateparks.com/park/onondaga-cave-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Book your cave tour tickets well in advance as they sell out quickly during peak season.","Bring a light jacket, as the cave maintains a constant temperature of 57 degrees Fahrenheit year-round.","Wear sturdy, closed-toe shoes for the cave tour as paths can be damp and uneven."],nearbyAttractions:["Meramec State Park","Huzzah Conservation Area","Cuba, Missouri","Steelville, Missouri"],entranceFees:"Free park entry; cave tour fees apply.",fishingBoating:"