he distant Black Hills on clear days."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 22 modern cabins, 52 electrical campsites, and numerous tent sites, along with a group camp facility.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Wild turkeys","Bighorn sheep","Coyotes","Various raptors"],visitorCenter:{name:"Chadron State Park Office",info:"Provides park permits, trail maps, and information on local history and CCC exhibits.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(308) 432-6167"},hours:"Open year-round, 24 hours a day for camping; day use from sunrise to sunset.",address:"15951 US-385, Chadron, NE 69337",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Chadron+State+Park+NE",officialWebsite:"https://outdoornebraska.gov/location/chadron-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal weather and full facility access.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent wildlife viewing, especially at dawn and dusk.","Book cabins well in advance as they are highly popular during summer months.","Check the weather forecast, as Pine Ridge can experience rapid temperature changes."],nearbyAttractions:["Museum of the Fur Trade","Fort Robinson State Park","Chadron","Agate Fossil Beds National Monument"],entranceFees:"A valid Nebraska State Park Entry Permit is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The park features a small pond stocked with trout, perfect for youth fishing and relaxing afternoons."},{id:"fort-robinson-state-park",name:"Fort Robinson State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7bffe1da2d6f9373.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in the scenic Pine Ridge region, Fort Robinson State Park offers a unique blend of Old West history and expansive outdoor recreation.",fullDescription:"Fort Robinson State Park is a historic site that served as a U.S. Army fort from 1874 to 1948. Today, it spans over 22,000 acres of dramatic Pine Ridge escarpments, offering visitors a chance to explore preserved military buildings, museums, and rugged landscapes that once played a pivotal role in the Indian Wars.",thingsToDo:["Guided jeep tours","Horseback riding","Historical museum tours","Trout fishing","Mountain biking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Red Cloud Buttes Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through the iconic Pine Ridge buttes, offering panoramic views of the surrounding valley."},{name:"Soldier Creek Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A gentle path following the creek, perfect for birdwatching and observing local wildlife in a riparian habitat."}],campingInfo:"The park offers extensive lodging options, including historic officer quarters, cabins, and modern RV campsites with full hookups.",wildlife:["Bighorn sheep","Mule deer","Wild turkeys","Elk","Golden eagles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Robinson History Center",info:"Features exhibits on the fort's military history, the Cheyenne Outbreak, and the local geology of the Pine Ridge.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(308) 665-2900"},hours:"Open daily, 24 hours for registered guests; visitor facilities operate seasonally.",address:"3200 W Hwy 20, Crawford, NE 69339",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort+Robinson+State+Park+NE",officialWebsite:"https://outdoornebraska.gov/location/fort-robinson/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal weather and full facility operations.",tips:["Book lodging well in advance as historic cabins fill up quickly during summer months.","Bring binoculars for spotting bighorn sheep on the nearby cliffs.","Check the schedule for the summer rodeo events held at the park arena."],nearbyAttractions:["Agate Fossil Beds National Monument","Chadron State Park","Toadstool Geologic Park","Crawford"],entranceFees:"A valid Nebraska State Park Entry Permit is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The park features several stocked ponds and access to Soldier Creek, which is popular for trout fishing."},{id:"platte-river-state-park",name:"Platte River State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/4f61c1553112d249.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled between Lincoln and Omaha, Platte River State Park offers a scenic retreat with wooded hills, modern cabins, and diverse recreational activities.",fullDescription:"Platte River State Park is situated on the bluffs overlooking the Platte River. The park is well-known for its unique glamping opportunities, modern cabins, and the scenic Jenny Newman Observation Tower, which provides panoramic views of the river valley. The area features a mix of hardwood forests and rolling hills, providing a beautiful backdrop for outdoor enthusiasts interested in hiking, archery, and water activities.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running","Archery and trap shooting","Swimming in the seasonal pool","Fishing in the park ponds","Horseback riding"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Stone Creek Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through the wooded hills and leads to a picturesque waterfall feature."},{name:"Riverview Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail follows the ridge line, offering stunning elevated views of the Platte River valley below."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a variety of lodging options including modern cabins, camper cabins, and glamping tents. There are no traditional tent or RV campsites available for public reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red-tailed hawk","Great horned owl","Coyote"],visitorCenter:{name:"Platte River State Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on trail conditions, activity permits, and cabin check-in services.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(402) 234-2217"},hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 10:00 PM",address:"14421 346th St, Louisville, NE 68037",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Platte+River+State+Park+NE",officialWebsite:"https://outdoornebraska.gov/location/platte-river-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Book cabins well in advance as they are highly popular and fill up quickly.","Bring insect repellent during the summer months due to the wooded terrain.","Check the official website for seasonal activity schedules like the pool and archery range."],nearbyAttractions:["Eugene T. Mahoney State Park","Strategic Air Command & Aerospace Museum","Louisville State Recreation Area","Schramm Park State Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"A valid Nebraska State Park vehicle permit is required for entry.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is available in the park's stocked ponds. No boat ramps are available for the Platte River within the park boundaries."},{id:"ponca-state-park",name:"Ponca State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-ponca-state-park-nebraska-by.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched on the bluffs overlooking the Missouri River, Ponca State Park is a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts in northeast Nebraska.",fullDescription:"Ponca State Park encompasses over 2,400 acres of heavily wooded hills and river bottomlands. It serves as the gateway to the Missouri National Recreational River, offering a unique blend of rugged topography, diverse ecosystems, and rich history related to the Lewis and Clark expedition.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and mountain biking","Missouri River fishing","Educational programs at the Resource and Education Center","Camping and cabin stays","Bird watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Old Oak Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through mature hardwood forests and offers glimpses of the Missouri River valley."},{name:"Bloodroot Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"2.2 miles",description:"A challenging hike featuring significant elevation changes and rewarding panoramic views from the bluffs."}],campingInfo:"The park offers modern campgrounds with electrical hookups, primitive tent sites, and a variety of climate-controlled rental cabins.",wildlife:["Wild turkeys","White-tailed deer","Bald eagles","Red-tailed hawks","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Missouri National Recreational River Resource and Education Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the Missouri River ecosystem, local history, and the Lewis and Clark expedition.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(402) 755-2284"},hours:"Open daily, 24 hours for registered campers; day use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"88090 Spur 26E, Ponca, NE 68770",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ponca+State+Park+NE",officialWebsite:"https://outdoornebraska.gov/location/ponca/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers and fall for the vibrant autumn foliage.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities along the river.","Check the park website for seasonal educational programs and guided hikes.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as the bluff trails can be steep and uneven."],nearbyAttractions:["City of Ponca","Sioux City, Iowa","Lewis and Clark State Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"A valid Nebraska State Park Entry Permit is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Public boat ramp access to the Missouri River is available, providing excellent opportunities for catfish and sauger fishing."},{id:"niobrara-state-park",name:"Niobrara State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/05e11b17ebb4073d.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located at the confluence of the Niobrara and Missouri Rivers, this park offers stunning vistas and diverse outdoor recreation in northeastern Nebraska.",fullDescription:"Niobrara State Park is situated in the scenic bluffs where the Niobrara and Missouri Rivers meet. The park features a unique landscape of rolling hills, dense forests, and open prairies. Historically significant as a crossroads for explorers and indigenous tribes, the park now serves as a premier destination for nature lovers, offering a blend of modern amenities and rugged wilderness experiences.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running","Fishing in the Niobrara and Missouri Rivers","Bird watching and wildlife photography","Swimming in the park pool","Picnicking in designated areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"River Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail winds along the high bluffs providing panoramic views of the river confluence below."},{name:"Prairie Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A gentle path through restored native prairie grasses, perfect for spotting wildflowers and butterflies."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 51 modern campsites with electrical hookups, as well as 19 fully furnished cabins available for rent year-round.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Coyotes","Various migratory waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Niobrara State Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, local information, and assistance with cabin and campsite reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(402) 857-3373"},hours:"Open daily, 24 hours for registered campers; day use from sunrise to sunset.",address:"89261 522 Ave, Niobrara, NE 68760",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Niobrara+State+Park+NE",officialWebsite:"https://outdoornebraska.gov/location/niobrara/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal weather and wildlife viewing.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities near the river.","Book cabins well in advance as they are popular during summer months.","Check the park website for seasonal pool hours before planning your visit."],nearbyAttractions:["Lewis and Clark Lake","Santee Sioux Reservation","Verdigre, Nebraska","Ponca State Park"],entranceFees:"A valid Nebraska Park Entry Permit is required for all vehicles entering the park.",fishingBoating:"The park features boat ramps providing access to both the Niobrara and Missouri Rivers, popular for catfish and walleye fishing."},{id:"smith-falls-state-park",name:"Smith Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/f368121a9037307a.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to Nebraska's tallest waterfall, Smith Falls State Park offers a stunning natural oasis along the Niobrara National Scenic River.",fullDescription:"Smith Falls State Park is a geological gem featuring the tallest waterfall in Nebraska, cascading 63 feet over a ledge of sandstone. The park is situated along the Niobrara National Scenic River, providing a unique microclimate where eastern, western, and northern plant species converge. Visitors can explore the lush canyon, walk across the historic bridge, and enjoy the serene beauty of the Niobrara River valley.",thingsToDo:["Hiking to the waterfall","Canoeing and kayaking on the Niobrara River","Picnicking in the shaded groves","Wildlife photography","Camping under the stars"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Smith Falls Boardwalk",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A scenic, accessible boardwalk that leads visitors directly to the base of the majestic Smith Falls."},{name:"Niobrara River Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A winding trail that follows the riverbank, offering excellent views of the canyon walls and local flora."}],campingInfo:"The park offers tent camping sites with fire rings and picnic tables, as well as limited RV sites. Reservations are highly recommended during peak summer months.",wildlife:["Wild turkeys","White-tailed deer","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","Beavers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Smith Falls Park Office",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and assistance with camping permits.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(402) 376-1306"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"90155 Smith Falls Rd, Valentine, NE 69201",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Smith+Falls+State+Park+NE",officialWebsite:"https://outdoornebraska.gov/smithfalls/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal weather and river activities.",tips:["Bring water shoes for wading near the waterfall base.","Book your campsite well in advance as they fill up quickly during summer weekends.","Pack insect repellent, especially if visiting during the humid summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Valentine National Wildlife Refuge","Fort Niobrara National Wildlife Refuge","Merritt Reservoir State Recreation Area","City of Valentine"],entranceFees:"$6.00 daily vehicle permit for Nebraska residents; $12.00 for non-residents.",fishingBoating:"The Niobrara River is popular for canoeing, kayaking, and tubing. Fishing is permitted in the river with a valid Nebraska fishing license."},{id:"indian-cave-state-park",name:"Indian Cave State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e28d6fbdd1a46099.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled on the banks of the Missouri River, this park is famous for its massive sandstone cave and stunning hardwood forest vistas.",fullDescription:"Indian Cave State Park encompasses over 3,000 acres of rugged, forested terrain along the Missouri River. The park is named for the large sandstone cave featuring prehistoric petroglyphs carved into the walls. Visitors can explore the diverse ecosystem of the Loess Hills, which provides a unique landscape of steep ridges and deep ravines, offering some of the most scenic hiking and camping opportunities in Nebraska.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and backpacking","Horseback riding","Camping","Fishing","Bird watching","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Indian Cave Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short but steep trail that leads visitors directly to the historic sandstone cave and its ancient petroglyphs."},{name:"Hardwood Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"3.2 miles",description:"A challenging loop through dense oak-hickory forests that offers panoramic views of the Missouri River valley."}],campingInfo:"The park offers extensive camping facilities including modern RV sites with electrical hookups, primitive tent sites, and several rustic cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Red-tailed hawks","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Indian Cave State Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, trail information, and educational displays regarding the history of the cave and local geology.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(402) 883-2575"},hours:"Open daily from 8:00 AM to 10:00 PM",address:"65296 720A Rd, Shubert, NE 68437",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Indian+Cave+State+Park+NE",officialWebsite:"https://outdoornebraska.gov/location/indian-cave/",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn is the best time to visit to witness the spectacular fall foliage across the Loess Hills.",tips:["Bring insect repellent, especially during the summer months when ticks and mosquitoes are active.","Check the official website for seasonal road closures due to heavy rain or flooding.","Visit during the early morning hours for the best chance to spot wild turkeys and deer."],nearbyAttractions:["Brownville State Recreation Area","Auburn, Nebraska","Peru State College","Missouri River"],entranceFees:"A valid Nebraska State Park entry permit is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The park features access to the Missouri River for fishing and non-motorized boating, though no formal boat ramp is available within the park boundaries."}],"north dakota":[{id:"fort-abraham-lincoln-state-park",name:"Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/a74c76d719a4c5e2.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"North Dakota park near Mandan with a rebuilt Mandan earth-lodge village and Custer's reconstructed house.",fullDescription:"Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park lies 7 miles south of Mandan on the Missouri River. It holds the reconstructed On-A-Slant Mandan village, inhabited from the late 1500s to about 1781, and rebuilt military buildings of the fort, including the Custer House and blockhouses.",thingsToDo:["Custer House tours","On-A-Slant Indian Village","Hiking","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Scouts Trail System",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"8.8 miles",description:"A non-motorized, multi-use series of loops and connecting trails, mostly hard-packed single-track with manageable grades and only a few steeper sections, open to hiking, snowshoeing, horseback riding and mountain biking."},{name:"Little Soldier Loop Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1.8 miles",description:"Starting at the Valley picnic shelter and meeting the Young Hawk Interpretive Trail, this segment offers excellent vistas of On-A-Slant Village, the Missouri and Heart rivers and the city of Bismarck."},{name:"Young Hawk Interpretive Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1.1 miles",description:"This trail starts and ends at the Valley picnic shelter and has numbered posts keyed to a trail guide, available at the Visitor Center, describing the area's history, plants and wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park has 51 modern campsites, 15 modern group campsites, 15 primitive campsites, 1 primitive group tent campsite, 4 primitive horse campsites and two seasonal camping cabins along the Missouri River, with campsites bookable 95 days in advance online or at 800-807-4723.",fishingBoating:"Anglers can fish along the shorelines of both the Missouri and Heart Rivers, and the park lists a kayak launch among its amenities.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Abraham Lincoln State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(701) 667-6340"},hours:"7 a.m. to 10 p.m. daily, year-round",entranceFees:"$7 per vehicle per day; $35 annual vehicle permit",address:"4480 Fort Lincoln Rd, Mandan, ND 58554",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort%20Abraham%20Lincoln%20State%20Park%20north%20dakota",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/fort-abraham-lincoln-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer is ideal, since the Custer House living history tours and On-A-Slant Village tours run Memorial Day weekend through Labor Day, while October brings the Haunted Fort event.",tips:["Tours of the Custer House and On-A-Slant Village require an Interpretive Tour Pass ($8 adult, $5 student K-12), sold at the Visitor Center or Commissary. Tours operate Memorial Day weekend through Labor Day, and large groups should call 701-667-6340 in advance.","Campsites can be booked 95 days in advance online or by calling 800-807-4723. Camping cabins require a two-night minimum, do not allow pets and do not provide bedding or towels.","Equestrians need a horse pass ($6/horse daily or $30/horse annual) in addition to the park entrance fee, and may ride only on trails designated for horse use."],nearbyAttractions:["Mandan-Fort Lincoln Bike Trail","Five Nations Arts (former Northern Pacific Railway station, Main Street, Mandan)"]},{id:"lake-metigoshe-state-park",name:"Lake Metigoshe State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-lake-metigoshe-state-park-north-dakota.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Turtle Mountains lake park on the Canadian border near Bottineau, for water sports in summer and snow sports in winter.",fullDescription:"Lake Metigoshe State Park occupies about 1,500 acres on Lake Metigoshe in the Turtle Mountains, 14 miles northeast of Bottineau, next to Manitoba's Turtle Mountain Provincial Park. The lake has northern pike, walleye and perch; winter brings snowmobiling, cross-country skiing and ice fishing.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Canoeing and sailing","Fishing","Camping","Snowmobiling and cross-country skiing","Ice fishing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Old Oak Nature Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"3 miles",description:"North Dakota's first National Recreation Trail, this self-guided interpretive hiking trail was built by the Youth Conservation Corps in 1974 and takes about two hours to hike."},{name:"Lake Metigoshe Cub Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.4 miles",description:"AllTrails describes it as an easy hike with 9 feet of elevation gain that takes about 0.5 to 1 hour to complete."},{name:"Hemerick Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This trail runs through the wooded Turtle Mountains, and the park's White Pelican cabin is located along it."}],campingInfo:"The park has 84 modern campsites with electricity and water ($25-30 per night), 39 primitive campsites ($15 per night) and 3 modern group campsites, plus cabins, a yurt and group dorms, with reservations available online or by calling 800-807-4723.",fishingBoating:"The park has a boat ramp, four fishing docks, and canoe, kayak, paddleboard and pontoon rentals, and Lake Metigoshe is fished for northern pike, walleye and perch, including ice fishing in winter.",wildlife:["Elk","Northern pike","Walleye","Perch"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Metigoshe State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(701) 263-4651"},hours:"7 a.m. to 10 p.m. daily, year-round",entranceFees:"$7 per vehicle per day; $35 annual vehicle permit",address:"2 Lake Metigoshe State Pk, Bottineau, ND 58318",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake%20Metigoshe%20State%20Park%20north%20dakota",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/lake-metigoshe-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Lake Metigoshe is a year-round destination: summer suits paddling, swimming and fishing, while winter brings cross-country skiing, snowshoeing, sledding and ice fishing.",tips:["Campsites can be booked 95 days in advance, either online or by calling 800-807-4723.","The backcountry Osprey Cabin is reached only by a 2-mile hike or a 1.5-mile paddle and short portage. Water must be packed in, and bedding and towels are not provided at any park cabin.","A free all-terrain wheelchair is available for visitors with mobility challenges. Reserve it in advance by calling 701-263-4651."],nearbyAttractions:["Turtle Mountain Provincial Park","International Peace Garden","Bottineau Winter Park"]},{id:"cross-ranch-state-park",name:"Cross Ranch State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-cross-ranch-state-park-north-dakota.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Missouri River park in North Dakota with cottonwood bottomlands, next to a nature preserve with a bison herd.",fullDescription:"Cross Ranch State Park covers 569 acres on the west bank of the Missouri River nine miles south of Washburn. It borders The Nature Conservancy's 6,000-acre Cross Ranch Nature Preserve, home to a herd of over 200 bison. Together they offer 16 miles of trails, and the park has campsites, cabins and yurts.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Kayaking and canoeing","Cross-country skiing","Camping, cabins and yurts"],hikingTrails:[{name:'Matah "River" Trail',difficulty:"Not listed",length:"3.1 miles",description:"Starting at the Visitor Center, this loop circles the park's main use areas, with interpretive signs along the river describing the area's history, wildlife and geology."},{name:'Ma-ak-oti "Old Village" Trail',difficulty:"Not listed",length:"3.4 miles",description:"This two-section trail winds through prairie bluffs and wooded channels with vistas of the Missouri River and includes steep stairs without handrails on portions."},{name:"TNC Self-Guided Prairie Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail crosses preserve prairie and wooded draws within the Nature Conservancy bison pasture, with a guidebook at the trailhead covering wildlife, plants and history."}],campingInfo:"The park has two campgrounds with 35 standard electric sites ($25-30 per night) and 22 primitive sites ($15 per night), plus cabins and yurts, and campsites can be booked 95 days in advance online or at 800-807-4723.",fishingBoating:"A boat ramp provides access to a free-flowing stretch of the Missouri River where anglers find walleye, trout, catfish, salmon, pike and bass, and the park rents canoes and kayaks and offers a nine-mile shuttle run from Washburn to the Sanger boat landing.",wildlife:["Bison","Walleye","Catfish","Northern pike","Trout"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cross Ranch State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(701) 794-3731"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"$7 per vehicle per day; $35 annual vehicle permit",address:"1403 River Road, Center, ND 58530",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cross%20Ranch%20State%20Park%20north%20dakota",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/cross-ranch-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"The park is open year-round: warmer months suit hiking, biking and paddling the Missouri River, while winter brings groomed cross-country ski trails and snowshoeing.",tips:["Bison are present on the TNC Self-Guided Prairie Trail, so keep a safe distance and remember they are wild animals.","The Ma-ak-oti Trail has steep stairs without handrails, and some areas may be muddy or impassable due to seasonal water fluctuations.","All vehicles entering a state park, including those of overnight guests, need a valid daily or annual vehicle entrance permit."],nearbyAttractions:["Cross Ranch Nature Preserve","Washburn (start of the park's nine-mile Missouri River kayak/canoe run)"]},{id:"icelandic-state-park",name:"Icelandic State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/fd32a0b5670aeabb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Lake Renwick, Icelandic State Park offers a serene escape with rich cultural history and beautiful woodland trails.",fullDescription:"Icelandic State Park is situated on the north shore of Lake Renwick in Cavalier County. The park preserves the heritage of early Icelandic settlers in the region and features the Gunlogson State Nature Preserve, which protects a unique riparian forest. Visitors can explore the Pioneer Heritage Center, which houses exhibits on local history, and enjoy the diverse ecosystems ranging from prairie to mature hardwood forests.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the beach","Fishing for northern pike and walleye","Exploring the Pioneer Heritage Center","Bird watching in the nature preserve","Picnicking in shaded areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Gunlogson Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the lush hardwood forest of the Gunlogson State Nature Preserve."},{name:"Lake Renwick Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail follows the shoreline of Lake Renwick, offering excellent views of the water and local waterfowl."}],campingInfo:"The park offers modern campsites with electrical hookups, as well as primitive tent sites and a group camping area.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Great blue herons","Red foxes","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pioneer Heritage Center",info:"Features historical exhibits, artifacts from Icelandic settlers, and information about the local natural environment.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(701) 265-4561"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"13561 ND-5, Cavalier, ND 58220",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Icelandic+State+Park+North+Dakota",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/icelandic-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal weather and bird migration.",tips:["Bring insect repellent during the summer months as the wooded areas can have mosquitoes.","Visit the Pioneer Heritage Center early in your trip to understand the historical context of the area.","Check the park website for seasonal swimming beach closures or water quality updates."],nearbyAttractions:["Cavalier","Pembina Gorge State Recreation Area","Walhalla"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle daily entrance fee",fishingBoating:"The park features a boat ramp on Lake Renwick and is a popular spot for shore fishing and non-motorized boating."},{id:"grahams-island-state-park",name:"Grahams Island State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-grahams-island-state-park-north-dakota-lake.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Devils Lake, Grahams Island State Park is a premier destination for world-class fishing and outdoor recreation in North Dakota.",fullDescription:"Grahams Island State Park is situated on a large island in Devils Lake, connected to the mainland by a causeway. The park is renowned for its exceptional perch and walleye fishing, attracting anglers from across the country. The landscape features rolling hills, wooded areas, and expansive views of the lake, providing a serene environment for camping, hiking, and birdwatching.",thingsToDo:["Fishing","Boating","Camping","Hiking","Birdwatching","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Woodland Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path winding through the wooded areas of the island, perfect for spotting local wildlife."},{name:"Lakeside Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail offers scenic views of Devils Lake and follows the shoreline for much of the journey."}],campingInfo:"The park offers modern campsites with electrical hookups, as well as primitive tent sites. Cabin rentals are also available for those seeking a more comfortable stay.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Pelicans","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Grahams Island Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on fishing regulations, trail maps, and camping reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(701) 766-4015"},hours:"Open 24 hours for registered campers; day use from 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM",address:"152 S Grahams Island Rd, Devils Lake, ND 58301",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Grahams+Island+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/grahams-island-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal fishing and camping conditions.",tips:["Check current lake levels before arrival as they can fluctuate.","Bring insect repellent during the summer months.","Book campsites well in advance, especially for summer weekends."],nearbyAttractions:["White Horse Hill National Game Preserve","City of Devils Lake","Sullys Hill National Game Preserve"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle daily entrance fee.",fishingBoating:"The park features a high-quality boat ramp and fish cleaning station, making it a top-tier location for boaters and anglers."},{id:"turtle-river-state-park",name:"Turtle River State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c76280dfc53fa7f0.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the scenic Turtle River valley, this park offers a lush, wooded escape featuring diverse recreational activities and historic Civilian Conservation Corps structures.",fullDescription:"Turtle River State Park is located in the scenic Turtle River valley, providing a unique landscape of wooded hills and river bottomlands that contrast with the surrounding flat plains of the Red River Valley. Developed in the 1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC), the park retains much of its historic charm through its rustic log buildings and stone bridges. The park is a popular destination for outdoor enthusiasts seeking hiking, mountain biking, and winter sports, while also serving as a peaceful retreat for families and nature lovers.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and mountain biking","Cross-country skiing and sledding","Fishing in the Turtle River","Picnicking in historic shelters","Camping in wooded sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Woodland Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path winding through the dense forest canopy, perfect for birdwatching and spotting local wildlife."},{name:"River Loop Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail follows the meandering path of the Turtle River, offering scenic views of the water and the historic CCC-built stone structures."}],campingInfo:"The park offers modern campsites with electrical hookups, as well as primitive tent sites. Several rustic cabins are available for rent, providing a cozy experience in the heart of the woods.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red foxes","Various songbirds","Great horned owls"],visitorCenter:{name:"Turtle River State Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on trail conditions, camping reservations, and historical exhibits regarding the CCC era.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(701) 343-9099"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"3084 Park Ave, Arvilla, ND 58214",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Turtle+River+State+Park+Arvilla+ND",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/turtle-river-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for hiking, or winter for cross-country skiing.",tips:["Bring insect repellent during the summer months as the wooded areas can be buggy.","Visit during the autumn to see the vibrant fall foliage along the river valley.","Check the official website for seasonal road conditions before planning a winter visit."],nearbyAttractions:["Grand Forks","University of North Dakota","Larimore Dam Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle daily entrance fee",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Turtle River, which is stocked with trout. No motorized boats are allowed due to the shallow nature of the river."},{id:"lake-sakakawea-state-park",name:"Lake Sakakawea State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/fbc24a11ece78146.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of one of the largest man-made reservoirs in the United States, this park is a premier destination for water recreation and outdoor adventure.",fullDescription:"Lake Sakakawea State Park is situated at the south end of the massive Lake Sakakawea, created by the Garrison Dam. The park offers expansive views of the reservoir and serves as a gateway to the North Country National Scenic Trail. Its geology is defined by the rolling prairie hills and the vast, deep waters of the Missouri River system, providing a unique habitat for diverse wildlife and a playground for boating enthusiasts.",thingsToDo:["Boating and sailing","Fishing for walleye and northern pike","Hiking the North Country National Scenic Trail","Camping in modern or primitive sites","Picnicking with lake views"],hikingTrails:[{name:"North Country National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5.0 miles",description:"A scenic segment of the national trail that winds through prairie grasses and offers elevated views of the lake."},{name:"Park Perimeter Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that circles the main recreation area, perfect for families and bird watching."}],campingInfo:"The park features over 200 campsites, including modern sites with full electrical and water hookups, as well as primitive tent sites and several rental cabins.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Pelicans","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Sakakawea State Park Office",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and assistance with camping reservations.",hours:"Monday - Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(701) 487-3315"},hours:"Open daily from 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM",address:"6921 24th St NW, Pick City, ND 58545",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Sakakawea+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/lake-sakakawea-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water temperatures and hiking conditions.",tips:["Bring plenty of sunscreen as there is limited shade in the open prairie areas.","Check the wind forecast before heading out on the water, as the lake can get choppy.","Make camping reservations well in advance during the peak summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Garrison Dam","Knife River Indian Villages National Historic Site","Fort Stevenson State Park","Beulah, ND"],entranceFees:"$7.00 daily vehicle permit required for entry.",fishingBoating:"The park features a world-class marina with multiple boat ramps, fuel docks, and fish cleaning stations."},{id:"fort-stevenson-state-park",name:"Fort Stevenson State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-fort-stevenson-state-park-north-dakota.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Lake Sakakawea, this park offers premier fishing, boating, and a rich history centered around the 19th-century military fort.",fullDescription:"Fort Stevenson State Park is situated on the north shore of Lake Sakakawea, one of the largest man-made reservoirs in the United States. The park is named after the historic military post established in 1867 to protect the overland route between Fort Totten and Fort Buford. Today, visitors can explore the interpretive center which details the history of the fort and the Missouri River valley. The park is a hub for outdoor recreation, featuring extensive marina facilities, modern camping, and miles of trails that wind through the rolling prairie landscape.",thingsToDo:["Boating and fishing on Lake Sakakawea","Exploring the Interpretive Center","Bird watching and wildlife photography","Camping in modern sites or cabins","Cross-country skiing in winter"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Deapolis Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that offers beautiful views of the lake and opportunities to spot local prairie wildlife."},{name:"Fort Stevenson Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.8-mile loop",description:"This trail winds through the historic areas of the park, providing educational markers about the original fort site."}],campingInfo:"The park offers over 100 campsites with electrical and water hookups, as well as several modern camping cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Red foxes","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Stevenson Interpretive Center",info:"Features exhibits on the history of the military fort, the Missouri River, and the construction of the Garrison Dam.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(701) 337-5576"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"1252A 41st Ave NW, Garrison, ND 58540",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort+Stevenson+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.parkrec.nd.gov/fort-stevenson-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for water activities and pleasant hiking weather.",tips:["Check the lake water levels before planning a boating trip as they can fluctuate.","Bring insect repellent during the summer months, especially near the shoreline.","Visit the interpretive center early in your stay to better understand the historical significance of the area."],nearbyAttractions:["Garrison Dam","Lake Sakakawea State Park","City of Garrison","Audubon National Wildlife Refuge"],entranceFees:"$7.00 per vehicle daily entrance fee",fishingBoating:"The park features a full-service marina, boat ramps, and is widely considered one of the best walleye fishing spots in North Dakota."}],ohio:[{id:"hocking-hills-state-park",name:"Hocking Hills State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/415b7e811ad60a43.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Hocking Hills State Park is a world-renowned destination featuring dramatic sandstone cliffs, deep gorges, and cascading waterfalls in southeastern Ohio.",fullDescription:"Hocking Hills State Park is defined by its unique Black Hand Sandstone geology, which has been carved over millennia by water and ice into spectacular caves, recess shelters, and gorges. The park encompasses several distinct areas including Old Man's Cave, Ash Cave, and Cedar Falls, each offering a unique glimpse into the region's rich natural history and diverse forest ecosystems.",thingsToDo:["Hiking through deep gorges","Photography of waterfalls","Bird watching","Picnicking in designated areas","Educational nature programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Old Man's Cave to Cedar Falls",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"This scenic trail follows the gorge floor, passing through tunnels and past impressive rock formations and waterfalls."},{name:"Ash Cave Gorge Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A flat, accessible path leading to the massive recess cave and the tallest waterfall in the park."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with over 150 electric sites, full-hookup sites, and primitive sites, along with several camper cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Barred owls","Red foxes","Various salamander species"],visitorCenter:{name:"Hocking Hills State Park Visitor Center",info:"The center features interactive exhibits on the park's geology, history, and wildlife, along with a gift shop and information desk.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(740) 385-6842"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"19852 State Route 664 S, Logan, OH 43138",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hocking+Hills+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/family-getaway/state-parks/hocking-hills-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for waterfalls or Autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Visit on weekdays to avoid heavy weekend crowds.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as trails can be slippery near waterfalls.","Download offline maps as cell service is limited in the deep gorges."],nearbyAttractions:["Lake Logan State Park","Conkle's Hollow State Nature Preserve","Rock House","Logan, Ohio"],entranceFees:"Admission to Hocking Hills State Park is free for all visitors.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in designated areas of the park, though boating is not available within the main gorge areas."},{id:"john-bryan-state-park",name:"John Bryan State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3e461b19b86970a9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"John Bryan State Park features a remarkable limestone gorge carved by the Little Miami River, offering stunning scenery and diverse recreational opportunities.",fullDescription:"John Bryan State Park is a 752-acre park located in Greene County, Ohio. It is widely considered the most scenic state park in western Ohio, characterized by the dramatic Clifton Gorge, which is a National Natural Landmark. The park's geology is defined by massive limestone cliffs and the rushing waters of the Little Miami State and National Scenic River. The area was once home to the Miami people and later became a site for early industrial mills. Today, it provides a lush habitat for diverse flora and fauna, including rare wildflowers and towering hardwood trees.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain Biking","Picnicking","Rock Climbing","Wildlife Watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"North Rim Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail follows the top of the gorge, providing spectacular elevated views of the Little Miami River below."},{name:"South Gorge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.8 miles",description:"A scenic path that winds along the riverbank, allowing hikers to get up close to the limestone rock formations."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a campground with 100 sites, including both electric and non-electric options, suitable for tents and RVs. Amenities include showers, flush toilets, and a dump station.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red-tailed hawk","Great blue heron","Eastern chipmunk"],visitorCenter:{name:"John Bryan State Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, trail information, and educational materials about the gorge's geology.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(937) 767-1274"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"3790 State Route 370, Yellow Springs, OH 45387",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=John+Bryan+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/family-getaway/state-parks/john-bryan-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Visit during the spring to see the spectacular display of trillium and other wildflowers.","The gorge can be slippery; wear sturdy hiking boots with good traction.","Combine your visit with a trip to the adjacent Clifton Gorge State Nature Preserve for more trails."],nearbyAttractions:["Clifton Gorge State Nature Preserve","Glen Helen Nature Preserve","Village of Yellow Springs","Young's Jersey Dairy"],entranceFees:"Admission to John Bryan State Park is free.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Little Miami River, which is known for smallmouth bass and rock bass. No boat ramps are available due to the shallow, rocky nature of the river."},{id:"maumee-bay-state-park",name:"Maumee Bay State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-maumee-bay-state-park-ohio.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Lake Erie, Maumee Bay State Park offers a unique blend of recreational facilities and diverse natural habitats.",fullDescription:"Maumee Bay State Park is situated on the shores of Lake Erie, featuring 1,336 acres of diverse landscape including marshes, meadows, and woodlands. The park is a premier destination for birdwatchers, as it sits along a major migratory path. The area was once part of the Great Black Swamp, and today it provides essential habitat for wildlife while offering visitors modern amenities like a resort lodge, golf course, and sandy beaches.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the Lake Erie beach","Birdwatching along the boardwalk","Golfing at the 18-hole links-style course","Hiking through coastal wetlands","Camping in modern facilities"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trautman Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A scenic boardwalk trail that winds through marshes and woodlands, offering excellent opportunities to spot migratory birds."},{name:"Mouse Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A flat, multi-use trail that traverses through meadows and forest edges, perfect for a leisurely walk or bike ride."}],campingInfo:"The park features a modern campground with 256 sites, all equipped with electrical hookups, showers, flush toilets, and laundry facilities.",wildlife:["Bald Eagles","Great Blue Herons","White-tailed Deer","Red Fox","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Nature Center",info:"The Nature Center features interactive exhibits about the Great Black Swamp, local wildlife, and the ecology of Lake Erie.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(419) 836-7758"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"1400 State Park Rd, Oregon, OH 43616",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Maumee+Bay+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/maumee-bay-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for bird migration, Summer for beach activities.",tips:["Bring binoculars as the park is a world-renowned birding destination.","Check the Lake Erie water quality reports online before planning a swim.","Visit during the weekday to avoid crowds at the beach and golf course."],nearbyAttractions:["Toledo Zoo","National Museum of the Great Lakes","Toledo Museum of Art","Ottawa National Wildlife Refuge"],entranceFees:"Free admission to the park; fees apply for camping, golf, and lodge rentals.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the inland lake and along the Lake Erie shoreline. A boat ramp is available for access to Lake Erie."},{id:"mohican-state-park",name:"Mohican State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d0ed09efe224ecff.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the heart of Ohio's scenic Mohican River valley, this park offers breathtaking views, rugged gorges, and lush forests.",fullDescription:"Mohican State Park is a premier destination in Ohio, renowned for its dramatic landscape carved by the Clear Fork of the Mohican River. The park features steep, hemlock-lined gorges and expansive hardwood forests that provide a stunning backdrop for outdoor recreation. Historically significant, the area was once home to the Delaware Indians and later became a hub for early settlers. Today, it is celebrated for its diverse ecosystem, including rare plant species and abundant wildlife, making it a favorite for nature enthusiasts and adventure seekers alike.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and mountain biking","Canoeing and kayaking on the Mohican River","Fishing for trout and bass","Picnicking in designated areas","Wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Gorge Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.0-mile loop",description:"This trail offers spectacular views of the deep gorge and the river below, winding through beautiful hemlock groves."},{name:"Hemlock Gorge Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A scenic path that follows the riverbank, providing excellent opportunities to observe the park's unique geological formations."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large, modern campground with over 100 sites, including full hookups, electric-only sites, and primitive tent sites, along with seasonal cabin rentals.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Great blue herons","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mohican State Park Nature Center",info:"The nature center provides educational exhibits on local flora, fauna, and the geological history of the Mohican River valley.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(419) 994-5125"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"3116 State Route 3, Loudonville, OH 44842",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mohican+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/mohican-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for the vibrant fall foliage or spring for blooming wildflowers.",tips:["Visit during the weekdays to avoid heavy weekend crowds.","Bring waterproof footwear if you plan on exploring the creek beds.","Check the official website for seasonal river water levels before planning a kayaking trip."],nearbyAttractions:["Mohican State Scenic River","Loudonville","Malabar Farm State Park","Pleasant Hill Lake"],entranceFees:"There is no daily vehicle entrance fee for Mohican State Park.",fishingBoating:"The Mohican River is a popular destination for trout fishing, and the park provides access points for canoes and kayaks."},{id:"geneva-state-park",name:"Geneva State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/21d0cf45512ca2eb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Lake Erie, Geneva State Park offers beautiful beaches, extensive trails, and a full-service marina.",fullDescription:"Geneva State Park is a premier destination on the shores of Lake Erie in Ashtabula County. The park features a diverse landscape of meadows, woodlands, and sandy beaches. Historically, the area was shaped by the glacial activity that formed the Great Lakes, and today it serves as a vital recreational hub for boating, fishing, and birdwatching. The park is well-known for its expansive marina and its proximity to the Geneva-on-the-Lake resort area.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the public beach","Boating and fishing on Lake Erie","Hiking the woodland trails","Picnicking in designated areas","Cross-country skiing in winter"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Breakwater Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 mile",description:"A flat, scenic path that offers excellent views of the Lake Erie shoreline and the marina."},{name:"Woodland Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail winds through the park's forested areas, providing opportunities to spot local wildlife and enjoy the changing seasons."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with 93 full-service sites offering electric, water, and sewer hookups, as well as primitive sites and cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Bald eagle","Red fox"],visitorCenter:{name:"Geneva State Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on trail conditions, camping reservations, and local area attractions.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(440) 466-8400"},hours:"6:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"4499 Padanarum Rd, Geneva, OH 44041",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Geneva+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/family-getaways/state-parks/geneva-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer for swimming and boating, or autumn for vibrant foliage.",tips:["Visit the nearby Geneva-on-the-Lake 'Strip' for classic summer entertainment.","Check the Lake Erie water quality reports online before planning a swim.","Bring binoculars as the park is a great spot for migratory bird watching."],nearbyAttractions:["Geneva-on-the-Lake","Ashtabula Maritime Museum","Covered Bridge Tour of Ashtabula County","Ferrante Winery"],entranceFees:"Admission to Geneva State Park is free.",fishingBoating:"The park features a world-class marina with boat slips, launch ramps, and excellent fishing opportunities for walleye, perch, and smallmouth bass."},{id:"salt-fork-state-park",name:"Salt Fork State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/669c90f71f449dc1.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Ohio's largest state park, Salt Fork offers a massive reservoir, rugged hills, and the historic Stone House.",fullDescription:"Salt Fork State Park is Ohio's largest state park, encompassing 17,229 acres of lush forests, open meadows, and the expansive 2,952-acre Salt Fork Lake. The park is renowned for its diverse topography, which includes deep valleys and rolling hills that provide a scenic backdrop for outdoor recreation. Historically, the area is home to the Kennedy Stone House, a structure built in 1837 that stands as a testament to the early settlers of the region. The park's geology is characterized by sandstone formations and rich coal deposits, which have shaped the landscape over millions of years.",thingsToDo:["Boating and fishing on Salt Fork Lake","Golfing at the 18-hole championship course","Hiking through diverse forest trails","Swimming at the large public beach","Wildlife viewing and birdwatching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Stone House Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.4 miles",description:"A scenic trail that leads hikers to the historic Kennedy Stone House, offering views of the lake and surrounding woodlands."},{name:"Morgan Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path perfect for families, winding through mature hardwood forests and providing excellent opportunities for spotting local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park features a massive campground with over 600 sites, including full-hookup RV sites, electric-only sites, and primitive camping. Cabins are also available for rent near the lodge.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Red foxes","Great blue herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Salt Fork State Park Office",info:"Provides maps, park information, and assistance with camping reservations and recreational permits.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(740) 439-3521"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for registered campers; day-use areas open from 6:00 AM to 11:00 PM.",address:"14755 Cadiz Rd, Lore City, OH 43755",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Salt+Fork+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/salt-fork-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for water activities and fall foliage viewing.",tips:["Visit the Kennedy Stone House early in the day to avoid crowds.","Bring binoculars as the park is a prime location for bald eagle sightings.","Check the official website for lake water quality alerts before planning a swimming trip."],nearbyAttractions:["Cambridge Glass Museum","The Wilds Safari Park","Seneca Lake","Downtown Cambridge"],entranceFees:"Admission to Salt Fork State Park is free for all visitors.",fishingBoating:"The park features multiple boat launch ramps and a full-service marina. Fishing is popular for largemouth bass, crappie, and walleye."},{id:"marblehead-lighthouse-state-park",name:"Marblehead Lighthouse State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/98de3f4551d3a227.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Marblehead Lighthouse is one of the most iconic and photographed landmarks in Ohio, offering stunning views of Lake Erie and the surrounding islands.",fullDescription:"Established in 1821, the Marblehead Lighthouse is the oldest lighthouse in continuous operation on the U.S. side of the Great Lakes. The park features a limestone lighthouse tower, a keeper's house museum, and expansive grounds that provide panoramic views of Sandusky Bay and Kelleys Island. The site is listed on the National Register of Historic Places and serves as a vital piece of maritime history for the region.",thingsToDo:["Tour the historic lighthouse tower","Explore the Keeper's House Museum","Picnicking on the lakefront grounds","Photography of the lighthouse and lake","Bird watching along the shoreline"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lighthouse Grounds Walk",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A scenic paved path that winds around the lighthouse grounds and along the rocky shoreline of Lake Erie."},{name:"Marblehead Peninsula Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A gentle trail connecting the park to nearby natural areas, offering great views of the limestone cliffs."}],campingInfo:"There is no camping available at Marblehead Lighthouse State Park. Nearby private campgrounds are available in the Marblehead area.",wildlife:["Herring Gulls","Double-crested Cormorants","Monarch Butterflies","White-tailed Deer"],visitorCenter:{name:"Marblehead Lighthouse Keeper's House",info:"The museum features exhibits on the history of the lighthouse, the life of the keepers, and the maritime heritage of Lake Erie.",hours:"Daily: 12:00 PM - 4:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(419) 734-4424"},hours:"Grounds open daily from sunrise to sunset.",address:"110 Lighthouse Dr, Marblehead, OH 43440",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Marblehead+Lighthouse+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/marblehead-lighthouse-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and lighthouse tour availability.",tips:["Visit during sunset for the most spectacular photography opportunities.","Check the official website for lighthouse tower tour schedules as they are seasonal.","Wear comfortable walking shoes to navigate the rocky shoreline areas."],nearbyAttractions:["Cedar Point Amusement Park","Kelleys Island","East Harbor State Park","Put-in-Bay"],entranceFees:"Admission to the park grounds is free. A small fee is charged for lighthouse tower tours.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the shoreline, but there are no boat ramps or docks located within the state park boundaries."},{id:"kelleys-island-state-park",name:"Kelleys Island State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/afe55c7eec72eb1e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the north shore of Kelleys Island in Lake Erie, this park offers stunning glacial grooves and beautiful limestone beaches.",fullDescription:"Kelleys Island State Park is a 677-acre park that showcases the unique geological history of the Lake Erie islands. The park is most famous for the Glacial Grooves, which are the largest and most accessible examples of glacial scouring in the world. Visitors can explore the rugged limestone shoreline, enjoy expansive views of the lake, and learn about the island's rich history of quarrying and winemaking.",thingsToDo:["View the National Natural Landmark Glacial Grooves","Swimming at the public beach","Kayaking and canoeing along the shoreline","Bird watching during migration seasons","Fishing for smallmouth bass and walleye"],hikingTrails:[{name:"North Shore Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds along the northern coastline, offering excellent views of the lake and access to rocky beaches."},{name:"Glacial Grooves Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible path that leads directly to the massive, ancient glacial grooves carved into the limestone bedrock."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with 127 electric sites and 40 non-electric sites, including amenities like showers, flush toilets, and a dump station.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Migratory songbirds","White-tailed deer","Garter snakes","Painted turtles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Kelleys Island State Park Office",info:"Provides information on park programs, maps, and educational displays regarding the island's geology.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(419) 746-2546"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"920 Division St, Kelleys Island, OH 43438",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Kelleys+Island+State+Park+OH",officialWebsite:"https://ohiodnr.gov/go-and-do/plan-a-visit/find-a-property/kelleys-island-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal weather and ferry accessibility.",tips:["Check the Kelleys Island Ferry Boat schedule in advance as it is the primary way to reach the island.","Bring a bicycle or rent one on the island to easily navigate between the park and the village.","Visit the Glacial Grooves early in the morning to avoid crowds during peak summer weekends."],nearbyAttractions:["Inscription Rock Petroglyphs","Kelleys Island Brewery","East Quarry Trail","South Bass Island"],entranceFees:"Free admission to the park; camping fees vary by site type and season.",fishingBoating:"The park offers a boat launch ramp and excellent fishing access along the northern shoreline of Lake Erie."}],"south dakota":[{id:"custer-state-park",name:"Custer State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/91ebea21649fed00.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Spanning 71,000 acres in the Black Hills, Custer State Park is world-renowned for its free-roaming bison herd and stunning granite spires.",fullDescription:"Established in 1919, Custer State Park is South Dakota's first and largest state park. It features a diverse landscape ranging from rolling prairie grasslands to rugged mountain terrain. The park is famous for its 'Begging Burros' and the annual Buffalo Roundup, where thousands of bison are gathered. Visitors can explore the Needles Highway, a scenic drive through towering granite formations, and Iron Mountain Road, known for its unique pigtail bridges and tunnels framing Mount Rushmore.",thingsToDo:["Wildlife viewing along the Wildlife Loop Road","Scenic driving on Needles Highway","Hiking the Black Elk Peak trail","Swimming and kayaking at Sylvan Lake","Fishing in mountain streams and lakes"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Black Elk Peak Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7.1 miles",description:"The highest point in South Dakota, offering panoramic views of the Black Hills and a historic fire tower at the summit."},{name:"Sunday Gulch Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through narrow granite canyons and lush forest near Sylvan Lake."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 13 campgrounds with over 350 sites, including tent sites, RV hookups, and several historic lodges and cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["American Bison","Pronghorn Antelope","Elk","Bighorn Sheep","Mountain Goats","Wild Turkeys"],visitorCenter:{name:"Peter Norbeck Visitor Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the park's history, geology, and the legacy of Peter Norbeck, along with ranger-led programs.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(605) 394-2693"},hours:"Open 24 hours a day, year-round.",address:"13329 US Highway 16A, Custer, SD 57730",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Custer+State+Park+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/custer-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn, specifically September for the Buffalo Roundup.",tips:["Stay inside your vehicle when bison are near the road.","Purchase your park entrance license online in advance to save time at the gate.","Visit Sylvan Lake early in the morning to secure parking during peak summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Mount Rushmore National Memorial","Wind Cave National Park","Crazy Horse Memorial","Jewel Cave National Monument"],entranceFees:"$20.00 per vehicle for a 7-day temporary entrance license.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in several lakes including Sylvan, Stockade, and Center Lake. Boat rentals are available seasonally at Sylvan Lake."},{id:"bear-butte-state-park",name:"Bear Butte State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/00ffc1a254e773f3.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Bear Butte is a geological marvel and a sacred site to many Native American tribes, offering a unique blend of spiritual significance and rugged hiking.",fullDescription:"Bear Butte is an intrusive igneous laccolith that rises 1,250 feet above the surrounding plains. Formed millions of years ago, this geological landmark is a place of deep spiritual importance for many Plains Indian tribes, who visit to conduct prayer ceremonies and leave offerings. The park provides a unique opportunity to experience the high plains ecosystem while respecting the cultural heritage of the site.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the Summit Trail","Wildlife viewing","Photography","Cultural education","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Summit Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"3.5 miles round trip",description:"A steep, challenging climb that rewards hikers with panoramic views of the Black Hills and the surrounding prairie."},{name:"Centennial Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds around the base of the butte, offering excellent opportunities to spot local wildlife and wildflowers."}],campingInfo:"The park features a campground with 15 sites, including some with electrical hookups, suitable for tents and small RVs. Reservations are highly recommended during peak season.",wildlife:["Bison","Mule deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Coyotes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bear Butte Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides information on the geological history of the butte and the cultural significance of the site to Native American tribes.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(605) 347-5240"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"20241 Hwy 79, Sturgis, SD 57785",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bear+Butte+State+Park+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/bear-butte-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for the best weather and trail conditions.",tips:["Respect the prayer cloths and offerings left by Native American visitors; do not touch or remove them.","Bring plenty of water, as the Summit Trail is exposed and can get very hot in the summer.","Check the weather forecast before hiking, as high winds are common at the summit."],nearbyAttractions:["Sturgis","Deadwood","Black Hills National Forest","Mount Rushmore National Memorial"],entranceFees:"$8.00 daily vehicle entrance fee or valid South Dakota State Park annual pass.",fishingBoating:"There is no fishing or boating available at Bear Butte State Park."},{id:"palisades-state-park",name:"Palisades State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8b37401cd0aef193.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Palisades State Park is famous for its dramatic Sioux Quartzite rock formations that tower above the rushing waters of Split Rock Creek.",fullDescription:"Located near Garretson, South Dakota, Palisades State Park features unique geological formations known as Sioux Quartzite. These pink-hued rock spires rise up to 50 feet above the creek, creating a stunning landscape that has been carved over millions of years. The park is a popular destination for rock climbers, photographers, and nature enthusiasts looking to explore the rugged beauty of the Big Sioux River valley.",thingsToDo:["Rock climbing","Hiking","Fishing","Camping","Photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Split Rock Creek Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that follows the edge of the creek, offering excellent views of the quartzite cliffs."},{name:"King and Queen Rock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A short but rugged path that leads to the park's most iconic rock formations."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 37 campsites, including several with electrical hookups, as well as six camping cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Great blue herons","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Palisades State Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on trail conditions, climbing permits, and local history.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(605) 773-6245"},hours:"Open daily, 24 hours for registered campers; day-use areas open from 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM.",address:"25495 485th Ave, Garretson, SD 57030",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Palisades+State+Park+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/palisades-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal hiking and climbing conditions.",tips:["Bring sturdy hiking boots as the terrain near the cliffs can be uneven and rocky.","Check the official website for rock climbing regulations and safety requirements before arrival.","Visit during the golden hour to capture the best light on the pink quartzite rock formations."],nearbyAttractions:["Devil's Gulch","Jesse James Bridge","Sioux Falls","Big Sioux Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"A daily park entrance license is $8.00 per vehicle or $36.00 for an annual pass.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted along the banks of Split Rock Creek, which is known for smallmouth bass and channel catfish."},{id:"newton-hills-state-park",name:"Newton Hills State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-newton-hills-state-park-south-dakota.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in a lush, wooded valley, Newton Hills State Park offers a unique escape with its dense forests, rolling hills, and abundant wildlife in southeastern South Dakota.",fullDescription:"Newton Hills State Park is a geological and ecological gem located in the Coteau des Prairies. The park is characterized by its deep, wooded ravines and diverse hardwood forests, which stand in stark contrast to the surrounding prairie landscape. Historically, the area served as a refuge for early settlers and was a significant site for local indigenous tribes. Today, it provides a sanctuary for hikers, birdwatchers, and campers looking to explore the unique flora and fauna of the Big Sioux River valley.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running","Bird watching","Camping","Cross-country skiing","Horseback riding"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Woodland Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through the dense hardwood forest, offering excellent opportunities to spot deer and songbirds."},{name:"Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"This trail climbs to the higher elevations of the park, providing panoramic views of the surrounding valley and the Big Sioux River basin."}],campingInfo:"The park features modern campgrounds with electrical hookups, as well as primitive tent sites and several camping cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red foxes","Great horned owls","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Newton Hills Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on trail conditions, local history, and educational programs for visitors.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(605) 987-2263"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"28767 482nd Ave, Canton, SD 57013",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Newton+Hills+State+Park+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/newton-hills-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for wildflowers or autumn for the vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Bring insect repellent, as the wooded areas can have mosquitoes during summer months.","Visit during the fall to witness the spectacular change of colors in the hardwood forest.","Check the official website for seasonal hunting regulations, as some areas may be restricted."],nearbyAttractions:["Canton, SD","Falls Park in Sioux Falls","Good Earth State Park","Big Sioux River"],entranceFees:"A South Dakota State Park entrance license is required for all vehicles, costing $8.00 per day or $36.00 for an annual pass.",fishingBoating:"While the park does not have a large lake, there is access to small ponds for fishing, and the nearby Big Sioux River offers opportunities for canoeing and kayaking."},{id:"sica-hollow-state-park",name:"Sica Hollow State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-sica-hollow-state-park-south-dakota-off.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Sica Hollow State Park is a unique, geologically significant area known for its lush, wooded ravines and rich Native American history.",fullDescription:"Sica Hollow State Park, located in the Coteau des Prairies, is a National Natural Landmark. The park features a deep, wooded ravine carved by glacial activity, offering a stark contrast to the surrounding prairie landscape. The area is steeped in Dakota Sioux history and folklore, with the name 'Sica' meaning 'bad' in the Dakota language, referring to the challenging terrain and mysterious atmosphere of the hollow.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Horseback riding","Bird watching","Photography","Nature study"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trail of the Spirits",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.1 miles",description:"A scenic loop trail that winds through the dense forest and crosses small streams, offering a glimpse into the park's unique ecosystem."},{name:"Sica Hollow Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"8.0 miles",description:"A longer, more rugged trail that traverses the length of the hollow, providing extensive views of the diverse flora and fauna."}],campingInfo:"There are no developed campgrounds within Sica Hollow State Park; however, primitive camping is allowed in designated areas with a permit.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red foxes","Various songbirds","Raccoons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Sica Hollow Information Kiosk",info:"Provides maps, trail information, and historical context about the park's significance.",hours:"Open daily: Sunrise to Sunset",phone:"(605) 448-5701"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"11455 463rd Ave, Sisseton, SD 57262",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sica+Hollow+State+Park+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/sica-hollow-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for wildflowers or autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Wear sturdy hiking boots as the trails can be muddy and uneven.","Bring insect repellent, especially during the summer months.","Check the official website for any seasonal trail closures due to weather."],nearbyAttractions:["Fort Sisseton Historic State Park","Pickerel Lake Recreation Area","Sisseton, SD"],entranceFees:"A valid South Dakota State Park entrance license is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not a primary activity at Sica Hollow; however, nearby lakes like Pickerel Lake offer excellent fishing and boating opportunities."},{id:"angostura-recreation-area",name:"Angostura Recreation Area",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-angostura-recreation-area-south-dakota.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Angostura Recreation Area features 36 miles of shoreline and sandy beaches, making it a premier destination for water sports and camping in the Black Hills region.",fullDescription:"Located in the southern Black Hills, Angostura Recreation Area surrounds a massive reservoir created by the Angostura Dam on the Cheyenne River. The park is renowned for its clear, clean water and sandy beaches, which are rare in the region. The area offers a diverse landscape ranging from open prairie to rugged hills, providing a habitat for a variety of wildlife and excellent opportunities for outdoor recreation including boating, fishing, and hiking.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at sandy beaches","Boating and water skiing","Fishing for walleye and smallmouth bass","Camping in modern campgrounds","Hiking the scenic trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cheyenne River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A relatively flat trail that follows the shoreline, offering excellent views of the reservoir and local birdlife."},{name:"Angostura Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"This trail winds through the hills surrounding the lake, providing elevated vantage points and a more rugged hiking experience."}],campingInfo:"The park offers several campgrounds including modern sites with electrical hookups, tent-only sites, and camping cabins available for reservation.",wildlife:["Mule deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Coyotes","Various waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Angostura Park Office",info:"The park office provides information on park regulations, fishing licenses, and local trail maps.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(605) 773-2886"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping; day use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"12250 Hwy 18 Bypass, Hot Springs, SD 57747",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Angostura+Recreation+Area+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/angostura-recreation-area/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water activities.",tips:["Book campsites well in advance during summer months as they fill up quickly.","Bring plenty of sunscreen and water, as shade is limited on the beaches.","Check the South Dakota GFP website for current water levels and boat ramp conditions."],nearbyAttractions:["Wind Cave National Park","Mammoth Site of Hot Springs","Black Hills Wild Horse Sanctuary","Evans Plunge Mineral Springs"],entranceFees:"A daily park entrance license is required for all vehicles, currently $8.00 per vehicle or $36.00 for an annual park entrance license.",fishingBoating:"The reservoir is a popular fishing spot for walleye, smallmouth bass, and northern pike. Multiple boat ramps are available throughout the recreation area."},{id:"lewis-and-clark-recreation-area",name:"Lewis and Clark Recreation Area",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/32a5b78c0e6cd76b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Lewis and Clark Lake, this expansive recreation area offers premier water sports, camping, and scenic views along the Missouri River.",fullDescription:"Lewis and Clark Recreation Area is one of South Dakota's most popular outdoor destinations, situated along the massive Lewis and Clark Lake created by Gavins Point Dam. The park features diverse landscapes ranging from sandy beaches to wooded bluffs, providing a rich habitat for wildlife and a variety of recreational opportunities. It serves as a hub for boating, fishing, and camping, with a history deeply tied to the Lewis and Clark Expedition that passed through this region in the early 19th century.",thingsToDo:["Boating and jet skiing","Swimming at designated beaches","Fishing for walleye and bass","Disc golf","Biking on paved trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Gavins Point Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path winding through wooded areas with excellent opportunities for bird watching."},{name:"Lewis and Clark Lake Shoreline Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.2 miles",description:"A scenic trail following the lakefront, offering panoramic views of the water and surrounding bluffs."}],campingInfo:"The park features multiple modern campgrounds with electrical hookups, showers, flush toilets, and reservable sites for both tents and RVs.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Pelicans","Great blue herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lewis and Clark Visitor Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the history of the Lewis and Clark Expedition and the construction of Gavins Point Dam.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(605) 668-2985"},hours:"Open 24 hours for registered campers; day-use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"43346 SD-52, Yankton, SD 57078",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lewis+and+Clark+Recreation+Area+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/lewis-and-clark-recreation-area/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water activities and camping.",tips:["Book campsites well in advance during summer months as they fill up quickly.","Bring insect repellent if visiting during the peak of summer.","Check the official website for current water safety alerts and beach conditions."],nearbyAttractions:["Yankton, SD","Gavins Point Dam","Meridian Bridge","Pierson Ranch Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"A daily park entrance license is required, typically $8.00 per vehicle or $36.00 for an annual pass.",fishingBoating:"The park offers multiple boat ramps and extensive shoreline access for fishing, with the lake being a renowned destination for walleye and smallmouth bass."},{id:"hartford-beach-state-park",name:"Hartford Beach State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2f59fdd99e4d5d8a.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled on the shores of Big Stone Lake, this park offers a serene escape with lush woodlands, historic sites, and excellent water recreation.",fullDescription:"Hartford Beach State Park is located on the shores of Big Stone Lake in northeastern South Dakota. The park is known for its beautiful wooded areas, which provide a stark contrast to the surrounding prairie landscape. Historically, the area was a site for Native American encampments and later served as a steamboat landing. Today, visitors can explore the unique geology of the glacial lake valley, enjoy birdwatching, or relax on the sandy beaches.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Boating and fishing","Hiking","Birdwatching","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Woodland Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path winding through the park's dense hardwood forest, perfect for spotting local wildlife."},{name:"Lakeview Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"Offers stunning panoramic views of Big Stone Lake and the surrounding glacial valley."}],campingInfo:"The park features modern campsites with electrical hookups, as well as tent-only sites and a camping cabin available for reservation.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Bald eagles","Various waterfowl","Red foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Hartford Beach Park Office",info:"Provides park information, maps, and assistance with camping reservations and local area inquiries.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(605) 432-6374"},hours:"Open year-round, 24 hours a day for camping; day use areas open from sunrise to sunset.",address:"13691 Hartford Beach Rd, Corona, SD 57227",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Hartford+Beach+State+Park+SD",officialWebsite:"https://gfp.sd.gov/parks/detail/hartford-beach-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for optimal water activities and foliage.",tips:["Bring insect repellent during the summer months as the wooded areas can be buggy.","Book campsites well in advance during peak summer weekends as they fill up quickly.","Check the water conditions at the boat ramp before heading out for a day on Big Stone Lake."],nearbyAttractions:["Big Stone Lake State Park (Minnesota side)","City of Milbank","Grant County Historical Society Museum"],entranceFees:"A South Dakota State Park entrance license is required for all vehicles, costing $8.00 per day or $36.00 for an annual pass.",fishingBoating:"The park features a boat ramp providing access to Big Stone Lake, which is renowned for walleye, northern pike, and perch fishing."}],wisconsin:[{id:"devil-s-lake-state-park",name:"Devil's Lake State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0c90673d003b2425.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Wisconsin's largest state park, known for 500-foot quartzite bluffs around glacier-formed Devil's Lake.",fullDescription:"Devil's Lake State Park covers 9,217 acres in the Baraboo Range just south of Baraboo, about 35 miles northwest of Madison. It is known for 500-foot quartzite bluffs around the 360-acre Devil's Lake, formed about 12,000 years ago when glacial moraines plugged both ends of a gap in the bluffs. The Ice Age Trail passes through.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the bluffs","Rock climbing","Swimming","Boating","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Devil's Lake via West Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.8 miles",description:"This loop near Baraboo, which AllTrails rates moderately challenging, takes an average of about 2 hours 27 minutes to complete."},{name:"Ice Age National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Devil's Lake State Park is situated along the Ice Age National Scenic Trail."}],campingInfo:"Camping is available at Devil's Lake State Park, and the official DNR page links to a 'Camping at Devil's Lake' information page.",fishingBoating:"The park's 360-acre lake offers paddling opportunities and a North Shore boat launch, which will be unavailable at times during the 2026-2027 construction projects.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Devil's Lake State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(608) 356-8301"},hours:"6 a.m. to 11 p.m. year-round",entranceFees:"Vehicle admission sticker required; daily pass $13 (Wisconsin plates) / $16 (out-of-state)",address:"S5975 Park Rd, Baraboo, WI 53913",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Devil's%20Lake%20State%20Park%20wisconsin",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/devilslake",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through fall is prime time for bluff hikes, swimming and paddling, but weekdays or early mornings are best because parking lots often fill before 9 a.m. on May–October weekends and holidays.",tips:["From May through October, parking lots often fill before 9 a.m. on weekends and holidays, and park access may be temporarily restricted until congestion lessens, so arrive early or visit on a weekday.","Facility improvement projects began in 2026, and North Shore parking lots and the boat launch will be unavailable at times. South Shore facilities remain open.","The park is upgrading its wayfinding maps and signs, so maps on the property may not match. Check the current map before hiking."],nearbyAttractions:["Al Ringling Theatre","Balanced Rock Winery","Devil's Head Resort"]},{id:"peninsula-state-park",name:"Peninsula State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/72570ea65e1afc05.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Door County park with eight miles of Green Bay shoreline, limestone bluffs and the Eagle Bluff Lighthouse.",fullDescription:"Peninsula State Park covers 3,776 acres in Door County near Fish Creek, with eight miles of Green Bay shoreline, and draws about a million visitors a year. Established in 1909, it is Wisconsin's third-largest state park. It includes the Eagle Bluff Lighthouse, a golf course, Nicolet Bay beach and the outdoor Northern Sky Theater.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Biking","Eagle Bluff Lighthouse","Golf","Swimming at Nicolet Bay","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Eagle Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"2 miles",description:"A 2-mile loop that can be challenging because of seasonal ice and rocky, uneven conditions."},{name:"Hemlock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.8 miles",description:"A 1.8-mile one-way trail that the Wisconsin DNR rates as moderate."},{name:"Sunset Bike Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"5.1 miles",description:"A paved and gravel trail through the park's hardwoods and marshes that gives bikers and hikers a safe, scenic alternative to Shore Road."}],campingInfo:"The park offers over 460 campsites and three group camps.",fishingBoating:"The DNR lists boating, canoeing, kayaking and winter ice fishing among the park's recreation, and visitors note a boat landing and a canoe/kayak launch.",wildlife:["Deer"],visitorCenter:{name:"Peninsula State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(920) 868-3258"},hours:"6 a.m. to 11 p.m. year-round",entranceFees:"A vehicle admission sticker is required",address:"9462 Shore Rd, Fish Creek, WI 54212",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Peninsula%20State%20Park%20wisconsin",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/peninsula",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer is ideal for the sand beach, the evening Northern Sky Theater musicals and full facilities; the park is open year-round, but some features may not be accessible outside peak season.",tips:["Buy a vehicle admission sticker before entering, because Wisconsin charges an extra $5 fee to anyone who fails to pay admission before using the park.","Wear sturdy footwear on Eagle Trail, which has rocky, uneven footing year-round and can be icy in season.","Touring Eagle Bluff Lighthouse requires park admission plus a donation."],nearbyAttractions:["Ephraim","Kangaroo Lake","Baileys Harbor"]},{id:"copper-falls-state-park",name:"Copper Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/58b955fbb224d752.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Northern Wisconsin park where the Bad River and Tylers Forks drop over waterfalls in a rocky gorge.",fullDescription:"Copper Falls State Park in Ashland County protects a stretch of the Bad River and its tributary Tylers Forks, which run through a gorge and over several waterfalls. Created in 1929, it was developed by the Civilian Conservation Corps. Its 17 miles of trails include part of the North Country National Scenic Trail, and Loon Lake allows swimming and paddling.",thingsToDo:["Waterfall hiking","Swimming and paddling on Loon Lake","Trout fishing","Biking","Cross-country skiing","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Doughboy's Nature Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1.7 miles",description:"This trail runs along the Bad River, and the official park page calls it one of the best hikes in Wisconsin."},{name:"North Country National Scenic Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A portion of this national scenic trail is part of the park's 17-mile trail network."}],campingInfo:"The park offers camping, including a campground with 24 electric sites and a camper cabin accessible to persons with disabilities.",fishingBoating:"Loon Lake offers fishing, kayaking, canoeing and boating with electric motors only, and trout fishing is found on the Bad River and Tylers Forks.",wildlife:["trout"],visitorCenter:{name:"Copper Falls State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(715) 274-5123"},hours:"6 a.m. to 11 p.m. year-round",entranceFees:"A vehicle admission sticker is required",address:"36764 Copper Falls Rd, Mellen, WI 54546",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Copper%20Falls%20State%20Park%20wisconsin",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/copperfalls",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through fall is ideal for viewing the waterfalls, hiking the gorge trails and swimming at Loon Lake, while winter brings cross-country skiing and snowshoeing on the park's trails.",tips:["A Wisconsin DNR vehicle admission sticker is required to enter the park.","For an easy option, the 0.5-mile universal trail leads to an overlook of Copper Falls and crosses a historic CCC bridge over the Tyler Fork Cascades.","Campsites can be registered and paid for at the park office, or reserved online or by phone at 1-888-947-2757."],nearbyAttractions:["Penokee-Gogebic Mineral Range"]},{id:"wyalusing-state-park",name:"Wyalusing State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/371d15ad98c2b148.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched high above the confluence of the Wisconsin and Mississippi Rivers, Wyalusing State Park offers some of the most spectacular blufftop views in the Midwest.",fullDescription:"Wyalusing State Park is one of Wisconsin's oldest state parks, located at the junction of the Wisconsin and Mississippi Rivers. The park features 500-foot bluffs, ancient Native American burial mounds, and a diverse landscape of hardwood forests and prairies. Its unique geography provides a sanctuary for wildlife and a historic window into the region's geological past.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Camping","Bird watching","Canoeing","Stargazing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Old Wagon Road Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.8 miles",description:"A historic trail that winds through the forest, offering glimpses of the river valley below."},{name:"Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail follows the edge of the bluffs, providing breathtaking panoramic views of the river confluence."}],campingInfo:"The park features two main campgrounds, Wisconsin Ridge and Homestead, offering over 100 campsites with some electrical hookups and modern shower facilities.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Wild turkeys","White-tailed deer","Pileated woodpeckers","Red foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wyalusing State Park Nature Center",info:"Features educational exhibits on the park's geology, history, and local flora and fauna.",hours:"Seasonal: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(608) 996-2261"},hours:"6:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"13081 State Park Ln, Bagley, WI 53801",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wyalusing+State+Park+WI",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/wyalusing",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for wildflowers or autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching, especially during eagle migration.","Visit the Sentinel Ridge area to see the ancient Native American burial mounds.","Book campsites well in advance as they fill up quickly during summer weekends."],nearbyAttractions:["Prairie du Chien","Effigy Mounds National Monument","Villa Louis Historic Site"],entranceFees:"A Wisconsin State Park vehicle admission sticker is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Boat launch access is available at the base of the bluffs on the Mississippi River, with excellent fishing for walleye, bass, and panfish."},{id:"high-cliff-state-park",name:"High Cliff State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/bfc84b0ec4cd3f7e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched atop the Niagara Escarpment, High Cliff State Park offers breathtaking panoramic views of Lake Winnebago and rich historical significance.",fullDescription:"High Cliff State Park is the only state-owned recreational area on the shores of Lake Winnebago. The park is named for the limestone cliffs of the Niagara Escarpment, which run through the property. Visitors can explore ancient effigy mounds built by Native Americans, hike through diverse hardwood forests, and enjoy the expansive marina facilities. The park's geology is unique, featuring exposed Silurian dolomite cliffs that provide a dramatic backdrop for outdoor recreation.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running","Boating and fishing on Lake Winnebago","Picnicking in designated areas","Exploring historical effigy mounds","Cross-country skiing in winter"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Red Bird Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"This trail winds along the top of the Niagara Escarpment, offering spectacular views of Lake Winnebago and passing by several historical effigy mounds."},{name:"Forest Management Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A gentle loop through the park's wooded areas, perfect for birdwatching and observing local flora."}],campingInfo:"The park features a family campground with 112 sites, including some with electrical hookups, as well as a group camping area and a remote hike-in site.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red foxes","Various migratory songbirds","Great blue herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"High Cliff State Park Office",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and educational displays regarding the Niagara Escarpment and local history.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(920) 989-1106"},hours:"6:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"N7630 State Park Rd, Sherwood, WI 54169",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=High+Cliff+State+Park+Sherwood+WI",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/highcliff",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for hiking and boating, or winter for snowshoeing.",tips:["Visit the observation tower for the best panoramic views of the lake.","Bring binoculars to spot eagles and other raptors along the cliff edge.","Make camping reservations well in advance as sites fill up quickly during summer weekends."],nearbyAttractions:["Appleton","Neenah","Menasha","Fox Cities Exhibition Center"],entranceFees:"A Wisconsin State Park vehicle admission sticker is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The park features a large marina on Lake Winnebago with multiple boat slips and a public boat launch for easy access to one of Wisconsin's largest inland lakes."},{id:"interstate-state-park",name:"Interstate State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/2819e9cbe2ab6ded.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Established in 1900, this is Wisconsin's oldest state park, featuring dramatic basalt cliffs along the St. Croix National Scenic Riverway.",fullDescription:"Interstate State Park is located in St. Croix Falls and offers a unique look at the geological history of the region. The park is situated on the Dalles of the St. Croix, a deep gorge carved by glacial meltwater. Visitors can explore the unique rock formations, including glacial potholes, and enjoy the scenic beauty of the St. Croix River, which serves as a border between Wisconsin and Minnesota.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Canoeing and Kayaking","Rock Climbing","Picnicking","Wildlife Viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pothole Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.4 miles",description:"A short but rugged trail that takes you past several unique glacial potholes formed thousands of years ago."},{name:"Skyline Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.0 mile",description:"This trail offers elevated views of the St. Croix River valley and the surrounding basalt cliffs."}],campingInfo:"The park features 37 family campsites, including some with electrical hookups, and a group campsite. Reservations are highly recommended during the summer season.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Bald eagles","Wild turkeys","Red foxes","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ice Age Interpretive Center",info:"Features exhibits on the glacial history of the area and the formation of the St. Croix River gorge.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM (Seasonal)",phone:"(715) 483-3747"},hours:"6:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"1271 State Rd 35, St Croix Falls, WI 54024",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Interstate+State+Park+Wisconsin",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/interstate",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for hiking and river activities.",tips:["Visit the Ice Age Interpretive Center first to understand the unique geology of the park.","Bring sturdy hiking boots as the terrain near the river can be rocky and uneven.","Check the river conditions before planning any kayaking or canoeing trips."],nearbyAttractions:["St. Croix Falls","Wild Mountain Recreation Area","Osceola & St. Croix Valley Railway"],entranceFees:"A Wisconsin State Park vehicle admission sticker is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The St. Croix River offers excellent fishing for smallmouth bass and walleye. There is a boat landing available for public use."},{id:"willow-river-state-park",name:"Willow River State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9648bfad0b37fb32.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Famous for its stunning 45-foot waterfall and scenic river valley, Willow River State Park offers a rugged escape just minutes from the Twin Cities.",fullDescription:"Willow River State Park encompasses 2,891 acres of rolling countryside, prairie, and forest. The park is defined by the Willow River, which carved a deep, dramatic gorge through the landscape, culminating in the iconic Willow Falls. The area has a rich history, having served as a site for early milling operations in the 19th century, and today provides a diverse habitat for wildlife and a premier destination for outdoor recreation.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the gorge trails","Swimming at the beach","Fishing for trout","Camping in the modern campground","Cross-country skiing in winter"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Willow Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A popular path that leads visitors down into the gorge for a spectacular view of the multi-tiered waterfall."},{name:"Burkhardt Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.8 miles",description:"This trail winds through wooded areas and offers elevated views of the river valley and surrounding prairie."}],campingInfo:"The park features a large campground with 124 sites, including 42 sites with electrical hookups, modern restrooms, and showers.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Great blue herons","Beavers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Willow River State Park Nature Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the park's geology, local history, and native wildlife, along with educational programming.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(715) 386-5931"},hours:"6:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"1034 County Rd A, Hudson, WI 54016",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Willow+River+State+Park+WI",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/willowriver",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for waterfall flow or autumn for vibrant foliage.",tips:["Arrive early on summer weekends as the parking lots fill up quickly.","Wear sturdy footwear as the trails leading to the waterfall can be steep and rocky.","Check the DNR website for current water quality reports before planning a swim."],nearbyAttractions:["Hudson, WI","St. Croix National Scenic Riverway","Minneapolis-St. Paul Metro Area"],entranceFees:"Wisconsin resident vehicle admission is $8.00 daily; non-resident vehicle admission is $11.00 daily.",fishingBoating:"The Willow River is a designated trout stream, and Willow River Flowage offers opportunities for canoeing and kayaking."},{id:"governor-dodge-state-park",name:"Governor Dodge State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7e27df07b7607087.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Spanning over 5,000 acres of steep hills, sandstone bluffs, and deep valleys, Governor Dodge is one of Wisconsin's largest and most diverse state parks.",fullDescription:"Located in the heart of the Driftless Area, Governor Dodge State Park features two man-made lakes, Cox Hollow and Twin Valley, surrounded by rolling hills and dramatic rock formations. The park's unique geology, untouched by the last glaciers, provides a rugged landscape perfect for outdoor recreation. Historically, the area was once home to lead miners in the 1800s, and remnants of this era can still be explored throughout the park's extensive trail system.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at Cox Hollow and Twin Valley beaches","Horseback riding on designated equestrian trails","Fishing for panfish, bass, and trout","Cross-country skiing in winter","Mountain biking on the Mill Creek Trail"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Stephens Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short but scenic trail that leads visitors down into a lush, shaded gorge to view the picturesque Stephens Falls."},{name:"Lost Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail winds through a beautiful wooded valley with impressive sandstone rock outcroppings and diverse forest flora."}],campingInfo:"The park offers two large campgrounds, Cox Hollow and Twin Valley, featuring over 260 campsites, including electric sites, group sites, and accessible facilities.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Great blue herons","Beavers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Governor Dodge State Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, trail information, firewood sales, and assistance from park rangers.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(608) 935-2315"},hours:"6:00 AM - 11:00 PM daily",address:"4175 State Highway 23 N, Dodgeville, WI 53533",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Governor+Dodge+State+Park+WI",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.wisconsin.gov/topic/parks/govdodge",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for wildflowers or autumn for spectacular fall foliage.",tips:["Visit the Stephens Falls area early in the morning to avoid crowds.","Bring insect repellent during the summer months, especially near the lakes.","Check the official website for seasonal trail closures due to wet conditions."],nearbyAttractions:["House on the Rock","Taliesin (Frank Lloyd Wright estate)","Dodgeville","Mineral Point"],entranceFees:"A Wisconsin State Park vehicle admission sticker is required for all vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The park features two lakes, Cox Hollow and Twin Valley, both offering boat launches for non-motorized or electric-motor boats and excellent fishing opportunities."}]},s={alabama:[{id:"gulf-state-park",name:"Gulf State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e632a38992e5875a.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A scenic beachside park featuring white sand beaches, freshwater lakes, and miles of hiking and biking trails on the Gulf of Mexico.",fullDescription:"Gulf State Park, located in Gulf Shores, Alabama, is a pristine coastal sanctuary spanning over 6,500 acres of diverse ecosystems. Boasting two miles of sugar-white sand beaches along the Gulf of Mexico, the park transitions into pine forests, freshwater dunes, and three spring-fed lakes, most notably Lake Shelby. It is famous for its massive fishing pier, the Lodge at Gulf State Park, and its commitment to eco-friendly, sustainable tourism. Visitors can swim, fish, kayak, or cycle through a labyrinth of paved multi-use trails that connect different coastal habitats.",thingsToDo:["Beach swimming and sunbathing","Pier fishing at the Gulf State Park Pier","Kayaking, canoeing, and paddleboarding on Lake Shelby","Biking or jogging on the Hugh S. Branyon Backcountry Trail","Birdwatching and alligator spotting","Taking a guided Segway or nature tour","Visiting the butterfly garden and interpretive nature exhibits"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Hugh S. Branyon Backcountry Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"15.0 miles",description:"A paved, multi-use trail system winding through nine distinct ecosystems, featuring alligator lookouts, butterfly gardens, swamp boardwalks, and ancient coastal dunes."},{name:"Gopher Tortoise Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A quiet, sandy path showcasing native scrub oak and rosemary habitats, with frequent sightings of the endangered gopher tortoise."},{name:"Lake Shelby Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A scenic boardwalk trail offering breathtaking, unobstructed views of Lake Shelby and opportunities to spot osprey and nesting bald eagles."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a massive, modern campground with 496 full-hookup campsites (water, sewer, 20/30/50 amp electricity) and 11 primitive sites. Additionally, there are 20 rustic lakeside cabins, 3 premium lakeside glamping safari tents, and the upscale eco-lodge.",fishingBoating:"Lake Shelby offers excellent freshwater fishing, kayaking, and boating with onsite rentals. The Gulf State Park Pier extends 1,542 feet into the Gulf of Mexico, providing outstanding saltwater fishing, complete with a bait shop, rod rentals, and fish cleaning stations.",wildlife:["Alligators (frequently spotted in swamps and lakes)","White-tailed deer","Bald eagles and Osprey","Gopher tortoises","Bottlenose dolphins (visible from the Gulf Pier)","Great blue herons and Snowy egrets"],visitorCenter:{name:"Gulf State Park Nature Center",info:"A living museum of native plants and animals, offering daily educational programs, guided nature walks, and hands-on interactive exhibits.",phone:"(251) 948-7275",hours:"Monday - Saturday: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily (Beach access and pier have extended hours)",entranceFees:"Vehicles: $10.00 daily parking fee. Gulf State Park Pier access: $3.00 for sightseers, $9.00 for fishing.",address:"20115 State Park Rd, Gulf Shores, AL 36542",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Gulf+State+Park+Gulf+Shores+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/gulf-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"March to May and September to November for comfortable temperatures and active bird migrations.",tips:["Rent a bicycle at the park headquarters or utilize the park's free bike sharing program to explore the Backcountry Trail.","Bring insect repellent for the interior wooded trails and boardwalks.","Visit the Pier at sunrise or sunset for incredible photo opportunities and dolphin sightings."],nearbyAttractions:["Gulf Shores Public Beach","Alabama Gulf Coast Zoo","Fort Morgan Historic Site","The Wharf at Orange Beach"]},{id:"cheaha-state-park",name:"Cheaha State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d6f521306a489a17.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched atop Alabamas highest peak, Cheaha offers dramatic mountain vistas, serene waterfalls, and direct access to the Talladega National Forest.",fullDescription:'Cheaha State Park is Alabamas oldest continuously operating state park, established in 1933. Located in the foothills of the Appalachian Mountains, it encompasses Mount Cheaha, the highest point in Alabama at 2,407 feet above sea level. This "island-in-the-sky" park is surrounded by the 392,567-acre Talladega National Forest. It is a haven for hikers, rock climbers, and nature lovers, offering magnificent rock outcroppings, deep granite gorges, and rushing mountain streams that cascade into scenic waterfalls. The historic stone Bunker Tower at the summit provides panoramic 360-degree views of the surrounding valleys.',thingsToDo:["Hiking and backcountry backpacking","Rock climbing and rappelling at Pulpit Rock","Gem panning and searching for gold at the stream beds","Picnicking at historic CCC pavilions","Watching magnificent sunrises from Bald Rock","Landscape photography and stargazing","Visiting the high-altitude mountain pool"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Bald Rock Boardwalk & Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.6 miles",description:"A fully wheelchair-accessible wooden boardwalk that leads directly to a spectacular cliffside overlook with panoramic valley views."},{name:"Pulpit Rock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.8 miles",description:"A rocky, uneven descent leading to a popular sandstone ledge favored by rock climbers and sunset photographers."},{name:"Lake Chinnabee Silent Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"6.0 miles",description:"Winds along a cold mountain stream, passing by pristine waterfalls and ending at a gorgeous alpine lake."}],campingInfo:"Features 73 modern campsites (semi-primitive and full hookups), a dedicated group camp, primitive trail-side backpacking sites, 17 historic stone cabins built by the CCC, and the Cheaha Lodge containing 30 hotel-style rooms.",fishingBoating:"A quiet 6-acre lake (Cheaha Lake) is located at the base of the mountain, offering hand-launched paddling, swimming, and peaceful bank fishing for bass, bluegill, and catfish.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer (extremely common and friendly)","Wild turkeys","Broad-winged hawks and Golden eagles","Black bears (very rare)","Pileated woodpeckers","Eastern chipmunks"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cheaha State Park Interpretive Center & Museum",info:"Features historical exhibits documenting the Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC) construction, Native American history, and native mountain wildlife.",phone:"(256) 488-5111",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},hours:"7:00 AM to 10:00 PM daily for day-use visitors.",entranceFees:"Adults: $5.00, Children (4-11): $2.00, Seniors (62+): $2.00, Children under 3: Free.",address:"19644 Hwy 281, Delta, AL 36258",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cheaha+State+Park+Delta+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/cheaha-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"October to November for breathtaking fall foliage, and April to May for blooming mountain laurel.",tips:["The Bald Rock Boardwalk is fully lit at dusk, making it a perfect spot for sunset and star viewing.","Temperatures at the summit are typically 10 degrees cooler than the surrounding valleys; bring a light jacket even in summer.","Check the weather forecast for fog, as low cloud cover can completely block mountain views."],nearbyAttractions:["Talladega National Forest","Pinhoti Trail System","High Falls Trailhead","Cheaha Wilderness Area"]},{id:"oak-mountain-state-park",name:"Oak Mountain State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3e0c594b93eb4bf5.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Alabamas largest state park, offering over 9,900 acres of outdoor adventure including world-class mountain biking, lakes, and a wildlife center.",fullDescription:"Oak Mountain State Park is the crown jewel of Alabamas state park system and its largest. Spanning nearly 10,000 acres, it lies just 20 miles south of Birmingham. The park is characterized by the rugged ridges of Double Oak Mountain, lush hardwood valleys, and pristine pine forests. It features over 50 miles of award-winning multi-use trails, three scenic lakes, an 18-hole championship golf course, a cable-wakeboarding park, and the Alabama Wildlife Center, which rehabilitates injured native birds. Oak Mountain is a premier destination for both adrenaline-seekers and families seeking a natural retreat.",thingsToDo:["Mountain biking on world-class singletrack trails","Cable wakeboarding and waterskiing on Double Oak Lake","Swimming and paddleboarding at the beach area","Visiting the Alabama Wildlife Center and demonstration farm","Playing 18 holes of championship golf","Horseback riding (rentals and guided tours available)","Hiking to Peavine Falls"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Peavine Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.9 miles",description:"A rocky trail descending into a steep ravine to reach a stunning, 65-foot spring-fed waterfall that cascades into a clear pool."},{name:"Double Oak Trail (BUMP Mountain Bike Trail)",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"22.0 miles",description:"An internationally acclaimed singletrack trail loop featuring technical rock gardens, flow sections, and steep climbs, built by the Birmingham Urban Mountain Pedalers."},{name:"King's Chair Loop Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.2 miles",description:"Leads hikers to a breathtaking rock outcrop called King's Chair, offering panoramic views of the Shelby County valleys."}],campingInfo:"The campground features 85 full-hookup sites, 60 water/electric sites, and a primitive camping area. There are also 10 fully equipped modern lakeside cabins available for rent year-round.",fishingBoating:"Three lakes (Double Oak Lake, Beaver Lake, and Lunker Lake) are stocked with bass, bream, and catfish. Motorized boats are not allowed, making them peaceful for canoeing, kayaking, and paddleboarding. Rowboats and canoes are available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Red-tailed hawks","Red foxes","Southern flying squirrels","Wild turkeys","Rehabilitated raptors (at the Alabama Wildlife Center)"],visitorCenter:{name:"Oak Mountain Park Headquarters & Wildlife Center",info:"Provides trail maps, park passes, and information on the park's history. The adjacent Alabama Wildlife Center features a boardwalk overlooking raptor cages and a rehabilitation clinic.",phone:"(205) 620-2520",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},hours:"7:00 AM to Sunset daily.",entranceFees:"Adults: $5.00, Seniors (62+): $2.00, Children (6-11): $2.00, Children under 6: Free.",address:"200 Terrace Dr, Pelham, AL 35124",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Oak+Mountain+State+Park+Pelham+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/oak-mountain-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"September to November for pleasant hiking temperatures and autumn colors, and June to August for lake activities.",tips:["If hiking to Peavine Falls, wear sturdy shoes with good traction as the descent into the gorge is steep and can be slippery.","Rent a kayak or paddleboard on Double Oak Lake during the summer weekends for a relaxing water excursion.","Check out the Alabama Wildlife Center's treetop nature trail—it's a free, elevated boardwalk that lets you see owls and hawks up close."],nearbyAttractions:["Birmingham Botanical Gardens","McWane Science Center","Vulcan Park and Museum","Barber Vintage Motorsports Museum"]},{id:"desoto-state-park",name:"DeSoto State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/22916b1c35bcef66.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled on Lookout Mountain, DeSoto features rushing mountain streams, waterfalls, and stunning spring blooms of wild azaleas.",fullDescription:"DeSoto State Park is situated on the scenic Lookout Mountain in Northeast Alabama. Spanning over 3,500 acres, the park is famed for its rugged beauty, mountain streams, and roaring waterfalls. Developed in the 1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps, the park preserves a rustic, historic charm with stone structures and bridges. In the spring, the park erupts with the vibrant pink and white blooms of native wild azaleas and rhododendrons. DeSoto is located just minutes from the deep and awe-inspiring Little River Canyon National Preserve.",thingsToDo:["Viewing spectacular waterfalls including DeSoto Falls","Kayaking and canoeing on the West Fork of the Little River","Biking and hiking on historic CCC-built trails","Participating in guided wildflower and ecology walks","Bouldering and geocaching","Swimming in the Olympic-sized seasonal pool","Dining at the historic Mountain Inn Restaurant"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Talmadge Butler Boardwalk Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.7 miles",description:"A fully accessible wooden boardwalk winding through a peaceful forest canopy, ending at a scenic overlook of the rapid Azalea Cascade."},{name:"DeSoto Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.9 miles",description:"A rocky path that leads down to the base of the majestic, 104-foot DeSoto Falls, one of the tallest and most photographed waterfalls in the state."},{name:"Silver Shingle Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.0 miles",description:"Follows a rocky mountain stream bed with picturesque rock formations and excellent opportunities for children to search for fossils."}],campingInfo:"Offers 94 full-hookup campsites, 18 primitive tent camping sites, and a modern group camp. Lodging includes 22 historic CCC-built rustic stone cabins, 14 modern chalets, and the historic DeSoto Lodge containing hotel rooms.",fishingBoating:"The West Fork of the Little River is popular for peaceful flat-water kayaking and canoeing. Fishing is excellent for spotted bass, bluegill, and rainbow trout (stocked seasonally). Paddlecraft can be rented nearby.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Southern flying squirrels","Green salamanders","Broad-winged hawks","Eastern screech owls"],visitorCenter:{name:"DeSoto State Park Lodge & Nature Center",info:"Provides historical displays about the CCC, trail maps, and wildlife guides. The park naturalist hosts regular weekend programs and educational hikes here.",phone:"(256) 845-0051",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 6:00 PM"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily for day-use areas.",entranceFees:"No general park entrance fee! (Some specific areas or trailheads have a $4.00 parking fee).",address:"7104 DeSoto Parkway NE, Fort Payne, AL 35967",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=DeSoto+State+Park+Fort+Payne+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/desoto-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"April to May for roaring waterfalls and blooming wild azaleas, and October for peak mountain fall foliage.",tips:["DeSoto Falls is located in a separate, non-contiguous parcel of the park about 7 miles from the main park area; make sure to get directions from the lodge.","Visit the Little River Canyon overlook, just a short drive down the Parkway, to see one of the deepest canyon gorges east of the Mississippi.","Book the stone cabins months in advance, especially for the autumn foliage season, as they are highly coveted."],nearbyAttractions:["Little River Canyon National Preserve","Historic Fort Payne Depot Museum","Orbix Hot Glass Studio","Mentone Scenic Brow Overlooks"]},{id:"monte-sano-state-park",name:"Monte Sano State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/bc0ceea87de3c56b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Famed for its Mountain of Health, Monte Sano offers fresh mountain air, historical CCC stone cabins, and a serene Japanese garden.",fullDescription:'Monte Sano State Park is located in Huntsville, Alabama, sitting high atop the plateau of Monte Sano Mountain (Spanish for "Mountain of Health"). Spanning over 2,100 acres, the park is known for its cool mountain breezes and fresh spring waters, which made it a popular health resort in the late 19th century. Built by the CCC during the Great Depression, the park features stunning hand-carved stone cabins and extensive hiking and mountain biking trails that wind past scenic limestone sinks, waterfalls, and lookouts. The park is also home to a beautiful public Japanese Garden, featuring a tea house and cherry blossom trees.',thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running through limestone valleys","Mountain biking on singletrack plateau trails","Exploring the scenic Monte Sano Japanese Garden","Picnicking at the CCC-built stone pavilions","Stargazing at the on-site Von Braun Astronomical Society Planetarium","Discovering seasonal waterfalls and limestone sinkholes"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Stone Cuts Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"A fascinating trail that takes hikers through deep, narrow rock fissures and natural stone arches carved out of the mountain's limestone."},{name:"Mackay Hollow Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"3.1 miles",description:"A rugged descent into a deep forest hollow, passing seasonal waterfalls, streams, and offering a challenging climb back up to the plateau."},{name:"North Plateau Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A flat trail tracing the rim of the mountain, offering panoramic overlooks of the city of Huntsville below."}],campingInfo:"Features 89 campsites (both full-hookup and water/electric), a primitive campground, and 14 historic CCC-built stone cabins with fireplaces and fully equipped kitchens.",fishingBoating:"No fishing or boating is available in this high-altitude mountain park, but scenic streams and small ponds dot the hiking routes.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Grey foxes","Broad-winged hawks","Eastern bluebirds","Eastern box turtles","Flying squirrels"],visitorCenter:{name:"Monte Sano State Park Office & Planetarium",info:"Provides maps and cabin check-ins. The Von Braun Planetarium, located within the park, offers public astronomy shows and telescope viewings on Saturday nights.",phone:"(256) 534-3757",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},hours:"8:00 AM to Sunset daily.",entranceFees:"Adults: $5.00, Children (4-11): $2.00, Seniors (62+): $2.00, Active Military: Free.",address:"5105 Nolen SE, Huntsville, AL 35801",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Monte+Sano+State+Park+Huntsville+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/monte-sano-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"April for blooming azaleas and cherry blossoms in the Japanese Garden, and late October for vibrant fall forest colors.",tips:["Attend a Saturday night planetarium show at the Von Braun Astronomical Society inside the park—it's a wonderful, family-friendly educational experience.","Bring robust hiking boots if you plan to tackle the Stone Cuts Trail, as the rocky crevasses require stable footing.","Watch for deer near the cabin loop in the early morning and late evening—they are very accustomed to visitors."],nearbyAttractions:["US Space & Rocket Center","Huntsville Botanical Garden","Burritt on the Mountain","Downtown Huntsville Historic Districts"]},{id:"cathedral-caverns-state-park",name:"Cathedral Caverns State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9f5ddb50d04d6cd1.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:'Home to a massive cave entrance, spectacular stalagmites, and "Goliath"—one of the largest stalagmites in the world.',fullDescription:'Cathedral Caverns State Park, located in Woodville, Alabama, is home to one of the most spectacular cave formations in North America. Originally called "Kennamer Cave," the cavern was renamed by its developer due to its cathedral-like chambers. The cave entrance is a record-breaking 126 feet wide and 25 feet high. Inside, the cave maintains a cool 60 degrees Fahrenheit year-round. Guided tours lead visitors along a concrete pathway to marvel at "Goliath," a colossal stalagmite measuring 45 feet tall and 243 feet in circumference, a stalagmite forest, a frozen waterfall of stone, and an ancient, mysterious underground river.',thingsToDo:["Taking a 90-minute guided underground cavern tour","Gemstone panning at the custom mining sluice","Hiking on the scenic forest trails of Kennamer Cove","Exploring cave history at the visitor center","Backcountry camping in the hardwood forest"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cathedral Caverns Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.1 miles",description:"A scenic, uphill hike winding through a dense hardwood forest and limestone outcroppings, offering beautiful views of the cove below."},{name:"Kennamer Cove Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A peaceful, flat loop trail tracing the edge of the mountain and featuring interpretive markers explaining the unique geology of the valley."}],campingInfo:"The park has a primitive campground with tent sites, 15 back-in RV campsites with water and electric hookups, and a climate-controlled bathhouse.",fishingBoating:"No fishing or boating is available within the cave system or park, though nearby Guntersville Lake offers extensive water sports.",wildlife:["Little brown bats (visible in the cave)","Cave crickets and Blind cave fish","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-bellied woodpeckers","Eastern box turtles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cathedral Caverns Welcome Center & Gift Shop",info:"The hub for booking cave tours, purchasing gemstone panning bags, and exploring geologic displays of cave development.",phone:"(256) 888-0230",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:30 PM"},hours:"9:00 AM to 5:30 PM daily. Cavern tours are scheduled throughout the day.",entranceFees:"Park entry is free! Cave tours are: Adults: $20.05, Children (5-12): $9.00, Children under 5: Free.",address:"637 Cave Rd, Woodville, AL 35776",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cathedral+Caverns+State+Park+Woodville+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/cathedral-caverns-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Year-round! Since the cave is a constant 60 degrees, it is an excellent escape from the summer heat or winter cold.",tips:["Book your cave tour tickets online in advance, as tour sizes are strictly limited and popular weekend times sell out quickly.","Wear a light sweater or jacket during the cave tour, even in the dead of summer, as 60 degrees can feel chilly after 90 minutes.","Cavern walkways are fully wheelchair and stroller accessible, making this one of the most accessible cave tours in the country."],nearbyAttractions:["Unclaimed Baggage Center in Scottsboro","Lake Guntersville State Park","Sauta Cave National Wildlife Refuge (famous for summer bat flights)","Walls of Jericho Trailhead"]},{id:"lake-guntersville-state-park",name:"Lake Guntersville State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0e45e58f4124281e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A premier lakeside resort park featuring bald eagle watching, championship golf, zip-line canopy tours, and expansive water sports.",fullDescription:"Lake Guntersville State Park spans 6,000 acres along the banks of Alabamas largest lake, the 69,000-acre Lake Guntersville. Nestled in the foothills of the Appalachian Mountains, this resort-style park is a nature-lovers dream. It is internationally renowned for its Bald Eagle Awareness programs, which draw visitors every winter to see nesting eagles. The park features an 18-hole championship golf course, a massive beach complex, a full-service marina, the Screaming Eagle Zip Line Canopy Tour, over 36 miles of hiking trails, and a premier Lodge perched high on a ridge overlooking the water.",thingsToDo:["Boating, jet skiing, and pontoon rentals at the marina","Lakeside swimming and volleyball at the beach complex","Gliding through the forest on the Screaming Eagle Zip Line","Playing 18 holes at the Eagle's Nest Golf Course","Attending winter Eagle Awareness weekend seminars","Hiking and mountain biking on extensive ridge trails","Dining at the Pine Ledge Restaurant in the Lodge"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Tom Bevill Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.2 miles",description:"A beautiful ridge trail climbing through dense pine forests and offering scenic overlooking spots of the massive lake below."},{name:"Cutchenmine Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.5 miles",description:"Follows an old coal mine tramroad along the banks of Town Creek, flat and peaceful, with frequent sightings of waterfowl and beaver dams."},{name:"Cave Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A rocky trail leading to a small, scenic cave opening that was historically mined for saltpeter during the Civil War."}],campingInfo:"The campground offers 322 improved campsites (most with full water/sewer/electric hookups) and 10 primitive tent sites. Lodging includes the Lake Guntersville Lodge (112 resort-style rooms), 20 mountain chalets, and 20 lakeside cabins.",fishingBoating:"Lake Guntersville is internationally recognized as one of the premier largemouth bass fishing lakes in the world. The full-service marina offers pontoon, bass boat, kayak, and paddleboard rentals, as well as boat slips and fuel.",wildlife:["Bald eagles (especially active November through February)","Great blue herons and Canada geese","White-tailed deer","Osprey","Red foxes","Beavers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Guntersville Lodge & Nature Center",info:"Perched on a 150-foot bluff, the Lodge serves as the park's command center. The Nature Center offers wildlife exhibits, snake demonstrations, and guided weekend hikes.",phone:"(256) 571-5440",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 8:00 PM"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily for day-use areas (Campground and Lodge open 24/7).",entranceFees:"No general park entrance fee! (Some specific trailheads or beach areas have a nominal $3.00 parking fee).",address:"1155 Lodge Dr, Guntersville, AL 35976",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Guntersville+State+Park+Guntersville+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/lake-guntersville-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"January to February for nesting bald eagles, and May to September for summer lake and boating activities.",tips:["Rent a pontoon boat for a half-day at the marina—it's the best way to see the massive scale of Lake Guntersville and its secluded mountain inlets.","Make reservations for Eagle Awareness weekends (January/February) months in advance, as these seminars draw visitors from all over the country.","Have a meal at the Pine Ledge restaurant in the Lodge around sunset; the floor-to-ceiling windows offer the most spectacular mountain lake views in Alabama."],nearbyAttractions:["Cathedral Caverns State Park","Guntersville Museum and Cultural Center","Buck's Pocket State Park","High Falls Park"]},{id:"wind-creek-state-park",name:"Wind Creek State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/ebe28e57938e6b35.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"One of the largest state-owned campgrounds in the nation, offering pristine lakeside camping, swimming, and hiking on beautiful Lake Martin.",fullDescription:"Wind Creek State Park spans 1,445 acres along the shores of the massive, crystal-clear Lake Martin, a 41,000-acre reservoir in East-Central Alabama. Wind Creek is famous for hosting one of the largest state-operated campgrounds in the United States, containing over 500 spacious campsites, many of which sit directly on the shoreline. This park is a premier destination for water sports lovers, offering an extensive beach area, a modern marina with double-slip boat ramps, and canoe and kayak rentals. The park also features scenic hiking trails through rolling hills and pine forests, and a beautiful miniature golf course.",thingsToDo:["Lakeside camping with direct water access","Swimming and building sandcastles at the sandy beach","Boating, sailing, and jet skiing on Lake Martin","Fishing for striped bass, crappie, and bluegill","Hiking through pine forests and climbing the forest fire tower","Playing miniature golf and archery at the park range","Kayaking and canoeing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Campfire Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.8 miles",description:"A pleasant forest loop that climbs gently through tall longleaf pines and hardwood hills, offering beautiful views of small creek beds."},{name:"Wind Creek Fire Tower Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.6 miles",description:"Leads hikers to a historic, 100-foot forest fire tower. Visitors can climb up the stairs to get an unparalleled bird's-eye view of Lake Martin."},{name:"Reunion Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.8 miles",description:"Meanders through mature pine stands and along small, quiet inlets of Lake Martin, perfect for birdwatching and photography."}],campingInfo:"Boasts an incredible 586 campsites. Out of these, 187 are full-hookup sites, and 399 are water and electric sites. Over 150 campsites are situated directly on the lake edge. There are also 7 lakeside camping cabins available for rent.",fishingBoating:"Lake Martin is renowned for clean, clear water and active fishing. The park marina offers double-slip launching, wet slips, marine fueling, and pontoon, canoe, and kayak rentals. Excellent for catching striped bass, large largemouth bass, and crappie.",wildlife:["Osprey and Bald eagles","White-tailed deer","Great blue herons","Canada geese","Fox squirrels","Belted kingfishers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wind Creek Welcome Center & Marina",info:"The central hub for registering campsites, purchasing marine gas, rent-a-boats, and grabbing fishing tackle or bait at the camp store.",phone:"(256) 329-0845",hours:"Daily: 7:00 AM - 7:00 PM"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily for day-use areas (Campground check-in is 24/7).",entranceFees:"Adults: $5.00, Children (6-11): $2.00, Seniors (62+): $2.00, Children under 6: Free.",address:"4325 Alabama Hwy 128, Alexander City, AL 35010",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wind+Creek+State+Park+Alexander+City+AL",officialWebsite:"https://www.alapark.com/parks/wind-creek-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"May to September for summer boating and swimming, and October for comfortable camping under autumn foliage.",tips:["If you are camping, try to book one of the waterfront sites in loops A, B, or C—there is nothing like waking up and walking straight into Lake Martin.","Don't miss climbing the historic fire tower; the panoramic vistas of Lake Martin are the absolute best in the region.","Bring your own fishing gear and purchase a local freshwater license; the shoreline fishing at Wind Creek is highly productive."],nearbyAttractions:["Children's Harbor with its iconic lighthouse","Chimney Rock (famous Lake Martin landmark)","Horseshoe Bend National Military Park","Alexander City Historic Downtown"]}],arkansas:[{id:"petit-jean-state-park",name:"Petit Jean State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/28e56e189da6b701.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Arkansas mountaintop park known for 95-foot Cedar Falls and the 1930s CCC-built Mather Lodge.",fullDescription:"Petit Jean State Park covers 3,471 acres atop Petit Jean Mountain above the Arkansas River, between the Ouachita Mountains and the Ozarks. The Civilian Conservation Corps built Mather Lodge and the Cedar Falls Trail, which drops into the canyon to the 95-foot falls. Other trails lead to Bear Cave and the Rock House cave.",thingsToDo:["Cedar Falls Trail","Hiking","Mather Lodge and cabins","Camping","Overlooks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cedar Falls Trail",difficulty:"Moderately strenuous",length:"1.9 miles",description:"This CCC-built trail runs from Mather Lodge down into the canyon and crosses Cedar Creek on a bridge, in the canyon where Cedar Creek drops in a 95-foot waterfall."},{name:"Seven Hollows Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"4.3 miles",description:"This trail passes through the Seven Hollows area, where climbing, rappelling and bouldering are also offered."},{name:"CCC Hike & Bike Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This trail is open to both hiking and biking, and its name honors the Civilian Conservation Corps, which built much of the park."}],campingInfo:"Wikipedia lists 127 campsites, 32 cabins and the 24-room Mather Lodge, and camping and lodging reservations are taken through the Arkansas State Parks reservation site.",fishingBoating:"The 100-acre Lake Bailey is used for fishing, kayaking and pedal boating, and its boathouse is open in the summer months with boat rentals, supplies and a snack bar.",wildlife:["Deer","Squirrels","Chipmunks","Rabbits","Opossums"],visitorCenter:{name:"Petit Jean State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(501) 727-5441"},hours:"Park office 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.",entranceFees:"Free",address:"1285 Petit Jean Mountain Rd, Morrilton, AR 72110",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Petit%20Jean%20State%20Park%20arkansas",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/petit-jean-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall offer mild weather for hiking to Cedar Falls and the canyon overlooks, while summer is the time for the pools and the Lake Bailey boathouse, which is open in the summer months.",tips:["Self-guided climbing, rappelling and bouldering in the Seven Hollows area requires a permit, which you can get at the Visitor Center or Mather Lodge.","The Petit Jean Via Ferrata is open only by guided reservation through the park's partner, Frontier Climbing Company, so book ahead.","If you plan to hike the full 12-mile trail loop, register at the visitor center first."],nearbyAttractions:["Petit Jean Mountain Museum of Automobiles","Rialto Theater (Morrilton)","Depot Museum (Morrilton)"]},{id:"mount-nebo-state-park",name:"Mount Nebo State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/8a0ae144b258d0ac.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Arkansas mountaintop park 1,350 feet above the valley, with views of Lake Dardanelle and the Arkansas River.",fullDescription:"Mount Nebo State Park sits on a plateau about 1,755 feet above sea level near Dardanelle, looking over Lake Dardanelle and the Arkansas River. A resort area in the late 1800s, it became a state park in 1928 and was developed by the Civilian Conservation Corps. It has cabins, campsites, a swimming pool and 25 miles of hiking and mountain biking trails.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain biking","Scenic overlooks","Cabins and camping","Swimming pool"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Rim Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.5 miles",description:"This trail goes around the top of the mountain and offers panoramic views of the Arkansas River."},{name:"Bench Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This level trail follows the mountain's bench, a terrace below the summit where numerous springs are found."},{name:"Nebo Springs/Nebo Steps Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This trail is listed among the park's trails on the official page and shares its name with Nebo Springs on the mountain's bench."}],campingInfo:"The park has 34 campsites (24 Class B and 10 hike-in tent sites), most with river valley views, plus 15 cabins, but there is no dump station.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["deer","birds","butterflies"],visitorCenter:{name:"Mount Nebo State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(479) 229-3655"},hours:"Visitor Center open daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. (closed New Year's Day, Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Eve and Christmas Day); campground/picnic area open year-round",entranceFees:"Free",address:"16728 West State Hwy. 155, Dardanelle, AR 72834",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Mount%20Nebo%20State%20Park%20arkansas",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/mount-nebo-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall offer comfortable weather for hiking the CCC-built trails and taking in the bluff-top views of the Arkansas River Valley, while summer adds access to the seasonal pool.",tips:["There is no dump station at the park, so RV campers should plan to dump tanks elsewhere.","The swimming pool requires a fee and is open only seasonally, 11 a.m. to 6 p.m. weather permitting: Wednesday to Sunday from Memorial Day weekend to August 9, then weekends only from August 15 through Labor Day.","The Visitor Center is closed on New Year's Day, Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Eve and Christmas Day."],nearbyAttractions:["Lake Dardanelle State Park","Petit Jean State Park","Old Post Park Mountain Bike Trail"]},{id:"crater-of-diamonds-state-park",name:"Crater of Diamonds State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/96e299bd5ef76943.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Arkansas park where visitors can search a plowed field for real diamonds and keep what they find.",fullDescription:"Crater of Diamonds State Park covers 911 acres near Murfreesboro and has a 37.5-acre plowed field, one of the few diamond-bearing sites open to the public. Visitors find about two diamonds a day on average and keep what they find. The Uncle Sam, found here in 1924 at over 40 carats, is the largest diamond ever found in the United States.",thingsToDo:["Diamond hunting","Diamond Discovery Center","Water park (summer)","Hiking","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Prospector Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"Not listed",description:"A self-guided loop trail in Crater of Diamonds State Park."},{name:"Little Missouri River Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A loop trail with interpretive features that AllTrails users rate as the best hike in the park."}],campingInfo:"The park has 47 Class AAA campsites with 50-, 30- and 20-amp electric, water and sewer hookups, plus walk-in tent sites, and the campground is open all year.",fishingBoating:"A boat launch onto the Little Missouri River, known for trout fishing, sits across from the park's entrance and is a common starting point for fishing trips.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Crater of Diamonds State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(870) 285-3113"},hours:"Diamond Search Area: daily 8 a.m. to 4 p.m.; Visitor Center/Gift Shop/Diamond Discovery Center: daily 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.; closed New Year's Day, Thanksgiving Day, Christmas Eve and Christmas Day. Campground/picnic area open all year.",entranceFees:"Diamond search area admission: $15 per person (ages 13+), $7 per person (ages 6-12), free under 6",address:"209 State Park Rd, Murfreesboro, AR 71958",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Crater%20of%20Diamonds%20State%20Park%20arkansas",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/crater-diamonds-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall offer the most comfortable weather for digging in the open diamond field, while summer visitors can cool off at the seasonal Diamond Springs Water Park (Memorial Day to Labor Day).",tips:["You may bring your own mining equipment or rent tools from the park, but ladders and battery-operated or motor-driven mining tools are not allowed. Admission does not include tool rental.","The Diamond Search Area closes at 4 p.m., an hour before the visitor center, so arrive early for a full day of digging. Park staff identify your finds for free.","Diamond Springs Water Park is closed Mondays and Tuesdays and may open on weekends only starting mid-August."],nearbyAttractions:["Little Missouri River"]},{id:"devils-den-state-park",name:"Devil's Den State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6c0402b298b756aa.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the Lee Creek Valley, Devil's Den State Park is one of the most iconic parks in Arkansas, featuring unique rock formations and historic CCC-built structures.",fullDescription:"Established in the 1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps, Devil's Den State Park showcases rustic stone and timber architecture that blends seamlessly into the rugged Ozark landscape. The park is renowned for its dramatic rock crevices, caves, and the scenic Lee Creek, which winds through the valley. Visitors can explore the unique geology of the area, including the famous Devil's Den cave, while enjoying the lush hardwood forests that provide stunning displays of color during the autumn months.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and backpacking","Mountain biking","Cave exploration","Fishing in Lee Creek","Swimming in the park pool","Wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Yellow Rock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"This trail leads to a stunning overlook providing a panoramic view of the Lee Creek Valley."},{name:"Devil's Den Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that takes hikers past the park's namesake cave and through impressive rock formations."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 135 campsites, including full hookup sites, as well as 17 historic cabins with kitchens and fireplaces available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Black bear","Wild turkey","Pileated woodpecker","Red fox"],visitorCenter:{name:"Devil's Den Visitor Center",info:"Features exhibits on the history of the Civilian Conservation Corps, local geology, and park wildlife.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(479) 761-3325"},hours:"Open 24 hours for registered campers; day-use areas open sunrise to sunset.",address:"11333 W. Arkansas Hwy. 74, West Fork, AR 72774",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Devil's+Den+State+Park+Arkansas",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/devils-den-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers and autumn for fall foliage.",tips:["Bring a flashlight if you plan to explore the caves.","The park is a popular destination for mountain bikers; check trail status before heading out.","Book cabins well in advance as they are highly sought after during peak seasons."],nearbyAttractions:["Fayetteville","Prairie Grove Battlefield State Park","Lake Fort Smith State Park"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Lee Creek; the park also features a small lake where pedal boats can be rented seasonally."},{id:"lake-ouachita-state-park",name:"Lake Ouachita State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/fff8dac84a54010c.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the Ouachita Mountains, this park offers access to Arkansas's largest and cleanest lake, perfect for water sports and nature lovers.",fullDescription:"Lake Ouachita State Park is located on the shores of Lake Ouachita, a 40,000-acre reservoir surrounded by the Ouachita National Forest. The lake is renowned for its crystal-clear water and is a premier destination for scuba diving, fishing, and boating. The park features a rich history of Civilian Conservation Corps development and offers visitors a chance to explore the unique geology of the Ouachita Mountains.",thingsToDo:["Scuba diving in crystal clear waters","Fishing for striped bass and bream","Boating and water skiing","Bird watching in the national forest","Guided lake tours"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Caddo Bend Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"A scenic loop that offers stunning panoramic views of Lake Ouachita and the surrounding mountain ridges."},{name:"Dogwood Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, interpretive nature trail perfect for families and those looking to identify local flora."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 92 campsites with water and electric hookups, as well as several fully equipped cabins overlooking the lake.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Black bears"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Ouachita State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features exhibits on the lake's history, geology, and local wildlife, along with a gift shop and park information.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(501) 767-9366"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"5451 Mountain Pine Rd, Mountain Pine, AR 71956",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Ouachita+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/lake-ouachita-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and beautiful foliage.",tips:["Bring polarized sunglasses to better see the fish and underwater structures in the clear lake water.","Book your campsite or cabin well in advance, especially during summer weekends.","Check the park website for current lake levels and water safety alerts before heading out on a boat."],nearbyAttractions:["Hot Springs National Park","Garvan Woodland Gardens","Magic Springs Theme and Water Park","Downtown Hot Springs"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park; fees apply for camping and cabin rentals.",fishingBoating:"The park features a full-service marina with boat rentals, fuel, and bait, plus multiple boat ramps for easy lake access."},{id:"pinnacle-mountain-state-park",name:"Pinnacle Mountain State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/460324bf5371e389.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A day-use park featuring the iconic Pinnacle Mountain, offering diverse trails and scenic views of the Big Maumelle and Little Maumelle Rivers.",fullDescription:"Pinnacle Mountain State Park is a 2,356-acre park located in Little Rock, Arkansas. It is known for its namesake mountain, a prominent peak that rises 1,011 feet above the surrounding landscape. The park is a geological treasure, featuring unique rock formations and diverse ecosystems ranging from bottomland hardwoods to rocky mountain slopes. It serves as a vital outdoor recreation hub for the central Arkansas region, preserving natural beauty while providing educational opportunities about the local environment.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain Biking","Bird Watching","Kayaking","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"East Summit Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A steep, rocky climb that rewards hikers with panoramic views of the Arkansas River Valley."},{name:"Kingfisher Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A flat, accessible trail that winds through the bottomland forest along the Little Maumelle River."}],campingInfo:"Pinnacle Mountain State Park is a day-use park and does not offer overnight camping or cabin rentals.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Red-tailed hawk","Bobcat"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pinnacle Mountain State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on the park's geology, history, and wildlife, along with a gift shop and helpful park rangers.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(501) 868-5806"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"11901 Pinnacle Valley Road, Little Rock, AR 72223",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pinnacle+Mountain+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/pinnacle-mountain-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Wear sturdy hiking boots as the summit trails are very rocky.","Bring plenty of water, especially during the summer months.","Check the park website for trail closures due to weather conditions."],nearbyAttractions:["Two Rivers Park","Arkansas Arboretum","Little Rock Central High School National Historic Site","William J. Clinton Presidential Library"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Big and Little Maumelle Rivers. Boat ramps are available for access to the rivers for kayaking and canoeing."},{id:"queen-wilhelmina-state-park",name:"Queen Wilhelmina State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/e5366a646620016e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched atop Rich Mountain, this park offers breathtaking views of the Ouachita Mountains and a historic lodge experience.",fullDescription:"Queen Wilhelmina State Park is located on the second-highest peak in Arkansas, Rich Mountain. Named after the Queen of the Netherlands, the park features the historic Queen Wilhelmina Lodge, which provides a unique mountain retreat experience. The area is renowned for its stunning vistas, diverse flora, and the scenic Talimena National Scenic Byway that winds through the surrounding Ouachita National Forest.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the Ouachita Trail","Dining at the Queen Wilhelmina Lodge","Bird watching and wildlife photography","Stargazing from the mountain summit","Exploring the Talimena National Scenic Byway"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lover's Leap Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.0-mile loop",description:"A scenic trail that leads to a dramatic rock outcropping offering panoramic views of the valley below."},{name:"Ouachita National Recreation Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"223 miles",description:"A long-distance trail that passes through the park, offering rugged terrain and deep forest immersion for experienced hikers."}],campingInfo:"The park features 41 campsites with water and electric hookups, as well as a modern lodge with 40 guest rooms for those seeking indoor accommodations.",wildlife:["Black bear","White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red-tailed hawk","Bobcat"],visitorCenter:{name:"Queen Wilhelmina Lodge Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and local wildlife exhibits.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(479) 394-2863"},hours:"Park grounds are open 24 hours; Lodge and Visitor Center hours vary by season.",address:"3877 Highway 88 West, Mena, AR 71953",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Queen+Wilhelmina+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/queen-wilhelmina-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for fall foliage or Spring for blooming wildflowers.",tips:["Bring a jacket as temperatures are significantly cooler on the mountain summit.","Check the weather before driving the Talimena Scenic Byway as fog can reduce visibility.","Book lodge rooms well in advance during peak foliage season."],nearbyAttractions:["Mena, Arkansas","Ouachita National Forest","Wolf Pen Gap ATV Trails","Cossatot River State Park-Natural Area"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park.",fishingBoating:"While there is no lake within the park, nearby streams and lakes in the Ouachita National Forest offer excellent fishing opportunities."},{id:"bull-shoals-white-river-state-park",name:"Bull Shoals-White River State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/3d4380bf765f46f2.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled along the banks of the White River and the shores of Bull Shoals Lake, this park is a premier destination for world-class trout fishing and outdoor recreation.",fullDescription:"Bull Shoals-White River State Park offers a unique blend of river and lake experiences. The park is situated at the base of the Bull Shoals Dam, where the cold, clear waters of the White River provide an ideal habitat for trophy trout. The surrounding Ozark landscape features rugged hills, lush forests, and expansive water views, making it a haven for anglers, boaters, and nature enthusiasts alike.",thingsToDo:["Trout fishing","Boating and kayaking","Guided dam tours","Picnicking","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Big Bluff Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through the forest and offers elevated views of the White River."},{name:"Lakeside Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.75 miles",description:"A gentle path along the shoreline of Bull Shoals Lake, perfect for a relaxing walk."}],campingInfo:"The park features 103 campsites with water and electric hookups, as well as several tent-only sites and modern bathhouses.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","River otters"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bull Shoals-White River State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features exhibits on the history of the dam, local geology, and the aquatic life of the White River.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(870) 445-3629"},hours:"6:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"153 Dam Overlook Lane, Bull Shoals, AR 72619",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bull+Shoals-White+River+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.arkansasstateparks.com/parks/bull-shoals-white-river-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild weather and peak fishing conditions.",tips:["Check the water release schedule from the dam before planning your fishing trip.","Bring polarized sunglasses to better spot trout in the clear river water.","Visit during the early morning hours to see the most wildlife activity."],nearbyAttractions:["Mountain Home","Cotter","Buffalo National River"],entranceFees:"Free entrance",fishingBoating:"The park provides multiple boat ramps for both the lake and the river, along with a full-service marina offering boat rentals and supplies."}],delaware:[{id:"cape-henlopen-state-park",name:"Cape Henlopen State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d9df66e285b424cb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Delaware park at the mouth of Delaware Bay, with ocean and bay beaches and World War II Fort Miles.",fullDescription:"Cape Henlopen State Park covers 5,450 acres on Cape Henlopen near Lewes. William Penn set its beaches aside for public use in 1682, making it one of the first public lands in what became the United States. It has lifeguarded swimming beaches in summer, WWII-era Fort Miles with an observation tower, and Gordons Pond.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Fort Miles","Biking","Fishing","Camping","Observation tower"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Gordons Pond Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"Not listed",description:"A popular, easy biking, hiking, running and bird-watching trail that crosses the park's salt marshes."},{name:"Junction and Breakwater Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"It appears on the park's official 'Gordons Pond, J&B and Wolfe Neck Trail Map' and is listed by AllTrails among popular trails in the park."}],campingInfo:"The park offers RV camping, cabins and tent camping at its campground, which is set among the pine-covered dunes.",fishingBoating:"Anglers can fish from the fishing pier on Delaware Bay or surf-fish from designated beach areas, and the park has a bait and tackle store and paddlesport rentals.",wildlife:["Horseshoe crab","Piping plover","Least tern","Black skimmer","Osprey"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cape Henlopen State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(302) 645-8983"},hours:"Open daily from 8 a.m. until sunset",entranceFees:"$10/day for Delaware-registered vehicles; $20/day for non-Delaware-registered vehicles; walking/biking free",address:"15099 Cape Henlopen Dr, Lewes, DE 19958",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cape%20Henlopen%20State%20Park%20delaware",officialWebsite:"https://www.destateparks.com/park/cape-henlopen/",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall is best for the guarded ocean beaches, which have lifeguards from Memorial Day Saturday to Labor Day Monday; spring and fall suit birding at the Point and in the shorebird habitat.",tips:["The Seaside Nature Center is closed October 19-27, so plan aquarium and touch-tank visits around that closure.","You can borrow a bike for free at the Seaside Nature Center to explore the park's extensive bike trails.","Most of Beach Plum Nature Preserve is off-limits for wildlife conservation. Only a small surf-fishing area and a pedestrian-only trail are open to the public."],nearbyAttractions:["Harbor of Refuge Light","Delaware Breakwater East End Light","Town of Lewes"]},{id:"delaware-seashore-state-park",name:"Delaware Seashore State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/63b1e22a035a6b7f.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Delaware barrier-island park between the Atlantic and Rehoboth and Indian River bays, near Dewey Beach.",fullDescription:"Delaware Seashore State Park covers 2,722 acres near Dewey Beach, between the Atlantic Ocean and Rehoboth and Indian River bays, with 6 miles of shoreline. Created in 1965, it has two ocean swimming areas with bathhouses, surf fishing, a marina at Indian River Inlet, and camping.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Surf fishing","Boating","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Burton Island Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This nature trail on Burton Island offers views of salt marshes and bay islands and has its own trailhead."},{name:"Assawoman Canal Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This trail in Ocean View is a separate recreation area overseen by Delaware Seashore State Park management, and a PDF map is available."}],campingInfo:"The park has two waterfront campgrounds for RV and tent camping, plus bayfront cottages and a four-bedroom house.",fishingBoating:"Anglers can surf fish along the ocean, including from surf fishing vehicle areas, or fish at Indian River Inlet, and boaters can use the park's boat ramp and the Indian River Marina.",wildlife:["Birds","Crabs","Clams"],visitorCenter:{name:"Delaware Seashore State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(302) 227-2800"},hours:"Open daily from 8 a.m. until sunset",entranceFees:"$10/day for DE-registered vehicles; $20/day for non-DE-registered vehicles; walking/biking free",address:"39415 Inlet Rd, Rehoboth Beach, DE 19971",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Delaware%20Seashore%20State%20Park%20delaware",officialWebsite:"https://www.destateparks.com/park/delaware-seashore/",bestTimeToVisit:"Visit from Memorial Day Saturday through Labor Day Monday if you want guarded swimming beaches, or come in spring or fall for birding, fishing and paddling the inland bays.",tips:["From September 15, 2026 to March 15, 2027, a sand bypass system will close portions of the beaches on the north and south sides of the Indian River Inlet. Avoid the construction zones.","The Delaware Seashore park office is closed for pass and permit sales, so buy passes and permits another way before you arrive.","Driving on the beach to surf fish requires a surf fishing permit, and daily entrance fees apply from March 1 to November 30."],nearbyAttractions:["Fenwick Island State Park","Holts Landing State Park","Thompson Island Nature Preserve"]},{id:"lums-pond-state-park",name:"Lums Pond State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/54779619be77df6f.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Delaware park around Lums Pond, a 19th-century reservoir built to feed the C&D Canal locks.",fullDescription:"Lums Pond State Park covers 1,790 acres near Bear and surrounds Lums Pond, built on St. Georges Creek to supply water to the locks of the Chesapeake and Delaware Canal in the early 1800s. Rowboats, kayaks, canoes and pedal boats can be rented, and the pond holds largemouth bass, crappie and stocked hybrid striped bass.",thingsToDo:["Boat rentals","Fishing","Hiking","Camping","Zipline course (Go Ape)"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Little Jersey Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"8 miles",description:"An 8-mile trail described as enjoyable for all skill levels that provides access to the Lums Pond Woods Nature Preserve."},{name:"Swamp Forest Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"Described by the park as a more challenging trail for experienced bikers."},{name:"Mile Loop Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1 mile",description:"A short forest walk with scenic views of Lums Pond."}],campingInfo:"The newly renovated year-round campground offers spacious RV sites with three-point hookups as well as tent sites, and new cabins are under construction.",fishingBoating:"Visitors can fish or paddle on Delaware's largest freshwater pond, which has a boat ramp and fishing pier, using rented kayaks, canoes or pedalboats or their own boats; the pond holds carp, pickerel, crappie, catfish, largemouth bass and stocked hybrid striped bass.",wildlife:["turtles","Great Blue Herons","osprey","largemouth bass","catfish"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lums Pond State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(302) 368-6989"},hours:"8:00 a.m. to sunset, daily",entranceFees:"DE registered vehicle: $5/day; Non-DE registered vehicle: $10/day; Walking/biking: Free",address:"1068 Howell School Rd, Bear, DE 19701",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lums%20Pond%20State%20Park%20delaware",officialWebsite:"https://www.destateparks.com/park/lums-pond/",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall is a great time to visit for foliage around the pond, while late spring through summer suits paddling and the Go Ape course, which is open March through November.",tips:["New cabin construction runs 7 a.m.-4 p.m. Monday through Friday through March 2027, so expect vehicle and equipment traffic, noise and detours in the campground.","The Whale Wallow Nature Center is open year-round, but only Wednesday through Sunday from 12 p.m. to 4 p.m.","The vehicle entrance fee is charged daily from March 1 to November 30. Walkers and bikers enter free."],nearbyAttractions:["Summit North Marina","C and D Canal Museum","Glasgow Park"]},{id:"trap-pond-state-park",name:"Trap Pond State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/56e86f5785dc7e6e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to one of the northernmost natural stands of bald cypress trees in the United States, Trap Pond offers a serene escape into a unique wetland ecosystem.",fullDescription:"Trap Pond State Park was established in 1951 and encompasses a large portion of the original bald cypress swamp that once covered much of the region. The park features a 90-acre pond created in the late 1700s to power a sawmill. Today, it serves as a vital habitat for diverse wildlife and provides recreational opportunities centered around its tranquil waters and towering, moss-draped cypress trees.",thingsToDo:["Canoeing and kayaking","Fishing for bass and pickerel","Bird watching","Picnicking in shaded groves","Biking on park trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cypress Point Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.3 miles",description:"A scenic loop that takes hikers through the heart of the bald cypress swamp with excellent views of the water."},{name:"Bob Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.6 miles",description:"A longer trail that winds through diverse forest habitats and offers a more immersive experience in the park's natural landscape."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 130 campsites, including sites with full hookups for RVs, as well as several rustic camping cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["Bald eagles","Great blue herons","White-tailed deer","Painted turtles","Barred owls"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bald Cypress Nature Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits about the swamp ecosystem, local history, and the unique flora and fauna of the area.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(302) 875-5153"},hours:"8:00 AM to Sunset daily",address:"33587 Bald Cypress Lane, Laurel, DE 19956",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Trap+Pond+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.destateparks.com/park/trap-pond/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild weather and peak bird migration activity.",tips:["Rent a kayak or canoe to explore the cypress swamp from the water for the best views.","Bring insect repellent, especially during the warmer summer months.","Visit during the early morning hours to increase your chances of spotting wildlife."],nearbyAttractions:["Laurel Heritage Museum","Nanticoke River","Delmarva Discovery Center","Bethel Maritime Museum"],entranceFees:"Daily vehicle entrance fee is $4.00 for Delaware residents and $8.00 for out-of-state visitors.",fishingBoating:"The park features a boat ramp for private vessels and offers seasonal rentals for canoes, kayaks, and pedal boats."},{id:"brandywine-creek-state-park",name:"Brandywine Creek State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/02664dfb8fb9a21b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled along the scenic Brandywine Creek, this park offers rolling hills, historic stone walls, and lush forests just minutes from Wilmington.",fullDescription:"Brandywine Creek State Park features a landscape shaped by centuries of agricultural use and the natural flow of the Brandywine River. The park is renowned for its steep slopes, tulip poplar forests, and expansive meadows that provide critical habitat for diverse wildlife. Visitors can explore the remnants of historic farmsteads and enjoy the tranquil beauty of the Piedmont region.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and trail running","Bird watching","Picnicking in designated areas","Photography","Cross-country skiing in winter"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Tulip Tree Woods Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A gentle path winding through a mature forest featuring impressive tulip poplar trees."},{name:"Rocky Run Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail follows the scenic Rocky Run stream, offering varied terrain and beautiful woodland views."}],campingInfo:"Brandywine Creek State Park does not offer overnight camping or cabin rentals.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Red fox","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Brandywine Creek Nature Center",info:"The nature center provides educational exhibits about the local ecosystem and park history.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(302) 577-3534"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"41 Adams Dam Rd, Wilmington, DE 19807",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Brandywine+Creek+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://destateparks.com/FieldsStreams/BrandywineCreek",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Wear sturdy hiking boots as some trails can be muddy after rain.","Bring binoculars for excellent bird watching opportunities in the meadows.","Check the official website for seasonal hunting closures before planning your hike."],nearbyAttractions:["Winterthur Museum, Garden and Library","Hagley Museum and Library","Wilmington city center","Longwood Gardens"],entranceFees:"Daily vehicle entrance fee is $4.00 for Delaware residents and $8.00 for out-of-state vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Brandywine Creek with a valid Delaware fishing license; no boat ramps are available."},{id:"killens-pond-state-park",name:"Killens Pond State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-killens-pond-state-park-delaware.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Killens Pond State Park is a scenic destination featuring a 66-acre millpond, a popular water park, and extensive trails through lush woodlands.",fullDescription:"Located in the heart of Kent County, Killens Pond State Park centers around a historic millpond created in the late 18th century. The park offers a diverse landscape of hardwood forests, wetlands, and open fields. It is widely recognized for its family-friendly amenities, including a large water park, boat rentals, and a well-maintained campground, making it a premier outdoor recreation hub in Delaware.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the Killens Pond Water Park","Kayaking and canoeing on the millpond","Fishing for bass and perch","Disc golfing on the 18-hole course","Picnicking in designated shaded areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pondside Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic path that winds along the edge of the millpond, offering excellent views of the water and local waterfowl."},{name:"Deep Woods Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.6 miles",description:"This trail takes hikers through dense hardwood forests and provides a quiet, immersive nature experience away from the main park areas."}],campingInfo:"The park features 59 campsites with water and electric hookups, as well as several camping cabins available for rent, all nestled in a wooded setting.",wildlife:["Great Blue Heron","White-tailed Deer","Painted Turtle","Red-tailed Hawk","Wild Turkey"],visitorCenter:{name:"Killens Pond Nature Center",info:"The Nature Center features interactive exhibits about the local ecosystem, pond life, and the history of the millpond.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(302) 284-4299"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"5025 Killens Pond Rd, Felton, DE 19943",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Killens+Pond+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.destateparks.com/park/killens-pond/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Summer for water activities and blooming flora.",tips:["Visit the water park early on weekends as it reaches capacity quickly during peak summer days.","Bring insect repellent if you plan to hike the woodland trails during the warmer months.","Check the official website for seasonal boat rental availability before arriving."],nearbyAttractions:["Delaware State Fairgrounds","Abbott's Mill Nature Center","Downtown Milford","Bombay Hook National Wildlife Refuge"],entranceFees:"Daily vehicle entrance fee is $4.00 for Delaware residents and $8.00 for out-of-state vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The 66-acre pond is open for fishing and boating. Boat rentals including kayaks, canoes, and pedal boats are available seasonally at the boat house."},{id:"white-clay-creek-state-park",name:"White Clay Creek State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/111009069e7b988e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A sprawling natural sanctuary featuring lush woodlands, scenic creek valleys, and an extensive network of multi-use trails.",fullDescription:"White Clay Creek State Park encompasses over 3,600 acres of diverse landscape, ranging from steep, wooded ravines to open meadows. The park is named for the creek that winds through its center, which is a designated National Wild and Scenic River. Historically, the area was home to various mills and agricultural operations, and today it serves as a vital corridor for wildlife and outdoor recreation enthusiasts.",thingsToDo:["Mountain biking","Fly fishing","Disc golf","Bird watching","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"David English Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle, scenic path that follows the creek, perfect for families and casual walkers."},{name:"Bryan's Field Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.2 miles",description:"This trail traverses through open fields and dense forest, offering excellent opportunities to spot local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park offers the Wedgewood Campground, which features sites for tents and RVs, including electric and water hookups.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Red fox","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Eastern box turtle"],visitorCenter:{name:"Carpenter Recreation Area Office",info:"Provides park maps, trail information, and educational displays about the local ecosystem.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(302) 368-6900"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"880 New London Rd, Newark, DE 19711",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=White+Clay+Creek+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://destateparks.com/FieldsStreams/WhiteClayCreek",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Check the park website for seasonal hunting closures on certain trails.","Bring insect repellent during the summer months, especially near the creek.","Visit the disc golf course early on weekends to avoid crowds."],nearbyAttractions:["University of Delaware","Newark Reservoir","Fair Hill Natural Resources Management Area","Main Street Newark"],entranceFees:"$4.00 for Delaware residents and $8.00 for out-of-state vehicles.",fishingBoating:"The creek is a popular destination for trout fishing; a valid Delaware fishing license and trout stamp are required."},{id:"alapocas-run-state-park",name:"Alapocas Run State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0e19406014b3f3a5.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A scenic urban oasis featuring dramatic rock cliffs, lush woodlands, and the historic Blue Ball Barn.",fullDescription:"Alapocas Run State Park is a unique natural area located in the heart of Wilmington, Delaware. The park is defined by the deep, rocky valley carved by Alapocas Run, which features impressive granite cliffs that are popular with rock climbers. The park also houses the Blue Ball Barn, a historic dairy barn that now serves as a cultural center and museum showcasing Delaware's history and folk art.",thingsToDo:["Rock climbing on the granite cliffs","Hiking and trail running","Visiting the Blue Ball Barn","Picnicking in the designated areas","Exploring the Northern Delaware Greenway Trail"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Northern Delaware Greenway Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A paved, multi-use trail that winds through the park's woodlands and connects to other regional greenway segments."},{name:"Alapocas Woods Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A natural surface trail that takes hikers along the creek and beneath the towering rock formations."}],campingInfo:"There is no overnight camping available at Alapocas Run State Park.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Red fox","Great blue heron","Wild turkey","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Blue Ball Barn",info:"The visitor center features exhibits on Delaware's history, local art, and information about the park's natural resources.",hours:"Wednesday-Sunday: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(302) 577-1164"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"1914 W Park Dr, Wilmington, DE 19803",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Alapocas+Run+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://www.destateparks.com/park/alapocas-run/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Check the park website for rock climbing permit requirements before visiting.","Visit the Blue Ball Barn during operating hours to see the impressive folk art collection.","The park is very popular on weekends; arrive early to secure parking near the main trailheads."],nearbyAttractions:["Rockford Park","Hagley Museum and Library","Wilmington Riverfront","Winterthur Museum, Garden and Library"],entranceFees:"Daily vehicle entrance fee is $4.00 for Delaware residents and $8.00 for out-of-state vehicles.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Alapocas Run, though the creek is generally too shallow for boating."}],florida:[{id:"john-pennekamp-coral-reef",name:"John Pennekamp Coral Reef State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c6959ab46b7186d2.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"The first undersea state park in the nation, famous for its vibrant coral reefs and the Christ of the Abyss statue.",fullDescription:'John Pennekamp Coral Reef State Park, located in Key Largo, Florida, is a spectacular marine sanctuary covering approximately 70 nautical square miles of Atlantic Ocean. It was the first underwater park established in the United States. The park is renowned for its living coral reefs, colorful marine life, mangrove swamps, and the famous "Christ of the Abyss" bronze statue submerged in 25 feet of water. Visitors can snorkel, scuba dive, take glass-bottom boat tours, or kayak through quiet mangrove channels.',thingsToDo:["Snorkeling and scuba diving on the coral reef","Taking a 2.5-hour Glass-Bottom Boat Tour","Kayaking and canoeing through mangrove canals","Relaxing on Cannon Beach","Viewing the giant saltwater aquariums at the visitor center"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Mangrove Wilderness Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A flat wooden boardwalk winding deep into a dense mangrove swamp, featuring interpretive signs explaining the unique coastal swamp ecosystem."},{name:"Wild Tamarind Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.4 miles",description:"A short loop path showcasing native hardwood hammock trees, wild tamarind, and beautiful tropical butterflies."}],campingInfo:"The park features 47 modern campsites with full RV hookups (water, sewer, and electricity), modern restrooms with hot showers, and a laundry station.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in designated areas of the park in accordance with Florida state laws. Boating is highly active. Snorkel boats and kayak rentals are available on-site at the park marina.",wildlife:["Bottlenose dolphins","West Indian manatees","Stingrays and Nurse sharks","Vibrant reef fish (parrotfish, angelfish)","Great white herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"John Pennekamp Visitor Center & Aquarium",info:"Features massive 30,000-gallon saltwater aquariums, historical marine displays, and a theater showing educational films about the coral reef.",phone:"(305) 451-1202",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM"},hours:"8:00 AM to Sunset daily.",entranceFees:"$8.00 per vehicle daily fee.",address:"102601 Overseas Hwy, Key Largo, FL 33037",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=John+Pennekamp+Coral+Reef+State+Park+Key+Largo+FL",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/john-pennekamp-coral-reef-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"December to May for calm ocean waters, clear snorkeling visibility, and comfortable weather.",tips:["Booking reef snorkel or glass-bottom boat tours online in advance is highly recommended, as they sell out daily.","Use reef-safe sunscreen to help protect the delicate, living coral reefs.","Rent a kayak to paddle through the quiet mangrove loops; it's one of the best ways to spot juvenile barracudas and wading herons."],nearbyAttractions:["Key Largo Hammock State Botanical Site","Dolphin Cove","Islamorada Founders Park","Everglades National Park (Main Entrance)"]},{id:"bahia-honda-state-park",name:"Bahia Honda State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Florida Keys park with a 2.5-mile white-sand beach and the old Bahia Honda Bridge.",fullDescription:"Bahia Honda State Park covers 524 acres on Bahia Honda Key, about 12 miles west of Marathon near the Seven Mile Bridge. Its 2.5-mile natural sand beach was rated America's top beach in 1992. A nature trail skirts a tidal lagoon, and a section of the old Bahia Honda railroad bridge serves as a viewpoint.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Snorkeling","Kayaking","Bicycling","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Old Bahia Honda Bridge Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"0.4 miles",description:"This walk onto part of Henry Flagler's old railroad bridge is steeped in history and offers a spectacular view of the waters below."},{name:"Silver Palm Nature Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This nature trail passed through hammock where rare silver palms grow, but Florida Hikes reported it was no longer accessible after Hurricane Irma damaged the Atlantic side of the park."}],campingInfo:"The park offers primitive and full hook-up campsites as well as vacation cabins, but reservations for the winter months can be very difficult to get.",fishingBoating:"The park has fishing spots, kayak rentals and a marina with a boat launch and overnight boat slips, but boat trailer parking is limited and can fill on holidays.",wildlife:["Sea turtles","Miami blue butterfly","Rays","Barracuda","Nurse shark"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bahia Honda State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(305) 872-2353"},hours:"8 a.m. to sunset, 365 days a year",entranceFees:"$8 per vehicle (two to eight people)",address:"36850 Overseas Highway, Big Pine Key, FL 33043",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bahia%20Honda%20State%20Park%20florida",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/bahia-honda-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"The park's tropical climate and year-round sea breezes make it pleasant in any season, and winter is the most popular time, so book campsites well ahead and arrive early on weekends and holidays.",tips:["The park may temporarily close when its day-use areas reach maximum capacity, so arrive early on weekends and holidays.","Shade is limited on the sandy beaches, so bring umbrellas or sunshades or rent them, along with chairs and snorkel gear, from the park concessionaire.","Boat trailer parking at the marina is limited and can fill on holidays, which prevents launching; book trailer parking in advance through the concessionaire at (305) 872-3210."],nearbyAttractions:["Seven Mile Bridge"]},{id:"silver-springs-state-park",name:"Silver Springs State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/7b776719c2030cc7.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Florida park on the Silver River, home of Silver Springs and its famous glass-bottom boat tours.",fullDescription:"Silver Springs State Park in Marion County contains Silver Springs, one of the largest artesian spring systems in the world and Florida's first major tourist attraction. Glass-bottom boat tours run from the historic springhead, and visitors can paddle the Silver River or use 15 miles of trails. The park also has a museum, a pioneer village and a campground.",thingsToDo:["Glass-bottom boat tours","Kayaking and canoeing","Hiking and biking","Camping","Silver River Museum"],hikingTrails:[{name:"South Silver Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"2.5 miles",description:"Connects the springs to the Silver River Museum parking lot and the campground."},{name:"Silver Spring River Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"Not listed",description:"Its river access provides a beautiful view of the Silver River."}],campingInfo:"The park offers campgrounds, cabins and primitive group camping; Wikipedia describes a 59-site full-facility campground and 10 cabins.",fishingBoating:"Visitors can paddle the five-mile Silver River from the park's canoe/kayak launch, rent kayaks, canoes or paddleboards from the park's outfitter, or ride the famous glass bottom boats; a $4 per vessel launch fee applies.",wildlife:["Manatees","American alligators","Rhesus macaques","White-tailed deer","Gopher tortoises"],visitorCenter:{name:"Silver Springs State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(352) 236-7149"},hours:"8 a.m. to sunset, 365 days a year; equestrian entrance and canoe/kayak launch close 90 minutes before park closes",entranceFees:"$8 per vehicle (2-8 people); $5 single-occupant vehicle or motorcycle; $2 pedestrians, bicyclists, extra passengers",address:"5656 E. Silver Springs Blvd, Silver Springs, FL 34488",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Silver%20Springs%20State%20Park%20florida",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/silver-springs-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Glass bottom boat tours and paddling over the clear spring run are offered year-round, but the cooler, drier months from fall through spring are the most comfortable time for hiking the park's sandhill trails.",tips:["The Sea Hunt Deck is closed; the new trail system is open and all trails are open.","The park has multiple entrances. The campground and Silver River Museum use a separate entrance at 1425 NE 58th Ave, Ocala, off Baseline Road (County Road 35).","The Silver River Museum and Environmental Education Center is open to the public only on weekends, 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. The equestrian entrance and canoe/kayak launch close 90 minutes before the park closes."],nearbyAttractions:["Juniper Springs Recreation Area","Ray Wayside Park","Forest Animal Rescue"]},{id:"myakka-river-state-park",name:"Myakka River State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"One of Florida's largest and oldest state parks: 37,000 acres of river and wetlands, with a treetop canopy walk.",fullDescription:"Myakka River State Park covers 37,000 acres east of Sarasota and was laid out by the Civilian Conservation Corps in the 1930s. It is noted for wildlife, including alligators and roseate spoonbills. A 6.5-mile road leads to a river boardwalk and to the canopy walkway, North America's first public treetop trail, with a suspension bridge and tower.",thingsToDo:["Canopy walkway","Boat tours","Canoeing","Biking","Wildlife viewing","Camping and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Canopy Walkway",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A treetop walkway with a suspension bridge and tower that gives expansive views of the prairie and an above-the-treetops view of the park."},{name:"The Birdwalk",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"A boardwalk that the park calls a phenomenal place to view wildlife, including birds and alligators."},{name:"Myakka Hiking Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"34.5 miles",description:"A long loop trail that AllTrails describes as moderately challenging, taking an average of nearly 10 hours to complete."}],campingInfo:"The park offers campgrounds and cabins, and six primitive campgrounds reachable by trail: Mossy Hammock, Bee Island, Panther Point, Honore, Oak Grove and Prairie.",fishingBoating:"Boating, fishing, canoeing and kayaking are popular on the Myakka River and its lakes, and the park has a boat ramp, a canoe/kayak launch, and canoe rentals at the Myakka Outpost.",wildlife:["American alligator","Turtles","Limpkin","Osprey","Roseate spoonbill"],visitorCenter:{name:"Myakka River State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(941) 361-6511"},hours:"8 a.m. to sunset, 365 days a year; North Gate open only Saturday, Sunday and state holidays, 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.",entranceFees:"$6 per vehicle (2-8 people); $4 single-occupant vehicle; $2 pedestrians, bicyclists, extra passengers",address:"13207 State Road 72, Sarasota, FL 34241",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Myakka%20River%20State%20Park%20florida",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/myakka-river-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"The cooler, drier months from late fall through spring are the most comfortable for hiking, biking and viewing the park's birds and alligators in Florida's warm climate.",tips:["Drinking water is temporarily unavailable in the park, so bring your own. Free bottled water is offered at the ranger station from 8 a.m. to sunset.","Only 30 hiking permits for the Deep Hole trail are issued each morning at opening. Camping in the park the night before improves your chances of getting one.","The North Gate is open only on Saturdays, Sundays and state holidays (8 a.m. to 5 p.m.). Expect heavy bicycle traffic on the main drive on weekends and holidays."],nearbyAttractions:["Crowley Museum & Nature Center","Myakka State Forest","Deer Prairie Creek Preserve"]},{id:"wakulla-springs-state-park",name:"Wakulla Springs State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Florida park south of Tallahassee around Wakulla Springs, one of the world's largest and deepest springs.",fullDescription:"Edward Ball Wakulla Springs State Park is a 6,000-acre wildlife sanctuary south of Tallahassee, listed on the National Register and a National Natural Landmark. Its spring is one of the world's largest and deepest freshwater springs. Manatees visit in winter, alligators live year-round, and the park has a historic lodge, river boat tours and a swimming area.",thingsToDo:["River boat tours","Swimming","Hiking","Wildlife viewing","Historic lodge"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Sally Ward Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"6.2 miles",description:"An easy trail with about 59 feet of elevation gain that takes roughly 1.5–2 hours to complete and can be combined with the Hammock Loop for a hike of nearly 10 miles through river forest landscapes."},{name:"Wakulla Hammock Spur Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A shorter loop listed among the park's trails, which wind through pine forests, bald cypress wetlands and hardwood hammock."}],campingInfo:"The material does not confirm a campground, but overnight stays are available at the historic Lodge at Wakulla Springs, a full-service hotel inside the park.",fishingBoating:"Guided riverboat tours of the spring and Wakulla River operate year-round, while fishing is not confirmed in the material.",wildlife:["West Indian manatee","American alligator","White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Gar"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wakulla Springs State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(850) 561-7276"},hours:"8 a.m. to sunset, 365 days a year (the Lodge is open 24 hours)",entranceFees:"$6 per vehicle (2-8 occupants); $4 single-occupant vehicle; $2 per extra vehicle occupant; $2 pedestrian or bicyclist",address:"465 Wakulla Park Drive, Wakulla Springs, FL 32327",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wakulla%20Springs%20State%20Park%20florida",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/edward-ball-wakulla-springs-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Winter is best for spotting manatees in the spring and river, while summer is ideal for swimming in the spring's refreshing 70-degree water.",tips:["The park recommends a guided riverboat tour as the best way to experience the river and see alligators, manatees and abundant bird life.","Don't count on a glass-bottom boat tour; they now run rarely because of reduced water clarity, though standard boat tours operate all year.","Florida State Parks annual passes can be bought at the park ranger station during regular business hours if you need immediate use."],nearbyAttractions:["St. Marks National Wildlife Refuge & Lighthouse","San Marcos de Apalache Historic State Park","Leon Sinks Geological Area"]},{id:"wekiwa-springs-state-park",name:"Wekiwa Springs State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Florida park near Orlando around a spring that pours about 42 million gallons a day into the Wekiva River.",fullDescription:"Wekiwa Springs State Park covers 7,000 acres in Apopka, about 20 minutes north of Orlando, and contains the headwaters of the Wekiva River. Its spring sends about 42 million gallons a day into the river; the swimming area ranges from under a foot to five feet deep. Visitors can paddle the river, hike and camp.",thingsToDo:["Spring swimming","Canoeing and kayaking","Hiking","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Wekiwa Springs Volksmarch Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"Not listed",description:"A loop trail through the park marked with orange diamonds."}],campingInfo:"The park supports camping from tents to RVs, with family, primitive, equestrian and group camping areas, plus four campsites reachable only by canoe or kayak.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted, and canoes and kayaks can be rented on-site for paddling Wekiwa Springs Run, Rock Springs Run and the Wekiva River.",wildlife:["black bear","alligator","deer","bobcat","gray fox"],visitorCenter:{name:"Wekiwa Springs State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(407) 553-4348"},hours:"8 a.m. to sunset, 365 days a year",entranceFees:"$6 per vehicle (limit of eight people); $4 single-occupant vehicle; $2 pedestrians, bicyclists, extra passengers",address:"1800 Wekiwa Circle, Apopka, FL 32712",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Wekiwa%20Springs%20State%20Park%20florida",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/wekiwa-springs-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"The spring stays a refreshing 72 degrees year-round, so summer is ideal for swimming, while the cooler months are more comfortable for hiking and biking the park's sandhill trails.",tips:["Reservations are required for day-use entry (effective Sept. 2, 2025), so book before you go.","The park entry fee is collected online when you make your day-use reservation, with no additional reservation fee.","Campers planning to arrive after sunset should call the park on the day of arrival at 407-553-4383."],nearbyAttractions:["Lower Wekiva River Preserve State Park","Lake Apopka Wildlife Drive","Wekiva Island"]},{id:"honeymoon-island-state-park",name:"Honeymoon Island State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/53bfe9f2d08fd12c.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Gulf barrier island off Dunedin with 4 miles of beaches, mangroves and the ferry to Caladesi Island.",fullDescription:"Honeymoon Island State Park protects 2,824 acres of a barrier island west of Dunedin, with about 4.25 miles of Gulf shoreline plus seagrass beds, mangroves and pine flatwoods. Amenities include North and South Beach bathhouses, a pet beach, and the Rotary Centennial Nature Center. A ferry runs from the park to Caladesi Island State Park.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Shelling","Hiking","Biking","Birdwatching","Ferry to Caladesi Island"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Osprey Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail runs through one of Florida's last remaining virgin slash pine forests and offers a close-up look at an osprey rookery."},{name:"Pelican Cove Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"0.75 miles",description:"A shorter trail that, with the Osprey Trail, explores the park's virgin slash pine forest."}],campingInfo:"Check the official park website for current camping details.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is one of the park's most popular activities, with the beach offering saltwater fish such as snook, Spanish mackerel, redfish and cobia, and paddling is also listed among park experiences.",wildlife:["Osprey","Great horned owl","Gopher tortoise","Loggerhead sea turtle","Armadillo"],visitorCenter:{name:"Honeymoon Island State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(727) 241-6106"},hours:"8 a.m. to sunset, 365 days a year",entranceFees:"$8 per vehicle (limit eight people); $4 single-occupant vehicle; $2 pedestrians",address:"#1 Causeway Blvd., Dunedin, FL 34698",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Honeymoon%20Island%20State%20Park%20florida",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/honeymoon-island-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late fall through spring brings mild Gulf Coast weather and the park's important wintering migratory shorebirds, while summer suits swimming but is hotter and coincides with sea turtle nesting.",tips:["Since April 9, 2024, erosion has cut off the northernmost mile of the sandspit from the main island; do not venture into or through the water there because of deep water, strong currents and hidden hazards.","The park is the ferry terminal for Caladesi Island State Park, and the Caladesi Island Ferry leaves from a dock near the beach concession.","The $8 vehicle fee covers up to eight people, a single-occupant vehicle pays $4, and pedestrians pay $2."],nearbyAttractions:["Caladesi Island State Park","Fred Marquis Pinellas Trail","Anclote Keys"]},{id:"ichetucknee-springs-state-park",name:"Ichetucknee Springs State Park",image:"",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Florida spring-fed river park famous for summer tubing on the clear Ichetucknee River.",fullDescription:"Ichetucknee Springs State Park covers 2,241 acres near Fort White and is a National Natural Landmark. It centers on the 6-mile Ichetucknee River, which flows through hammocks and wetlands to the Santa Fe River. Tubing down the clear river is the main summer activity, from late May to early September.",thingsToDo:["Tubing (summer)","Swimming and snorkeling","Kayaking and canoeing","Hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trestle Point Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This trail offers views of the park's blue spring water."},{name:"Pine Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This trail winds through sandhills with glimpses of springs from above."},{name:"Blue Hole Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This nature trail leads toward Blue Hole, a spring that stays a constant 72°F year-round and is popular for snorkeling."}],campingInfo:"Check the official park website for current camping details.",fishingBoating:"Paddlers can use the park's canoe and kayak launch, and canoe, kayak and paddleboard rentals are offered at the park; the official page says the upper river is best enjoyed by canoe or kayak during the cooler months.",wildlife:["beaver","otter","softshell turtle","wild turkey","limpkin"],visitorCenter:{name:"Ichetucknee Springs State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(386) 497-4690"},hours:"8 a.m. until sunset, 365 days a year",entranceFees:"$6 per vehicle (two to eight people); $4 single-occupant vehicle; $4 motorcycle (one or two people); $2 pedestrians, bicyclists, extra passengers",address:"12087 S.W. U.S. Highway 27, Fort White, FL 32038",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Ichetucknee%20Springs%20State%20Park%20florida",officialWebsite:"https://www.floridastateparks.org/parks-and-trails/ichetucknee-springs-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late May through early September is peak season for tubing on the 72°F spring-fed river, while the cooler months are best for a quieter canoe or kayak trip on the pristine upper river.",tips:["The Midpoint Tube Launch has been closed for repairs since March 17, 2026. Tubers can still launch from Dampier's Landing at the South Entrance until 5 p.m., and tube rentals end at 4:30 p.m.","Because Midpoint is closed, the tram fee has been temporarily reduced from $10 to $7.50.","Tubing from the North Entrance is seasonal. It runs from the Friday before Memorial Day and closes the day after Labor Day."],nearbyAttractions:["Santa Fe River","Ginnie Springs"]}],georgia:[{id:"tallulah-gorge-state-park",name:"Tallulah Gorge State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/278a42be7c279795.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Georgia park around a 1,000-foot-deep gorge with six waterfalls and a suspension bridge.",fullDescription:"Tallulah Gorge State Park covers 2,689 acres at Tallulah Falls in northeast Georgia. The Tallulah River runs through a gorge about 1,000 feet deep, dropping 500 feet in a mile over six waterfalls. Trails follow the rim to overlooks and a suspension bridge, and free permits from the Interpretive Center allow hiking to the gorge floor.",thingsToDo:["Rim trail overlooks","Suspension bridge","Gorge floor hike (permit)","Lake beach (seasonal)","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Gorge Floor (Sliding Rock) Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"Not listed",description:"This very strenuous trail, which includes 1,000 stairs, needs a free same-day permit issued after a safety briefing and is limited to 100 permits per day."},{name:"Stoneplace Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The Stoneplace Trail leaves park property and enters an adjacent Wildlife Management Area, so hikers and bikers are encouraged to wear reflective orange."},{name:"Rails to Trails path",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"1.7 miles",description:"This paved path follows an old railroad bed and is suited to strollers and bicycles."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 52 tent, trailer and RV campsites, a pioneer campground and 2 backcountry Adirondack shelters.",fishingBoating:"Fishing and paddling are offered at the park's 63-acre lake, but kayaking at the Terrora Day Use Area is unavailable until construction of a new ADA kayak launch is finished.",wildlife:["green salamander","peregrine falcon"],visitorCenter:{name:"Tallulah Gorge State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(706) 754-7981"},hours:"Park 8 a.m. to dark; office 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.",entranceFees:"$10 daily ParkPass per vehicle (parking fee required; annual passes available)",address:"338 Jane Hurt Yarn Drive, Tallulah Falls, GA 30573",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tallulah%20Gorge%20State%20Park%20georgia",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/TallulahGorge",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall is a great time to visit for leaf watching and the scheduled aesthetic and whitewater water releases, but if you want to hike the gorge floor, choose a non-release day.",tips:["Gorge floor permits are free but limited to 100 per day and cannot be reserved. They are issued at the Interpretive Center after safety briefings at 8 a.m., 10 a.m. and 12 p.m., and on busy days they often run out early in the morning.","Hiking and climbing in the gorge are not allowed on water release dates, including aesthetic releases on October 21, 23 and 31 and November release weekends. Call ahead, because weather and water flow can also restrict access.","Pets are not allowed on the gorge floor, the Sliding Rock trail or the Hurricane Falls staircase to the suspension bridge. Leashed pets are welcome on rim trails."],nearbyAttractions:["Panther Creek Recreation Area","Traveler's Rest State Historic Site","Black Rock Mountain State Park"]},{id:"cloudland-canyon-state-park",name:"Cloudland Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/069fe25b4c8770e6.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Georgia park on Lookout Mountain's west edge, overlooking a deep canyon with two waterfalls.",fullDescription:"Cloudland Canyon State Park covers 3,485 acres near Trenton on the western edge of Lookout Mountain, where Sitton Gulch Creek has cut a gorge with elevations from 800 to over 1,800 feet. A strenuous stair trail descends to Cherokee Falls and Hemlock Falls at the canyon bottom. The park has cottages, campsites and backcountry sites.",thingsToDo:["Waterfall hike","Canyon rim overlooks","Hiking","Disc golf","Camping and cottages"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1 mile",description:"A short round-trip trail that starts at the Main Overlook and offers views over the canyon."},{name:"Waterfalls Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"1.8 miles",description:"This round-trip trail descends into the canyon on about 600 stairsteps each way to Cherokee Falls and Hemlock Falls."},{name:"West Rim Loop Trail",difficulty:"Moderately Strenuous",length:"5 miles",description:"This loop starts at the Daniels Creek Bridge and offers panoramic views of the canyon, Trenton and neighboring Sand Mountain."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 75 tent, trailer and RV campsites, 30 walk-in campsites, 13 backcountry campsites and 4 pioneer campsites, plus 10 yurts and 18 cottages.",fishingBoating:"The park has a fishing pond, but fishing is not allowed in Daniels and Sitton Gulch creeks in the canyon.",wildlife:["Bears"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cloudland Canyon State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(706) 657-4050"},hours:"Park 7 a.m. to 10 p.m.; office 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.",entranceFees:"Parking fee required: $10 daily ParkPass per vehicle (annual ParkPass $70)",address:"122 Cloudland Canyon Park Road, Rising Fawn, GA 30738",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cloudland%20Canyon%20State%20Park%20georgia",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/CloudlandCanyon",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall is a highlight for leaf watching on the Cumberland Plateau, and spring is good for waterfall flow, since the creeks feeding the falls can run low or dry in summer and early fall.",tips:["Road work is underway at the park entrance, so expect one-lane traffic with flaggers during the day.","The Visitor Center is under construction. Follow the red and white signs to the temporary Visitor Center at the Group Lodge.","Parking is not included with overnight reservations, but overnight guests pay the daily ParkPass fee only once per vehicle for their whole stay."],nearbyAttractions:["Rock City","Ruby Falls","Chickamauga & Chattanooga National Military Park"]},{id:"amicalola-falls-state-park-lodge",name:"Amicalola Falls State Park & Lodge",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9a4485d1be853d2e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"North Georgia park home to 729-foot Amicalola Falls, with a lodge and the trail to the Appalachian Trail's start.",fullDescription:"Amicalola Falls State Park & Lodge covers 829 acres near Dawsonville. Its name comes from a Cherokee word meaning 'tumbling waters', and its 729-foot Amicalola Falls is called the highest in Georgia. An 8-mile trail from the park leads to Springer Mountain, the southern end of the Appalachian Trail. The park has a lodge, restaurant and cabins.",thingsToDo:["Waterfall trails","Appalachian Trail approach trail","Hiking","Lodge and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Lodge Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.25 miles",description:"A short, easy loop trail in Amicalola Falls State Park listed by Discover Georgia Outdoors."},{name:"Appalachian Approach Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"8.5 miles",description:"This trail begins at the base of Amicalola Falls and leads to Springer Mountain, the southern terminus of the Appalachian Trail."}],campingInfo:"The park's campground has 18 wooded campsites for tent camping and RVs, plus lodge rooms, cabins and glamping.",fishingBoating:"Check the official park website for current boating and fishing details.",wildlife:["Black bear"],visitorCenter:{name:"Amicalola Falls State Park & Lodge Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(706) 344-1500"},hours:"Park 7 a.m. to 10 p.m.; visitor center 8 a.m. to 5 p.m.",entranceFees:"$10 GA State Park Pass per vehicle",address:"418 Amicalola Falls Lodge Drive, Dawsonville, GA 30534",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Amicalola%20Falls%20State%20Park%20%26%20Lodge%20georgia",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/AmicalolaFalls",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall is a great time to visit for North Georgia's fall colors, when the park offers ranger-led hikes from the Visitor Center to the 729-foot waterfall.",tips:["All vehicles entering the park require a $10 GA State Park Pass.","Black bears live in the area and have an excellent sense of smell, so store food and scented items securely.","Guests of the backcountry Len Foote Hike Inn check in at the park before hiking about 5 miles to the inn."],nearbyAttractions:["Springer Mountain","Len Foote Hike Inn","Edge of the World on Amicalola Creek"]},{id:"providence-canyon-state-park",name:"Providence Canyon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/aeaccac9e1e668c4.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Known as Georgia's Little Grand Canyon, this park features massive gullies created by poor 19th-century farming practices.",fullDescription:"Providence Canyon State Park is a geological wonder located in Stewart County, Georgia. The deep, colorful gullies were formed primarily due to erosion caused by poor farming practices in the 1800s. Today, the park offers stunning views of layered soil in shades of pink, orange, red, and purple, providing a unique landscape that contrasts sharply with the surrounding pine forests.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the canyon floor","Photography of geological formations","Picnicking in designated areas","Stargazing","Bird watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Canyon Loop Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail takes hikers along the rim of the canyon before descending to the floor to explore the unique erosion patterns."},{name:"White Blaze Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7.0 miles",description:"A longer, more challenging trek that circles the entire canyon area, offering diverse terrain and expansive views."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 6 walk-in campsites for tent camping. There are no RV hookups available at this location.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Various songbirds","Box turtles","Red-tailed hawks"],visitorCenter:{name:"Providence Canyon Interpretive Center",info:"Features exhibits on the history of the canyon's formation and local geology.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(229) 838-6202"},hours:"7:00 AM - 6:00 PM daily",address:"8930 Canyon Rd, Lumpkin, GA 31815",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Providence+Canyon+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/ProvidenceCanyon",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and blooming wildflowers.",tips:["Wear sturdy hiking boots as the canyon floor can be muddy.","Bring plenty of water, especially if hiking the longer trails.","Visit during the week to avoid weekend crowds."],nearbyAttractions:["Florence Marina State Park","Westville Historic Village","Callaway Gardens"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle parking fee.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not permitted within the canyon area; however, nearby Florence Marina State Park offers extensive fishing and boating on Lake Walter F. George."},{id:"sweetwater-creek-state-park",name:"Sweetwater Creek State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/908e3b40e34723c7.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A stunning park featuring the ruins of a Civil War-era textile mill and beautiful rapids along Sweetwater Creek.",fullDescription:"Located just a short drive from downtown Atlanta, Sweetwater Creek State Park is a peaceful retreat known for its rich history and diverse landscape. The park is home to the ruins of the New Manchester Manufacturing Company, a textile mill burned during the Civil War. Visitors can explore the rugged terrain, which includes rocky bluffs, deep forests, and the rushing waters of the creek, providing a unique geological experience in the Georgia Piedmont.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Fishing","Kayaking","Historical Tours","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Red Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.1 miles",description:"This trail leads hikers directly to the historic mill ruins and offers scenic views of the creek rapids."},{name:"White Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"5.0 miles",description:"A challenging loop that takes visitors through diverse forest terrain and offers elevated views of the park."}],campingInfo:"The park offers several yurts for rent, as well as primitive campsites for tent camping. No full RV hookups are available.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Great blue herons","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Beavers"],visitorCenter:{name:"Sweetwater Creek Visitor Center",info:"A LEED-certified building featuring exhibits on the park's history, local wildlife, and a gift shop.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(770) 732-5871"},hours:"7:00 AM until dark daily",address:"1750 Mt Vernon Rd, Lithia Springs, GA 30122",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sweetwater+Creek+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/SweetwaterCreek",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild temperatures and beautiful foliage.",tips:["Arrive early on weekends as the parking lots fill up quickly.","Wear sturdy hiking shoes if you plan to visit the mill ruins, as the terrain is rocky.","Check the park website for guided historical tour schedules."],nearbyAttractions:["Six Flags Over Georgia","Douglasville","Atlanta","Chattahoochee River National Recreation Area"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle parking fee",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the reservoir and creek. Kayak and canoe rentals are available seasonally at the visitor center."},{id:"fort-mountain-state-park",name:"Fort Mountain State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/ac4f36d0caff0098.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Perched atop the Cohutta Mountains, this park is famous for its mysterious 855-foot-long ancient stone wall and breathtaking panoramic views.",fullDescription:"Fort Mountain State Park is located in the Chattahoochee National Forest and is renowned for its mysterious ancient stone wall, which stretches 855 feet along the mountain ridge. The park offers a diverse landscape of hardwood forests, blueberry thickets, and mountain streams. Visitors can explore the rich history of the area, including the CCC-built stone fire tower, while enjoying the rugged beauty of the Southern Appalachian Mountains.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain Biking","Swimming","Fishing","Picnicking","Horseback Riding"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Stone Wall Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.6 miles",description:"This trail leads visitors to the mysterious ancient stone wall and offers scenic views of the surrounding valley."},{name:"Cool Springs Loop",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A gentle path that winds through lush forest areas, perfect for families and bird watching."}],campingInfo:"The park features 70 campsites for tents, trailers, and RVs, as well as 15 fully equipped cottages available for rent.",wildlife:["Black bears","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Various songbirds","Red foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Fort Mountain State Park Office",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and educational exhibits about the stone wall.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(706) 422-1932"},hours:"7:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"181 Fort Mountain Park Rd, Chatsworth, GA 30705",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fort+Mountain+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/FortMountain",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for spectacular fall foliage.",tips:["Arrive early on weekends during the fall as the parking lots fill up quickly.","Bring sturdy hiking boots as the terrain can be rocky and uneven.","Check the official website for seasonal swimming beach hours before planning your trip."],nearbyAttractions:["Chatsworth","Cohutta Wilderness","Dalton","Ellijay"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle parking fee.",fishingBoating:"The park features a 17-acre lake where visitors can enjoy fishing for bass and bream, as well as pedal boat and canoe rentals."},{id:"vogel-state-park",name:"Vogel State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/919eb5924f90952b.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled at the base of Blood Mountain in the Blue Ridge Mountains, Vogel State Park is one of Georgia's oldest and most beloved parks.",fullDescription:"Established in 1931, Vogel State Park is located in the heart of the Chattahoochee National Forest. It features a 22-acre lake fed by mountain springs, offering a stunning backdrop for outdoor recreation. The park is famous for its Civilian Conservation Corps (CCC) history, with a museum dedicated to the men who built the park during the Great Depression. The geology is defined by the rugged terrain of the Blue Ridge Mountains, providing visitors with dramatic vistas and lush forest landscapes.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the lake beach","Fishing in Lake Trahlyta","Miniature golf","Picnicking in designated areas","Exploring the CCC Museum"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Trahlyta Lake Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A gentle path that circles the lake, offering beautiful views of the water and the surrounding mountains."},{name:"Bear Hair Gap Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.0 miles",description:"A scenic trail that climbs the ridge above the park, providing a challenging hike with rewarding panoramic views."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 102 tent, trailer, and RV campsites, as well as 35 cottages available for rent, providing a variety of lodging options for visitors.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Black bears","Wild turkeys","Various songbirds","Red-tailed hawks"],visitorCenter:{name:"Vogel State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, and houses the CCC Museum exhibits.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(706) 745-2628"},hours:"7:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"405 Vogel State Park Rd, Blairsville, GA 30512",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Vogel+State+Park+Blairsville+GA",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/Vogel",bestTimeToVisit:"Autumn for the spectacular fall foliage or summer for swimming.",tips:["Arrive early on weekends during the fall, as the park reaches capacity quickly.","Bring a camera for the stunning views of the lake from the dam.","Check the official website for seasonal events and guided ranger programs."],nearbyAttractions:["Helton Creek Falls","Brasstown Bald","Blairsville town center","Appalachian Trail access points"],entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle parking fee",fishingBoating:"Lake Trahlyta is open for fishing and non-motorized boating; pedal boat and kayak rentals are available seasonally."},{id:"fd-roosevelt-state-park",name:"F.D. Roosevelt State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/b21e36e4055d0ba1.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Georgia's largest state park, featuring the historic Pine Mountain Range and the scenic Liberty Bell Pool.",fullDescription:"Located on the Pine Mountain Range, F.D. Roosevelt State Park is a sprawling 9,049-acre park that served as a retreat for President Franklin D. Roosevelt. The park offers breathtaking views of the rolling hills and dense forests of West Georgia. It is home to the famous Dowdell's Knob, a favorite overlook of the President, and provides a rich history of the Civilian Conservation Corps, who built many of the park's stone structures in the 1930s.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and backpacking","Swimming in the Liberty Bell Pool","Horseback riding on designated trails","Fishing in the park's lakes","Picnicking at scenic overlooks"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Pine Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"23.0 miles",description:"A premier trail that traverses the ridge of Pine Mountain, offering stunning vistas and access to waterfalls."},{name:"Mountain Creek Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.5-mile loop",description:"A gentle path that winds through lush forests and along the banks of Mountain Creek, perfect for birdwatching."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 111 campsites for tents, trailers, and RVs, as well as 21 fully equipped cottages for overnight stays.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red-tailed hawks","Various songbirds","Foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Park Office and Visitor Center",info:"Provides maps, trail information, and historical exhibits regarding President Roosevelt's time in the area.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(706) 663-4858"},hours:"7:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"2970 Georgia Highway 190, Pine Mountain, GA 31822",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=F.D.+Roosevelt+State+Park+Pine+Mountain+GA",officialWebsite:"https://gastateparks.org/FDRoosevelt",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Visit Dowdell's Knob at sunset for the best views in the park.","Check the park website for seasonal pool hours before planning a swim.","Bring sturdy hiking boots if you plan to tackle the longer sections of the Pine Mountain Trail."],nearbyAttractions:["Callaway Gardens","The Little White House Historic Site","Warm Springs, GA","Wild Animal Safari"],entranceFees:"$5.00 parking fee per vehicle.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the park's lakes; however, no boat rentals are available, and only electric motors are allowed on private boats."}],kentucky:[{id:"cumberland-falls-state-resort-park",name:"Cumberland Falls State Resort Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/c0392abd9cc479aa.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Kentucky park named for 68-foot Cumberland Falls, one of the few places a moonbow is often seen.",fullDescription:"Cumberland Falls State Resort Park covers 1,657 acres southwest of Corbin, inside the Daniel Boone National Forest. Its main feature is 68-foot Cumberland Falls on the Cumberland River, one of the few places in the Western Hemisphere where a moonbow can often be seen on full-moon nights. About 20 miles of trails, a lodge and cabins are on site.",thingsToDo:["Waterfall viewing","Moonbow viewing (full moon)","Hiking","Horseback rides","Whitewater rafting","Fishing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Eagle Falls Trail #9",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"An out-and-back hike with a spur, listed by the park as one of its top five things to do."},{name:"Cumberland Falls Trail #6",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"0.5 miles",description:"The most popular route to the falls, beginning in front of Dupont Lodge with an excellent view of the river and Gatliff Bridge and a total descent of 200 ft."},{name:"Cumberland River Trail #2",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"5 miles",description:"Starts at the far end of the picnic area, follows the river upstream for 2 miles, then intersects the Moonbow Trail and ends at the falls parking area."}],campingInfo:"Clifty Campground has 11 primitive sites and Ridgeline Campground has 40 sites with electric and water hookups, plus a service building with showers, restrooms, a grocery and a dump station.",fishingBoating:"The Cumberland River offers bass, catfish, panfish and roughfish, and a Kentucky fishing license is required.",wildlife:["Wood Thrush","Carolina Chickadee","White-breasted Nuthatch","Carolina Wren","Northern Cardinal"],visitorCenter:{name:"Cumberland Falls State Resort Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(606) 528-4121"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"No entrance fee (per TripAdvisor; not stated on official page)",address:"7351 KY-90, Corbin, KY 40701",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cumberland%20Falls%20State%20Resort%20Park%20kentucky",officialWebsite:"https://parks.ky.gov/corbin/parks/resort/cumberland-falls-state-resort-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall is best: spring wildflowers bloom on Anvil Branch Trail, the stables and pool run Memorial Day to Labor Day, and a clear full-moon night gives a chance to see the moonbow.",tips:["Pets are not permitted on the McCreary County side of the park because it is a dedicated Kentucky State Nature Preserve.","Dupont Lodge and Riverview Restaurant have been closed for renovations since December 1, 2025. Cabins, the falls, gift shop, welcome center and trails remain open.","Guided horseback rides are for ages 6 and up, cost $27 per person, and start on the hour from 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. in season. Call ahead for weekend rides in May, September and October."],nearbyAttractions:["Daniel Boone National Forest","Big South Fork National River and Recreation Area","Sheltowee Trace Adventure Resort"]},{id:"natural-bridge-state-resort-park",name:"Natural Bridge State Resort Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/85eb57513fe00f01.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Kentucky park built around a 78-foot sandstone arch, next to the Red River Gorge.",fullDescription:"Natural Bridge State Resort Park lies on the Middle Fork of the Red River near Slade, next to the Red River Gorge and surrounded by the Daniel Boone National Forest. Its sandstone arch spans 78 feet and stands 65 feet high; Lover's Leap and Fat Man's Squeeze are other well-known spots. About 1,200 of its roughly 2,300 acres are a state nature preserve.",thingsToDo:["Hiking to the arch","Lover's Leap overlook","Hemlock Lodge"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Balanced Rock Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"0.75 miles",description:"Over 600 steps lead to Natural Bridge, passing the on-park cave entrance and a large weathered sandstone balanced rock (trail currently closed part way)."},{name:"The Original Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"Not listed",description:"This trail from Hemlock Lodge reaches the Natural Bridge arch and is described as deceptively strenuous and challenging."},{name:"Hood's Branch Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The official park page lists this trail as one of the best places in the park to spot birds."}],campingInfo:"The park has two campgrounds, including Whittleton Campground, with improved and primitive sites and two service buildings with showers and restrooms.",fishingBoating:"Mill Creek Lake holds bass, bluegill, crappie, rainbow trout and catfish, has a launch ramp for fishing boats (electric trolling motors only), and requires a Kentucky fishing license.",wildlife:["wild turkey","great blue heron","pileated woodpecker","belted kingfisher","northern cardinal"],visitorCenter:{name:"Natural Bridge State Resort Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(606) 663-2214"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"Check the official park website for current fees.",address:"2135 Natural Bridge Rd, Slade, KY 40376",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Natural%20Bridge%20State%20Resort%20Park%20kentucky",officialWebsite:"https://parks.ky.gov/slade/parks/resort/natural-bridge-state-resort-park",bestTimeToVisit:"May and June are best for birding, and October brings fall foliage while the sky lift still runs through the last weekend of October.",tips:["Dogs are not allowed on the park's hiking trails because of its Nature Preserve status, except the pet-friendly Whittleton Trail, which is accessed via Whittleton Campground.","Trail maps are available at Hemlock Lodge, and free fishing equipment can be borrowed through the park's loaner program.","The sky lift runs daily from the first weekend of April through the last weekend in October; call (606) 663-2922 for pricing."],nearbyAttractions:["Red River Gorge Geological Area","Kentucky Reptile Zoo","Auxier Ridge Trail #204"]},{id:"dale-hollow-lake-state-resort-park",name:"Dale Hollow Lake State Resort Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/29c7ee0ff3e414c1.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Kentucky resort park on Dale Hollow Lake's Frogue Peninsula, with an 18-hole golf course and lake overlooks.",fullDescription:"Dale Hollow Lake State Resort Park covers 3,400 acres on the Frogue Peninsula, on the north shore of the 27,600-acre Dale Hollow Reservoir. The park has an 18-hole golf course, about 12 miles of multi-use trails including the 1.8-mile Eagle Point Trail to Eagle Point Overlook, and the Island View Restaurant.",thingsToDo:["Golf","Hiking to Eagle Point Overlook","Horseback riding and mountain biking","Swimming"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Eagle Point Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The park's most important birding trail leads to Eagle Point Overlook, a good spot to view raptors and waterbirds."},{name:"Boom Ridge-Eagle Point Connector Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This connector trail offers less shrub-scrub habitat and more mixed forest habitat than Eagle Point Trail."},{name:"Wolf Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"0.2 miles",description:"A spur off the Boom Ridge Trail that follows an old logging road along a ridge top."}],campingInfo:"The campground has 145 sites, 24 of them set aside for campers with horses, plus camping cabins with bunk beds and AC.",fishingBoating:"The park marina has nearly 200 slips, a fuel dock and day boat rentals, a concrete launch ramp is available, and fishing is year-round with a Kentucky Fishing License required.",wildlife:["Cooper's Hawk","Northern Bobwhite","Wild Turkey","Green Heron","Smallmouth bass"],visitorCenter:{name:"Dale Hollow Lake State Resort Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(270) 433-7431"},hours:"Check the official park website for current hours.",entranceFees:"Check the official park website for current fees.",address:"5970 State Park Rd, Burkesville, KY 42717",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Dale%20Hollow%20Lake%20State%20Resort%20Park%20kentucky",officialWebsite:"https://parks.ky.gov/burkesville/parks/resort/dale-hollow-lake-state-resort-park",bestTimeToVisit:"April through October, when lodge rooms and cabins are available daily, suits boating and fishing, while December through February offers the best eagle sightings around the lake.",tips:["No fishing pole? Ask at the lodge desk about the free fishing equipment loaner program.","Guided wild tours of Cindy Cave run Memorial Day through Labor Day ($10 per person, $8 for children 9 and under), and self-guided tours are prohibited.","WiFi is not available in the campground, and the lodge pool is for lodge guests only."],nearbyAttractions:[]},{id:"carter-caves-state-resort-park",name:"Carter Caves State Resort Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/32d2f9e6a154d236.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the rolling hills of eastern Kentucky, this park is famous for its extensive cave systems and natural bridges.",fullDescription:"Carter Caves State Resort Park is a geological wonderland featuring one of the highest concentrations of caves in Kentucky. The park offers a unique blend of outdoor adventure and comfortable resort amenities, including the Lewis Caveland Lodge. Visitors can explore subterranean passages, hike through rugged sandstone cliffs, and marvel at the natural arches that define the landscape of the Appalachian foothills.",thingsToDo:["Guided cave tours","Canoeing on Smoky Valley Lake","Miniature golf","Horseback riding","Bird watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"4C's Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"9.0 miles",description:"A challenging loop that traverses the entire park, offering views of natural bridges and deep ravines."},{name:"Natural Bridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic path leading to one of the park's most iconic natural sandstone arches."}],campingInfo:"The park features a campground with 29 sites equipped with water and electricity, plus primitive tent sites and 10 rental cottages.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red fox","Various bat species","Barred owls"],visitorCenter:{name:"Carter Caves Welcome Center",info:"Provides information on cave tour schedules, trail maps, and educational exhibits about local geology.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(606) 286-4411"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"344 Caveland Dr, Olive Hill, KY 41164",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Carter+Caves+State+Resort+Park",officialWebsite:"https://parks.ky.gov/olive-hill/parks/resort/carter-caves-state-resort-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Book cave tours in advance as they sell out quickly during peak season.","Wear sturdy hiking boots as the terrain can be rocky and slippery.","Bring a light jacket even in summer, as cave temperatures remain around 55 degrees Fahrenheit."],nearbyAttractions:["Grayson Lake State Park","Cascade Caves","City of Olive Hill","Morehead State University"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park; fees apply for cave tours and recreational activities.",fishingBoating:"Smoky Valley Lake offers excellent fishing for bass and bluegill, with seasonal canoe and pedal boat rentals available."},{id:"greenbo-lake-state-resort-park",name:"Greenbo Lake State Resort Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/68b2c69faabd9526.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the rolling hills of northeastern Kentucky, Greenbo Lake State Resort Park offers a serene escape centered around a beautiful 225-acre lake.",fullDescription:"Greenbo Lake State Resort Park is a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts, featuring a 225-acre lake that provides excellent fishing and boating opportunities. The park is situated within a lush, forested landscape that showcases the natural beauty of the Appalachian foothills. Visitors can explore miles of scenic trails, enjoy the full-service lodge, or participate in various recreational activities. The park's history is deeply connected to the region's coal mining past, and it serves as a peaceful retreat for families and nature lovers alike.",thingsToDo:["Fishing for bass, bluegill, and catfish","Boating and paddle boat rentals","Hiking on scenic woodland trails","Swimming at the seasonal outdoor pool","Playing a round of miniature golf"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Jesse Stuart Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"This trail winds through beautiful hardwood forests and offers glimpses of the local wildlife and diverse flora."},{name:"Claylick Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path perfect for families, providing scenic views of the lake and surrounding hillsides."}],campingInfo:"The park features a campground with 50 sites, including water and electric hookups, as well as a central service building with showers and restrooms.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Red-tailed hawk","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Greenbo Lake Lodge",info:"The lodge serves as the main hub for guest services, information, and park activities.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 8:00 PM",phone:"(606) 473-7324"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"965 Lodge Rd, Greenup, KY 41144",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Greenbo+Lake+State+Resort+Park",officialWebsite:"https://kentuckystateparks.reserveamerica.com/camping/greenbo-lake-state-resort-park/r/campgroundDetails.do?contractCode=KY&parkId=91906",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Bring your own fishing gear as the lake is well-stocked and popular with anglers.","Check the park website for seasonal lodge events and dining hours.","Wear sturdy hiking boots if you plan to explore the more rugged sections of the trail system."],nearbyAttractions:["Ashland, Kentucky","Carter Caves State Resort Park","Ohio River","Grayson Lake State Park"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park grounds.",fishingBoating:"The lake allows motorized boats and offers a boat ramp for public use, along with seasonal rentals for paddle boats and canoes."},{id:"my-old-kentucky-home-state-park",name:"My Old Kentucky Home State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/06719b322fd3924e.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Experience the historic charm of Federal Hill, the inspiration for Stephen Foster's famous song, located in the heart of Bardstown.",fullDescription:"My Old Kentucky Home State Park centers around the historic Federal Hill mansion, built between 1795 and 1818. The site is famously associated with Stephen Foster, who visited his cousins here and was inspired to write the state song of Kentucky. The park offers a deep dive into 19th-century plantation life, architecture, and the cultural history of the Bluegrass region.",thingsToDo:["Guided mansion tours","Golfing at the 18-hole course","Picnicking in the historic grounds","Attending the outdoor musical drama","Bird watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cedar Hill Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A gentle walking path that winds through the wooded areas of the park, offering peaceful views of the landscape."},{name:"Park Perimeter Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"This trail circles the outer edges of the park, providing a mix of open fields and shaded forest sections."}],campingInfo:"The park features a well-maintained campground with 39 sites, offering water and electrical hookups, a dump station, and modern bathhouses.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red-tailed hawk","Eastern bluebird","Gray squirrel"],visitorCenter:{name:"My Old Kentucky Home Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides tickets for mansion tours, historical exhibits, and information on park events.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(502) 348-3502"},hours:"8:00 AM - 8:00 PM daily",address:"501 E Stephen Foster Ave, Bardstown, KY 40004",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=My+Old+Kentucky+Home+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://kentuckystateparks.reserveamerica.com/camping/my-old-kentucky-home-state-park/r/campgroundDetails.do?contractCode=KY&parkId=91820",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild weather and beautiful foliage.",tips:["Book your mansion tour in advance as they fill up quickly during peak season.","Visit during the summer months to catch the outdoor musical production.","Bring comfortable walking shoes for the historic grounds and trails."],nearbyAttractions:["Bernheim Arboretum and Research Forest","Oscar Getz Museum of Whiskey History","Bardstown Historic District","Wickland, Home of Three Governors"],entranceFees:"Free park admission; mansion tours require a paid ticket.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not permitted within the park boundaries; however, nearby lakes offer excellent fishing opportunities."},{id:"pine-mountain-state-resort-park",name:"Pine Mountain State Resort Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/29afb3f675344ffd.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Kentucky's first state park, Pine Mountain offers breathtaking views of the Cumberland Mountains and a rich history of Appalachian culture.",fullDescription:"Established in 1924, Pine Mountain State Resort Park is nestled in the heart of the Appalachian Mountains. The park is famous for its rugged terrain, ancient rock formations, and the historic Wasioto Winds Golf Course. Visitors can explore the Chained Rock formation, which offers a panoramic view of the valley below, and enjoy the serene atmosphere of the surrounding hardwood forests.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and nature photography","Golfing at Wasioto Winds","Bird watching","Dining at the Mountain View Restaurant","Interpretive nature programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Chained Rock Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.7 miles",description:"A scenic trail leading to a massive boulder chained to the cliffside, offering spectacular views of Pineville."},{name:"Hemlock Garden Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, relaxing walk through a lush forest area featuring native hemlock trees and diverse wildflowers."}],campingInfo:"The park features 29 campsites with water and electric hookups, as well as a full-service lodge with 30 rooms and several cottages.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red-tailed hawks","Black bears","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Pine Mountain State Resort Park Office",info:"Provides maps, trail information, and details on park events and lodging reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(606) 337-3066"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"1050 State Park Rd, Pineville, KY 40977",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Pine+Mountain+State+Resort+Park+Pineville+KY",officialWebsite:"https://parks.ky.gov/pineville/parks/resort/pine-mountain-state-resort-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Visit the Chained Rock at sunset for the best lighting and views.","Check the park website for seasonal golf course rates at Wasioto Winds.","Bring binoculars as the park is a prime location for bird watching."],nearbyAttractions:["Cumberland Gap National Historical Park","Dr. Thomas Walker State Historic Site","City of Pineville","Kingdom Come State Park"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park grounds.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is available in nearby waterways; however, the park itself is primarily focused on hiking and mountain recreation."},{id:"john-james-audubon-state-park",name:"John James Audubon State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/210836fc40785f44.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A beautiful tribute to the famous naturalist and artist, this park features a world-class museum and extensive nature trails.",fullDescription:"John James Audubon State Park honors the life and work of the renowned bird artist who lived in Henderson, Kentucky. The park is situated on rolling hills overlooking the Ohio River and features a museum housing one of the world's largest collections of Audubon's original art. The landscape is characterized by mature hardwood forests, serene lakes, and diverse habitats that support a wide variety of bird species, reflecting the natural beauty that inspired Audubon's masterpieces.",thingsToDo:["Visit the Audubon Museum","Bird watching","Hiking on nature trails","Picnicking","Golfing at the 9-hole course"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Warbler Road Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that winds through the forest, perfect for spotting migratory birds."},{name:"Eagle Glen Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail offers more elevation changes and leads hikers through dense woodland areas."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a campground with 45 sites featuring water and electric hookups, as well as modern bathhouses and a dump station.",wildlife:["Wild Turkey","White-tailed Deer","Great Blue Heron","Red-tailed Hawk","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Audubon Museum and Nature Center",info:"Features extensive exhibits on the life of John James Audubon, his art, and local natural history.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(270) 826-2247"},hours:"8:00 AM - Sunset daily",address:"3100 US-41, Henderson, KY 42420",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=John+James+Audubon+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://parks.ky.gov/henderson/parks/historic/john-james-audubon-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for optimal bird migration viewing and comfortable hiking temperatures.",tips:["Bring binoculars for bird watching as the park is a major stop for migratory species.","Check the museum schedule before visiting as hours can vary seasonally.","Wear sturdy hiking boots if you plan to explore the more rugged sections of the trail system."],nearbyAttractions:["Henderson County Riverfront","Ellis Park Racing & Gaming","Downtown Henderson historic district"],entranceFees:"Free entrance to the park grounds; museum admission fees apply.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the park's lakes, which are stocked with bass and bluegill. No motorized boats are allowed."}],louisiana:[{id:"chicot-state-park",name:"Chicot State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/cbbd8828005b2392.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Spanning over 6,400 acres of rolling hills and water, Chicot State Park is Louisiana's largest state park and a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts.",fullDescription:"Chicot State Park is located near Ville Platte, Louisiana, and features a massive 2,000-acre man-made lake filled with largemouth bass, crappie, bluegill, and redear sunfish. The park is characterized by its diverse landscape of beech-magnolia forests and bottomland hardwoods, providing a sanctuary for a wide variety of wildlife. Established in the 1940s, the park offers a rich history of Civilian Conservation Corps development and remains a central hub for camping, hiking, and water-based recreation in the region.",thingsToDo:["Fishing and boating on Chicot Lake","Hiking the multi-use trail system","Birdwatching and wildlife photography","Camping in modern cabins or RV sites","Visiting the Louisiana State Arboretum"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Chicot State Park Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"20 miles",description:"A long-distance trail that loops around the lake, offering hikers a chance to experience the diverse forest ecosystems and scenic water views."},{name:"Arboretum Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle path through the Louisiana State Arboretum, showcasing native trees and plants with educational signage along the way."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a variety of camping options including premium campsites with water and electrical hookups, primitive tent sites, and several comfortable cabins overlooking the lake.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Raccoons","Great blue herons","Alligators"],visitorCenter:{name:"Chicot State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, trail maps, fishing regulations, and educational exhibits about the local flora and fauna.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(337) 363-2403"},hours:"6:00 AM - 9:00 PM daily",address:"3469 Chicot Park Rd, Ville Platte, LA 70586",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Chicot+State+Park+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-holds/chicot-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and peak bird migration.",tips:["Bring insect repellent as the wooded areas can have mosquitoes.","Book your campsite or cabin well in advance, especially for weekends.","Visit the Louisiana State Arboretum located within the park boundaries for a unique botanical experience."],nearbyAttractions:["Ville Platte","Mamou","Opelousas","Cajun Music Hall of Fame"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; free for seniors 62+ and children 3 and under.",fishingBoating:"The park features multiple boat launches and a boathouse. Fishing is excellent for bass and panfish; a valid Louisiana fishing license is required."},{id:"fontainebleau-state-park",name:"Fontainebleau State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/54c5b204cad171b9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the north shore of Lake Pontchartrain, this park offers stunning views and the historic ruins of a 19th-century sugar mill.",fullDescription:"Fontainebleau State Park is situated on the site of a sugar plantation established in 1829 by Bernard de Marigny de Mandeville. The park encompasses 2,800 acres of diverse ecosystems, including pine forests, marshes, and hardwood forests. Visitors can explore the hauntingly beautiful ruins of the old sugar mill and enjoy panoramic views of Lake Pontchartrain from the sandy beach.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and nature walking","Swimming at the sandy beach","Bird watching","Picnicking","Camping","Exploring historic sugar mill ruins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.25 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the park's diverse forest habitats and offers excellent opportunities for wildlife viewing."},{name:"Old Rail Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.1 miles",description:"A flat, paved trail that connects the park to the surrounding community, perfect for cycling and walking."}],campingInfo:"The park features 126 campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as several premium cabins located along the lakefront.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Great blue herons","Ospreys","Alligators","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Fontainebleau State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, local flora and fauna, and assistance with camping reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(985) 624-4443"},hours:"7:00 AM - 9:00 PM daily",address:"62883 LA-1089, Mandeville, LA 70448",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Fontainebleau+State+Park+Mandeville+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-preserves/fontainebleau-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and active bird migration.",tips:["Bring insect repellent, especially during the warmer months.","Visit the sugar mill ruins at sunset for the best photography lighting.","Check the official website for seasonal swimming advisories at the beach."],nearbyAttractions:["Mandeville Lakefront","Abita Springs","Covington Historic District","Honey Island Swamp"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; children under 3 and seniors 62+ are free.",fishingBoating:"The park offers a boat launch for access to Lake Pontchartrain and excellent pier fishing opportunities."},{id:"grand-isle-state-park",name:"Grand Isle State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/406a40a47a38c2d3.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Grand Isle State Park offers a unique coastal experience where visitors can enjoy the Gulf of Mexico's pristine beaches and world-class saltwater fishing.",fullDescription:"Located on a barrier island at the end of Louisiana Highway 1, Grand Isle State Park is the only state-owned beach in Louisiana. The park provides a sanctuary for migratory birds and offers visitors a chance to explore the unique ecosystem of the Gulf Coast. With its history shaped by hurricanes and the resilience of the local community, the park serves as a vital recreational hub for swimming, birding, and fishing.",thingsToDo:["Saltwater fishing","Bird watching","Swimming","Beachcombing","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, scenic trail that winds through the coastal dunes and provides excellent opportunities for bird watching."},{name:"Beach Walk",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.0 miles",description:"A relaxing walk along the shoreline of the Gulf of Mexico, perfect for finding seashells and enjoying the ocean breeze."}],campingInfo:"The park features 49 premium campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as a designated tent camping area near the beach.",wildlife:["Brown pelicans","Roseate spoonbills","Bottlenose dolphins","Ghost crabs","Various migratory songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Grand Isle State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park history, local wildlife, and current beach conditions.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(985) 787-2559"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"1 Admiral Craik Dr, Grand Isle, LA 70358",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Grand+Isle+State+Park+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-holds/grand-isle-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and peak bird migration.",tips:["Bring plenty of sunscreen and insect repellent as the coastal sun and mosquitoes can be intense.","Check the official website for beach closure notices due to high tides or weather events.","Visit during the spring migration to see hundreds of species of birds passing through the island."],nearbyAttractions:["Elmer's Island Wildlife Refuge","Grand Isle Butterfly Dome","Port Fourchon","Caminada Headland"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; children 3 and under and seniors 62+ are free.",fishingBoating:"The park features a 400-foot fishing pier and excellent surf fishing opportunities for speckled trout, redfish, and flounder."},{id:"bogue-chitto-state-park",name:"Bogue Chitto State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/4b5c2cf2b6a3fff4.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the rolling hills of Washington Parish, Bogue Chitto State Park offers a diverse landscape ranging from cypress-tupelo swamps to upland forests.",fullDescription:"Bogue Chitto State Park is a 1,786-acre site that showcases the natural beauty of Louisiana's Florida Parishes. The park is defined by the Bogue Chitto River, which winds through the property, creating dramatic bluffs and sandy beaches. Visitors can explore a variety of ecosystems, including hardwood forests and lush wetlands, making it a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts seeking both adventure and tranquility.",thingsToDo:["Canoeing and kayaking on the Bogue Chitto River","Hiking and mountain biking on the Gorge Run Trail","Fishing for bass and bream","Picnicking in designated shaded areas","Bird watching and wildlife photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Gorge Run Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"4.5 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through the park's unique hilly terrain and offers views of the river bluffs."},{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A gentle loop perfect for families, showcasing the diverse flora and fauna of the local hardwood forest."}],campingInfo:"The park features 81 campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as 13 premium cabins available for overnight stays.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","River otters","Various species of turtles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bogue Chitto State Park Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park activities, trail maps, and educational exhibits about the local ecosystem.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(985) 839-5707"},hours:"7:00 AM - 9:00 PM daily",address:"17049 State Park Blvd, Franklinton, LA 70438",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bogue+Chitto+State+Park+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-holds/bogue-chitto-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal outdoor activity conditions.",tips:["Bring insect repellent, especially during the warmer months.","Book your campsite or cabin well in advance as they fill up quickly on weekends.","Check the river levels before planning a kayaking trip to ensure safe conditions."],nearbyAttractions:["Franklinton","Global Wildlife Center","Covington","Abita Springs"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; free for seniors 62+ and children under 3.",fishingBoating:"The park offers a boat launch for easy access to the Bogue Chitto River and several fishing ponds stocked with freshwater fish."},{id:"bayou-segnette-state-park",name:"Bayou Segnette State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-bayou-segnette-state-park-louisiana.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located just across the Mississippi River from New Orleans, this park offers a unique blend of swampy wetlands and modern recreational facilities.",fullDescription:"Bayou Segnette State Park provides access to both freshwater and saltwater environments, making it a premier destination for anglers and nature enthusiasts. The park features a wave pool, boat launches, and a variety of habitats ranging from cypress swamps to marshlands, showcasing the diverse ecosystem of coastal Louisiana.",thingsToDo:["Swimming in the wave pool","Boating and fishing","Bird watching","Picnicking","Hiking nature trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nutria Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"2.8 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through the swampy terrain, offering excellent opportunities to spot local wildlife."},{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible loop perfect for families looking to experience the park's diverse flora."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 98 campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as 16 vacation cabins located on the water.",wildlife:["Alligators","Nutria","Great Blue Herons","Egrets","Armadillos"],visitorCenter:{name:"Bayou Segnette Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park activities, trail maps, and educational exhibits about the local wetlands.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(504) 736-7140"},hours:"8:00 AM - 5:00 PM daily",address:"7777 Westbank Expressway, Westwego, LA 70094",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Bayou+Segnette+State+Park+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-holds/bayou-segnette-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for milder temperatures and active wildlife.",tips:["Bring plenty of insect repellent, especially during warmer months.","Check the wave pool schedule in advance as it is seasonal.","Early morning is the best time to spot alligators and migratory birds."],nearbyAttractions:["French Quarter, New Orleans","Jean Lafitte National Historical Park and Preserve","Audubon Aquarium of the Americas"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; children under 3 and seniors 62+ are free.",fishingBoating:"The park features a public boat launch providing access to Bayou Segnette and the surrounding waterways for both freshwater and saltwater fishing."},{id:"poverty-point-reservoir-state-park",name:"Poverty Point Reservoir State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-poverty-point-reservoir-state-park-louisiana.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A premier destination for water sports and outdoor recreation, featuring a massive 2,700-acre reservoir in Northeast Louisiana.",fullDescription:"Poverty Point Reservoir State Park offers a unique blend of modern recreational facilities and natural beauty. The park is centered around a 2,700-acre man-made reservoir that provides excellent opportunities for boating, fishing, and water skiing. The area is rich in history, located near the ancient Poverty Point World Heritage Site, and features diverse wetlands and pine forests that provide a habitat for a variety of wildlife.",thingsToDo:["Boating and water skiing","Fishing for bass and crappie","Bird watching","Picnicking","Hiking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.75 miles",description:"A gentle loop trail that winds through the wooded areas of the park, perfect for spotting local birds and wildlife."},{name:"Reservoir Perimeter Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"Offers scenic views of the water and access to various fishing spots along the shoreline."}],campingInfo:"The park features 54 premium campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as 8 deluxe cabins overlooking the reservoir.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Great blue herons","Osprey","Alligators","Various species of turtles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Poverty Point Reservoir Visitor Center",info:"Provides information on park activities, fishing regulations, and local history, along with a gift shop.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(318) 878-7536"},hours:"6:00 AM - 9:00 PM daily",address:"1500 Bayou Macon Rd, Delhi, LA 71232",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Poverty+Point+Reservoir+State+Park+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-holds/poverty-point-reservoir-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and optimal fishing conditions.",tips:["Bring insect repellent, especially during the warmer months.","Check the official website for current fishing tournament schedules that may affect boat ramp availability.","Book your cabin or campsite well in advance as they fill up quickly on weekends."],nearbyAttractions:["Poverty Point World Heritage Site","Black Bear Golf Club","Town of Delhi","Tensas River National Wildlife Refuge"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; free for seniors 62+ and children 3 and under.",fishingBoating:"The park features a world-class boat launch and a marina. Fishing is excellent for largemouth bass, crappie, and bluegill."},{id:"lake-fausse-pointe-state-park",name:"Lake Fausse Pointe State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/efb8c57e8ef397fa.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the heart of the Atchafalaya Basin, this park offers a gateway to the unique beauty of Louisiana's swamp and bayou landscapes.",fullDescription:"Lake Fausse Pointe State Park is located on the edge of the Atchafalaya Basin, the nation's largest river swamp. The park provides visitors with a chance to explore the diverse ecosystem of the bayou, featuring elevated boardwalks, cypress-tupelo forests, and extensive waterways. Historically, the area was once part of the Atchafalaya spillway, and today it serves as a premier destination for those looking to experience the authentic Cajun wilderness through boating, fishing, and wildlife observation.",thingsToDo:["Canoeing and kayaking through bayou trails","Fishing for bass, crappie, and bream","Bird watching in the Atchafalaya Basin","Hiking the elevated boardwalk trails","Picnicking in shaded pavilion areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"The Boardwalk Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"An accessible, elevated wooden path that winds through the cypress swamp, offering excellent views of the water and local flora."},{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A winding trail that takes hikers deeper into the hardwood forest, providing opportunities to spot deer and various bird species."}],campingInfo:"The park features 48 campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as 18 waterfront cabins that offer a comfortable stay directly on the bayou.",wildlife:["American Alligator","Great Blue Heron","White-tailed Deer","Nutria","Barred Owl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Lake Fausse Pointe Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides educational exhibits on the Atchafalaya Basin ecosystem, park maps, and assistance from knowledgeable park rangers.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(337) 229-4764"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"5400 Levee Rd, St. Martinville, LA 70582",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Lake+Fausse+Pointe+State+Park+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-preserves/lake-fausse-pointe-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall and Spring, when temperatures are mild and mosquito activity is lower.",tips:["Bring plenty of insect repellent, as the swamp environment can be buggy.","Rent a canoe or kayak from the park office to explore the water trails properly.","Check the water levels before your trip, as high water can occasionally impact trail accessibility."],nearbyAttractions:["St. Martinville","Longfellow-Evangeline State Historic Site","Breaux Bridge","Atchafalaya National Wildlife Refuge"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; children under 3 and seniors 62+ are free.",fishingBoating:"The park offers a boat launch for motorized boats and provides rentals for canoes and kayaks to navigate the scenic bayou waterways."},{id:"palmetto-island-state-park",name:"Palmetto Island State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/9a620525df8e6ba9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled along the Vermilion River, this park offers a lush, tropical escape featuring native palmetto trees and diverse wetland ecosystems.",fullDescription:"Palmetto Island State Park is a unique natural area in Vermilion Parish, Louisiana, characterized by its dense stands of dwarf palmetto trees and mature hardwood forests. The park provides visitors with an immersive experience in the Louisiana bayou landscape, featuring a boat launch, hiking trails, and a water playground. The geology is defined by the meandering Vermilion River, which creates a rich habitat for diverse wildlife and provides excellent opportunities for paddling and fishing.",thingsToDo:["Canoeing and kayaking on the Vermilion River","Hiking through palmetto-filled forests","Picnicking in shaded pavilions","Bird watching in the wetlands","Enjoying the seasonal water playground"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Palmetto Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the heart of the park's signature palmetto groves and hardwood forest."},{name:"River Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"Follows the banks of the Vermilion River, offering excellent views of the water and opportunities to spot local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park features 96 campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as six modern cabins available for rent, nestled in a quiet, wooded setting.",wildlife:["Alligators","White-tailed deer","Great blue herons","River otters","Various species of snakes and turtles"],visitorCenter:{name:"Palmetto Island Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides information on park history, local ecology, and trail maps, along with a small gift shop.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(337) 893-3930"},hours:"Daily: 7:00 AM - 9:00 PM",address:"19021 Verot School Rd, Abbeville, LA 70510",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Palmetto+Island+State+Park+LA",officialWebsite:"https://www.lastateparks.com/parks-holds/palmetto-island-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall and Spring, when temperatures are mild and the humidity is lower.",tips:["Bring plenty of insect repellent, especially during the warmer months.","Check the water playground schedule before visiting if you are traveling with children.","Early morning is the best time to spot alligators and other wildlife along the river."],nearbyAttractions:["Abbeville","Avery Island (Tabasco Factory)","Rip Van Winkle Gardens","Lafayette"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per person; free for seniors 62+ and children 3 and under.",fishingBoating:"The park features a boat launch providing access to the Vermilion River, popular for freshwater fishing and recreational boating."}],maryland:[{id:"assateague-state-park",name:"Assateague State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/46a8b5e0122719cb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Maryland barrier-island park on Assateague Island's north end, with ocean beach and wild horses.",fullDescription:"Assateague State Park sits at the north end of Assateague Island in Worcester County, between the Atlantic and Sinepuxent Bay, reached by the Verrazano Bridge and bordered by Assateague Island National Seashore. Feral horses roam freely on the beach, marshes and campground. The campground has 350 sites, and the restored 18th-century Rackliffe House holds a heritage center.",thingsToDo:["Swimming","Wild horse viewing","Kayaking","Camping","Rackliffe House"],hikingTrails:[],campingInfo:"The campground has 342 campsites and 3 Youth Group sites, each with a fire ring and picnic table, and most sites can be reserved from the last week of April through the last weekend of October at 1-888-432-2267 or parkreservations.maryland.gov.",fishingBoating:"The park's two miles of ocean beaches offer fishing (not permitted in the guarded swim sector), and the bayside lets visitors explore secluded coves by canoe or kayak.",wildlife:["Feral horses","Whitetail deer","Sika deer","Red fox","Osprey"],visitorCenter:{name:"Assateague State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"410-641-2120"},hours:"Day use area: 7 a.m. to sunset, year-round",entranceFees:"$5.00 per vehicle after Labor Day to the Friday before Memorial Day; $5.00 per person Memorial Day weekend through Labor Day",address:"7307 Stephen Decatur Highway, Berlin, MD 21811",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Assateague%20State%20Park%20maryland",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/pages/eastern/assateague.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through October suits this oceanfront barrier-island park: lifeguards are on duty Memorial Day to Labor Day, and campsites can be reserved through the last weekend of October.",tips:["Stay at least 40 feet (a bus length) from the wild horses. Do not stop or park along roadways, and secure all food, because the horses bite, kick and can carry rabies.","When the contact booth is unstaffed off-season, entry uses a self-registration honor system, and payment is cash only in the provided envelope.","Lifeguards are on duty only from Memorial Day to Labor Day, 10 a.m. to 5 p.m. Surfing, kayaking and fishing are not allowed in the flagged swim sector."],nearbyAttractions:["Assateague Island National Seashore","Historic Rackliffe House","Snow Hill"]},{id:"cunningham-falls-state-park",name:"Cunningham Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/746c83496a2cb3b4.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Catoctin Mountain park with 78-foot Cunningham Falls, Maryland's largest cascading waterfall, and a lake.",fullDescription:"Cunningham Falls State Park lies west of Thurmont on Catoctin Mountain. It is home to 78-foot Cunningham Falls, the largest cascading waterfall in Maryland, a 43-acre man-made lake, and the ruins of a historic Catoctin iron furnace. A boardwalk trail leads to the falls.",thingsToDo:["Hiking to the falls","Swimming at the lake","Boating","Camping","Catoctin Furnace ruins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cunningham Falls Nature Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"This is the park's most popular trail and leads to Cunningham Falls, the largest cascading waterfall in Maryland."},{name:"Cunningham Falls Lower Trail and Cliff Trail Loop",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"Not listed",description:"This loop near Thurmont reaches Maryland's tallest waterfall and has both easy and rugged terrain."},{name:"Catoctin Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"27 miles",description:"The longest trail in the park area, the Catoctin Trail runs 27 miles starting from Gambrill."}],campingInfo:"Campsites and camper cabins are available from April until October at the William Houck Area and Manor Area campgrounds.",fishingBoating:"The lake offers fishing, canoeing, boat rentals and a boat launch (no gasoline motors), and there is an accessible fishing pier in the William Houck Area.",wildlife:[],visitorCenter:{name:"Cunningham Falls State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"301-271-7574"},hours:"8 a.m. to sunset, April–October; 10 a.m. to sunset, November–March",entranceFees:"Off-peak (after Labor Day weekend to the Friday of Memorial Day weekend) and Manor Area: $3/vehicle MD residents, $5/vehicle non-residents. Houck Area, Memorial Day–Labor Day: per person, $3 MD/$5 non-MD on weekdays and $5 MD/$7 non-MD on weekends and holidays.",address:"14274 William Houck Dr, Thurmont, MD 21788",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Cunningham%20Falls%20State%20Park%20maryland",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/pages/western/cunningham.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall weekdays are ideal, because summer weekends and holidays often fill the park to capacity. The Maple Syrup Festival runs the 1st and 2nd weekends in March.",tips:["On warm spring and summer weekends and holidays, the Lake, Beach and Falls Area may close when full. Arrive early, and if your group is in several vehicles, arrive at the same time, since late cars are turned away even if others are already inside.","Cell service is limited, so do not rely on cell phones for communication in the park.","Campsites M1–M31 are at the Manor Area Campground, 6709 Cunningham Falls Park Rd, not at the main park address. Camping permits are also provided there."],nearbyAttractions:["Catoctin Mountain Park","Catoctin Wildlife Preserve and Zoo","Museum of the Ironworker"]},{id:"patapsco-valley-state-park",name:"Patapsco Valley State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/21e69b12bf4fe8a9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Maryland's largest state park, stretching 32 miles along the Patapsco River near Baltimore.",fullDescription:"Patapsco Valley State Park runs 32 miles along the Patapsco River south and west of Baltimore and covers over 14,000 acres, making it Maryland's largest state park; it dates from 1906. Its areas include McKeldin, with multi-use trails and a disc golf course, and Avalon, with the Swinging Bridge and a visitor center in an old mill building.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Mountain biking","Horseback riding","Fishing","Canoeing","Camping"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Grist Mill Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The official park page offers a Grist Mill Trail self-guided hike."},{name:"Medicine Tree Trail",difficulty:"Not listed",length:"Not listed",description:"The official park page lists the Medicine Tree Trail among its suggested activities."}],campingInfo:"Camping is offered, with campgrounds in the Hilton and Hollofield Areas, and reservations can be made at 1-888-432-CAMP or parkreservations.maryland.gov.",fishingBoating:"The park offers fishing and canoeing along the Patapsco River, and fishing and boat launch use are permitted outside regular park hours.",wildlife:["Barred owl","Deer","Bald eagle","Heron","Geese"],visitorCenter:{name:"Patapsco Valley State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"410-461-5005"},hours:"9 a.m. to sunset",entranceFees:"Apr–Oct: weekdays $2/vehicle MD residents, $4/vehicle out-of-state; weekends & holidays $3/person in-state, $5/person out-of-state",address:"8020 Baltimore National Pike, Ellicott City, MD 21043",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Patapsco%20Valley%20State%20Park%20maryland",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/pages/central/patapsco.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Fall is a great time to visit for foliage and owl-spotting, and weekdays or early mornings help you avoid weekend capacity closures.",tips:["Arrive early on weekends and summer holidays, because the Avalon, Hollofield and Daniels areas often close when they reach capacity.","Bring a park map with you.","Pets are prohibited in the Hilton Area campground and the Pickall Area, so check the pet policy before you bring a dog."],nearbyAttractions:["B&O Ellicott City Station Museum","Clark's Elioak Farm","Centennial Park"]},{id:"deep-creek-lake-state-park",name:"Deep Creek Lake State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-deep-creek-lake-state-park-maryland.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Deep Creek Lake State Park offers a stunning mountain retreat featuring Maryland's largest freshwater lake and extensive recreational opportunities.",fullDescription:"Located in Garrett County, Deep Creek Lake State Park encompasses over 1,800 acres of forest and shoreline. The park provides access to the 3,900-acre Deep Creek Lake, which was originally created in the 1920s for hydroelectric power. The area is characterized by its rolling Appalachian mountain terrain, diverse hardwood forests, and rich biodiversity, making it a premier destination for outdoor enthusiasts year-round.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the sandy beach","Boating and kayaking","Fishing for bass and trout","Picnicking in designated areas","Cross-country skiing in winter"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Meadow Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"5.5 miles",description:"This trail offers a scenic trek through dense forest and provides glimpses of local wildlife and native flora."},{name:"Indian Turnip Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A gentle, family-friendly loop that winds through the park's wooded areas, perfect for birdwatching."}],campingInfo:"The park features 112 campsites, including 26 sites with electric hookups, and offers modern bathhouses with hot showers.",wildlife:["Black bear","White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Red-tailed hawk","Great blue heron"],visitorCenter:{name:"Discovery Center",info:"The Discovery Center features interactive exhibits on the natural history, geology, and cultural heritage of the Deep Creek Lake area.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(301) 387-7067"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"898 State Park Road, Swanton, MD 21561",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Deep+Creek+Lake+State+Park+Maryland",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/Pages/western/deepcreek.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Summer for water activities or Autumn for spectacular fall foliage.",tips:["Arrive early on summer weekends as the park often reaches capacity by mid-morning.","Check the water quality reports on the official website before swimming.","Bring bug spray, especially if you plan on hiking the wooded trails."],nearbyAttractions:["Wisp Resort","Swallow Falls State Park","Garrett State Forest","Town of Oakland"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per vehicle for Maryland residents, $5.00 per vehicle for out-of-state visitors.",fishingBoating:"The park provides a public boat launch and a designated fishing area; a valid Maryland fishing license is required."},{id:"swallow-falls-state-park",name:"Swallow Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/422992d7502848a9.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to Maryland's highest free-falling waterfall, Swallow Falls State Park features breathtaking scenery along the Youghiogheny River.",fullDescription:"Swallow Falls State Park is located nine miles north of Oakland, Maryland, and is home to Muddy Creek Falls, a spectacular 53-foot waterfall. The park's landscape is defined by the Youghiogheny River, which flows through rocky gorges and ancient hemlock forests. The area is geologically significant for its rugged terrain and provides a glimpse into the natural beauty of the Allegheny Mountains.",thingsToDo:["Hiking the Canyon Trail","Photography of waterfalls","Picnicking in designated areas","Camping in the hemlock forest","Fishing in the Youghiogheny River"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Canyon Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"This trail winds along the edge of the Youghiogheny River and provides excellent views of Muddy Creek Falls and Swallow Falls."},{name:"Tolliver Falls Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that leads visitors to the smaller but picturesque Tolliver Falls, perfect for families."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 65 campsites, including some with electric hookups, nestled within a beautiful old-growth hemlock forest. Reservations are highly recommended during peak season.",wildlife:["Black bears","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Various songbirds","Raccoons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Swallow Falls Ranger Station",info:"The station provides park maps, safety information, and educational displays about the local ecosystem.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(301) 387-6938"},hours:"8:00 AM to Sunset daily",address:"2470 Maple Glade Rd, Oakland, MD 21550",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Swallow+Falls+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/Pages/western/swallowfalls.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring for high water levels or autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Wear sturdy hiking boots as the trails near the river can be slippery and rocky.","Visit on a weekday to avoid heavy crowds during the summer months.","Pack a camera, as the waterfall views are some of the best in the state."],nearbyAttractions:["Herrington Manor State Park","Deep Creek Lake","Garrett State Forest","Oakland, MD"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per vehicle for Maryland residents and $5.00 per vehicle for out-of-state visitors.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in the Youghiogheny River, which is a popular spot for trout fishing. No boat ramps are available within the park."},{id:"sandy-point-state-park",name:"Sandy Point State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/847e4b9a6d39e9fb.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"A premier outdoor recreation area located on the northwestern shore of the Chesapeake Bay, offering stunning views of the Bay Bridge.",fullDescription:"Sandy Point State Park is a 786-acre Maryland State Park located along the northwestern shore of the Chesapeake Bay. The park provides a variety of recreational activities, including swimming, fishing, crabbing, and boating. Its strategic location offers visitors unparalleled views of the iconic William Preston Lane Jr. Memorial Bridge, commonly known as the Chesapeake Bay Bridge. The park's diverse landscape includes sandy beaches, marshlands, and forest areas, making it a vital habitat for local wildlife and a popular destination for nature enthusiasts.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the public beach","Fishing and crabbing from the jetties","Boating and boat launching","Picnicking in designated areas","Bird watching along the shoreline"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Symbi Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, scenic nature trail that winds through the wooded areas of the park, perfect for a quick walk."},{name:"Shoreline Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2 miles",description:"A flat path that follows the coastline, offering excellent opportunities for bird watching and bay views."}],campingInfo:"Sandy Point State Park does not offer overnight camping or cabin rentals.",wildlife:["Osprey","Great Blue Heron","White-tailed Deer","Diamondback Terrapin","Various migratory waterfowl"],visitorCenter:{name:"Sandy Point Ranger Station",info:"The ranger station provides park information, maps, and assistance with facility rentals and park regulations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(410) 974-2149"},hours:"7:00 AM to Sunset daily",address:"1100 East College Parkway, Annapolis, MD 21409",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Sandy+Point+State+Park+Annapolis+MD",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/Pages/southern/sandypoint.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for swimming and outdoor activities.",tips:["The park reaches capacity quickly on summer weekends; arrive early in the morning.","Check the official website for real-time park closure updates during peak season.","Bring your own fishing gear and bait if you plan to fish from the jetties."],nearbyAttractions:["Historic Annapolis","United States Naval Academy","Quiet Waters Park","Chesapeake Bay Environmental Center"],entranceFees:"Fees range from $4.00 to $7.00 per person depending on residency and season; check the official website for current pricing.",fishingBoating:"The park features a large boat ramp facility with multiple lanes and a dedicated fishing area on the jetties."},{id:"elk-neck-state-park",name:"Elk Neck State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-elk-neck-state-park-maryland-by.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on a peninsula between the Elk and Northeast Rivers, this park offers stunning views of the Chesapeake Bay and diverse recreational opportunities.",fullDescription:"Elk Neck State Park is situated on a peninsula in Cecil County, Maryland, where the Elk and Northeast Rivers meet the Chesapeake Bay. The park's unique geology features sandy shorelines, marshlands, and wooded bluffs. It is home to the historic Turkey Point Lighthouse, which has guided mariners since 1833. The park provides a rich habitat for diverse wildlife and offers visitors a chance to explore the natural beauty of the upper Chesapeake region.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Camping","Boating","Fishing","Swimming","Bird watching"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Turkey Point Lighthouse Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.7 miles",description:"A scenic, mostly flat trail that leads visitors through a wooded area to the historic Turkey Point Lighthouse with panoramic views of the bay."},{name:"Beaver Marsh Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.5 miles",description:"This trail winds through diverse forest habitats and offers excellent opportunities for observing local wildlife and wetland ecosystems."}],campingInfo:"The park features several camping loops with over 250 campsites, including options for tent camping, RVs with electric hookups, and rustic cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Bald eagles","Wild turkeys","Ospreys","Great blue herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Elk Neck State Park Office",info:"The park office provides maps, trail information, and assistance with camping reservations and park regulations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(410) 287-5333"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"4395 Turkey Point Rd, North East, MD 21901",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Elk+Neck+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/Pages/central/elkneck.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild weather and migratory bird watching.",tips:["Visit the Turkey Point Lighthouse early in the morning to avoid crowds and enjoy the best lighting for photography.","Check the park website for seasonal swimming beach closures before planning your trip.","Bring insect repellent, especially if you plan to hike the marsh trails during the summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["North East Community Park","Mount Harmon Plantation","Havre de Grace","Fair Hill Natural Resources Management Area"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per vehicle for Maryland residents and $5.00 per vehicle for out-of-state visitors.",fishingBoating:"The park offers a boat launch ramp on the Northeast River and designated areas for shoreline fishing."},{id:"rocky-gap-state-park",name:"Rocky Gap State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/0047e7a2e0726bd4.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the mountains of Allegany County, Rocky Gap State Park features a stunning 243-acre lake and rugged mountain scenery.",fullDescription:"Rocky Gap State Park is located in Allegany County, Maryland, and encompasses over 3,000 acres of public land. The park is defined by the dramatic Rocky Gap Run, which cuts through a narrow gorge, and the expansive Lake Habeeb, known for its exceptionally clear water. The area is geologically significant, featuring the Evitts Mountain and the rugged terrain of the Appalachian region, providing a diverse habitat for wildlife and a scenic backdrop for outdoor recreation.",thingsToDo:["Swimming at the public beach","Boating and kayaking on Lake Habeeb","Hiking the mountain trails","Fishing for trout and bass","Picnicking in designated areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Evitts Homesite Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.2-mile loop",description:"A gentle trail that winds through the forest and offers a glimpse into the historical homesteading of the region."},{name:"Canyon Overlook Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail provides hikers with elevated views of the rugged gorge and the surrounding mountain landscape."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a large campground with 278 campsites, including sites with electric hookups and several mini-cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Black bear","Red-tailed hawk","Great blue heron"],visitorCenter:{name:"Rocky Gap Ranger Station",info:"Provides park information, trail maps, and assistance with camping reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(301) 722-1480"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"12500 Pleasant Valley Rd, Flintstone, MD 21530",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Rocky+Gap+State+Park",officialWebsite:"https://dnr.maryland.gov/publiclands/Pages/western/rockygap.aspx",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for water activities and fall foliage viewing.",tips:["Lake Habeeb is famous for its clear water, so bring goggles for swimming.","Make camping reservations well in advance during the summer months as sites fill up quickly.","Check the official website for seasonal swimming beach hours and lifeguard availability."],nearbyAttractions:["Cumberland, MD","Green Ridge State Forest","Western Maryland Scenic Railroad","C&O Canal National Historical Park"],entranceFees:"$3.00 per vehicle for Maryland residents; $5.00 per vehicle for out-of-state visitors.",fishingBoating:"Lake Habeeb is a popular fishing destination stocked with trout; boat rentals including kayaks and canoes are available seasonally."}],mississippi:[{id:"tishomingo-state-park",name:"Tishomingo State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/d391e17276eac497.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Mississippi park in the Appalachian foothills on the Natchez Trace, known for Bear Creek Canyon's sandstone outcrops.",fullDescription:"Tishomingo State Park lies about 45 miles northeast of Tupelo at Milepost 304 of the Natchez Trace Parkway. Its main feature is Bear Creek Canyon and its large sandstone outcrops. Seven trails of up to 3 miles pass springs, waterfalls, cliffs and rock walls, and the park has CCC-era facilities, cabins and campsites.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Canoeing Bear Creek","Rock climbing (permit)","Fishing","Camping and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"CCC Camp Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"3.6 miles",description:"An easy hiking route that winds through Tishomingo State Park."}],campingInfo:"The park offers an RV campground, a tent campground, cabins/cottages and a group camp, with overnight camping reservable through MDWFP.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is available in Bear Creek and in 45-acre Haynes Lake, which is stocked with catfish, bream and bass and has a boat launch; a seasonal 6.25-mile canoe float trip runs down Bear Creek.",wildlife:["Warblers","Vireos","Catfish","Bream","Bass"],visitorCenter:{name:"Tishomingo State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(662) 438-6914"},hours:"8 AM to 5 PM, seven days a week",entranceFees:"$2.00 per person; age 5 and under free",address:"105 County Road 90, Tishomingo, MS 38873",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tishomingo%20State%20Park%20mississippi",officialWebsite:"https://www.mdwfp.com/parks-destinations/park/tishomingo-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall are ideal: spring brings wildflowers along the trails and the seasonal Bear Creek float trip, and fall offers mild weather among the hardwood forests.",tips:["The Bear Creek canoe float trip is seasonal and requires an advance reservation through the park office at (662) 438-6914. It costs $40, allows a maximum of two people per canoe, and participants must report to the Park Office at 9:00 am.","Swimming is not allowed in Haynes Lake or Bear Creek. Use the park's swimming pool instead.","A Mississippi fishing license is required for ages 16 to 65 and can be purchased at the park office."],nearbyAttractions:["J.P. Coleman State Park","Bay Springs Lake","Iuka Mineral Springs Park"]},{id:"percy-quin-state-park",name:"Percy Quin State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-percy-quin-state-park-mississippi-day.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Mississippi park near McComb on Lake Tangipahoa, with cabins, a lodge and an 18-hole golf course.",fullDescription:"Percy Quin State Park lies about 7 miles southwest of McComb off I-55 and surrounds 490-acre Lake Tangipahoa, an impoundment of the Tangipahoa River. It has campsites, cabins, villas and a lodge, a 4-mile nature trail and the 18-hole Quail Hollow golf course.",thingsToDo:["Boating and waterskiing","Fishing","Golf (Quail Hollow)","Nature trail","Camping and cabins"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Percy Quin State Park Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"6.8 miles",description:"An out-and-back trail with about 265 ft of elevation gain that takes about 2–2.5 hours to complete."}],campingInfo:"The park offers an RV campground, a tent campground and cabins/cottages, with reservations available for day use and overnight camping.",fishingBoating:"The 490-acre Lake Tangipahoa has a boat launch (fee; daily or annual permits), a covered 36-slip marina, a fish cleaning station, and allows water and jet skiing.",wildlife:["alligators","deer","geese","largemouth bass","crappie"],visitorCenter:{name:"Percy Quin State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(601) 684-3938"},hours:"8 AM to 5 PM, seven days a week",entranceFees:"$2.00 per person; age 5 and under free",address:"2036 Percy Quin Drive, McComb, MS 39648",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Percy%20Quin%20State%20Park%20mississippi",officialWebsite:"https://www.mdwfp.com/parks-destinations/park/percy-quin-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and fall usually bring milder weather for hiking and lake activities in southern Mississippi, while summer is hot but suits water skiing and swimming.",tips:["Register at the ranger station/gate house when checking in to park facilities.","The boat launch on Lake Tangipahoa requires a fee, with daily or annual permits available.","Use of the five tennis courts is included with the $2.00 day-use entry fee. Call the park office to reserve one of the four picnic pavilions."],nearbyAttractions:["The Black History Gallery"]},{id:"j-p-coleman-state-park",name:"J.P. Coleman State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-j-p-coleman-state-park-mississippi.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Mississippi park on the Tennessee River and Pickwick Lake north of Iuka, known for smallmouth bass fishing.",fullDescription:"J.P. Coleman State Park lies ten miles north of Iuka on the banks of the Tennessee River and 47,500-acre Pickwick Lake, and is named for former Mississippi governor James P. Coleman. The lake is known for smallmouth bass. The park has campsites, cabins, cottages, a motel, a swimming pool and miniature golf.",thingsToDo:["Fishing","Boating and waterskiing","Swimming pool","Camping and cabins","Miniature golf"],hikingTrails:[],campingInfo:"The park offers RV and tent campgrounds plus cabins/cottages, and you check in for camping at the visitor center in the J.P. Coleman Lodge.",fishingBoating:"Pickwick Lake, a 47,500-acre freshwater lake, offers fishing, boating and water skiing, and the park has a boat launch ramp with trailer parking and a marina with fuel sales.",wildlife:["Smallmouth bass","Crappie","Bream","Catfish","Sauger"],visitorCenter:{name:"J.P. Coleman State Park Visitor Center",info:"Check the official park website for visitor center details.",phone:"(662) 423-6515"},hours:"8 AM to 5 PM, seven days a week",entranceFees:"$2.00 per person; ages 5 and under free",address:"613 CR 321, Iuka, MS 38852",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=J.P.%20Coleman%20State%20Park%20mississippi",officialWebsite:"https://www.mdwfp.com/parks-destinations/park/jp-coleman-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early fall is best for water sports and the seasonal swimming pool and miniature golf, while cooler months suit quieter fishing and camping.",tips:["The marina fuel dock runs 24/7 with a card reader, but it sells only unleaded non-ethanol fuel and no diesel.","The pool is open seasonally Thursday through Sunday, 10 a.m. to 6 p.m. It has no lifeguards, armbands are required, and children under 16 need an adult.","Register at the visitor center in the J.P. Coleman Lodge to use park facilities and to check in for lodging and camping."],nearbyAttractions:["Tishomingo State Park","Cooper Falls","Grand Harbor Resort & Marina"]},{id:"clarkco-state-park",name:"Clarkco State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-clarkco-state-park-mississippi.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the rolling hills of East Mississippi, Clarkco State Park offers a serene escape with lush pine forests and a beautiful 65-acre lake.",fullDescription:"Clarkco State Park is a hidden gem located in the pine-forested hills of Clarke County. Established in 1938, the park features a 65-acre lake that serves as the centerpiece for outdoor recreation. The park's landscape is characterized by towering loblolly pines and hardwoods, providing a tranquil environment for visitors to enjoy nature, history, and outdoor activities. It is a popular destination for families and outdoor enthusiasts looking for a peaceful retreat in the heart of Mississippi.",thingsToDo:["Fishing in the 65-acre lake","Boating and canoeing","Hiking through pine forests","Picnicking in designated areas","Camping in RV or tent sites"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through the dense pine forest, offering great opportunities for bird watching and spotting local wildlife."},{name:"Lake Perimeter Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail follows the shoreline of the lake, providing scenic water views and access to various fishing spots."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 43 RV campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as primitive tent sites. Additionally, there are 20 cabins available for rent overlooking the lake.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Squirrels","Various songbirds","Great blue herons"],visitorCenter:{name:"Clarkco State Park Office",info:"The main office provides park information, camping registration, and assistance with facility rentals.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(601) 776-6651"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"281 Clarkco Park Rd, Quitman, MS 39355",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Clarkco+State+Park+Quitman+MS",officialWebsite:"https://visitmississippi.org/things-to-do/outdoors/clarkco-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and beautiful foliage.",tips:["Make cabin reservations well in advance as they fill up quickly during peak seasons.","Bring insect repellent, especially during the warmer summer months.","Check the official website for current fishing regulations and permit requirements."],nearbyAttractions:["Quitman","Meridian","Bonita Lakes Park"],entranceFees:"$4.00 per vehicle entrance fee.",fishingBoating:"The 65-acre lake is stocked with bass, bream, and catfish. Boat ramps are available for private boats, and electric trolling motors are permitted."},{id:"roosevelt-state-park",name:"Roosevelt State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-roosevelt-state-park-mississippi.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located in the Bienville National Forest, Roosevelt State Park offers a scenic escape with beautiful pine forests and the tranquil Shadow Lake.",fullDescription:"Roosevelt State Park is situated in the heart of Mississippi, offering a diverse landscape of rolling hills and lush forests. Named after President Franklin D. Roosevelt, the park was developed during the 1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps. It features the 114-acre Shadow Lake, which serves as a centerpiece for water-based recreation, and provides a peaceful retreat for nature enthusiasts, families, and outdoor adventurers alike.",thingsToDo:["Fishing in Shadow Lake","Boating and canoeing","Swimming in the park pool","Picnicking in designated areas","Hiking the nature trails"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through the pine forest, offering great opportunities for bird watching and spotting local flora."},{name:"Lakeview Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail follows the perimeter of Shadow Lake, providing scenic water views and varied terrain."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 109 RV campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as 15 vacation cabins overlooking the lake.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Various songbirds","Squirrels"],visitorCenter:{name:"Roosevelt State Park Office",info:"The main office provides park information, check-in services for campers, and maps of the trail system.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(601) 732-6316"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"2149 Highway 13 South, Morton, MS 39117",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Roosevelt+State+Park+Morton+MS",officialWebsite:"https://visitmississippi.org/things-to-do/outdoors/roosevelt-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and beautiful foliage.",tips:["Make cabin reservations well in advance as they are popular year-round.","Bring insect repellent during the warmer months.","Check the