s and lush forest trails.",fullDescription:"Tombigbee State Park is located just outside of Tupelo and provides a scenic retreat centered around a 100-acre lake. Established in the 1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps, the park features historic stone structures and offers a glimpse into the natural beauty of the region. The park's geology is characterized by the rolling hills of the Tombigbee River valley, providing diverse habitats for local wildlife and excellent opportunities for outdoor recreation.",thingsToDo:["Fishing in the 100-acre lake","Disc golf on the 18-hole course","Picnicking in designated areas","Hiking through wooded trails","Boating and water sports"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop that winds through the hardwood forest, offering great opportunities for birdwatching."},{name:"Ridge Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail traverses the higher elevations of the park, providing scenic overlooks of the lake below."}],campingInfo:"The park features 20 RV campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as several rustic cabins available for rent overlooking the lake.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Great blue heron","Squirrels","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Tombigbee State Park Office",info:"The main office provides park information, maps, and assistance with camping and cabin reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(662) 842-7669"},hours:"Sunrise to Sunset daily",address:"264 Cabin Dr, Tupelo, MS 38804",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Tombigbee+State+Park+Tupelo+MS",officialWebsite:"https://visitmississippi.org/things-to-do/camping/tombigbee-state-park/",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Bring your own fishing gear as the lake is well-stocked with bass and bream.","Check the official website for seasonal disc golf tournament closures.","Reservations for cabins are highly recommended during peak summer months."],nearbyAttractions:["Natchez Trace Parkway","Elvis Presley Birthplace","Tupelo Buffalo Park and Zoo","Downtown Tupelo"],entranceFees:"$4.00 per vehicle entrance fee",fishingBoating:"The park offers a boat ramp for private watercraft and allows fishing in the lake with a valid Mississippi fishing license."}],"north carolina":[{id:"jockeys-ridge-state-park",name:"Jockey's Ridge State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/4b6fbea151c12f51.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to the tallest living sand dune on the Atlantic coast, Jockey's Ridge offers breathtaking views of the Outer Banks and unique recreational opportunities.",fullDescription:"Jockey's Ridge State Park is a geological marvel located in Nags Head, North Carolina. It features the tallest active sand dune system in the eastern United States. The park's landscape is constantly shifting due to the powerful winds coming off the Atlantic Ocean and the Roanoke Sound. Historically, the area has been a hub for aviation pioneers, including the Wright brothers who frequented the dunes for their early gliding experiments. Today, the park serves as a protected environment for coastal flora and fauna while providing a premier destination for hang gliding, kite flying, and hiking.",thingsToDo:["Hang gliding","Kite flying","Hiking the dunes","Picnicking","Educational programs"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Tracks in the Sand Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.1 miles",description:"A short, accessible boardwalk trail that provides an introduction to the dune ecosystem."},{name:"Soundside Nature Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.3 miles",description:"A scenic loop that winds through the maritime thicket and leads to the shores of the Roanoke Sound."}],campingInfo:"There is no overnight camping permitted within the boundaries of Jockey's Ridge State Park.",wildlife:["Ghost crabs","Hognose snakes","Ospreys","Brown pelicans","Red foxes"],visitorCenter:{name:"Jockey's Ridge Visitor Center",info:"Features interactive exhibits on dune ecology, local history, and the science of wind and sand.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(252) 441-7132"},hours:"8:00 AM to sunset daily",address:"300 W. Carolista Drive, Nags Head, NC 27959",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Jockey's+Ridge+State+Park+NC",officialWebsite:"https://ncparks.gov/state-parks/jockeys-ridge-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for milder temperatures and consistent winds for kite flying.",tips:["Bring plenty of water as the sand can get extremely hot during summer months.","Wear sturdy footwear or be prepared to hike barefoot, as the sand can reach temperatures over 100 degrees.","Visit during the late afternoon for spectacular sunset views over the Roanoke Sound."],nearbyAttractions:["Wright Brothers National Memorial","Roanoke Island Festival Park","Bodine Island Lighthouse","Jennette's Pier"],entranceFees:"Admission to the park is free of charge.",fishingBoating:"While there is no boat ramp, the park offers access to the Roanoke Sound for non-motorized watercraft like kayaks and paddleboards."},{id:"stone-mountain-state-park",name:"Stone Mountain State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/541727deca923baa.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Stone Mountain State Park is a premier destination in North Carolina, famous for its massive 600-foot granite dome and stunning cascading waterfalls.",fullDescription:"Stone Mountain State Park encompasses over 14,000 acres of rugged terrain in the Blue Ridge Mountains. The park is anchored by a spectacular granite pluton that rises 600 feet above the surrounding landscape. Formed millions of years ago, this geological wonder offers panoramic views of the Blue Ridge Escarpment. The park also features historic homesteads, including the mid-19th century Hutchinson Homestead, providing a glimpse into early mountain life.",thingsToDo:["Rock climbing 