climbing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Profile Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7.2 miles round trip",description:"A challenging climb that leads hikers through diverse forest types to the ridgeline, offering stunning views of the Profile rock formation."},{name:"Grandfather Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"2.4 miles one way",description:"A technical trail featuring ladders and cables that traverses the rugged spine of the mountain, connecting several peaks."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 13 primitive, backcountry campsites located along the trails. Permits are required for all overnight stays, and there are no amenities like water or toilets at these sites.",wildlife:["Black bear","White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Peregrine falcon","Red squirrel"],visitorCenter:{name:"Grandfather Mountain State Park Office",info:"The park office provides trail maps, backcountry permits, and information regarding park regulations and safety.",hours:"Monday-Friday: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(828) 963-9522"},hours:"Open daily from sunrise to sunset",address:"9872 NC Highway 105 South, Banner Elk, NC 28604",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Grandfather+Mountain+State+Park+NC",officialWebsite:"https://www.ncparks.gov/grandfather-mountain-state-park/home",bestTimeToVisit:"Late spring through early autumn for mild temperatures and clear views.",tips:["Always check the weather forecast, as conditions at high elevations can change rapidly.","Bring plenty of water, as there are no reliable water sources along the backcountry trails.","Wear sturdy, broken-in hiking boots due to the rocky and uneven terrain."],nearbyAttractions:["Linville Falls","Banner Elk","Blowing Rock","Blue Ridge Parkway"],entranceFees:"There is no entrance fee for Grandfather Mountain State Park, though backcountry camping permits require a small fee.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in designated areas in accordance with North Carolina Wildlife Resources Commission regulations; no boating facilities are available."},{id:"carolina-beach-state-park",name:"Carolina Beach State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/988fbc64c36ac009.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the banks of the Cape Fear River, this park is famous for its rare Venus flytraps and diverse coastal habitats.",fullDescription:"Carolina Beach State Park offers a unique blend of coastal environments, including sand dunes, salt marshes, and pine forests. It is internationally recognized for being one of the few places where the rare Venus flytrap grows naturally in the wild. The park's history dates back to the Civil War, with remnants of Sugarloaf, a massive sand dune used as a strategic lookout point, still visible today.",thingsToDo:["Hiking through rare carnivorous plant habitats","Boating and fishing on the Cape Fear River","Camping in wooded sites","Birdwatching along the riverfront","Picnicking in shaded areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Flytrap Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short, accessible loop that takes visitors through the habitat of the rare Venus flytrap."},{name:"Sugarloaf Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.0 miles",description:"A scenic trail that winds through maritime forest and leads to the historic Sugarloaf sand dune overlooking the river."}],campingInfo:"The park features 83 campsites, including sites with full hookups for RVs and tent-only sites, all nestled within a beautiful coastal forest.",wildlife:["Venus flytraps","Ospreys","White-tailed deer","Gray foxes","Various shorebirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Carolina Beach State Park Visitor Center",info:"Features educational exhibits on the park's unique carnivorous plants and local coastal ecology.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(910) 458-8206"},hours:"8:00 AM - Sunset daily",address:"1010 State Park Rd, Carolina Beach, NC 28428",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Carolina+Beach+State+Park+NC",officialWebsite:"https://www.ncparks.gov/state-parks/carolina-beach-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and blooming wildflowers.",tips:["Visit in the spring to see the Venus flytraps in their prime blooming season.","Bring insect repellent, especially during the warmer months near the marsh areas.","Check the park website for guided ranger programs regarding the carnivorous plants."],nearbyAttractions:["Carolina Beach Boardwalk","Fort Fisher State Historic Site","Wilmington Riverwalk","Kure Beach"],entranceFees:"There is no daily vehicle entrance fee for Carolina Beach State Park.",fishingBoating:"The park provides a public boat ramp with access to the Cape Fear River and the Intracoastal Waterway, along with excellent pier fishing opportunities."}],oklahoma:[{id:"beavers-bend-state-park",name:"Beavers Bend State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-beavers-bend-state-park-oklahoma.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the scenic Ouachita Mountains, Beavers Bend State Park offers a lush retreat featuring towering pines, crystal-clear waters, and abundant outdoor recreation.",fullDescription:"Located in southeastern Oklahoma, Beavers Bend State Park is a premier destination known for its dramatic landscape of pine-covered hills and the pristine Mountain Fork River. The park was developed in the 1930s by the Civilian Conservation Corps, and many of the original stone structures remain today. It serves as a gateway to Broken Bow Lake, providing visitors with a unique blend of rugged wilderness and accessible amenities, making it a favorite for nature enthusiasts and families alike.",thingsToDo:["Fly fishing on the Mountain Fork River","Boating and water sports on Broken Bow Lake","Hiking through pine forests","Train rides on the Beavers Bend Depot and Trail Rides","Golfing at the Cedar Creek Golf Course"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Friends Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A scenic loop that offers beautiful views of the Mountain Fork River and unique rock formations."},{name:"Cedar Bluff Nature Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.0 mile",description:"This trail winds through the forest and provides elevated views overlooking the river valley."}],campingInfo:"The park features multiple campgrounds with over 300 campsites ranging from primitive to full RV hookups, plus 47 cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Bald eagles","Wild turkeys","Black bears","Various species of owls"],visitorCenter:{name:"Forest Heritage Center",info:"Features exhibits on the history of forestry in Oklahoma, including dioramas and historical tools.",hours:"Daily: 10:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(580) 494-6300"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for park access",address:"4350 S Highway 259A, Broken Bow, OK 74728",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Beavers+Bend+State+Park+OK",officialWebsite:"https://www.travelok.com/state-parks/beavers-bend-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Book cabins well in advance as they fill up months ahead of time.","Bring water shoes if you plan to wade in the Mountain Fork River.","Check the park website for seasonal burn bans before planning a campfire."],nearbyAttractions:["Broken Bow","Hochatown","Ouachita National Forest","Beavers Bend Wildlife Museum"],entranceFees:"No general entrance fee, though specific activities like boat rentals or golf have associated costs.",fishingBoating:"The Mountain Fork River is famous for trout fishing, while Broken Bow Lake offers excellent opportunities for bass fishing, boating, and jet skiing."},{id:"robbers-cave-state-park",name:"Robbers Cave State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/51383ef943a9a34a.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the scenic Sans Bois Mountains, this park is famous for its rugged sandstone cliffs and history as a hideout for outlaws like Jesse James.",fullDescription:"Robbers Cave State Park spans over 8,000 acres of forested hills and rocky bluffs. Historically significant as a refuge for notorious outlaws, the park offers a unique blend of geological wonder and frontier history. Visitors can explore the famous cave, hike through dense hardwood forests, and enjoy the serene waters of Lake Carlton and Coon Creek.",thingsToDo:["Hiking and rock climbing","Fishing in Lake Carlton","Horseback riding","Canoeing and paddle boating","Wildlife viewing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Robbers Cave Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A short but steep climb leading directly to the historic cave entrance with panoramic views of the valley."},{name:"Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"3.2 miles",description:"A challenging loop that traverses the rugged ridges of the Sans Bois Mountains, offering excellent vantage points."}],campingInfo:"The park features multiple campgrounds with RV hookups, tent sites, and a lodge. There are also several cabins available for rent, including some with modern amenities.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bobcats","Red-tailed hawks","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Robbers Cave Nature Center",info:"Provides educational exhibits on local geology, history, and wildlife, along with park maps and information.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(918) 465-2562"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for day-use and camping",address:"2850 NW 137th St, Wilburton, OK 74578",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Robbers+Cave+State+Park+OK",officialWebsite:"https://www.travelok.com/state-parks/robbers-cave-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Wear sturdy hiking boots as the terrain around the cave is rocky and uneven.","Bring a flashlight if you plan to explore the interior of the cave.","Book cabins well in advance as they are highly popular during peak seasons."],nearbyAttractions:["Wilburton","Talimena National Scenic Byway","Lake Wister State Park"],entranceFees:"No daily vehicle entrance fee; camping and cabin fees apply.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Lake Carlton, Coon Creek, and Wayne Wallace Reservoir. Boat ramps are available for non-motorized and small motorized watercraft."},{id:"gloss-mountain-state-park",name:"Gloss Mountain State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/05ef77b085b3aeec.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Famous for its sparkling selenite crystals, Gloss Mountain offers dramatic mesa views and unique geological formations in Major County.",fullDescription:"Gloss Mountain State Park, also known as the Glass Mountains, is a geological wonder located in northwestern Oklahoma. The mountains are named for the high concentration of selenite crystals found in the gypsum-capped mesas, which shimmer like glass in the sunlight. The park features a steep trail leading to the top of Cathedral Mountain, providing panoramic views of the surrounding red-bed plains and the unique landscape shaped by erosion over millions of years.",thingsToDo:["Hiking to the top of Cathedral Mountain","Photography of unique geological formations","Bird watching","Picnicking in designated areas","Stargazing"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Cathedral Mountain Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A steep climb featuring stairs that leads visitors to the summit of the mesa for expansive views of the surrounding countryside."},{name:"Base Loop Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A gentle path around the base of the mountains, perfect for viewing the selenite crystals up close."}],campingInfo:"There is no overnight camping available at Gloss Mountain State Park. The park is strictly for day-use activities.",wildlife:["Coyotes","Red-tailed hawks","Mule deer","Scorpions","Various lizard species"],visitorCenter:{name:"Gloss Mountain Information Kiosk",info:"The park provides informational signage regarding the geology and history of the area at the trailhead.",