ey","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","Various songbirds"],visitorCenter:{name:"Greenleaf State Park Office",info:"Provides park information, maps, and assistance with cabin and campsite reservations.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 4:30 PM",phone:"(918) 487-5622"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for registered campers; day use from sunrise to sunset.",address:"15429 Greenleaf Rd, Braggs, OK 74423",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Greenleaf+State+Park+Braggs+OK",officialWebsite:"https://www.travelok.com/state-parks/greenleaf-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Fall for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Bring insect repellent during the warmer months.","Book cabins well in advance as they are very popular.","Check the official website for current lake water levels before planning water activities."],nearbyAttractions:["Tenkiller State Park","Sequoyah National Wildlife Refuge","Muskogee","Fort Gibson Lake"],entranceFees:"No daily vehicle entrance fee; camping and cabin fees vary by site type.",fishingBoating:"The park features a boat ramp, a fishing dock, and a marina where visitors can enjoy boating and fishing for largemouth bass, channel catfish, and sunfish."},{id:"natural-falls-state-park",name:"Natural Falls State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/6d681b487a1fc720.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Home to a stunning 77-foot waterfall, this park offers a serene escape into the lush, forested landscape of the Ozark Highlands.",fullDescription:"Natural Falls State Park is located in the scenic Ozark Highlands region of northeastern Oklahoma. The park is famous for its 77-foot waterfall, which cascades through a narrow, rock-lined canyon. The area features a unique microclimate that supports a variety of ferns and mosses, creating a lush, temperate rainforest-like environment. Historically, the site was a popular destination for locals and was featured in the movie 'Where the Red Fern Grows'.",thingsToDo:["Waterfall viewing","Hiking","Bird watching","Picnicking","Photography"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Dripping Springs Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.2 miles",description:"This trail leads visitors down into the canyon floor, offering spectacular views of the waterfall and the surrounding rock formations."},{name:"Red Fern Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"0.5 miles",description:"A gentle path that winds through the hardwood forest, perfect for a quick nature walk and spotting local wildlife."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 44 RV sites with water and electric hookups, as well as several tent campsites. There are also yurts available for rent for a unique glamping experience.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Various songbirds","Squirrels"],visitorCenter:{name:"Natural Falls Visitor Center",info:"The visitor center provides information on park history, trail maps, and local flora and fauna.",hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(918) 422-5802"},hours:"8:00 AM - 10:00 PM daily",address:"19225 E 578 Rd, Colcord, OK 74338",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Natural+Falls+State+Park+OK",officialWebsite:"https://www.travelok.com/state-parks/natural-falls-state-park",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring and Autumn for mild temperatures and vibrant foliage.",tips:["Wear sturdy hiking shoes as the trail to the waterfall base can be slippery.","Bring a camera with a tripod to capture the best long-exposure shots of the waterfall.","Visit during the week to avoid weekend crowds and enjoy a quieter experience."],nearbyAttractions:["Siloam Springs, AR","Lake Eucha","Spavinaw Wildlife Management Area"],entranceFees:"There is no daily entrance fee, but camping and yurt rental fees apply.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is not permitted within the waterfall area to protect the delicate ecosystem, but nearby lakes offer ample fishing opportunities."},{id:"sequoyah-state-park",name:"Sequoyah State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/venues/park-sequoyah-state-park-oklahoma.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Located on the shores of Fort Gibson Lake, Sequoyah State Park is Oklahoma's largest state park, offering extensive outdoor recreation and scenic waterfront views.",fullDescription:"Sequoyah State Park, named after the famous Cherokee scholar who developed the Cherokee syllabary, spans over 3,000 acres along the banks of Fort Gibson Lake. The park features diverse terrain ranging from dense woodlands to open meadows, providing a habitat for a wide variety of wildlife. It is a premier destination for water sports, equestrian activities, and family camping, serving as a hub for outdoor enthusiasts in eastern Oklahoma.",thingsToDo:["Boating and fishing on Fort Gibson Lake","Horseback riding at the Sequoyah Riding Stables","Golfing at the Sequoyah Bay Golf Course","Wildlife viewing at the Nature Center","Swimming at the designated beach areas"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Three Forks Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop trail that winds through wooded areas and offers excellent opportunities for birdwatching."},{name:"Sequoyah Lakeview Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"3.2 miles",description:"This trail follows the shoreline of Fort Gibson Lake, providing panoramic water views and access to secluded coves."}],campingInfo:"The park offers a wide range of camping options, including over 200 RV sites with full or partial hookups, numerous tent sites, and several climate-controlled cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkey","Bald eagles","Great blue herons","Bobcats"],visitorCenter:{name:"Sequoyah State Park Nature Center",info:"The nature center features educational exhibits on local flora and fauna, as well as live animal displays and information on park history.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 4:00 PM",phone:"(918) 772-2545"},hours:"Open 24 hours daily for camping; day-use areas open from 6:00 AM to 10:00 PM",address:"17131 Park 10, Hulbert, OK 74441",goo