 Park offers stunning granite mountain views and pristine mountain lakes.",fullDescription:"Table Rock State Park encompasses over 3,000 acres of rugged mountain terrain in the South Carolina Upstate. The park is famous for the massive granite dome of Table Rock Mountain, which rises above the surrounding forest. Built by the Civilian Conservation Corps in the 1930s, the park features historic cabins and lodge structures that reflect the rustic architectural style of the era. Visitors can explore diverse ecosystems ranging from hardwood forests to mountain streams and waterfalls.",thingsToDo:["Hiking","Swimming","Fishing","Birdwatching","Picnicking"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Table Rock Trail",difficulty:"Strenuous",length:"7.2 miles round-trip",description:"A challenging climb to the summit of Table Rock Mountain offering panoramic views of the Blue Ridge Escarpment."},{name:"Carrick Creek Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"1.9-mile loop",description:"A scenic trail that follows the creek, passing several small waterfalls and lush forest vegetation."}],campingInfo:"The park offers 94 campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as several historic cabins available for rent.",wildlife:["Black bears","White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Red-tailed hawks","Salamanders"],visitorCenter:{name:"Table Rock State Park Nature Center",info:"Provides educational exhibits on local geology, flora, and fauna, along with park maps and ranger assistance.",hours:"Daily: 9:00 AM - 5:00 PM",phone:"(864) 878-9813"},hours:"Daily: 8:00 AM - 9:00 PM",address:"158 Ellison Ln, Pickens, SC 29671",googleMapsLocation:"https://maps.google.com/?q=Table+Rock+State+Park+SC",officialWebsite:"https://southcarolinaparks.com/table-rock",bestTimeToVisit:"Spring for wildflowers or Autumn for vibrant fall foliage.",tips:["Arrive early on weekends as the parking lots for popular trails fill up quickly.","Wear sturdy hiking boots, as the trails are rocky and can be slippery.","Check the weather forecast before attempting the summit trail, as conditions change rapidly at higher elevations."],nearbyAttractions:["Caesars Head State Park","Jones Gap State Park","Pickens County Museum","Downtown Greenville"],entranceFees:"$6.00 for adults, $3.75 for SC seniors, $3.50 for children ages 6-15, free for children 5 and under.",fishingBoating:"Fishing is permitted in Lake Oolenoy and Lake Pinnacle. Non-motorized boats and electric trolling motors are allowed on the lakes."},{id:"devils-fork-state-park",name:"Devils Fork State Park",image:"https://kosher-travel-img.sites.tab.bot/wm/1dfa605f4c0e4338.webp",photoGallery:[],shortDescription:"Nestled in the foothills of the Blue Ridge Mountains, Devils Fork State Park offers unparalleled access to the pristine, spring-fed waters of Lake Jocassee.",fullDescription:"Devils Fork State Park is the only public access point to the 7,500-acre Lake Jocassee, a deep, clear mountain lake surrounded by steep, forested slopes. The park is renowned for its unique geology, including rare flora like the Oconee Bell, and its status as a premier destination for scuba diving, trout fishing, and boating. The area was once part of the Cherokee Nation and remains a place of significant natural beauty and historical intrigue.",thingsToDo:["Scuba diving in the clear lake waters","Trout fishing","Boating and kayaking","Hiking mountain trails","Picnicking by the lake"],hikingTrails:[{name:"Oconee Bell Nature Trail",difficulty:"Easy",length:"1.5 miles",description:"A gentle loop trail that winds through lush forest, famous for blooming Oconee Bells in the spring."},{name:"Bear Cove Trail",difficulty:"Moderate",length:"2.0 miles",description:"This trail offers scenic views of the lake and passes through diverse hardwood forests."}],campingInfo:"The park features 59 paved campsites with water and electrical hookups, as well as 20 lakeside villas available for rent.",wildlife:["White-tailed deer","Wild turkeys","Black bears","Bald eagles","Ospreys"],visitorC